export const PAGE_INTERVIEW_QUESTIONS = {
  'accessibility-testing': [
    { id: 'IQ-ACC-001', question: 'What is WCAG 2.1 and why is it important for banking applications?', answer: 'WCAG 2.1 (Web Content Accessibility Guidelines) is a set of recommendations for making web content more accessible to people with disabilities. In banking, it ensures that all customers including those with visual, auditory, motor, or cognitive impairments can access critical financial services. RBI mandates digital inclusion, making WCAG compliance essential for Indian banks. Non-compliance can lead to regulatory penalties and exclusion of a significant customer base.', difficulty: 'Easy', category: 'Concept' },
    { id: 'IQ-ACC-002', question: 'How do you test screen reader compatibility for a banking application?', answer: 'Screen reader testing involves using tools like NVDA, JAWS, or VoiceOver to navigate through the banking application. You verify that all interactive elements like account balance displays, fund transfer forms, and transaction history tables are properly announced. ARIA labels must be correctly applied to custom components. You also ensure logical reading order and that dynamic content updates like OTP timers or transaction confirmations are announced to assistive technology users.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Practical' },
    { id: 'IQ-ACC-003', question: 'What are the key accessibility concerns in UPI payment flows?', answer: 'UPI payment flows must ensure that the VPA input field has proper labels, amount fields have clear instructions, and the UPI PIN entry screen is accessible via screen readers without exposing sensitive data. Timer-based OTP screens need sufficient time allowances for users with motor impairments. Error messages for failed transactions must be descriptively announced. Color contrast ratios must meet AA standards so visually impaired users can distinguish success and failure states.', difficulty: 'Hard', category: 'Scenario' },
    { id: 'IQ-ACC-004', question: 'What tools do you use for automated accessibility testing in web banking portals?', answer: 'Common tools include axe-core for automated WCAG rule checking, Lighthouse for accessibility audits, WAVE for visual feedback on accessibility issues, and Pa11y for CI/CD integration. For Indian banking apps, you also test with screen readers like NVDA and verify keyboard navigation across all CBS module screens. Automated tools catch about 30-40% of issues; manual testing with assistive technologies is essential for comprehensive coverage.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Technical' },
    { id: 'IQ-ACC-005', question: 'How would you handle accessibility testing for a multi-language banking application supporting Hindi and English?', answer: 'You must verify that screen readers correctly pronounce content in both Hindi and English by checking lang attributes on HTML elements. Font rendering must maintain readability at 200% zoom for both scripts. Bidirectional text handling should be tested where Hindi and English content coexist. All ARIA labels must be localized, and error messages in both languages must be semantically meaningful. Form validation messages should be in the user selected language and announced by assistive technologies.', difficulty: 'Hard', category: 'Scenario' },
    { id: 'IQ-ACC-006', question: 'What is the difference between WCAG Level A, AA, and AAA compliance?', answer: 'Level A is the minimum accessibility standard covering basic requirements like text alternatives for images and keyboard accessibility. Level AA addresses the most common barriers including color contrast ratios of 4.5:1 and consistent navigation, and is the standard most banking regulators including RBI recommend. Level AAA is the highest standard with requirements like sign language interpretation and enhanced contrast of 7:1. Most banking applications target AA compliance as AAA is often impractical for all content.', difficulty: 'Easy', category: 'Concept' },
    { id: 'IQ-ACC-007', question: 'Describe how you would test keyboard navigation in an internet banking dashboard.', answer: 'You start by ensuring all interactive elements receive focus via Tab key in a logical order matching the visual layout. Verify that account summary cards, transaction filters, fund transfer buttons, and menu items are all reachable without a mouse. Test that focus indicators are visible with sufficient contrast. Ensure modal dialogs like session timeout warnings trap focus correctly. Test Escape key to close modals, Enter to activate buttons, and arrow keys for dropdown menus and date pickers used in statement downloads.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Practical' },
    { id: 'IQ-ACC-008', question: 'How do you ensure PDF bank statements are accessible?', answer: 'Accessible PDF statements require proper document structure with tagged headings, tables, and reading order. Transaction tables must have header cells associated with data cells. Images like bank logos need alt text. The PDF must be compatible with screen readers and allow text selection for copy-paste of IFSC codes and account numbers. You test using PAC (PDF Accessibility Checker) and Adobe Acrobat accessibility checker. RBI guidelines recommend providing HTML alternatives alongside PDF statements.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Technical' },
    { id: 'IQ-ACC-009', question: 'What accessibility challenges arise with CAPTCHA in banking login pages and how do you address them?', answer: 'Visual CAPTCHAs are inaccessible to blind users and difficult for users with cognitive disabilities. Banking apps should provide audio CAPTCHA alternatives with clear playback controls. Modern approaches include invisible reCAPTCHA or device-based authentication like biometric login which avoids CAPTCHA entirely. For Indian banking, Aadhaar-based biometric authentication can serve as an accessible alternative. Any CAPTCHA timeout must be generous enough for users with motor impairments to complete the challenge.', difficulty: 'Hard', category: 'Scenario' }
  ],
  'advanced-testing': [
    { id: 'IQ-ADV-001', question: 'What is mutation testing and how does it improve test quality in banking software?', answer: 'Mutation testing introduces small deliberate changes (mutants) into the source code to verify that existing tests can detect these changes. In banking software, this is crucial because undetected mutations in interest calculation or transaction processing logic could cause financial losses. If a test suite fails to kill a mutant such as changing a greater-than to less-than in overdraft checking, it reveals gaps in test coverage. Tools like PIT for Java and mutmut for Python are commonly used.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Concept' },
    { id: 'IQ-ADV-002', question: 'Explain property-based testing and give a banking domain example.', answer: 'Property-based testing generates random inputs to verify that certain properties always hold true, unlike example-based testing which uses specific inputs. In banking, a property could be that the sum of all debit transactions minus credit transactions equals the net balance change. Another property is that transferring money between two accounts in the same bank never changes the total system balance. Tools like Hypothesis for Python generate thousands of test cases automatically, catching edge cases humans might miss such as floating-point rounding in interest calculations.', difficulty: 'Hard', category: 'Technical' },
    { id: 'IQ-ADV-003', question: 'How do you implement chaos engineering in a banking microservices environment?', answer: 'Chaos engineering involves deliberately injecting failures to test system resilience. In banking, you might simulate CBS database latency, NPCI gateway timeouts, or network partitions between payment microservices. Tools like Chaos Monkey or LitmusChaos inject controlled failures in non-production environments. You verify that NEFT/RTGS transactions gracefully handle downstream failures with proper retry mechanisms and that account balances remain consistent. Start with game days in staging before progressing to controlled production experiments with blast radius limits.', difficulty: 'Hard', category: 'Practical' },
    { id: 'IQ-ADV-004', question: 'What is contract testing and why is it important for banking APIs?', answer: 'Contract testing verifies that API providers and consumers agree on the interface contract without requiring integration tests. In banking, where multiple teams build microservices for accounts, payments, and loans, contract testing using tools like Pact ensures that changes to the account service API do not break the loan service that depends on it. This is critical for UPI and IMPS integrations where NPCI defines strict API contracts. Consumer-driven contracts let each team deploy independently while maintaining compatibility.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Concept' },
    { id: 'IQ-ADV-005', question: 'Describe how you would implement visual regression testing for a banking application.', answer: 'Visual regression testing captures screenshots of UI components and compares them against baselines to detect unintended visual changes. For banking apps, you use tools like Percy, Applitools, or BackstopJS to monitor critical screens like account dashboards, fund transfer forms, and statement views. You configure acceptable diff thresholds to ignore anti-aliasing differences. Test across multiple browsers and resolutions since banking customers use diverse devices. Pay special attention to numeric formatting of amounts, IFSC code displays, and transaction status indicators.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Technical' },
    { id: 'IQ-ADV-006', question: 'What is fuzz testing and how would you apply it to banking API endpoints?', answer: 'Fuzz testing sends malformed, unexpected, or random data to API endpoints to discover crashes, memory leaks, or security vulnerabilities. For banking APIs, you fuzz account number fields with special characters, amount fields with negative values and extreme numbers, and IFSC code fields with SQL injection payloads. Tools like AFL, Burp Suite Intruder, or RESTler systematically generate malformed inputs. This is critical for NEFT/RTGS APIs where input validation failures could lead to transaction fraud or system crashes.', difficulty: 'Hard', category: 'Practical' },
    { id: 'IQ-ADV-007', question: 'How does combinatorial testing reduce the test case count while maintaining coverage?', answer: 'Combinatorial testing uses algorithms like pairwise or t-way covering arrays to test all combinations of parameter values with fewer test cases. For a banking loan application with fields like loan type, tenure, interest rate, and collateral type, full combinatorial testing might require thousands of cases. Pairwise testing ensures every pair of parameter values is covered in at least one test case, typically reducing test cases by 80% while catching most interaction bugs. Tools like PICT or AllPairs generate these optimized test suites.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Concept' },
    { id: 'IQ-ADV-008', question: 'Explain the concept of metamorphic testing with a banking example.', answer: 'Metamorphic testing addresses the oracle problem by defining relationships between inputs and outputs of successive test executions. In banking, if you calculate EMI for a loan of Rs 10 lakhs at 10% for 20 years, doubling the principal should exactly double the EMI amount. If transferring Rs 1000 from A to B followed by Rs 500 from B to A results in certain balances, performing a single transfer of Rs 500 from A to B should yield the same final balances. This technique catches subtle calculation errors that individual test cases might miss.', difficulty: 'Hard', category: 'Technical' },
    { id: 'IQ-ADV-009', question: 'What is the role of AI/ML in test case generation for banking applications?', answer: 'AI/ML can analyze production logs and user behavior patterns to generate test cases that cover real-world scenarios. In banking, ML models can identify common transaction patterns like salary credits followed by EMI debits and generate test sequences mimicking actual user flows. AI can predict high-risk code changes likely to introduce defects in interest calculation modules. Tools like Diffblue for Java auto-generate unit tests. ML-based visual testing tools like Applitools use AI to distinguish meaningful UI changes from noise in banking dashboards.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Concept' }
  ],
  'agentic-ai-lab': [
    { id: 'IQ-AAL-001', question: 'What is agentic AI and how does it differ from traditional AI in software testing?', answer: 'Agentic AI refers to AI systems that can autonomously plan, execute, and adapt testing strategies without explicit step-by-step instructions. Unlike traditional AI which performs specific tasks like image classification, agentic AI can independently explore a banking application, identify testable flows, generate test cases, execute them, and report findings. It uses LLM-based reasoning to understand context, make decisions about what to test next, and adapt its approach based on results. This is particularly valuable for complex banking workflows involving multiple CBS modules.', difficulty: 'Easy', category: 'Concept' },
    { id: 'IQ-AAL-002', question: 'How would you design an agentic AI testing system for UPI payment validation?', answer: 'The system would use an LLM agent with tools for API interaction, database querying, and assertion checking. The agent receives a high-level goal like validate UPI payment flow and autonomously plans test scenarios including valid payments, insufficient balance, invalid VPA, timeout handling, and duplicate transaction detection. It executes each scenario via NPCI sandbox APIs, validates responses against expected outcomes, and generates detailed reports. The agent adapts by exploring edge cases discovered during execution such as concurrent payments from the same account.', difficulty: 'Hard', category: 'Scenario' },
    { id: 'IQ-AAL-003', question: 'What are the key components of an agentic AI testing framework?', answer: 'Key components include an LLM reasoning engine for planning and decision making, a tool registry containing API callers, database connectors, and UI automation drivers, a memory system for maintaining context across test steps, an observation module for capturing and analyzing results, and a reporting engine. For banking applications, you also need domain knowledge modules covering RBI regulations, NPCI protocols, and CBS transaction flows. The agent loop follows observe-think-act-reflect cycles to systematically test application behavior.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Technical' },
    { id: 'IQ-AAL-004', question: 'What are the risks of using agentic AI for testing in a banking environment?', answer: 'Key risks include the AI agent accidentally modifying production data if environment boundaries are not strictly enforced. LLM hallucinations could lead to false positive or negative test results, especially in financial calculations. The agent might generate test data that violates PMLA or data privacy regulations. There are also concerns about determinism since LLM-based agents may produce different results across runs. Mitigation includes strict sandbox isolation, human review gates for critical findings, deterministic seed configurations, and compliance guardrails in the agent prompt.', difficulty: 'Hard', category: 'Behavioral' },
    { id: 'IQ-AAL-005', question: 'How can agentic AI help in exploratory testing of banking applications?', answer: 'Agentic AI excels at exploratory testing by autonomously navigating the banking application, identifying interesting states, and probing for unexpected behavior. It can discover that entering a specific combination of account type and transaction amount triggers an unhandled error in the CBS interface. The agent maintains a map of explored states and prioritizes unexplored paths. It can test scenarios humans might overlook such as simultaneous NEFT and RTGS requests for the same beneficiary or edge cases in interest calculation during leap years.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Practical' },
    { id: 'IQ-AAL-006', question: 'Explain the ReAct pattern in agentic AI testing.', answer: 'ReAct (Reasoning and Acting) is a prompting pattern where the AI agent alternates between reasoning about the current state and taking actions. In banking test automation, the agent might reason that it needs to verify overdraft protection, then act by creating a test account with zero balance, reason about what transaction to attempt, act by submitting a withdrawal request, reason about the expected error response, and act by asserting the response matches RBI-mandated overdraft notification format. This pattern creates transparent, auditable test execution traces.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Concept' },
    { id: 'IQ-AAL-007', question: 'How do you validate the accuracy of test results generated by an agentic AI system?', answer: 'Validation involves multiple layers. First, establish baseline test results from manually verified test cases and compare agent-generated results against them. Second, implement assertion verification where the agent cites specific evidence for each finding. Third, use a separate verification agent to cross-check critical findings like balance discrepancies. Fourth, maintain a human review queue for high-severity findings before they are reported as defects. For banking, financial calculation accuracy must be verified against certified CBS computation engines.', difficulty: 'Hard', category: 'Practical' },
    { id: 'IQ-AAL-008', question: 'What is tool-augmented testing in the context of agentic AI?', answer: 'Tool-augmented testing gives the AI agent access to specialized tools beyond its language capabilities. In banking test automation, these tools include REST API clients for calling NPCI endpoints, SQL query executors for verifying database state, Selenium drivers for UI interaction, log analyzers for parsing CBS transaction logs, and calculation validators for verifying interest and EMI computations. The agent decides which tool to use based on the testing objective, orchestrating complex multi-tool workflows like verifying that a UPI transaction correctly updates both the API response and the database ledger.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Technical' },
    { id: 'IQ-AAL-009', question: 'How would you measure the effectiveness of an agentic AI testing system compared to traditional test automation?', answer: 'Measure effectiveness across several dimensions. Coverage metrics compare code paths and user scenarios tested by the agent versus scripted tests. Defect detection rate tracks unique bugs found by the agent that scripted tests missed, especially in complex banking workflows. Time efficiency measures setup and execution time versus equivalent manual or scripted test efforts. Adaptability measures how quickly the agent adapts to UI or API changes without script maintenance. For banking, also measure regulatory compliance coverage and accuracy of financial calculation validations.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Behavioral' }
  ],
  'agile-uat': [
    { id: 'IQ-AUA-001', question: 'How does UAT differ in Agile versus Waterfall for banking projects?', answer: 'In Waterfall banking projects, UAT is a distinct phase at the end with formal sign-off from business stakeholders after all development is complete. In Agile, UAT is integrated into every sprint where business users validate features incrementally. For Indian banking, Agile UAT allows early feedback on CBS module integrations, enabling corrections before NEFT/RTGS processing logic is fully built. This reduces the risk of late-stage failures in regulatory compliance features. Sprint demos serve as mini-UAT sessions with bank operations teams.', difficulty: 'Easy', category: 'Concept' },
    { id: 'IQ-AUA-002', question: 'How do you write effective UAT acceptance criteria for a fund transfer feature?', answer: 'Effective UAT criteria follow the Given-When-Then format grounded in banking domain rules. For example: Given a customer with Rs 50,000 balance, When they initiate an NEFT transfer of Rs 10,000 to a valid IFSC beneficiary, Then the transaction should be processed within the next NEFT batch window, the sender balance should reflect Rs 40,000 minus applicable charges, and an SMS/email notification should be sent. Each criterion must be testable, unambiguous, and reference specific RBI-mandated limits and processing rules for the transaction type.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Practical' },
    { id: 'IQ-AUA-003', question: 'What challenges arise during UAT for CBS migration projects in Indian banks?', answer: 'Major challenges include data migration validation where millions of account records must match between old and new CBS systems. Business users struggle with new interfaces while comparing outputs against legacy systems. Batch processing differences between old and new CBS platforms cause reconciliation mismatches. Regional language support in the new system may differ from the legacy system. Holiday calendar processing for NEFT/RTGS must match RBI schedules. Parallel run periods require double verification of every transaction type across both systems, creating enormous UAT workload for bank staff.', difficulty: 'Hard', category: 'Scenario' },
    { id: 'IQ-AUA-004', question: 'How do you manage UAT defect triage in an Agile banking project?', answer: 'UAT defects are categorized by business impact: Critical defects like incorrect interest calculation or failed RTGS processing block sprint completion and require immediate fixes. High-severity issues like incorrect transaction status display go into the current sprint backlog. Medium issues like minor UI alignment in reports are added to the next sprint. A daily UAT standup with business users, developers, and testers ensures quick resolution. All defects are tagged with the affected banking module like loans, deposits, or payments for traceability and RBI audit compliance.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Practical' },
    { id: 'IQ-AUA-005', question: 'What is the role of business analysts in Agile UAT for banking applications?', answer: 'Business analysts serve as the bridge between bank operations teams and the development team during Agile UAT. They translate complex RBI regulations and banking processes into testable user stories with clear acceptance criteria. During UAT, they help business users understand new features, clarify expected behavior versus actual behavior discrepancies, and determine whether deviations are defects or feature gaps. They maintain a domain knowledge repository covering PMLA rules, KYC requirements, and CBS processing logic that informs UAT test case design.', difficulty: 'Easy', category: 'Behavioral' },
    { id: 'IQ-AUA-006', question: 'How do you handle UAT for regulatory compliance features like PMLA reporting?', answer: 'PMLA reporting UAT requires close collaboration with the compliance team who validates that suspicious transaction reports are generated correctly. Test scenarios include cash transactions above Rs 10 lakhs, multiple related transactions below the threshold that aggregate above it, and cross-border wire transfers. UAT must verify report formats match FIU-IND specifications. You create test data that mimics real suspicious patterns without using actual customer data. The compliance officer signs off on each report template and threshold calculation logic before the feature is considered UAT-complete.', difficulty: 'Hard', category: 'Scenario' },
    { id: 'IQ-AUA-007', question: 'Describe the UAT environment setup for a banking application.', answer: 'The UAT environment must mirror production as closely as possible with anonymized customer data. It connects to CBS test instances, NPCI UAT sandbox for UPI/IMPS testing, and RBI test endpoints for regulatory reporting. Test IFSC codes and bank codes are configured. The environment includes representative data volumes for performance-realistic testing. Access controls replicate production RBAC with maker-checker workflows. Separate UAT environments may be needed for different banking channels like internet banking, mobile banking, and branch operations to avoid interference.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Technical' },
    { id: 'IQ-AUA-008', question: 'How do you ensure UAT coverage for all banking transaction types in a sprint?', answer: 'Create a UAT coverage matrix mapping each transaction type like NEFT, RTGS, IMPS, UPI, demand draft, and cheque clearing to specific acceptance criteria. Prioritize transaction types by business volume and risk. Use risk-based testing to focus on high-value transactions like RTGS and regulatory transactions like PMLA reports. Track UAT progress with a dashboard showing tested versus untested scenarios per banking module. Involve operations staff from different departments like retail, corporate, and treasury to ensure comprehensive coverage across all customer segments.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Practical' },
    { id: 'IQ-AUA-009', question: 'What metrics do you track to measure UAT effectiveness in Agile banking projects?', answer: 'Key metrics include UAT defect discovery rate per sprint which indicates test thoroughness, defect leakage rate measuring bugs found in production that UAT missed, UAT cycle time from start to sign-off per sprint, and business user participation rate. Banking-specific metrics include regulatory compliance test coverage percentage, transaction type coverage ratio, and reconciliation accuracy rate. Track the ratio of UAT defects by severity to identify if critical banking logic errors are being caught early. Sprint velocity should account for UAT rework time.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Behavioral' }
  ],
  'aiml-testing-lab': [
    { id: 'IQ-AIT-001', question: 'What are the unique challenges of testing AI/ML models in banking applications?', answer: 'AI/ML models in banking face challenges including non-deterministic outputs where the same input may produce slightly different results across runs. Model drift occurs when production data patterns change from training data, affecting credit scoring accuracy. Bias testing is critical to ensure loan approval models do not discriminate based on protected attributes like gender, caste, or religion as per RBI fair lending guidelines. Explainability is required for regulatory audit since RBI mandates that automated lending decisions must be justifiable. Testing requires domain-specific datasets reflecting Indian banking patterns.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Concept' },
    { id: 'IQ-AIT-002', question: 'How do you test a credit scoring ML model for fairness and bias?', answer: 'Fairness testing involves analyzing model predictions across different demographic groups to ensure equal treatment. You measure metrics like demographic parity, equalized odds, and disparate impact ratio. For Indian banking, test that the model does not unfairly penalize customers from rural areas, specific states, or certain income brackets. Use techniques like counterfactual testing where you change a protected attribute while keeping everything else constant and verify the score does not change significantly. RBI requires that automated credit decisions include human review mechanisms for rejected applications.', difficulty: 'Hard', category: 'Practical' },
    { id: 'IQ-AIT-003', question: 'What is model validation versus model verification in banking ML systems?', answer: 'Model verification checks that the ML model is built correctly by confirming the implementation matches the mathematical specification. This includes verifying training pipeline correctness, feature engineering logic, and hyperparameter settings. Model validation checks that the right model is built by confirming it performs well on real-world banking data and meets business requirements. In Indian banking, validation includes testing on data from different regions, customer segments, and economic conditions. RBI guidelines require independent model validation for credit risk models by a team separate from the model developers.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Concept' },
    { id: 'IQ-AIT-004', question: 'How do you test for model drift in a fraud detection system?', answer: 'Model drift testing involves comparing the statistical distribution of incoming production data against training data distributions using tests like PSI (Population Stability Index) and KS (Kolmogorov-Smirnov) statistic. For banking fraud detection, monitor feature distributions for transaction amounts, merchant categories, and geographic patterns. Set up automated alerts when PSI exceeds 0.2, indicating significant drift. Track model performance metrics like precision, recall, and F1 score on a rolling window. In Indian banking, seasonal patterns like festival spending and year-end salary disbursements must be accounted for in drift analysis.', difficulty: 'Hard', category: 'Technical' },
    { id: 'IQ-AIT-005', question: 'What test data strategies do you use for ML model testing in banking?', answer: 'Strategies include creating synthetic data that preserves statistical properties of real banking transactions without exposing PII. Use techniques like SMOTE for balancing fraud versus legitimate transaction datasets. Create adversarial test data designed to fool the model such as transactions that mimic legitimate patterns but are actually fraudulent. Maintain holdout test sets from different time periods to test temporal generalization. For Indian banking, ensure test data covers diverse transaction types like UPI, NEFT, RTGS across urban and rural customer profiles with appropriate volume distributions.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Practical' },
    { id: 'IQ-AIT-006', question: 'How do you perform A/B testing for an ML-based loan recommendation engine?', answer: 'A/B testing involves routing a percentage of users to the new ML model while others continue using the existing system. For a loan recommendation engine, measure conversion rates, loan application completion rates, default rates, and customer satisfaction scores between groups. Ensure statistical significance with adequate sample sizes across customer segments. In Indian banking, comply with RBI guidelines by ensuring both groups receive fair treatment and that the experimental model does not violate any regulatory requirements. Track metrics over sufficient time to capture loan performance through at least one EMI cycle.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Scenario' },
    { id: 'IQ-AIT-007', question: 'What is adversarial testing for banking AI systems?', answer: 'Adversarial testing involves crafting inputs specifically designed to cause the AI system to make incorrect predictions. For banking, this includes creating transactions that evade fraud detection by mimicking legitimate spending patterns, submitting manipulated documents for KYC verification that appear genuine to the AI, or crafting inputs to a chatbot that cause it to reveal sensitive account information. Test with adversarial examples using techniques like FGSM or PGD for neural network models. This testing is critical for banks where adversarial attacks could lead to direct financial losses.', difficulty: 'Hard', category: 'Technical' },
    { id: 'IQ-AIT-008', question: 'How do you test the explainability of an AI-driven banking decision system?', answer: 'Explainability testing verifies that the AI system can provide understandable reasons for its decisions. Test that loan rejection reasons are specific and actionable such as insufficient income for requested amount rather than vague outputs. Verify SHAP or LIME explanations align with domain expert expectations. Test that feature importance rankings make business sense for Indian banking context. RBI mandates that customers must be informed of reasons for automated adverse decisions. Test that explanations are consistent across similar applications and do not reveal proprietary model details.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Practical' },
    { id: 'IQ-AIT-009', question: 'What regulatory requirements affect AI/ML testing in Indian banking?', answer: 'RBI has issued guidelines on responsible AI usage in banking covering fairness, transparency, and accountability. All AI-driven credit decisions must be explainable and auditable. Banks must maintain model risk management frameworks with independent validation. PMLA requires that AI-based transaction monitoring for money laundering must have documented testing and validation. Data privacy under the Digital Personal Data Protection Act requires that ML models handle Aadhaar and PAN data with appropriate safeguards. Regular model revalidation is mandated to ensure continued accuracy and fairness.', difficulty: 'Easy', category: 'Concept' }
  ],
  'api-tester': [
    { id: 'IQ-APT-001', question: 'What are the key aspects of testing RESTful APIs in a banking application?', answer: 'Testing banking REST APIs involves validating HTTP methods, status codes, request/response payloads, authentication, and error handling. You verify that GET endpoints for account balance return correct data with proper pagination. POST endpoints for fund transfers validate input schemas including IFSC codes and account numbers. Test authentication via OAuth2 tokens or API keys. Verify rate limiting prevents abuse. Test idempotency for payment APIs to prevent duplicate transactions. Validate that error responses follow a consistent envelope format with meaningful error codes.', difficulty: 'Easy', category: 'Concept' },
    { id: 'IQ-APT-002', question: 'How do you test API authentication and authorization for banking services?', answer: 'Test that unauthenticated requests to protected endpoints like account details or fund transfer return 401 Unauthorized. Verify that expired tokens are rejected with appropriate error messages. Test role-based access control ensuring a retail user cannot access corporate banking APIs. Validate that API keys with read-only scope cannot perform write operations. Test token refresh flows and session timeout handling. For Indian banking, verify that sensitive APIs require additional authentication factors like OTP for transactions above RBI-mandated thresholds.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Practical' },
    { id: 'IQ-APT-003', question: 'How would you test an API for NEFT fund transfer end-to-end?', answer: 'Start by testing the beneficiary validation API with valid and invalid IFSC codes, account numbers, and names. Test the transfer initiation API with various amounts including boundary values at RBI limits. Verify the response includes a unique transaction reference number. Test the transaction status API to track NEFT batch processing. Validate that the sender account is debited and the transaction appears in the statement API. Test error scenarios like insufficient balance, daily limit exceeded, beneficiary not registered, and bank holiday processing. Verify webhook callbacks for transaction status updates.', difficulty: 'Hard', category: 'Scenario' },
    { id: 'IQ-APT-004', question: 'What is API contract testing and why is it important for banking integrations?', answer: 'API contract testing verifies that the API provider and consumer agree on the request/response structure, data types, and status codes without running full integration tests. In banking, where services like accounts, payments, and loans interact, contract tests prevent breaking changes. For NPCI integrations, the contract defines exact field formats for UPI collect requests. Tools like Pact create consumer-driven contracts where the consuming service defines its expectations. This enables independent deployment of banking microservices while ensuring compatibility.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Concept' },
    { id: 'IQ-APT-005', question: 'How do you perform negative testing for banking APIs?', answer: 'Negative testing involves sending invalid inputs to verify proper error handling. Test with malformed JSON payloads, SQL injection in account number fields, XSS payloads in beneficiary name fields, negative transfer amounts, zero amounts, and amounts exceeding numeric field limits. Send requests with missing required fields like IFSC code in transfer requests. Test with invalid data types like strings in amount fields. Verify that all negative test cases return appropriate HTTP status codes (400, 422) with descriptive error messages and do not expose internal system details in error responses.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Practical' },
    { id: 'IQ-APT-006', question: 'How do you test API response times for banking transactions?', answer: 'Measure response times for individual API calls and set SLA thresholds based on banking requirements. Account balance queries should respond within 200ms, fund transfer initiation within 500ms, and statement generation within 2 seconds. Use tools like Apache JMeter, k6, or Locust to simulate concurrent users and measure percentile response times (p50, p95, p99). For UPI APIs, NPCI mandates response within 3 seconds. Monitor response time degradation under load and identify bottlenecks in database queries, CBS integration calls, or encryption operations.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Technical' },
    { id: 'IQ-APT-007', question: 'What is API versioning and how do you handle it in banking systems?', answer: 'API versioning manages backward-compatible changes to banking APIs without breaking existing integrations. Common strategies include URL versioning like /api/v1/accounts and /api/v2/accounts, header versioning using Accept-Version, and query parameter versioning. In banking, versioning is critical because third-party fintech apps and NPCI integrations depend on stable API contracts. When adding fields to the account API for new RBI reporting requirements, v1 continues working for existing consumers while v2 includes new fields. Deprecation notices should follow a timeline giving integrators adequate migration time.', difficulty: 'Easy', category: 'Concept' },
    { id: 'IQ-APT-008', question: 'How do you test API idempotency for payment endpoints?', answer: 'Idempotency testing verifies that sending the same payment request multiple times produces the same result as a single request. Send a fund transfer request with an X-Idempotency-Key header, note the response, then resend the same request with the same key. The second request should return the original response without creating a duplicate transaction. Test with the same key but different payloads to verify it returns a conflict error. This is critical in banking where network timeouts might cause clients to retry payment requests, and duplicate transactions would cause financial discrepancies.', difficulty: 'Hard', category: 'Technical' },
    { id: 'IQ-APT-009', question: 'How do you test webhook callbacks for banking transaction notifications?', answer: 'Set up a mock webhook receiver endpoint that captures incoming callbacks. Initiate banking transactions like NEFT transfers and verify that webhooks are triggered with correct payload structure including transaction ID, status, amount, and timestamps. Test retry logic by returning HTTP 500 from the mock receiver and verifying the banking system retries with exponential backoff. Validate webhook signature verification using HMAC to prevent spoofing. Test ordering of webhooks for transaction lifecycle events like initiated, processing, completed, or failed. Verify timeout handling when the webhook receiver is unreachable.', difficulty: 'Hard', category: 'Practical' }
  ],
  'api-testing-scenarios': [
    { id: 'IQ-ATS-001', question: 'What are boundary value test scenarios for a banking fund transfer API?', answer: 'Boundary value testing for fund transfers includes testing minimum transfer amount of Re 1, maximum single transaction limits set by RBI for NEFT (no limit currently but bank-imposed caps), RTGS minimum of Rs 2 lakhs, and IMPS limit of Rs 5 lakhs. Test amounts at exact boundaries like Rs 199999 and Rs 200000 for RTGS eligibility. Test daily cumulative limits, beneficiary-wise limits, and international remittance thresholds under FEMA. Verify proper error messages when boundaries are exceeded and that amounts with more than 2 decimal places are correctly handled.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Practical' },
    { id: 'IQ-ATS-002', question: 'How would you design test scenarios for a multi-currency banking API?', answer: 'Test currency conversion with real-time exchange rates ensuring calculations match treasury-approved rates. Test cross-border SWIFT transfer scenarios with sender and beneficiary in different currencies. Verify that FEMA (Foreign Exchange Management Act) limits are enforced for retail and corporate customers. Test scenarios where exchange rates change between transaction initiation and settlement. Validate rounding rules for different currencies noting that Japanese Yen has no decimals. Test handling of restricted currencies and OFAC-sanctioned country transactions which must be blocked.', difficulty: 'Hard', category: 'Scenario' },
    { id: 'IQ-ATS-003', question: 'What test scenarios do you create for concurrent API requests in banking?', answer: 'Concurrent testing scenarios include two simultaneous withdrawals from the same account with insufficient balance for both, ensuring only one succeeds and the other fails with proper error. Test concurrent NEFT and RTGS transfers to verify correct debit sequencing. Simulate multiple UPI collect requests for the same payer to ensure proper locking. Test simultaneous statement generation and fund transfer to verify read consistency. Race condition testing for beneficiary addition and immediate transfer. All scenarios must verify data integrity with no phantom debits or credits.', difficulty: 'Hard', category: 'Scenario' },
    { id: 'IQ-ATS-004', question: 'How do you design test scenarios for API rate limiting in banking?', answer: 'Create scenarios that send requests at rates just below, at, and above the configured limit such as 100 requests per minute. Verify that requests exceeding the limit receive HTTP 429 with a Retry-After header. Test that rate limits are applied per API key or per IP as configured. Verify that different endpoints have appropriate limits noting that login attempts should have stricter limits than balance inquiries. Test burst scenarios where many requests arrive simultaneously. For banking APIs, ensure rate limiting does not interfere with legitimate high-volume operations like bulk salary processing.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Technical' },
    { id: 'IQ-ATS-005', question: 'What end-to-end API test scenarios would you create for KYC verification?', answer: 'Test the complete KYC flow: submit Aadhaar number via API and verify OTP-based e-KYC response. Test PAN verification API with valid, invalid, and deactivated PAN numbers. Test document upload API with supported formats like PDF, JPEG and reject unsupported formats. Verify CKYC (Central KYC) record creation and retrieval APIs. Test re-KYC scenarios for existing customers when documents expire. Validate address verification through API integration with India Post or utility databases. Test bulk KYC verification API for corporate account onboarding with multiple directors.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Practical' },
    { id: 'IQ-ATS-006', question: 'How do you test API pagination for transaction history endpoints?', answer: 'Test with various page sizes including minimum of 1, default of 50, and maximum allowed value. Verify that total count is accurate and consistent across pages. Test forward and backward navigation ensuring no duplicate or missing transactions. Verify ordering consistency when new transactions occur during pagination. Test with filters like date range, transaction type, and amount range combined with pagination. For banking, ensure that the total across all pages matches the actual transaction count in the database. Test edge cases like requesting a page beyond the last page and empty result sets.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Technical' },
    { id: 'IQ-ATS-007', question: 'What scenarios do you test for API timeout and retry handling in payment gateways?', answer: 'Simulate network timeouts at different stages of payment processing: before request reaches the payment gateway, during processing at NPCI, and before response reaches the client. Test that the payment gateway correctly handles idempotent retries using transaction reference numbers. Verify that timeout results in a pending status rather than failed, allowing reconciliation. Test the inquiry API that checks transaction status after timeout. Simulate partial failures where debit succeeds but credit fails, verifying that reversal mechanisms work. Test configurable timeout values for different payment modes like UPI (30 seconds) and NEFT (batch-based).', difficulty: 'Hard', category: 'Scenario' },
    { id: 'IQ-ATS-008', question: 'How do you create test scenarios for API backward compatibility in banking?', answer: 'Maintain a suite of contract tests from previous API versions and run them against new releases. Test that v1 clients continue to work when v2 is deployed by sending v1 format requests and verifying responses. Check that new required fields have default values to avoid breaking existing consumers. Verify that deprecated fields are still accepted but ignored. Test that removed endpoints return 410 Gone with migration guidance. For banking, this is critical because NPCI and third-party fintech integrations cannot be updated simultaneously with the bank API release.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Concept' },
    { id: 'IQ-ATS-009', question: 'What security-focused test scenarios do you create for banking APIs?', answer: 'Test for SQL injection in all input fields like account numbers, IFSC codes, and beneficiary names. Attempt IDOR (Insecure Direct Object Reference) by accessing other users account details by manipulating account IDs. Test BOLA (Broken Object-Level Authorization) by using one users token to access another users resources. Verify that sensitive data like Aadhaar, PAN, and account numbers are masked in API responses and logs. Test CSRF protection on state-changing endpoints. Verify TLS 1.2+ enforcement and certificate pinning. Test for mass assignment vulnerabilities in account update APIs.', difficulty: 'Hard', category: 'Technical' }
  ],
  'api-testing-script': [
    { id: 'IQ-ATSC-001', question: 'What scripting languages and frameworks are commonly used for API test automation in banking?', answer: 'Python with pytest and requests library is widely used for its readability and extensive assertion capabilities. JavaScript with Mocha/Chai or Jest and axios suits teams with Node.js backends. Java with RestAssured is popular in enterprise banking environments running on JVM. For Indian banks using CBS systems, Python scripts often integrate with SOAP APIs using zeep library. Postman/Newman enables non-developers to create and run API tests. The choice depends on the existing tech stack, team skills, and CI/CD integration requirements of the banking project.', difficulty: 'Easy', category: 'Concept' },
    { id: 'IQ-ATSC-002', question: 'How do you structure an API test automation framework for banking services?', answer: 'Structure includes a config module for environment-specific settings like UAT and production URLs, an API client layer abstracting HTTP calls with retry and timeout logic, a test data module generating valid banking test data like account numbers and IFSC codes, a models module with Pydantic or dataclass schemas for request/response validation, a tests module organized by banking domain like accounts, payments, and loans, and a reporting module generating detailed HTML or Allure reports. Include shared utilities for authentication token management, database verification queries, and test data cleanup.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Technical' },
    { id: 'IQ-ATSC-003', question: 'How do you handle test data management in API test scripts for banking?', answer: 'Use factory functions to generate realistic test data with valid check digits for account numbers and valid IFSC codes. Maintain a test data pool with pre-created accounts in the UAT environment for transaction testing. Implement cleanup routines that reverse test transactions after each test run to maintain environment stability. For sensitive data, use tokenized or synthetic data that passes validation but does not correspond to real accounts. Store test data configurations in YAML or JSON files separate from test logic. Implement data seeding scripts that create required reference data before test execution.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Practical' },
    { id: 'IQ-ATSC-004', question: 'How do you implement data-driven API testing for banking transaction types?', answer: 'Use parameterized test cases where test data is externalized in CSV, Excel, or JSON files containing various transaction scenarios. Each row represents a test case with input parameters like source account, destination account, amount, transaction type, and expected outcome. For banking, create data files covering NEFT, RTGS, IMPS, and UPI transactions with valid, invalid, and boundary values. Pytest parametrize decorator or TestNG data providers iterate through each row. This approach efficiently tests hundreds of transaction scenarios with a single test function.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Practical' },
    { id: 'IQ-ATSC-005', question: 'How do you handle API authentication in test scripts for banking applications?', answer: 'Implement a centralized authentication module that obtains and caches OAuth2 tokens, refreshes them before expiry, and injects them into all API requests via session headers. For banking apps with multi-factor authentication, script the OTP retrieval from test email or SMS simulators. Store API keys and credentials in environment variables or a secrets manager, never in test code. Implement token rotation for long-running test suites. Support multiple authentication modes since some banking APIs use certificate-based mTLS authentication alongside bearer tokens.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Technical' },
    { id: 'IQ-ATSC-006', question: 'How do you validate API response schemas in test scripts?', answer: 'Use JSON Schema validation to verify response structure, data types, required fields, and value constraints. Libraries like jsonschema in Python or Joi in JavaScript validate responses against predefined schemas. For banking APIs, create strict schemas that verify account number format, IFSC code pattern, amount precision, and date formats. Implement schema version management to handle API versioning. Validate that error responses follow the consistent error envelope pattern with detail, error_code, and correlation_id fields. Generate schemas from Swagger/OpenAPI specifications to maintain consistency.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Technical' },
    { id: 'IQ-ATSC-007', question: 'How do you implement end-to-end workflow testing in API test scripts for banking?', answer: 'Chain multiple API calls to simulate complete banking workflows. For a loan origination workflow, script the sequence of customer creation, KYC verification, credit score check, loan application submission, document upload, approval, disbursement, and EMI schedule generation. Each step validates the response and extracts data needed for the next step. Implement rollback mechanisms for failed workflows to clean up partial data. Use session-scoped fixtures that maintain state across the workflow. Assert intermediate states at each step and final state consistency.', difficulty: 'Hard', category: 'Scenario' },
    { id: 'IQ-ATSC-008', question: 'How do you integrate API test scripts with CI/CD pipelines for banking projects?', answer: 'Configure test scripts to run in CI/CD tools like Jenkins, GitHub Actions, or GitLab CI triggered on every code push and pull request. Organize tests into smoke, regression, and full suites with different trigger conditions. Smoke tests run on every commit, regression on merge to develop, and full suite nightly. Generate JUnit XML reports for CI dashboard integration and Allure reports for detailed analysis. Set quality gates that block deployment if critical banking API tests fail. Configure environment-specific test runs against UAT, staging, and pre-production environments.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Practical' },
    { id: 'IQ-ATSC-009', question: 'How do you handle API test script maintenance as banking APIs evolve?', answer: 'Implement the Page Object pattern equivalent for APIs by abstracting endpoint URLs, request builders, and response parsers into reusable API client classes. When an API changes, you update only the client class, not every test. Use API specification files like OpenAPI/Swagger as the source of truth and auto-generate client code. Maintain backward compatibility tests alongside new version tests. Implement schema change detection that alerts the team when API contracts change. Review and update test data when new fields are added or validation rules change.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Concept' }
  ],
  'arch-test-data-hub': [
    { id: 'IQ-ATD-001', question: 'What is a test data hub and why is it important for banking test environments?', answer: 'A test data hub is a centralized platform that manages, generates, and provisions test data across all testing environments. In banking, it solves the challenge of creating realistic test data that covers diverse scenarios like different account types, transaction histories, and customer profiles without using real customer data. It ensures compliance with data privacy regulations by generating synthetic data that mimics production patterns. The hub maintains referential integrity across related entities like customers, accounts, and transactions, and provisions consistent data sets for parallel testing teams.', difficulty: 'Easy', category: 'Concept' },
    { id: 'IQ-ATD-002', question: 'How do you design a test data architecture for Indian banking systems?', answer: 'The architecture includes a synthetic data generator that creates valid Indian banking entities with proper format validation for account numbers, IFSC codes, PAN, and Aadhaar numbers. A data subsetting engine creates manageable extracts from production-like datasets while maintaining relational integrity across CBS tables. A data masking layer anonymizes sensitive fields using format-preserving encryption. The architecture includes a self-service portal where testers request specific data profiles like NRI accounts with FCNR deposits. Data versioning tracks which test data set was used for each test execution for audit traceability.', difficulty: 'Hard', category: 'Technical' },
    { id: 'IQ-ATD-003', question: 'How do you generate synthetic banking transaction data that is realistic?', answer: 'Use statistical models trained on anonymized production data patterns to generate synthetic transactions. Model transaction frequency distributions for different account types like salary accounts showing monthly credits. Generate realistic merchant names and categories for debit card transactions. Apply temporal patterns like higher ATM withdrawals on weekends and salary credits on month-end. For Indian banking, model UPI transaction patterns with appropriate VPA formats, NEFT batched processing timings, and RTGS high-value transfer distributions. Validate generated data against business rules like minimum RTGS amount of Rs 2 lakhs.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Practical' },
    { id: 'IQ-ATD-004', question: 'What data masking techniques are appropriate for banking test data?', answer: 'Format-preserving encryption maintains the format of account numbers and PAN while making values fictional. Substitution replaces real names with synthetic names from a dictionary. Shuffling rearranges real values within a column to break the association with specific records. Date shifting moves dates by a random offset while preserving relative chronology. For Aadhaar numbers, generate valid format numbers with correct check digits that do not correspond to real Aadhaar IDs. Masking must be consistent across related tables so that a masked customer ID links correctly to masked accounts and transactions.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Technical' },
    { id: 'IQ-ATD-005', question: 'How do you handle test data provisioning for parallel testing teams in a banking project?', answer: 'Implement a reservation system where teams can check out isolated data sets that do not overlap. Use database schemas or tenant isolation to prevent data conflicts between teams testing different modules like loans, payments, and deposits simultaneously. Provide self-service APIs for teams to request specific data profiles. Implement automatic data refresh mechanisms that reset modified data to a known good state after test runs. For banking, ensure that test account ranges are partitioned so that team A fund transfer tests do not affect team B balance inquiry tests.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Practical' },
    { id: 'IQ-ATD-006', question: 'What compliance considerations affect test data management in Indian banking?', answer: 'RBI guidelines prohibit using actual customer data in non-production environments without proper anonymization. The Digital Personal Data Protection Act requires that PII including Aadhaar, PAN, and biometric data must be masked or synthetic in test environments. PMLA audit requirements mean test data used for compliance testing must be documented and traceable. Data residency requirements ensure test data stays within Indian infrastructure. SOX compliance for listed banks requires audit trails of who accessed test data. Test data refresh processes must have proper approvals and data destruction procedures.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Concept' },
    { id: 'IQ-ATD-007', question: 'How do you validate test data quality before use in banking test environments?', answer: 'Implement automated data quality checks that verify referential integrity across customer, account, and transaction tables. Validate that generated IFSC codes correspond to valid bank branch codes, account numbers have correct check digits, and PAN numbers follow the correct alphanumeric pattern. Run business rule validations like ensuring savings account balances are not negative without overdraft facility. Check data completeness ensuring required fields like KYC status and account opening date are populated. Profile the data distribution to ensure it covers edge cases needed for test scenarios.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Practical' },
    { id: 'IQ-ATD-008', question: 'How do you architect test data for performance testing of banking systems?', answer: 'Performance test data requires production-scale volumes with realistic distributions. Generate millions of accounts with transaction histories matching production patterns. Create hot accounts with high transaction volumes to simulate peak load scenarios like salary day processing. Include diverse data distributions for query performance testing across different CBS modules. Pre-load data into the performance test environment with proper indexing and statistics. For Indian banking, simulate realistic NEFT batch sizes, RTGS queue depths, and UPI transaction volumes based on NPCI published statistics. Partition data to test database scaling behavior.', difficulty: 'Hard', category: 'Technical' },
    { id: 'IQ-ATD-009', question: 'What is test data as a service and how does it benefit banking testing teams?', answer: 'Test Data as a Service (TDaaS) provides on-demand test data through APIs and self-service portals. Testers request data profiles like create 100 savings accounts with varying balances and 6-month transaction history and receive provisioned data sets within minutes. Benefits include reduced test setup time, consistent data quality across teams, centralized compliance controls for data privacy, and automated cleanup. For banking, TDaaS enables agile testing by eliminating the traditional multi-day process of requesting test data through DBA teams. It integrates with CI/CD pipelines for automated test environment provisioning.', difficulty: 'Easy', category: 'Concept' }
  ],
  'arch-test-results': [
    { id: 'IQ-ATR-001', question: 'What are the key components of a test results architecture for banking applications?', answer: 'A test results architecture includes a centralized results repository storing execution data from all test types including functional, regression, performance, and security tests. It features a results aggregation engine that correlates results across test runs and environments. A reporting dashboard provides real-time visibility into test health across banking modules. Analytics capabilities identify trends like increasing failures in payment processing tests. Integration with defect management tools like Jira enables automatic defect creation. Audit trail capabilities meet RBI requirements for documenting test evidence.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Concept' },
    { id: 'IQ-ATR-002', question: 'How do you design a test results dashboard for banking test management?', answer: 'The dashboard should display module-wise test execution status for banking domains like core banking, payments, lending, and cards. Include trend charts showing pass/fail rates over sprints. Display critical metrics like defect leakage rate, test coverage percentage per CBS module, and mean time to detect failures. Provide drill-down capability from summary to individual test case results. Include regulatory compliance test status as a separate section. Show environment health indicators and data freshness. For Indian banking, include specific panels for NPCI integration test results and RBI compliance test coverage.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Technical' },
    { id: 'IQ-ATR-003', question: 'How do you implement test result traceability for banking regulatory audits?', answer: 'Implement bidirectional traceability linking requirements to test cases to test results to defects. Every test result must be traceable to the original business requirement or RBI circular it validates. Store test evidence including screenshots, API responses, and database snapshots alongside results. Maintain immutable audit logs of test executions with timestamps, executor identity, and environment details. For banking audits, demonstrate that all PMLA transaction monitoring rules have been tested, all KYC validation scenarios are covered, and all NPCI integration test cases pass before production deployment.', difficulty: 'Hard', category: 'Practical' },
    { id: 'IQ-ATR-004', question: 'What metrics do you derive from test results to assess banking application quality?', answer: 'Key metrics include test pass rate per banking module, defect density per module indicating code quality, test execution trend showing improvement or degradation over time, and requirement coverage percentage. Banking-specific metrics include transaction accuracy rate from end-to-end test results, reconciliation match rate between test and expected outcomes, performance SLA compliance rate for NEFT/RTGS processing times, and security vulnerability count from automated security scans. Derive risk indicators by correlating failed test areas with upcoming release scope.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Concept' },
    { id: 'IQ-ATR-005', question: 'How do you handle flaky test results in banking test automation suites?', answer: 'Identify flaky tests by analyzing historical results for tests that alternate between pass and fail without code changes. Quarantine flaky tests into a separate suite that runs independently and does not block CI/CD pipelines. Root cause common flakiness sources like database connection timeouts, CBS system latency, environment instability, and race conditions in transaction processing tests. Implement retry logic with a maximum of two retries for known infrastructure-related flakiness. Track flaky test metrics and prioritize fixes because flaky tests erode confidence in the test suite and mask real failures in critical banking logic.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Practical' },
    { id: 'IQ-ATR-006', question: 'How do you correlate test results across different testing types for banking releases?', answer: 'Create a unified release quality view that aggregates results from unit tests, API tests, UI tests, integration tests, performance tests, and security scans. Map results to banking features so stakeholders see that the fund transfer feature passed 98% of functional tests but has 2 medium security findings. Implement a release readiness score calculated from weighted test results across all types. For banking releases, include compliance test results and UAT sign-off status in the correlation view. Use risk-based analysis to determine if partial test failures are acceptable for the release scope.', difficulty: 'Hard', category: 'Technical' },
    { id: 'IQ-ATR-007', question: 'What is test result analytics and how does it improve banking software quality?', answer: 'Test result analytics applies data analysis techniques to historical test data to identify patterns, predict risks, and optimize testing effort. Trend analysis reveals modules with consistently high failure rates indicating design issues in CBS integrations. Failure clustering identifies common root causes across seemingly unrelated test failures. Predictive analytics uses historical patterns to forecast likely failure areas in upcoming releases. For banking, analytics can reveal that interest calculation tests fail more often during quarter-end processing, enabling targeted test focus during those periods.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Concept' },
    { id: 'IQ-ATR-008', question: 'How do you ensure test result integrity and prevent tampering in banking projects?', answer: 'Store test results in an immutable append-only repository with cryptographic checksums. Implement role-based access control so only automated systems can write results and humans have read-only access. Maintain detailed audit logs of any result modifications with justification requirements. Use digital signatures on test reports submitted for regulatory compliance. For banking, test result integrity is critical for audit compliance since auditors must trust that reported test coverage is accurate. Implement separation of duties where the team executing tests cannot modify historical results.', difficulty: 'Hard', category: 'Scenario' },
    { id: 'IQ-ATR-009', question: 'How do you report test results to different stakeholders in a banking project?', answer: 'Create tiered reporting for different audiences. Executive dashboards show release readiness, critical defects, and risk indicators in business terms for bank management. Module-level reports for project managers show test progress, defect trends, and schedule impact. Detailed technical reports for QA leads show individual test results, failure analysis, and environment issues. Compliance reports for audit teams show regulatory requirement coverage and evidence. For Indian banking, prepare specific reports for RBI inspection teams showing PMLA compliance testing, KYC validation results, and security assessment outcomes.', difficulty: 'Easy', category: 'Behavioral' }
  ],
  'auth-identity-lab': [
    { id: 'IQ-AIL-001', question: 'What authentication mechanisms are commonly used in Indian banking applications?', answer: 'Indian banking applications use multi-layered authentication including username/password with CAPTCHA for internet banking, MPIN for mobile banking, UPI PIN for payment authentication, Aadhaar-based biometric authentication for e-KYC, OTP via SMS and email for transaction verification, and hardware token or soft token based two-factor authentication for corporate banking. RBI mandates additional factor authentication (AFA) for all online transactions. Some banks implement device binding, geolocation-based risk scoring, and behavioral biometrics for enhanced security.', difficulty: 'Easy', category: 'Concept' },
    { id: 'IQ-AIL-002', question: 'How do you test OAuth 2.0 implementation in a banking open API platform?', answer: 'Test the complete OAuth 2.0 flow including authorization code grant for user-facing apps and client credentials for server-to-server banking integrations. Verify token expiry enforcement and refresh token rotation. Test scope restrictions ensuring tokens with read-only scope cannot perform transactions. Validate PKCE (Proof Key for Code Exchange) implementation for mobile banking apps. Test token revocation and verify revoked tokens are immediately rejected. Check that tokens are properly invalidated on password change. Verify that the authorization server follows RBI guidelines for API security.', difficulty: 'Hard', category: 'Technical' },
    { id: 'IQ-AIL-003', question: 'How do you test Aadhaar-based authentication for banking KYC?', answer: 'Test the e-KYC flow by submitting Aadhaar numbers to the UIDAI sandbox environment and verifying OTP delivery and validation. Test biometric authentication using test fingerprint and iris data provided by UIDAI testing framework. Verify that Aadhaar data is encrypted in transit using 2048-bit RSA encryption as mandated. Test offline Aadhaar XML verification with QR code scanning. Validate that Aadhaar numbers are stored in masked format showing only last 4 digits as per Supreme Court guidelines. Test failure scenarios like UIDAI service unavailability and expired Aadhaar-linked mobile numbers.', difficulty: 'Hard', category: 'Scenario' },
    { id: 'IQ-AIL-004', question: 'What is the maker-checker pattern and how do you test it in banking workflows?', answer: 'Maker-checker is a dual-control mechanism where one user (maker) initiates a transaction and another user (checker) approves it, preventing unauthorized transactions. Test that the maker cannot approve their own transactions. Verify that checkers see all pending approvals with full transaction details. Test that rejected transactions return to the maker with rejection reasons. Validate that approved transactions are processed within SLA timelines. Test scenarios with multiple approval levels for high-value transactions as per bank policy. Verify audit trail captures both maker and checker actions with timestamps.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Practical' },
    { id: 'IQ-AIL-005', question: 'How do you test session management security in internet banking?', answer: 'Verify that sessions expire after the configured idle timeout typically 5-10 minutes for banking. Test that concurrent session limits are enforced preventing the same user from logging in on multiple devices. Verify session tokens are regenerated after login to prevent session fixation attacks. Test that sessions are properly invalidated on logout across all tabs. Verify session cookies have Secure, HttpOnly, and SameSite attributes. Test that sensitive pages cannot be accessed by manipulating session tokens. For banking, test that active sessions are terminated when the user changes their password.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Technical' },
    { id: 'IQ-AIL-006', question: 'How do you test role-based access control (RBAC) in a banking application?', answer: 'Create a role-permission matrix covering all banking roles like teller, branch manager, loan officer, and admin. Test that each role can only access permitted functions. Verify that a teller cannot approve loans or modify interest rates. Test role hierarchy where a branch manager inherits teller permissions plus additional ones. Validate that role assignment requires appropriate authorization. Test privilege escalation scenarios where users attempt to access higher-privilege functions. For Indian banking, test regulatory roles like compliance officer access to PMLA reports and auditor read-only access to transaction logs.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Practical' },
    { id: 'IQ-AIL-007', question: 'What is adaptive authentication and how is it tested in banking?', answer: 'Adaptive authentication adjusts the authentication strength based on risk factors like transaction amount, user location, device fingerprint, and behavioral patterns. Test that low-risk actions like balance inquiry require only password, while high-risk actions like large fund transfers require additional OTP or biometric verification. Verify that logging in from a new device triggers step-up authentication. Test that transactions from unusual locations flag for additional verification. For Indian banking, test that cross-border transactions trigger enhanced authentication per RBI guidelines on international card usage.', difficulty: 'Hard', category: 'Concept' },
    { id: 'IQ-AIL-008', question: 'How do you test Single Sign-On (SSO) integration in banking ecosystems?', answer: 'Test that successful authentication in the primary banking portal provides seamless access to linked services like credit card management, mutual fund platform, and insurance portal. Verify that SSO tokens carry appropriate scope limitations preventing the insurance portal from accessing core banking APIs. Test session propagation and single logout ensuring that logging out of one service terminates all linked sessions. Validate SAML assertion or OIDC token integrity. Test timeout synchronization across all connected services. For banking groups, test SSO across subsidiary applications while maintaining separate authorization boundaries.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Technical' },
    { id: 'IQ-AIL-009', question: 'How do you test password policies and account lockout mechanisms for banking applications?', answer: 'Verify that password creation enforces minimum length, complexity requirements including uppercase, lowercase, digits, and special characters as per bank policy. Test that passwords cannot be reused within the last 12 cycles. Verify account lockout after the configured number of failed attempts typically 3-5 for banking. Test that lockout duration is appropriate and that the account can be unlocked by authorized personnel. Test password expiry notifications and forced password change on first login. Validate that passwords are hashed using strong algorithms like bcrypt. Test that the forgot password flow validates identity before allowing reset.', difficulty: 'Easy', category: 'Practical' }
  ],
  'automation-hub': [
    { id: 'IQ-AUH-001', question: 'What is a test automation hub and why do banking organizations need one?', answer: 'A test automation hub is a centralized platform that manages test automation assets, execution, and reporting across multiple banking projects and teams. Banks need it because they typically run thousands of automated tests across CBS, internet banking, mobile banking, and payment systems. The hub provides a single view of automation health, enables reuse of test components across projects, standardizes frameworks and practices, and provides governance over test automation investments. It helps banks achieve consistent quality across all digital channels and regulatory compliance testing.', difficulty: 'Easy', category: 'Concept' },
    { id: 'IQ-AUH-002', question: 'How do you design a reusable test automation framework for banking applications?', answer: 'Use a layered architecture with a core framework layer providing utilities for logging, reporting, configuration, and data management. Build a banking domain layer with reusable components for common banking operations like account creation, fund transfers, and KYC verification. Create a test execution layer supporting parallel execution across browsers and environments. Implement a data-driven approach where test data is externalized in banking-specific formats. Use the page object model for UI tests and API client abstractions for service tests. The framework should support both SOAP for CBS interfaces and REST for modern microservices.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Technical' },
    { id: 'IQ-AUH-003', question: 'How do you decide what to automate versus manually test in banking applications?', answer: 'Automate repetitive regression tests like daily account balance verification, transaction processing validations, and NEFT/RTGS batch processing checks. Automate data-intensive scenarios like testing interest calculations across multiple account types and tenures. Keep manual testing for exploratory testing of new features, complex UI workflows requiring visual judgment, and user experience validation. The automation pyramid for banking should have extensive API-level tests for CBS and payment gateway integrations, moderate UI tests for critical customer journeys, and manual tests for edge cases and usability. ROI analysis should consider test execution frequency and criticality.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Behavioral' },
    { id: 'IQ-AUH-004', question: 'How do you manage test automation for SOAP-based CBS integrations?', answer: 'Use libraries like zeep for Python or Apache CXF for Java to generate client stubs from CBS WSDL definitions. Create wrapper classes that abstract the SOAP complexity and expose simple methods like get_account_balance and process_transfer. Handle CBS-specific authentication including WS-Security username tokens. Manage complex XML namespaces and nested structures common in banking SOAP services. Implement response parsing that extracts relevant fields from verbose SOAP responses. Create mock CBS services using tools like WireMock for isolated testing when the CBS test environment is unavailable.', difficulty: 'Hard', category: 'Technical' },
    { id: 'IQ-AUH-005', question: 'What test automation governance practices are important for banking organizations?', answer: 'Establish automation coding standards specific to banking test code including naming conventions for test cases referencing banking module codes. Implement mandatory code reviews for all test automation changes. Maintain a test automation inventory tracking which banking processes are automated, their reliability, and last execution status. Define test automation KPIs including automation coverage percentage per banking module, test execution time, flakiness rate, and maintenance cost. Conduct quarterly automation audits to retire obsolete tests and identify new automation candidates. Ensure automation scripts handle sensitive banking data securely.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Behavioral' },
    { id: 'IQ-AUH-006', question: 'How do you implement parallel test execution for banking test suites?', answer: 'Configure test frameworks like pytest-xdist or TestNG to distribute tests across multiple workers. Ensure test isolation so parallel tests do not share accounts or transaction data that could cause conflicts. Partition test data by assigning dedicated account ranges to each parallel worker. For banking tests, be mindful of CBS connection pool limits and avoid overwhelming test environments with too many concurrent sessions. Implement thread-safe logging with correlation IDs to trace results from parallel executions. Use containerized test environments with Docker to scale execution infrastructure on demand.', difficulty: 'Hard', category: 'Technical' },
    { id: 'IQ-AUH-007', question: 'How do you maintain test automation scripts when banking regulations change?', answer: 'Structure tests so that regulatory parameters like transaction limits, reporting thresholds, and validation rules are externalized in configuration files. When RBI updates NEFT processing windows or RTGS limits, you update only the configuration without modifying test code. Maintain a regulatory change log that maps RBI circulars to affected test cases. Implement impact analysis automation that identifies which tests need updating when a regulation changes. Create a regulatory test suite tag so all compliance-related tests can be re-executed independently after regulatory updates.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Practical' },
    { id: 'IQ-AUH-008', question: 'What role does containerization play in banking test automation?', answer: 'Docker containers provide consistent test execution environments eliminating works on my machine problems. Each test run spins up isolated containers with the exact browser versions, database schemas, and mock services needed. For banking, containers can include pre-configured CBS simulators and NPCI sandbox environments. Docker Compose orchestrates multi-container test environments including the application, database, and mock external services. Kubernetes enables scaling test execution across a cluster for large banking regression suites. Container images are versioned and stored in a private registry for auditability.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Concept' },
    { id: 'IQ-AUH-009', question: 'How do you handle test automation for banking mobile applications across platforms?', answer: 'Use cross-platform tools like Appium for native mobile banking app testing on both Android and iOS. Implement a device farm strategy using cloud services like BrowserStack or AWS Device Farm for testing across real devices commonly used by Indian banking customers. Create platform-specific page objects for UI differences between Android and iOS banking apps. Test mobile-specific features like biometric authentication, push notifications for transaction alerts, and UPI intent handling. Automate deep link testing for payment flows initiated from third-party apps. Test offline mode capabilities and data synchronization.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Practical' }
  ],
  'automation-testing-lab': [
    { id: 'IQ-ATL-001', question: 'What is the test automation pyramid and how does it apply to banking applications?', answer: 'The test automation pyramid recommends more unit tests at the base, fewer integration tests in the middle, and minimal UI tests at the top. For banking, the base includes unit tests for interest calculation, fee computation, and validation logic. The middle layer covers API integration tests for CBS communication, NPCI gateway interactions, and inter-service calls. The top includes critical UI journey tests like fund transfer, account opening, and statement download flows. Banking often has an inverted pyramid with too many UI tests due to legacy CBS systems lacking testable APIs, which the team should work to correct.', difficulty: 'Easy', category: 'Concept' },
    { id: 'IQ-ATL-002', question: 'How do you implement Page Object Model (POM) for internet banking automation?', answer: 'Create separate page classes for each banking screen like LoginPage, DashboardPage, FundTransferPage, and StatementPage. Each class encapsulates element locators and interaction methods. LoginPage has methods like enter_credentials and submit_login. FundTransferPage has methods like select_beneficiary, enter_amount, enter_remarks, confirm_transfer, and verify_confirmation. Use a BasePage class with common methods for waiting, scrolling, and OTP handling. Store locators as class attributes using descriptive names. This pattern makes tests readable and maintainable when the banking UI changes since only the affected page class needs updating.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Technical' },
    { id: 'IQ-ATL-003', question: 'How do you handle dynamic elements and waits in banking application automation?', answer: 'Banking applications have dynamic elements like transaction reference numbers, OTP timers, real-time balance updates, and CAPTCHA. Use explicit waits with WebDriverWait to handle elements that appear after CBS processing delays. Implement custom wait conditions for banking-specific scenarios like waiting for transaction status to change from processing to completed. Handle loading spinners that appear during account switching and fund transfer processing. For OTP handling in test automation, integrate with SMS gateway test APIs or email service test endpoints to retrieve one-time passwords programmatically.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Practical' },
    { id: 'IQ-ATL-004', question: 'What are the challenges of automating testing for legacy banking systems?', answer: 'Legacy CBS systems often use terminal-based interfaces like IBM 3270 green screens requiring specialized automation tools like BlueZone or Micro Focus. SOAP-based web services have complex XML structures with dozens of mandatory fields. Thick client banking applications built on Java Swing or .NET WinForms need desktop automation tools like WinAppDriver. Legacy systems lack stable test environments and have long provisioning cycles. Data dependencies are complex with tightly coupled modules. Limited API exposure means many tests must go through the UI. Character encoding issues arise with multi-language support in older systems.', difficulty: 'Hard', category: 'Scenario' },
    { id: 'IQ-ATL-005', question: 'How do you implement BDD (Behavior-Driven Development) for banking test automation?', answer: 'Write Gherkin scenarios in business language that banking domain experts can review and validate. Feature files describe banking workflows like Scenario: Successful NEFT transfer between two accounts with Given, When, Then steps. Step definitions in Python (behave) or Java (Cucumber) map to automation code. Create a banking domain-specific step library with reusable steps like Given a savings account with balance Rs {amount}. The BDD approach ensures test scenarios are traceable to business requirements and understandable by bank operations teams during UAT reviews. Maintain a living documentation of all banking test scenarios.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Practical' },
    { id: 'IQ-ATL-006', question: 'How do you automate database validation as part of banking test automation?', answer: 'After executing banking transactions through the UI or API, validate database state by querying CBS tables. Verify that account balances are updated correctly, transaction records are created with proper status codes, and audit logs capture the complete transaction trail. Use database fixtures to set up preconditions and tear down test data. Connect to test databases using parameterized credentials from environment variables. For banking, validate double-entry bookkeeping where every debit has a corresponding credit. Check that running balances are consistent with transaction history and that interest accrual entries are posted correctly.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Technical' },
    { id: 'IQ-ATL-007', question: 'What is keyword-driven testing and how is it used in banking automation?', answer: 'Keyword-driven testing separates test design from automation code by defining reusable keywords that represent banking actions. Keywords like Login, TransferFunds, VerifyBalance, and GenerateStatement abstract the underlying automation implementation. Business users create test cases by combining keywords in spreadsheets without knowing the automation code. Robot Framework is a popular keyword-driven tool used in banking. Custom banking keyword libraries encapsulate complex CBS interactions. This approach enables bank operations teams to create and maintain test cases while the automation team maintains the keyword implementations.', difficulty: 'Easy', category: 'Concept' },
    { id: 'IQ-ATL-008', question: 'How do you implement test automation for banking batch processing systems?', answer: 'Banking batch processes like end-of-day (EOD) processing, interest calculation, and NEFT settlement run at scheduled times. Automate by triggering batch jobs via APIs or scripts, monitoring job completion status, and validating outcomes. Verify that EOD batch correctly calculates interest across all account types, processes standing instructions, and generates regulatory reports. Test batch failure recovery by simulating mid-batch failures and verifying restart from checkpoint. Validate that concurrent batches do not cause data corruption. Monitor batch execution times against SLA thresholds and alert on performance degradation.', difficulty: 'Hard', category: 'Scenario' },
    { id: 'IQ-ATL-009', question: 'How do you measure and improve test automation effectiveness in banking projects?', answer: 'Track metrics including automation coverage percentage of total test cases automated, test execution time trend, test reliability rate percentage of runs without infrastructure failures, defect detection effectiveness how many production bugs the automated suite would have caught, and maintenance effort hours spent updating tests versus creating new ones. For banking, also track regulatory test coverage and CBS integration test stability. Improve effectiveness by refactoring flaky tests, increasing API-level testing to reduce UI test dependency, implementing smart test selection that runs only tests affected by code changes, and conducting regular automation code reviews.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Behavioral' }
  ],
  'banking-api-test-suite': [
    { id: 'IQ-BAT-001', question: 'What are the essential API test categories for a banking test suite?', answer: 'A comprehensive banking API test suite includes functional tests validating business logic for each endpoint, security tests covering authentication, authorization, and input validation, performance tests measuring response times and throughput under load, integration tests verifying CBS and NPCI gateway interactions, contract tests ensuring API schema stability, negative tests with invalid inputs and error handling, and compliance tests validating RBI-mandated behaviors like transaction limits and reporting. Each category should cover all banking domains including accounts, payments, loans, cards, and deposits.', difficulty: 'Easy', category: 'Concept' },
    { id: 'IQ-BAT-002', question: 'How do you organize test suites for different banking API environments?', answer: 'Create environment-aware test configurations using profiles for development, SIT, UAT, and pre-production. Each profile specifies the environment URL, test credentials, test data sets, and feature flags. Development tests run fast smoke suites. SIT tests run complete functional and integration suites against CBS test instances. UAT tests focus on business scenario validations with production-like data. Pre-production tests run the full regression suite including performance tests against production-equivalent infrastructure. Use environment variables and configuration files to switch between profiles without code changes.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Practical' },
    { id: 'IQ-BAT-003', question: 'How do you design API test cases for UPI payment integration?', answer: 'Cover the complete UPI lifecycle: VPA validation to check beneficiary existence, collect request initiation with proper payload structure, payment authorization with UPI PIN simulation, transaction status check, and callback handling. Test all transaction types including P2P, P2M, collect, and intent-based payments. Include negative scenarios like invalid VPA, expired collect request, insufficient balance, UPI PIN retry exhaustion, and NPCI timeout handling. Validate NPCI response codes and map them to user-friendly messages. Test transaction limits as per NPCI guidelines including per-transaction and daily cumulative limits.', difficulty: 'Hard', category: 'Scenario' },
    { id: 'IQ-BAT-004', question: 'How do you handle test data dependencies in banking API test suites?', answer: 'Implement a test data lifecycle management approach with setup, execute, and teardown phases. Setup creates required entities like test accounts and beneficiaries using API calls. Tests execute business scenarios using the created entities. Teardown reverses test transactions and cleans up created data. Use test data builders that create complex object graphs like a customer with multiple accounts and linked beneficiaries. For banking, maintain a pool of reusable test accounts with sufficient balances that are reset daily. Isolate test data per test run using unique identifiers to prevent parallel execution conflicts.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Technical' },
    { id: 'IQ-BAT-005', question: 'What is the approach for testing banking API error handling comprehensively?', answer: 'Create a systematic error testing matrix covering client errors (4xx) and server errors (5xx) for each endpoint. Test 400 Bad Request with malformed JSON, missing required fields, and invalid data types. Test 401 Unauthorized with missing, expired, and invalid tokens. Test 403 Forbidden for insufficient permissions. Test 404 for non-existent resources like invalid account numbers. Test 409 Conflict for duplicate transactions. Test 429 for rate limiting. Test 500 and 503 by simulating CBS downtime. Verify that all error responses follow the standard error envelope format and do not leak internal implementation details.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Practical' },
    { id: 'IQ-BAT-006', question: 'How do you implement smoke tests for banking APIs in CI/CD pipelines?', answer: 'Smoke tests validate that core banking APIs are functional after every deployment. Include health check endpoint verification, authentication flow validation, basic account inquiry, simple balance check, and one successful fund transfer. Smoke tests should execute in under 2 minutes and cover the critical path through each banking module. Configure CI/CD pipeline to run smoke tests immediately after deployment and automatically rollback if they fail. Use minimal test data that is always available in every environment. Tag smoke tests distinctly from regression tests for selective execution.', difficulty: 'Easy', category: 'Practical' },
    { id: 'IQ-BAT-007', question: 'How do you test banking API performance and scalability?', answer: 'Define performance baselines for each banking API endpoint based on SLA requirements. Use tools like k6, JMeter, or Gatling to simulate realistic load patterns. Model banking peak load scenarios like salary day with thousands of concurrent NEFT transfers, festival season UPI transaction spikes, and month-end EMI processing. Measure response times at p50, p95, and p99 percentiles. Test gradual load increase to identify breaking points. Monitor backend resource utilization during load tests. For Indian banking, simulate NPCI-specified transaction volumes and verify the system meets NPCI performance benchmarks.', difficulty: 'Hard', category: 'Technical' },
    { id: 'IQ-BAT-008', question: 'How do you maintain a large banking API test suite over time?', answer: 'Implement test categorization with tags for banking modules, test priority, and test type. Regularly review and retire obsolete tests for deprecated API versions. Refactor common test patterns into shared utilities and fixtures. Monitor test execution times and optimize slow tests. Track test reliability metrics and fix flaky tests promptly. Maintain test documentation linking each test to business requirements or RBI guidelines. Conduct quarterly test suite reviews with banking domain experts to ensure coverage remains relevant. Use test impact analysis to identify which tests need updating when API changes are made.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Behavioral' },
    { id: 'IQ-BAT-009', question: 'How do you test API backward compatibility when banking APIs are versioned?', answer: 'Maintain contract tests from each API version and run all previous version tests against the current deployment. Verify that v1 request formats are still accepted when v2 is deployed. Test that deprecated fields generate warnings but do not cause failures. Validate that new required fields have default values for backward compatibility. Run compatibility tests across all active API versions simultaneously. For banking, this is critical because multiple fintech partners and banking channels may be on different API versions. Document a deprecation timeline and verify sunset behavior for end-of-life versions.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Concept' }
  ],
  'banking-knowledge': [
    { id: 'IQ-BKN-001', question: 'What is the Core Banking Solution (CBS) and why is it critical for banking operations?', answer: 'Core Banking Solution (CBS) is the centralized software system that manages all banking operations including deposits, loans, payments, and customer management across all branches. It enables anywhere banking where a customer can transact from any branch or digital channel. In India, RBI mandated CBS adoption for all scheduled commercial banks. CBS processes real-time transactions, maintains the general ledger, and interfaces with external systems like NPCI for payments and CERSAI for charge registration. Testing CBS is critical because any defect directly impacts financial transactions and regulatory compliance.', difficulty: 'Easy', category: 'Concept' },
    { id: 'IQ-BKN-002', question: 'Explain the NEFT and RTGS payment systems and their key differences.', answer: 'NEFT (National Electronic Funds Transfer) processes transactions in hourly batches throughout the day, handling transfers of any amount with settlement in the next batch. RTGS (Real Time Gross Settlement) processes transactions individually in real-time for high-value transfers with a minimum of Rs 2 lakhs. NEFT is suitable for non-urgent transfers while RTGS is used for time-critical high-value payments. Both are operated by RBI. For testing, NEFT requires batch processing validation and cut-off time handling, while RTGS requires real-time processing, immediate confirmation, and handling of the queue-based settlement mechanism.', difficulty: 'Easy', category: 'Concept' },
    { id: 'IQ-BKN-003', question: 'What is UPI and how does its architecture impact testing?', answer: 'Unified Payments Interface (UPI) is an instant payment system developed by NPCI enabling real-time inter-bank transactions via mobile phones. Its architecture involves the payer PSP (Payment Service Provider), payee PSP, NPCI switch, and remitter/beneficiary banks. Testing must cover the complete transaction flow across these entities. Key test areas include VPA resolution, device binding, MPIN validation, collect request lifecycle, transaction timeout handling (30 seconds per NPCI mandate), and callback processing. UPI 2.0 features like mandate, invoice, and signed intent add additional testing complexity. Testing requires NPCI sandbox environment access.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Technical' },
    { id: 'IQ-BKN-004', question: 'What are the KYC requirements in Indian banking and how do they affect testing?', answer: 'KYC (Know Your Customer) in Indian banking is mandated by RBI under PMLA rules. It requires customer identity verification using documents like Aadhaar, PAN, passport, or voter ID. Banks must perform Customer Due Diligence (CDD) and Enhanced Due Diligence (EDD) for high-risk customers. Video KYC (V-KYC) is now permitted for remote onboarding. Testing must validate document verification workflows, Aadhaar e-KYC integration with UIDAI, CKYC (Central KYC Registry) record creation and retrieval, periodic re-KYC processes, and risk categorization logic. Test data must use synthetic identities to avoid privacy violations.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Practical' },
    { id: 'IQ-BKN-005', question: 'Explain the concept of PMLA compliance and its implications for testing.', answer: 'The Prevention of Money Laundering Act (PMLA) requires banks to monitor and report suspicious transactions to FIU-IND (Financial Intelligence Unit). Banks must file Cash Transaction Reports (CTR) for cash transactions above Rs 10 lakhs and Suspicious Transaction Reports (STR) for any transaction suspected of money laundering. Testing must verify that the transaction monitoring system correctly identifies reportable transactions including structuring attempts where multiple sub-threshold transactions aggregate above the limit. Report format validation against FIU-IND specifications is critical. Test that alerts are generated and routed to compliance officers for review.', difficulty: 'Hard', category: 'Scenario' },
    { id: 'IQ-BKN-006', question: 'What is the IFSC code system and how is it used in banking transactions?', answer: 'IFSC (Indian Financial System Code) is an 11-character alphanumeric code assigned by RBI to uniquely identify bank branches in India. The first four characters represent the bank, the fifth is always zero, and the last six identify the branch. IFSC is mandatory for electronic fund transfers via NEFT, RTGS, and IMPS. For testing, validate IFSC format, verify against the RBI master directory, test behavior with invalid codes, retired codes from merged bank branches, and validate that the correct beneficiary bank is identified. After bank mergers like SBI associates or PNB-OBC-United, IFSC code migration testing is critical.', difficulty: 'Easy', category: 'Concept' },
    { id: 'IQ-BKN-007', question: 'What are the different types of bank accounts in India and their testing implications?', answer: 'Indian banks offer savings accounts, current accounts, fixed deposits (FD), recurring deposits (RD), NRI accounts (NRE, NRO, FCNR), BSBD (Basic Savings Bank Deposit) accounts, and demat accounts. Each has different rules for minimum balance, interest calculation, transaction limits, and TDS applicability. Testing must cover interest calculation methods like daily product basis for savings and quarterly compounding for FDs. Validate TDS deduction on FD interest exceeding Rs 40,000 per year. Test NRE/NRO repatriation rules and FEMA compliance. BSBD accounts must enforce zero minimum balance and limited transaction counts.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Practical' },
    { id: 'IQ-BKN-008', question: 'How does the RBI regulatory framework impact banking software testing?', answer: 'RBI issues circulars and guidelines that directly affect banking software functionality. Key areas include interest rate changes that require recalculation across all loan portfolios, new payment system rules affecting NEFT/RTGS processing windows, digital lending guidelines requiring specific disclosures, priority sector lending norms requiring classification logic, and Basel III capital adequacy calculations. Testing teams must maintain a regulatory change tracker and create test cases whenever new circulars are issued. Regression testing after regulatory updates is critical to ensure existing functionality is not broken while new rules are correctly implemented.', difficulty: 'Hard', category: 'Concept' },
    { id: 'IQ-BKN-009', question: 'What is the role of NPCI in Indian payment systems and how does it affect testing?', answer: 'NPCI (National Payments Corporation of India) operates retail payment systems including UPI, IMPS, RuPay, NACH, AePS, BBPS, and FASTag. It defines transaction protocols, message formats, settlement procedures, and dispute resolution mechanisms. For testing, banks must integrate with NPCI sandbox environments that simulate the production switch behavior. Test certification by NPCI is mandatory before going live with any payment product. NPCI conducts periodic load testing and mandates minimum performance thresholds. Testing must cover NPCI-defined response codes, timeout handling, and reconciliation file processing in formats specified by NPCI.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Technical' }
  ],
  'banking-modules': [
    { id: 'IQ-BMD-001', question: 'What are the core modules in a banking application and their testing priorities?', answer: 'Core banking modules include Customer Information File (CIF) for customer master data, Deposits module for savings, current, FD, and RD accounts, Loans module for retail and corporate lending, Payments module for NEFT, RTGS, UPI, and IMPS, Cards module for debit and credit card management, Trade Finance for LC and bank guarantees, and Treasury for investment management. Testing priority is highest for Payments and Deposits as they handle real-time financial transactions. Loans module requires extensive business rule testing. All modules must be tested for inter-module interactions and data consistency.', difficulty: 'Easy', category: 'Concept' },
    { id: 'IQ-BMD-002', question: 'How do you test the Deposits module in a banking application?', answer: 'Test account opening workflows with different account types and proper KYC validation. Verify interest calculation for savings accounts using daily product method with quarterly crediting. Test FD creation with various tenures and interest rates, including premature withdrawal penalty calculations. Validate RD installment processing and missed installment handling. Test sweep-in and sweep-out between savings and FD accounts. Verify TDS computation and Form 26AS reporting. Test dormant account handling after 2 years of inactivity per RBI guidelines. Validate account closure with final interest settlement and applicable charges.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Practical' },
    { id: 'IQ-BMD-003', question: 'How do you test the Loans module for different loan products?', answer: 'Test the complete loan lifecycle from application to closure. Verify eligibility checks based on income, credit score, and existing obligations. Test EMI calculation using reducing balance method with different frequencies. Validate disbursement processing including partial disbursements for construction loans. Test prepayment and foreclosure with applicable charges. Verify NPA (Non-Performing Asset) classification at 90 days overdue per RBI norms. Test loan restructuring scenarios. Validate interest rate changes for floating rate loans when RBI repo rate changes. Test priority sector lending classification logic. Verify CERSAI charge registration for secured loans.', difficulty: 'Hard', category: 'Scenario' },
    { id: 'IQ-BMD-004', question: 'What testing considerations exist for the Cards module in banking?', answer: 'Test card issuance workflow including instant card generation, PIN setting, and activation. Verify transaction authorization with limit checks for daily, per-transaction, and merchant category limits. Test card block and replacement flows for lost/stolen cards. Validate EMI conversion for credit card transactions. Test reward points accrual and redemption. Verify billing cycle processing, minimum amount due calculation, and late payment fee application. Test international transaction processing with currency conversion and GST on forex markup. Validate PCI-DSS compliance by ensuring card numbers are masked in all displays and logs.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Technical' },
    { id: 'IQ-BMD-005', question: 'How do you test inter-module interactions in a banking application?', answer: 'Create end-to-end test scenarios that span multiple modules. For example, test a home loan application that involves the CIF module for customer verification, Loans module for eligibility and sanction, Deposits module for disbursement to savings account, Payments module for EMI auto-debit via NACH, and Cards module for co-branded card issuance. Verify data consistency across module databases. Test that a customer address update in CIF reflects across all modules. Validate that account closure in Deposits module triggers loan repayment check in Loans module. Test maker-checker workflows that span modules.', difficulty: 'Hard', category: 'Scenario' },
    { id: 'IQ-BMD-006', question: 'What is the GL (General Ledger) module and how do you test it?', answer: 'The General Ledger module maintains the banks financial books following double-entry accounting principles. Every transaction creates corresponding debit and credit entries in GL accounts. Testing verifies that all transactions across deposits, loans, payments, and cards generate correct GL postings. Validate that GL balances tally at end-of-day processing. Test profit and loss account aggregation and balance sheet generation. Verify that interest accrual entries are posted correctly. Test branch-wise and consolidated GL reporting. Validate that regulatory reporting like SLR/CRR calculations derive correctly from GL data.', difficulty: 'Hard', category: 'Technical' },
    { id: 'IQ-BMD-007', question: 'How do you test the Trade Finance module for Letter of Credit operations?', answer: 'Test LC issuance with different types including sight LC, usance LC, and standby LC. Verify margin amount calculation and blocking from the applicants account. Test document presentation and discrepancy handling workflows. Validate amendment processing for changes in LC terms. Test payment processing under LC terms including acceptance and negotiation. Verify commission and charges calculation including GST. Test LC cancellation and margin release. Validate SWIFT message generation in MT700 format for international LCs. Test regulatory reporting for trade finance transactions under FEMA guidelines.', difficulty: 'Hard', category: 'Scenario' },
    { id: 'IQ-BMD-008', question: 'What are the testing requirements for the Payments module supporting multiple channels?', answer: 'Test that payments initiated from internet banking, mobile banking, branch counter, and API all follow the same processing rules and generate consistent results. Verify channel-specific limits where mobile banking may have lower transaction limits than branch banking. Test that payment status is visible across all channels. Validate that duplicate payment prevention works regardless of the initiating channel. Test NEFT, RTGS, IMPS, and UPI with channel-specific authentication requirements. Verify that bulk payment files uploaded through corporate banking portal are processed correctly. Test payment scheduling and recurring payment setup across channels.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Practical' },
    { id: 'IQ-BMD-009', question: 'How do you test the Customer Information File (CIF) module?', answer: 'Test customer creation with mandatory field validation including Aadhaar, PAN, and address proof. Verify duplicate detection logic that prevents creating multiple CIFs for the same customer. Test KYC document upload, verification, and expiry tracking. Validate customer risk categorization for PMLA compliance. Test customer search functionality across multiple parameters. Verify that CIF updates propagate to all linked accounts and services. Test minor to major account conversion on reaching 18 years. Validate joint account holder management. Test customer merge functionality for duplicate CIF resolution. Verify data masking of sensitive fields in CIF displays.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Practical' }
  ],
  'banking-use-cases': [
    { id: 'IQ-BUC-001', question: 'Describe an end-to-end test use case for customer onboarding in an Indian bank.', answer: 'The use case starts with the customer visiting the bank portal or branch for account opening. Test the Aadhaar e-KYC flow including OTP verification with UIDAI. Verify PAN validation with income tax database. Test Video KYC process for remote onboarding. Validate customer risk categorization based on occupation and income. Verify account number generation following the banks numbering scheme. Test initial deposit processing including cheque deposit and NEFT credit. Validate welcome kit generation including debit card, chequebook, and internet banking credentials. Verify CKYC record creation and upload to the central registry.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Scenario' },
    { id: 'IQ-BUC-002', question: 'What test use cases are important for salary processing in banking?', answer: 'Test bulk salary file upload in prescribed format from corporate clients. Verify file validation including account number format, IFSC code validation, and amount totals matching the control record. Test successful crediting of salaries to all employee accounts with proper narration. Handle exceptions like closed accounts, dormant accounts, and accounts under hold. Test the exception report generation for failed credits. Verify that the corporate accounts debit matches the total of successful credits. Test TDS handling for salary payments. Validate that salary credits trigger auto-sweep to FD if configured. Test across pay cycles ensuring month-end processing handles the load.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Practical' },
    { id: 'IQ-BUC-003', question: 'Describe test use cases for a loan against fixed deposit scenario.', answer: 'Test that the loan amount is correctly calculated as a percentage of FD value typically up to 90%. Verify that a lien is marked on the FD preventing premature withdrawal during the loan tenure. Test interest rate calculation which should be FD rate plus a markup like 1-2%. Validate that the loan tenure cannot exceed the FD maturity date. Test EMI auto-debit from the linked savings account. Handle the scenario where FD matures before loan repayment triggering auto-renewal or loan recall. Test premature FD closure request rejection when lien exists. Verify lien release on loan closure and proper GL entries throughout.', difficulty: 'Hard', category: 'Scenario' },
    { id: 'IQ-BUC-004', question: 'What test use cases cover the cheque clearing cycle in Indian banking?', answer: 'Test inward clearing where cheques drawn on the bank are presented for payment. Verify sufficient balance check, signature verification workflow, and stop payment instruction handling. Test outward clearing where customer-deposited cheques are sent for collection. Validate CTS (Cheque Truncation System) image-based clearing processing. Test cheque return scenarios with proper return memo reasons like insufficient funds, account closed, or signature mismatch. Verify charges for cheque returns as per bank tariff. Test positive pay system for cheques above Rs 50,000 as mandated by RBI. Validate clearing cycle timelines for local and outstation cheques.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Practical' },
    { id: 'IQ-BUC-005', question: 'Describe test use cases for NRI banking operations.', answer: 'Test NRE account operations including foreign currency remittance credit, repatriation of funds, and tax-free interest credit. Test NRO account with restricted repatriation and TDS deduction on interest as per the Double Taxation Avoidance Agreement. Test FCNR deposit creation in different currencies with exchange rate validation. Verify FEMA compliance rules for transaction limits and permissible debits. Test the conversion between NRE and NRO accounts on change of residential status. Validate joint account rules where NRI can hold jointly with resident Indian. Test power of attorney based operations on NRI accounts with appropriate restrictions.', difficulty: 'Hard', category: 'Scenario' },
    { id: 'IQ-BUC-006', question: 'What use cases should be tested for ATM transaction processing?', answer: 'Test cash withdrawal with balance verification and daily limit enforcement. Verify that transactions across multiple ATMs within the same day cumulate toward the daily limit. Test cash deposit at recycler ATMs with denomination handling. Validate mini-statement generation with last 10 transactions. Test PIN change workflow at ATM. Verify inter-bank ATM transactions through NFS (National Financial Switch) with appropriate charges after the free transaction limit as per RBI guidelines. Test ATM card capture after 3 incorrect PIN attempts. Validate that ATM transactions are immediately reflected in internet banking and mobile banking.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Practical' },
    { id: 'IQ-BUC-007', question: 'Describe test use cases for standing instructions and auto-debit mandates.', answer: 'Test creation of standing instructions for recurring transfers like rent payment on a specific date each month. Verify execution on the scheduled date with balance check and proper error handling for insufficient funds. Test NACH mandate registration for EMI auto-debit including the mandate creation, approval, and activation lifecycle. Validate that mandates respect the maximum amount limit set during registration. Test mandate amendment and cancellation workflows. Verify that failed standing instruction attempts generate customer notifications. Test the interaction between standing instructions and account holds or freezes. Validate proper narration in account statements for automated debits.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Scenario' },
    { id: 'IQ-BUC-008', question: 'What use cases cover the bank merger and migration testing?', answer: 'Test account number migration from the merged bank to the surviving bank format. Verify IFSC code changes and customer communication. Test that all products like FDs, loans, and credit cards are migrated with correct terms. Validate that standing instructions and mandates continue functioning post-migration. Test that customer login credentials work in the new system. Verify historical transaction data accessibility in the migrated system. Test that regulatory reporting correctly consolidates data from both entities. Validate that debit cards from the merged bank continue working during the transition period. Test inter-operability during the parallel run period.', difficulty: 'Hard', category: 'Scenario' },
    { id: 'IQ-BUC-009', question: 'Describe test use cases for digital lending through a banking application.', answer: 'Test the pre-approved loan offer display based on customer banking relationship and credit history. Verify instant eligibility check using bureau score integration with CIBIL or Experian. Test loan application submission with minimal documentation for existing customers. Validate e-agreement generation and digital signature workflow. Test instant disbursement to the customers account. Verify that RBI digital lending guidelines are followed including mandatory disclosures of APR, total repayment amount, and cooling-off period. Test the cooling-off period cancellation within 3 days. Validate that loan details are reported to credit bureaus within the mandated timeline.', difficulty: 'Hard', category: 'Practical' }
  ],
  'challenges': [
    { id: 'IQ-CHL-001', question: 'What are the biggest challenges in testing banking applications?', answer: 'Major challenges include complex business rules involving multiple interacting modules where a single transaction may touch deposits, payments, GL, and compliance systems. Massive test data requirements with realistic financial data while protecting PII. Legacy system integration where modern APIs must coexist with decades-old CBS platforms. Regulatory compliance testing that changes with every RBI circular. High availability requirements with zero downtime deployment testing. Real-time transaction processing that demands strict performance SLAs. Multi-channel consistency across internet banking, mobile, ATM, and branch systems. Security testing against evolving threat landscapes.', difficulty: 'Easy', category: 'Concept' },
    { id: 'IQ-CHL-002', question: 'How do you handle test environment challenges in banking projects?', answer: 'Banking test environments face challenges like CBS environment unavailability due to shared usage, data refresh delays, and configuration drift from production. Address by implementing service virtualization to mock unavailable CBS endpoints. Use containerized test environments for components within team control. Establish environment booking systems to prevent conflicts between testing teams. Implement environment health monitoring that alerts when configurations drift. Maintain environment parity checklists comparing test and production configurations. For Indian banking, coordinate with NPCI for sandbox environment access and manage multiple SSL certificates across environments.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Practical' },
    { id: 'IQ-CHL-003', question: 'How do you address the challenge of testing banking regulatory changes within tight timelines?', answer: 'RBI circulars often require implementation within 30-60 days, creating testing pressure. Address by maintaining a pre-built regulatory test template library covering common change types like interest rate changes, limit modifications, and new reporting requirements. Implement risk-based testing to focus on critical paths affected by the regulation. Use automated regression suites to quickly verify that changes do not break existing functionality. Establish a fast-track testing process with dedicated regulatory change testing teams. Maintain close collaboration with compliance teams who can clarify regulatory intent and help design test scenarios quickly.', difficulty: 'Hard', category: 'Scenario' },
    { id: 'IQ-CHL-004', question: 'What challenges arise in cross-border payment testing and how do you overcome them?', answer: 'Cross-border payments involve multiple currencies, exchange rate fluctuations, SWIFT message processing, FEMA compliance, correspondent banking relationships, and time zone differences. Testing challenges include simulating real-time forex rates, validating SWIFT MT message formats, testing sanctions screening against OFAC and UN lists, and verifying nostro/vostro account reconciliation. Overcome by using forex rate simulation services, SWIFT test infrastructure for message validation, sandbox sanctions databases for screening tests, and pre-calculated expected outcomes for currency conversion scenarios. Coordinate testing across time zones for end-to-end payment chain validation.', difficulty: 'Hard', category: 'Technical' },
    { id: 'IQ-CHL-005', question: 'How do you manage the challenge of test data in banking with strict data privacy requirements?', answer: 'Banking test data must be realistic but cannot use production customer data due to privacy regulations. Challenges include maintaining referential integrity in synthetic data, generating valid financial patterns for testing, and creating edge case data that mimics real-world scenarios. Solutions include implementing synthetic data generators that produce valid account numbers, IFSC codes, and PAN with correct formats but fake values. Use data masking for production data subsets with format-preserving encryption. Implement a test data management platform with self-service provisioning. Maintain separate data pools for each testing team to prevent conflicts.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Practical' },
    { id: 'IQ-CHL-006', question: 'What are the challenges in performance testing banking applications during peak periods?', answer: 'Peak periods like salary days, tax filing deadlines, and festival seasons create massive transaction volumes. Challenges include accurately modeling production traffic patterns, provisioning sufficient load generation infrastructure, simulating realistic user behavior including think times and navigation patterns, and testing CBS batch processing under concurrent online load. Solutions include analyzing production traffic data to model realistic load profiles, using distributed load generation across multiple regions, implementing gradual ramp-up scenarios, and coordinating with infrastructure teams for capacity planning. Test failover scenarios under peak load to verify system resilience.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Scenario' },
    { id: 'IQ-CHL-007', question: 'How do you address challenges in mobile banking testing across diverse devices?', answer: 'Indian banking customers use a wide range of Android devices with varying OS versions, screen sizes, and hardware capabilities. Challenges include testing on budget smartphones with limited memory, handling network variability from 2G to 5G, supporting multiple Indian languages, and testing biometric authentication across different hardware. Solutions include maintaining a device matrix based on analytics of actual customer device usage, using cloud device farms for broad coverage, implementing network condition simulation, testing app behavior under low memory conditions, and validating that the app degrades gracefully on older devices while utilizing new features on modern ones.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Technical' },
    { id: 'IQ-CHL-008', question: 'What challenges exist in testing banking application security and how do you address them?', answer: 'Challenges include the evolving threat landscape with new attack vectors emerging frequently, compliance with multiple security standards like PCI-DSS and RBI cybersecurity framework, testing encrypted transaction flows without compromising security, and validating complex authentication mechanisms. Address by implementing continuous security testing in the CI/CD pipeline using SAST and DAST tools. Conduct regular penetration testing by certified third parties. Test for OWASP Top 10 vulnerabilities specific to financial applications. Validate certificate pinning, token management, and session handling. Test data encryption at rest and in transit across all banking channels.', difficulty: 'Hard', category: 'Practical' },
    { id: 'IQ-CHL-009', question: 'How do you overcome the challenge of testing banking batch processes that run during non-business hours?', answer: 'Banking batch processes like EOD processing, interest calculation, and NEFT settlement typically run during nights and weekends. Challenges include testing availability, long execution times, complex dependencies between batch jobs, and difficulty reproducing failures. Solutions include creating a dedicated batch testing environment that can run batches on demand during business hours. Implement batch job simulation that executes individual jobs in isolation with test data. Use checkpoint and restart testing to verify recovery from mid-batch failures. Monitor batch execution logs in real-time and create automated assertions on batch outcomes. Document batch job dependencies and test the entire chain.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Practical' }
  ],
  'chaos-resilience-arch': [
    { id: 'IQ-CRA-001', question: 'What is chaos engineering and why is it important for banking systems?', answer: 'Chaos engineering is the discipline of experimenting on a distributed system to build confidence in its ability to withstand turbulent conditions in production. For banking, where system downtime can result in financial losses, regulatory penalties, and customer trust erosion, chaos engineering validates that NEFT/RTGS processing, UPI payments, and CBS operations continue functioning during infrastructure failures. It helps banks discover weaknesses before they cause production outages. RBI expects banks to have robust disaster recovery capabilities, and chaos engineering provides evidence of system resilience beyond traditional DR testing.', difficulty: 'Easy', category: 'Concept' },
    { id: 'IQ-CRA-002', question: 'How would you design chaos experiments for a UPI payment processing system?', answer: 'Start with steady-state hypothesis like all UPI payments complete within 3 seconds with 99.9% success rate. Design experiments including network latency injection between PSP and NPCI switch, CPU throttling on payment processing nodes, database connection pool exhaustion, memory pressure on transaction caching layer, and killing one of the redundant payment processing instances. Each experiment should have a blast radius limit such as affecting only 1% of traffic. Monitor transaction success rate, response time, and error rates during experiments. Implement automatic abort if metrics breach safety thresholds.', difficulty: 'Hard', category: 'Practical' },
    { id: 'IQ-CRA-003', question: 'What are the key resilience patterns used in banking microservices?', answer: 'Circuit breaker pattern prevents cascading failures when CBS or NPCI services are down by failing fast instead of waiting. Retry with exponential backoff handles transient failures in payment processing. Bulkhead pattern isolates different banking services so that a failure in the loans module does not affect payment processing. Timeout pattern ensures no request waits indefinitely for CBS response. Fallback pattern provides degraded functionality like showing cached balance when real-time balance service is unavailable. Rate limiting protects services from traffic spikes. Each pattern must be tested under failure conditions to verify correct behavior.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Technical' },
    { id: 'IQ-CRA-004', question: 'How do you implement and test circuit breaker patterns in banking services?', answer: 'A circuit breaker transitions between closed (normal flow), open (reject immediately), and half-open (test with limited traffic) states. Configure thresholds such as opening after 5 consecutive failures to the CBS service and resetting after 60 seconds. Test by injecting failures into the downstream service and verifying that the circuit opens at the threshold. Verify that subsequent requests fail fast without waiting. Test half-open state where limited requests are sent to check if the service has recovered. For banking, verify that the circuit breaker state is visible in monitoring dashboards and that alerts are triggered on state changes.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Technical' },
    { id: 'IQ-CRA-005', question: 'What tools and platforms are used for chaos engineering in banking environments?', answer: 'LitmusChaos is an open-source platform for Kubernetes environments running cloud-native banking applications. Chaos Monkey from Netflix randomly terminates instances to test auto-recovery. Gremlin provides enterprise-grade chaos engineering with fine-grained control suitable for regulated banking environments. AWS Fault Injection Simulator works for banks running on AWS. Toxiproxy simulates network conditions between banking microservices. Custom chaos agents can inject banking-specific failures like simulating CBS timeout or NPCI message format errors. For Indian banks, the choice depends on infrastructure with many using private clouds where custom chaos tooling is needed.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Concept' },
    { id: 'IQ-CRA-006', question: 'How do you test disaster recovery for banking critical systems?', answer: 'DR testing for banking involves validating RTO (Recovery Time Objective) and RPO (Recovery Point Objective) targets set by RBI. Test failover from primary to DR site by simulating complete data center loss. Verify that CBS, payment systems, and internet banking come up at the DR site within the RTO period. Test data synchronization to ensure RPO compliance with zero or near-zero data loss. Validate that all channels including ATMs, POS terminals, and mobile banking reconnect to DR site. Test failback to primary after DR event resolution. RBI mandates banks conduct DR drills at least twice a year with documented results.', difficulty: 'Hard', category: 'Scenario' },
    { id: 'IQ-CRA-007', question: 'How do you measure system resilience in banking applications?', answer: 'Measure resilience through key metrics including Mean Time To Detect (MTTD) failures, Mean Time To Recover (MTTR) from failures, error budget consumption rate, and availability percentage against SLA targets. Track transaction success rates during chaos experiments compared to steady state. Measure graceful degradation capability such as whether the system serves cached balances when real-time service fails. Calculate blast radius for each failure type. For banking, track specific metrics like payment settlement success rate during infrastructure disruptions and CBS response time percentiles under degraded conditions.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Practical' },
    { id: 'IQ-CRA-008', question: 'What is the gameday approach to resilience testing and how is it applied in banking?', answer: 'A gameday is a planned chaos engineering exercise where teams simulate failure scenarios in a controlled environment to test response procedures. For banking, a gameday might simulate CBS primary database failure, requiring the operations team to perform failover while monitoring transaction processing. The team practices incident response procedures, communication protocols, and recovery steps. Post-gameday analysis identifies gaps in runbooks, monitoring, and automation. Banking gamedays should involve operations, development, DBA, and management teams. RBI audit teams may observe gamedays as evidence of the banks resilience preparedness.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Behavioral' },
    { id: 'IQ-CRA-009', question: 'How do you handle data consistency during chaos experiments in banking?', answer: 'Data consistency is paramount in banking where even temporary inconsistencies can cause financial discrepancies. Before chaos experiments, establish baseline data checksums for account balances and transaction counts. During experiments, monitor reconciliation between systems in real-time. After experiments, run comprehensive data consistency checks comparing account balances, transaction logs, and GL entries across primary and replica databases. Implement compensating transactions for any discovered inconsistencies. For banking, verify that in-flight transactions during chaos events are either completed or properly rolled back with no partial state. Test idempotency mechanisms under failure conditions.', difficulty: 'Hard', category: 'Technical' }
  ],
  'chat-testing': [
    { id: 'IQ-CHT-001', question: 'What are the key test areas for a banking chatbot?', answer: 'Key test areas include natural language understanding accuracy for banking queries like balance inquiry, fund transfer, and loan eligibility. Test intent recognition across multiple Indian languages. Validate entity extraction for account numbers, IFSC codes, and amounts from conversational text. Test conversation flow management for multi-turn banking transactions. Verify security measures like customer authentication before sharing sensitive account information. Test handoff to human agents for complex queries. Validate compliance with RBI guidelines on digital customer service and data privacy in chat logs.', difficulty: 'Easy', category: 'Concept' },
    { id: 'IQ-CHT-002', question: 'How do you test conversational AI for banking customer service?', answer: 'Use a test dataset of real customer queries collected from call center logs, categorized by intent. Test with variations in phrasing for the same intent like check balance, show me my balance, and how much money do I have. Validate responses for accuracy against CBS data. Test edge cases like ambiguous queries, out-of-scope requests, and adversarial inputs. Measure conversation completion rate for banking tasks. Test that the chatbot correctly handles sensitive information by never displaying full account numbers in chat. Verify that session timeouts properly terminate authenticated chat sessions.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Practical' },
    { id: 'IQ-CHT-003', question: 'How do you test chatbot security in a banking context?', answer: 'Test that the chatbot requires authentication before providing account-specific information. Verify that social engineering attempts like I forgot my password, can you show me my balance without OTP are properly rejected. Test that chat logs do not store sensitive information like passwords or PINs in plain text. Validate that the chatbot does not reveal internal system information in error messages. Test injection attacks through chat input attempting to manipulate backend queries. Verify that authenticated sessions expire after inactivity. Test that the chatbot properly handles concurrent sessions from the same customer.', difficulty: 'Hard', category: 'Scenario' },
    { id: 'IQ-CHT-004', question: 'What metrics do you use to evaluate banking chatbot performance?', answer: 'Key metrics include intent recognition accuracy (target above 90% for banking queries), task completion rate for end-to-end banking transactions, average conversation length in terms of turns to complete a task, customer satisfaction score from post-chat surveys, fallback rate measuring how often the bot cannot understand the query, escalation rate to human agents, response time for each message, and containment rate showing percentage of conversations resolved without human intervention. For banking, also track transaction accuracy ensuring fund transfers and bill payments initiated through the chatbot are correctly processed.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Technical' },
    { id: 'IQ-CHT-005', question: 'How do you test a banking chatbot that supports multiple Indian languages?', answer: 'Create test datasets in each supported language covering common banking queries. Test language detection accuracy to ensure the bot correctly identifies Hindi, Tamil, Telugu, and other Indic languages. Verify that code-mixed queries combining Hindi and English words are handled correctly since many Indian customers use Hinglish. Test script rendering for languages like Devanagari, Tamil, and Bengali in chat responses. Validate that financial amounts and dates are formatted according to Indian conventions in each language. Test language switching mid-conversation and verify context is maintained across the switch.', difficulty: 'Hard', category: 'Practical' },
    { id: 'IQ-CHT-006', question: 'How do you perform regression testing on a banking chatbot after model updates?', answer: 'Maintain a golden test dataset of banking queries with verified correct responses. After every model update, run the entire golden dataset and compare responses against baselines. Track intent classification changes using confusion matrices to identify newly confused intents. Verify that previously working banking transaction flows still complete successfully. Test that newly added intents do not interfere with existing ones. Measure accuracy metrics before and after the update to ensure no degradation. For banking, specifically regression test sensitive flows like fund transfer, loan application, and account information retrieval.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Technical' },
    { id: 'IQ-CHT-007', question: 'What are the challenges in testing voice-based banking assistants?', answer: 'Voice assistants face additional challenges compared to text chatbots including speech recognition accuracy for Indian accents and regional pronunciations, handling background noise during phone banking, correctly interpreting spoken numbers for account numbers and amounts, managing homophone confusion for banking terms, and testing DTMF (touch-tone) fallback for IVR integration. Test that the voice assistant correctly handles interruptions and barge-in scenarios. Verify that voice biometric authentication for speaker verification works across different recording conditions. Test compliance with RBI guidelines on recording and storing voice conversations for audit purposes.', difficulty: 'Hard', category: 'Scenario' },
    { id: 'IQ-CHT-008', question: 'How do you test the integration between a chatbot and core banking systems?', answer: 'Test that chatbot queries correctly invoke CBS APIs for real-time account information. Verify that transaction requests from the chatbot are processed through the same validation pipeline as other channels. Test timeout handling when CBS response is slow and the chatbot needs to inform the customer about the delay. Validate that the chatbot correctly interprets CBS error codes and presents user-friendly messages. Test concurrent CBS requests from multiple chat sessions. Verify that transaction limits applied through the chatbot match those configured for the digital channel in CBS. Test the audit trail for chatbot-initiated transactions.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Technical' },
    { id: 'IQ-CHT-009', question: 'How do you test chatbot conversation flow for a multi-step banking transaction?', answer: 'Map the complete conversation flow for transactions like fund transfer which requires collecting beneficiary details, amount, and confirmation. Test happy path where the customer provides all information correctly. Test interruptions where the customer changes their mind mid-flow or asks an unrelated question. Verify context retention when the customer resumes the transaction after a digression. Test validation at each step such as verifying the beneficiary exists before proceeding to the amount step. Test timeout handling for incomplete conversations. Validate that the chatbot provides clear summaries before executing the transaction and confirms completion with a reference number.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Practical' }
  ],
  'ci-cd-guide': [
    { id: 'IQ-CIC-001', question: 'What is CI/CD and why is it important for banking application development?', answer: 'CI/CD (Continuous Integration/Continuous Delivery) is a software development practice where code changes are automatically built, tested, and prepared for deployment. For banking, CI/CD ensures that every code change to CBS modules, payment processing, or customer-facing applications is validated through automated test suites before reaching production. This reduces the risk of deploying defective code that could impact financial transactions. CI/CD pipelines enforce quality gates including code review, security scanning, and compliance checks, ensuring that banking software meets RBI and NPCI standards consistently.', difficulty: 'Easy', category: 'Concept' },
    { id: 'IQ-CIC-002', question: 'How do you design a CI/CD pipeline for a banking microservices application?', answer: 'The pipeline starts with source code commit triggering static analysis with linting and security scanning using tools like SonarQube and Bandit. Next, unit tests run with coverage thresholds. Integration tests execute against containerized dependencies including database and mock CBS services. API contract tests verify backward compatibility. Security scanning with SAST and dependency vulnerability checks follow. If all gates pass, the artifact is built and pushed to a private registry. Deployment to the test environment triggers automated smoke tests. UAT environment deployment requires manual approval. Production deployment uses blue-green or canary strategy with automated rollback on health check failure.', difficulty: 'Hard', category: 'Technical' },
    { id: 'IQ-CIC-003', question: 'What quality gates should be included in a banking CI/CD pipeline?', answer: 'Essential quality gates include code coverage minimum of 80%, zero critical or high severity SAST findings, all unit and integration tests passing, API contract test compliance, no known vulnerable dependencies from pip-audit or npm-audit, code review approval from at least two reviewers, successful deployment to staging with smoke test pass, performance test within SLA thresholds, and security team approval for changes touching authentication or payment modules. For banking, additional gates include regulatory compliance test suite pass and data handling review for changes involving PII or financial data.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Practical' },
    { id: 'IQ-CIC-004', question: 'How do you handle database migrations in a banking CI/CD pipeline?', answer: 'Use numbered migration scripts stored in version control executed in order during deployment. The pipeline runs migrations against a test database first to validate SQL syntax and data integrity. Implement forward-only migrations since rolling back financial data changes is dangerous. Each migration must be idempotent to handle deployment retries. For banking, test migration scripts with production-scale data volumes in a staging environment to identify performance issues. Include data validation checks that verify referential integrity across CBS tables after migration. Maintain a migration rollback plan for emergencies but prefer forward-fix approaches.', difficulty: 'Hard', category: 'Technical' },
    { id: 'IQ-CIC-005', question: 'What are the challenges of implementing CI/CD in a banking environment?', answer: 'Banking environments face challenges including regulatory change control requirements that mandate formal approval processes conflicting with rapid automated deployments. Legacy CBS systems lack automated deployment capabilities requiring manual steps in the pipeline. Complex test environments with CBS, NPCI, and payment gateway dependencies are difficult to provision automatically. Data privacy requirements restrict test data availability. Multiple deployment approvers create bottlenecks. Solutions include implementing automated compliance checks within the pipeline, creating service virtualization for external dependencies, and establishing a risk-based approval matrix where low-risk changes have faster approval paths.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Scenario' },
    { id: 'IQ-CIC-006', question: 'How do you implement blue-green deployment for banking applications?', answer: 'Blue-green deployment maintains two identical production environments. The blue environment serves live traffic while green receives the new deployment. After deploying to green, run automated smoke tests and health checks including transaction processing verification. Switch traffic to green using a load balancer or DNS change. Keep blue running as an immediate rollback option. For banking, the switchover must be seamless with no transaction loss. Test that in-flight transactions complete on the blue environment while new transactions route to green. Verify that sessions are handled correctly during the switch. CBS connections must be validated on the green environment before traffic switch.', difficulty: 'Hard', category: 'Technical' },
    { id: 'IQ-CIC-007', question: 'How do you integrate security testing into a banking CI/CD pipeline?', answer: 'Integrate SAST (Static Application Security Testing) tools like Bandit for Python or SpotBugs for Java early in the pipeline. Add SCA (Software Composition Analysis) to detect vulnerable dependencies. Include DAST (Dynamic Application Security Testing) against deployed test environments. Add secret scanning to prevent credentials from being committed. For banking, include specific checks for SQL injection patterns, hardcoded encryption keys, and PII logging. Implement container image scanning for vulnerabilities. Configure the pipeline to fail on critical security findings and alert the security team. Run periodic comprehensive penetration tests outside the pipeline.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Practical' },
    { id: 'IQ-CIC-008', question: 'What is infrastructure as code and how does it support banking CI/CD?', answer: 'Infrastructure as Code (IaC) uses tools like Terraform, Ansible, or CloudFormation to define banking infrastructure in version-controlled code. This ensures that test, staging, and production environments are identical, eliminating configuration drift that causes environment-specific banking transaction failures. IaC enables rapid provisioning of new test environments for feature branches. For banking, IaC must incorporate security configurations like network segmentation, encryption settings, and access controls. Changes to infrastructure go through the same CI/CD pipeline with reviews and approvals, ensuring compliance with RBI IT governance requirements.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Concept' },
    { id: 'IQ-CIC-009', question: 'How do you handle CI/CD for banking applications with multiple release trains?', answer: 'Large banking organizations may have separate release trains for internet banking, mobile banking, payment systems, and CBS modules. Use a trunk-based development model with feature flags to manage multiple parallel workstreams. Implement pipeline fan-out where shared library changes trigger CI runs across all dependent services. Coordinate integration testing windows where all release trains deploy to a shared integration environment. For banking regulatory releases that require simultaneous deployment across multiple systems, implement orchestrated deployments with dependency ordering. Use release management dashboards showing all train statuses and interdependencies.', difficulty: 'Hard', category: 'Scenario' }
  ],
  'cloud-native-testing-arch': [
    { id: 'IQ-CNT-001', question: 'What is cloud-native testing and how does it differ from traditional testing approaches?', answer: 'Cloud-native testing is designed for applications built using microservices, containers, and orchestration platforms like Kubernetes. Unlike traditional monolithic testing where you test a single deployment unit, cloud-native testing must validate service interactions, container orchestration, auto-scaling behavior, and distributed system resilience. For banking, this means testing that payment microservices correctly communicate via API gateways, service meshes handle mTLS for secure inter-service communication, and Kubernetes pods recover automatically after failures. Test strategies must account for the ephemeral nature of containers and dynamic service discovery.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Concept' },
    { id: 'IQ-CNT-002', question: 'How do you test banking microservices deployed on Kubernetes?', answer: 'Test at multiple levels: unit tests within each service, contract tests between services, integration tests within the Kubernetes cluster, and end-to-end tests across the entire banking platform. Test Kubernetes-specific behaviors like pod scaling under load, rolling updates without transaction loss, health check and liveness probe responses, and resource limit enforcement. Use tools like Kind or Minikube for local testing. Test service mesh configurations for traffic routing, mTLS, and retry policies. For banking, verify that horizontal pod autoscaling correctly handles UPI transaction volume spikes without degrading response times below NPCI SLA thresholds.', difficulty: 'Hard', category: 'Technical' },
    { id: 'IQ-CNT-003', question: 'What are the testing challenges for banking applications running in the cloud?', answer: 'Challenges include testing across multiple cloud regions for disaster recovery, validating data residency compliance ensuring banking data stays within India per RBI guidelines, testing network latency between cloud services and on-premises CBS systems, verifying security group and firewall configurations, and testing auto-scaling behavior under varying banking transaction loads. Multi-cloud or hybrid cloud deployments add complexity with different API semantics. Cost management requires optimizing test environment provisioning. For Indian banking, SEBI and RBI cloud usage guidelines impose additional testing requirements around data encryption, access controls, and audit logging.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Scenario' },
    { id: 'IQ-CNT-004', question: 'How do you test container security for banking application deployments?', answer: 'Scan container images for vulnerabilities using tools like Trivy, Clair, or Aqua Security before pushing to the registry. Verify that containers run as non-root users. Test that no sensitive data like database credentials or encryption keys is baked into container images. Validate that containers have minimal attack surface with only necessary packages installed. Test Kubernetes pod security policies that restrict privilege escalation. Verify network policies that isolate banking service namespaces. For banking, ensure that container runtime security monitoring detects anomalous behavior like unexpected process execution or network connections.', difficulty: 'Hard', category: 'Technical' },
    { id: 'IQ-CNT-005', question: 'How do you test service mesh configurations for banking microservices?', answer: 'Test that mTLS is enforced between all banking microservices preventing unencrypted communication. Verify that traffic routing rules correctly direct requests to appropriate service versions during canary deployments. Test circuit breaker policies configured in the service mesh for CBS and NPCI integrations. Validate retry policies with appropriate timeouts for banking transaction services. Test rate limiting at the mesh level for API gateway protection. Verify observability integration with distributed tracing across the payment transaction flow. Test that service mesh access policies correctly implement RBAC preventing unauthorized service-to-service communication.', difficulty: 'Hard', category: 'Technical' },
    { id: 'IQ-CNT-006', question: 'What is observability testing in cloud-native banking applications?', answer: 'Observability testing validates that the three pillars of metrics, logs, and traces provide sufficient visibility into banking application behavior. Test that all microservices emit proper metrics for request rates, error rates, and latencies. Verify that distributed tracing captures the complete transaction flow from API gateway through payment service to CBS backend. Test that logs are structured with correlation IDs enabling end-to-end transaction tracking. Validate alerting rules that trigger when banking SLAs are breached. Test dashboard accuracy by comparing displayed metrics with actual transaction data. Verify log retention meets RBI audit requirements.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Concept' },
    { id: 'IQ-CNT-007', question: 'How do you test auto-scaling for banking applications in the cloud?', answer: 'Configure load tests that simulate banking transaction patterns triggering horizontal pod autoscaling. Test that new instances are provisioned within acceptable timeframes and begin serving requests correctly. Verify that scale-down does not terminate pods handling in-flight banking transactions. Test vertical scaling for memory-intensive operations like batch processing. Validate that auto-scaling policies have appropriate maximum limits to prevent runaway costs. For banking, test that auto-scaling during UPI transaction spikes maintains response times below the 3-second NPCI threshold. Verify that scaled instances correctly connect to CBS and maintain session affinity where required.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Practical' },
    { id: 'IQ-CNT-008', question: 'How do you test data persistence in containerized banking applications?', answer: 'Test that persistent volumes correctly retain banking data across pod restarts and rescheduling. Verify that volume snapshots capture consistent database state for backup and recovery. Test storage class performance characteristics meet banking transaction throughput requirements. Validate that encryption at rest is enforced on all persistent volumes containing financial data. Test that volume expansion works without downtime for growing transaction databases. For banking, verify that database containers handle storage full conditions gracefully without data corruption. Test cross-availability-zone volume replication for disaster recovery of banking data.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Technical' },
    { id: 'IQ-CNT-009', question: 'What is the testing strategy for serverless banking functions?', answer: 'Test serverless functions for cold start latency which can impact banking transaction response times. Verify that functions correctly handle concurrent invocations for high-volume payment processing. Test timeout configurations for long-running operations like loan processing. Validate that functions have appropriate memory allocations for different banking operations. Test idempotency since serverless platforms may retry failed invocations. Verify that event triggers from banking event sources like transaction queues correctly invoke functions. Test that serverless functions maintain proper security context and do not expose credentials in logs. Validate cost implications under expected banking transaction volumes.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Practical' }
  ],
  'code-quality-lab': [
    { id: 'IQ-CQL-001', question: 'What code quality metrics are most important for banking applications?', answer: 'Critical metrics include cyclomatic complexity which should be low in financial calculation functions to reduce bug risk, code coverage targeting 80% minimum for banking modules, code duplication which creates maintenance risk when regulatory rules change, technical debt ratio indicating future maintenance burden, and security hotspot count for code handling financial data. Banking-specific metrics include SQL parameterization compliance ensuring no SQL injection risk, error handling coverage verifying all CBS interaction points have proper exception handling, and logging compliance checking that sensitive financial data is never logged in plain text.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Concept' },
    { id: 'IQ-CQL-002', question: 'How do you implement static code analysis for banking application code?', answer: 'Configure tools like SonarQube, ruff for Python, or ESLint for JavaScript with banking-specific rules. Custom rules should flag SQL strings constructed with f-strings, detect hardcoded credentials or encryption keys, identify missing input validation on financial transaction endpoints, flag direct database calls in router/controller layer, and detect missing error handling around CBS communication. Integrate analysis into the CI/CD pipeline to fail builds on critical findings. Create a quality gate dashboard showing trends across banking modules. Regularly review and update rules as new security patterns emerge in the banking domain.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Technical' },
    { id: 'IQ-CQL-003', question: 'What is technical debt and how do you manage it in banking projects?', answer: 'Technical debt in banking projects includes outdated CBS integration patterns, legacy code without tests, duplicated business rule implementations across services, and non-standard error handling. Manage by maintaining a technical debt register categorized by risk and effort. Allocate sprint capacity typically 20% for debt reduction. Prioritize debt that impacts transaction processing reliability or regulatory compliance. For Indian banking, address debt related to deprecated NPCI API versions, outdated encryption algorithms, and non-compliant data handling practices first. Track technical debt metrics over time and include them in management reporting.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Behavioral' },
    { id: 'IQ-CQL-004', question: 'How do you perform code reviews effectively for banking application code?', answer: 'Code reviews for banking applications should check business logic correctness against RBI and NPCI specifications, security vulnerabilities in financial transaction handling, proper input validation for all external inputs, correct error handling with no swallowed exceptions, database query optimization for high-volume transaction tables, and adherence to the layered architecture pattern. Use a review checklist specific to banking including items like verifying double-entry accounting logic, checking for race conditions in balance updates, and confirming that PII handling follows data privacy requirements. Automate routine checks to focus human reviewers on business logic and design.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Practical' },
    { id: 'IQ-CQL-005', question: 'What is the role of type checking in banking application quality?', answer: 'Static type checking using tools like mypy for Python or TypeScript catches type-related bugs before runtime. In banking, type errors can be financially impactful such as passing a string where a Decimal is expected for an amount field, or confusing account number types between internal and external formats. Type annotations serve as documentation for financial calculation functions. Pydantic models enforce runtime type validation for API request and response payloads. Type checking catches issues like passing rupees where paisa was expected or integer overflow in large transaction amount calculations. Configure strict type checking in the CI pipeline.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Technical' },
    { id: 'IQ-CQL-006', question: 'How do you measure and improve test code quality?', answer: 'Apply the same quality standards to test code as production code. Measure test code coverage of the test suite itself using mutation testing. Review test assertions for meaningfulness ensuring they validate business outcomes not just technical behavior. Check for test independence where each test can run in isolation without depending on execution order. Identify test smells like tests with no assertions, overly complex setup, or tests that test implementation details rather than behavior. For banking, verify that test data factories generate valid Indian banking entities and that test scenarios cover regulatory edge cases comprehensively.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Practical' },
    { id: 'IQ-CQL-007', question: 'What is the importance of coding standards in a banking development team?', answer: 'Coding standards ensure consistency across the banking codebase making it easier for team members to understand and maintain each others code. Standards prevent common banking software bugs by mandating patterns like parameterized SQL queries, proper decimal handling for financial calculations, and structured error handling. They reduce code review time by automating style checks. For banking compliance, standards enforce security practices like never logging PII, always encrypting sensitive data at rest, and following the separation of concerns between routing, business logic, and data access layers. Enforce standards using pre-commit hooks and CI pipeline checks.', difficulty: 'Easy', category: 'Concept' },
    { id: 'IQ-CQL-008', question: 'How do you handle code quality for legacy banking systems during modernization?', answer: 'Start by establishing a baseline of current code quality metrics for the legacy system. Implement a strangler fig pattern where new code follows strict quality standards while legacy code is gradually replaced. Add characterization tests to legacy code before refactoring to capture existing behavior. Increase test coverage incrementally focusing on high-risk financial calculation modules first. Introduce static analysis with a lenient baseline that gradually tightens over sprints. For Indian banking, prioritize quality improvements in modules handling regulatory calculations like interest computation, NPA classification, and PMLA monitoring since these carry the highest risk.', difficulty: 'Hard', category: 'Scenario' },
    { id: 'IQ-CQL-009', question: 'What automated tools and configurations support code quality in banking projects?', answer: 'Configure ruff for Python linting with banking-specific rules, black for consistent formatting, mypy for type checking, and isort for import ordering. Use SonarQube or CodeClimate for continuous code quality tracking. Configure pre-commit hooks to catch issues before code reaches the repository. Set up Bandit for Python security scanning focusing on banking-relevant rules like SQL injection and cryptographic issues. Use pip-audit for dependency vulnerability scanning. Configure pyproject.toml with strict settings for all tools. For banking, add custom rules that detect common financial calculation errors like using float instead of Decimal for monetary values.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Technical' }
  ],
  'compliance-regulatory-arch': [
    { id: 'IQ-CRG-001', question: 'What are the key regulatory compliance requirements for Indian banking software?', answer: 'Key requirements include RBI guidelines on IT governance and cybersecurity (RBI/2023-24/xx), PMLA compliance for anti-money laundering transaction monitoring and reporting, KYC/CKYC requirements for customer identity verification, PCI-DSS compliance for card transaction processing, Digital Personal Data Protection Act for handling customer PII, RBI digital lending guidelines for fintech integrations, Basel III capital adequacy reporting requirements, and SEBI regulations for banks offering demat and investment services. Each requirement translates into specific software features that must be tested for compliance before production deployment.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Concept' },
    { id: 'IQ-CRG-002', question: 'How do you design test cases for PMLA compliance validation?', answer: 'Design test cases covering all PMLA reporting requirements: CTR (Cash Transaction Report) generation for single cash transactions above Rs 10 lakhs, aggregated cash transactions exceeding Rs 10 lakhs within a month, cross-border wire transfers above the prescribed threshold, suspicious transaction identification based on defined rules, and STR (Suspicious Transaction Report) generation in FIU-IND format. Test structuring detection where a customer makes multiple deposits just below Rs 10 lakhs to avoid reporting. Validate that alerts are generated with appropriate priority, routed to the compliance officer, and closed with proper documentation.', difficulty: 'Hard', category: 'Practical' },
    { id: 'IQ-CRG-003', question: 'How do you test data privacy compliance in banking applications?', answer: 'Test that customer PII like Aadhaar, PAN, and account numbers is encrypted at rest in the database. Verify that PII is masked in application logs, API responses to unauthorized roles, and screen displays showing only last 4 digits. Test consent management ensuring customer data is processed only for consented purposes. Validate data access logging that tracks who accessed which customer records. Test the right to erasure workflow for customers requesting data deletion while maintaining regulatory retention requirements. Verify that data exports for customer portability requests include all personal data. Test cross-border data transfer restrictions per RBI guidelines.', difficulty: 'Hard', category: 'Scenario' },
    { id: 'IQ-CRG-004', question: 'What is the approach for testing regulatory reporting in banking?', answer: 'Test each regulatory report for completeness, accuracy, and format compliance. For RBI returns, verify that data aggregation logic correctly summarizes across all branches and accounts. Test SLR/CRR computation accuracy against GL data. Validate CRILC (Central Repository of Information on Large Credits) reporting for exposures above Rs 5 crores. Test Basel III capital adequacy calculations including risk-weighted asset computation. For NPCI, verify transaction reconciliation report generation. Test that reports are generated within mandated timelines and that the submission workflow including digital signatures works correctly. Maintain report test templates that are updated with each regulatory change.', difficulty: 'Hard', category: 'Technical' },
    { id: 'IQ-CRG-005', question: 'How do you ensure continuous compliance testing in an agile banking project?', answer: 'Embed compliance test cases in the automated regression suite so they run with every CI/CD build. Maintain a compliance requirement traceability matrix linking each RBI guideline to specific test cases. Implement compliance-as-code where regulatory rules are codified as automated checks. Create a regulatory change impact analysis process that identifies affected test cases when new circulars are issued. Conduct quarterly compliance test reviews with the audit team. For Indian banking, subscribe to RBI circular notifications and assess testing impact within 48 hours of each new circular. Include compliance test coverage in the definition of done for each sprint.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Practical' },
    { id: 'IQ-CRG-006', question: 'What is PCI-DSS compliance testing for banking card processing systems?', answer: 'PCI-DSS testing covers 12 requirement areas including network security, data protection, vulnerability management, access control, monitoring, and security policy. Test that card numbers are never stored in logs or temporary files. Verify encryption of cardholder data using approved algorithms. Test network segmentation isolating the cardholder data environment. Validate that default passwords are changed on all system components. Test intrusion detection system alerting. Verify that all access to cardholder data is logged and monitored. For Indian banks, test compliance with RBI card data storage guidelines which are stricter than PCI-DSS in some areas. Annual compliance testing with a QSA (Qualified Security Assessor) is mandatory.', difficulty: 'Hard', category: 'Technical' },
    { id: 'IQ-CRG-007', question: 'How do you test audit trail requirements in banking applications?', answer: 'Verify that every user action that modifies financial data creates an immutable audit trail entry with timestamp, user ID, action performed, before and after values, and IP address. Test that audit logs cannot be modified or deleted by any user including administrators. Validate that audit trail covers all banking operations including account opening, transaction processing, limit changes, and user access management. Test audit log search and retrieval functionality for specific time ranges, users, and transaction types. Verify that audit logs are retained for the RBI-mandated period of at least 8 years. Test that audit logs include maker-checker identification for dual-control transactions.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Practical' },
    { id: 'IQ-CRG-008', question: 'What testing is required for RBI cybersecurity framework compliance?', answer: 'Test implementation of all controls specified in the RBI cybersecurity framework. Validate that real-time threat monitoring is operational with proper alerting. Test the Security Operations Center (SOC) integration with banking application logs. Verify anti-phishing measures for internet banking. Test DDoS protection mechanisms for banking websites and APIs. Validate endpoint security controls on banking terminals. Test incident response procedures through simulated security incidents. Verify that vulnerability assessment and penetration testing is conducted regularly. For Indian banks, test compliance with RBI mandated cybersecurity audit requirements and verify that the cyber crisis management plan is documented and tested.', difficulty: 'Hard', category: 'Scenario' },
    { id: 'IQ-CRG-009', question: 'How do you handle regulatory compliance testing across multiple banking jurisdictions?', answer: 'Create a jurisdiction-specific compliance test layer that applies the appropriate regulatory rules based on the operating country. For Indian banks with international operations, test RBI regulations for domestic operations and local regulations for overseas branches. Test FEMA compliance for cross-border transactions. Validate that anti-money laundering rules of the host country are applied alongside PMLA requirements. Test that regulatory reports are generated in the format required by each jurisdiction. Implement a compliance configuration matrix that maps regulatory requirements to banking products by jurisdiction. Test that jurisdictional rules are correctly applied based on the branch code and transaction type.', difficulty: 'Hard', category: 'Concept' }
  ],
  'compliance-testing': [
    { id: 'IQ-CMT-001', question: 'What is compliance testing and how does it differ from functional testing?', answer: 'Compliance testing verifies that software adheres to regulatory, legal, and industry standards, while functional testing verifies that features work as specified. In banking, compliance testing checks that the application follows RBI guidelines, PMLA requirements, PCI-DSS standards, and data privacy laws. A fund transfer may be functionally correct but non-compliant if it does not enforce the RBI-mandated transaction limits or generate required audit trails. Compliance testing often requires domain expertise in banking regulations and involves reviewing outputs against specific regulatory formats and thresholds rather than just checking input-output correctness.', difficulty: 'Easy', category: 'Concept' },
    { id: 'IQ-CMT-002', question: 'How do you create a compliance test strategy for a banking application?', answer: 'Start by cataloguing all applicable regulations including RBI circulars, PMLA rules, PCI-DSS requirements, and NPCI specifications. Map each regulation to specific application features and derive test cases. Prioritize based on regulatory risk and penalty severity. Define test data requirements that cover both compliant and non-compliant scenarios. Establish a regulatory change management process to update tests when new circulars are issued. Include compliance testing in the CI/CD pipeline for continuous validation. Plan for periodic comprehensive compliance audits separate from sprint testing. Document all compliance test evidence for auditor review.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Practical' },
    { id: 'IQ-CMT-003', question: 'How do you test interest rate compliance with RBI directives?', answer: 'Verify that savings account interest rates are within RBI-prescribed bands. Test that fixed deposit rates do not exceed the maximum allowed under RBI guidelines. Validate that loan interest rates include all mandated components and the APR calculation follows RBI transparency guidelines. Test interest rate change propagation when RBI announces repo rate changes, ensuring floating rate loans are recalculated correctly. Verify that interest computation methods comply with RBI directives such as daily balance method for savings accounts. Test that penal interest charges follow RBI fair practice code. Validate that all interest rates are displayed in the RBI-mandated format.', difficulty: 'Hard', category: 'Scenario' },
    { id: 'IQ-CMT-004', question: 'What compliance tests are needed for digital lending platforms?', answer: 'Test that all RBI-mandated disclosures are presented before loan approval including APR, total repayment amount, and processing fees. Verify that the cooling-off period of typically 3 days allows loan cancellation without penalty. Test that loan disbursement is made directly to the borrower account not through a third-party pool. Validate that the digital loan agreement includes all RBI-required terms. Test that customer grievance redressal information is prominently displayed. Verify that data sharing with third parties follows consent requirements. Test that collection practices comply with RBI guidelines on timing and communication.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Practical' },
    { id: 'IQ-CMT-005', question: 'How do you test compliance for banking accessibility standards?', answer: 'Test against RBI guidelines on digital accessibility which align with WCAG 2.1 Level AA. Verify that internet banking and mobile banking are usable by people with visual, auditory, motor, and cognitive disabilities. Test screen reader compatibility for all financial transaction flows. Validate that alternative text is provided for all informational images. Test keyboard-only navigation for complete banking workflows. Verify color contrast ratios for financial data displays. Test that time-limited sessions provide adequate warnings and extensions. Validate that error messages in banking forms are descriptive and accessible. Document accessibility test results for RBI inspection.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Technical' },
    { id: 'IQ-CMT-006', question: 'How do you verify compliance with RBI master directions on fraud monitoring?', answer: 'Test that the fraud monitoring system correctly identifies suspicious patterns including unusual transaction volumes, geographic anomalies, and velocity checks. Verify real-time fraud alerts for card-not-present transactions. Test that customer notification is sent within RBI-mandated timeframes for suspected fraud. Validate that the fraud reporting mechanism allows customers to report unauthorized transactions. Test that disputed transactions are resolved within RBI-prescribed turn-around times. Verify that fraud data is reported to RBI in the mandated format. Test that the cooling period for new beneficiary registration prevents immediate high-value transfers.', difficulty: 'Hard', category: 'Scenario' },
    { id: 'IQ-CMT-007', question: 'What is the role of compliance testing in banking product launches?', answer: 'Before any banking product launch, compliance testing verifies that the product meets all applicable regulatory requirements. For a new credit card product, test that terms and conditions include all MITC (Most Important Terms and Conditions) as per RBI mandate. Verify fee structure compliance and transparent disclosure. Test that the product configuration in CBS correctly applies all regulatory limits and charges. Validate that marketing material claims are supported by actual product behavior. Compliance sign-off from the legal and compliance team is required before go-live. Create a compliance certificate documenting all tested regulatory requirements.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Behavioral' },
    { id: 'IQ-CMT-008', question: 'How do you handle compliance testing for changes in GST rules affecting banking services?', answer: 'When GST rates or rules change, test that all banking service charges correctly apply the updated GST rate. Verify that the GST component is correctly calculated and displayed in fee disclosures. Test that GST invoices generated by the banking system follow GSTN format requirements. Validate that inter-state versus intra-state GST rules are correctly applied based on branch and customer location. Test that GST input credit calculations for the bank are accurate. Verify that GST returns data generated from the banking system matches actual transactions. Test effective date handling ensuring old rates apply before the change date and new rates apply after.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Practical' },
    { id: 'IQ-CMT-009', question: 'How do you document and report compliance testing results for bank auditors?', answer: 'Create structured compliance test reports mapping each regulatory requirement to specific test cases, execution results, and evidence. Include screenshots, API responses, and database snapshots as proof of compliance. Document any compliance gaps with risk assessment and remediation plans. Maintain a compliance testing calendar showing when each regulation was last tested. Use a standard report template approved by the audit team. For RBI inspection, prepare a consolidated compliance matrix showing the status of all applicable guidelines. Store compliance test artifacts in an immutable repository with version control for historical audit trail. Include exception reports for any deviations with management approvals.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Behavioral' }
  ],
  'concurrency-testing-arch': [
    { id: 'IQ-CCT-001', question: 'What is concurrency testing and why is it critical for banking systems?', answer: 'Concurrency testing verifies that a system behaves correctly when multiple operations execute simultaneously. In banking, concurrent access to the same account from multiple channels like mobile, internet banking, and ATM can lead to race conditions causing incorrect balances. Two simultaneous withdrawals might both succeed even when the combined amount exceeds the available balance. Concurrency testing ensures data integrity through proper locking mechanisms, transaction isolation levels, and optimistic concurrency controls. RBI mandates that banking systems maintain accurate balances at all times, making concurrency testing a regulatory requirement.', difficulty: 'Easy', category: 'Concept' },
    { id: 'IQ-CCT-002', question: 'How do you design concurrency test scenarios for a fund transfer system?', answer: 'Design scenarios including simultaneous transfers from the same account testing balance consistency, concurrent transfers between the same pair of accounts testing for deadlocks, multiple users transferring to the same beneficiary testing credit accumulation, simultaneous NEFT and RTGS requests from the same account testing channel priority, and concurrent transfer and statement generation testing read consistency. For each scenario, define the expected outcome in terms of final balances, transaction status, and error messages. Use thread pools or async frameworks to simulate true concurrency. Verify results by querying database state after all concurrent operations complete.', difficulty: 'Hard', category: 'Practical' },
    { id: 'IQ-CCT-003', question: 'What database isolation levels are relevant for banking transaction testing?', answer: 'READ COMMITTED prevents dirty reads but allows non-repeatable reads, suitable for most banking queries. REPEATABLE READ prevents non-repeatable reads and is used for balance calculations that must remain consistent within a transaction. SERIALIZABLE provides the strongest isolation preventing phantom reads, used for critical financial calculations but with performance overhead. Test that the banking application uses appropriate isolation levels for different operations. Verify that balance inquiries during active transfers show consistent values. Test that long-running report queries do not block real-time transaction processing. Validate that CBS uses appropriate locking strategies.', difficulty: 'Hard', category: 'Technical' },
    { id: 'IQ-CCT-004', question: 'How do you test for deadlock scenarios in banking applications?', answer: 'Create test scenarios that simulate common deadlock patterns in banking. Two transactions each trying to update Account A then Account B in opposite order can deadlock. Test that the system detects deadlocks and resolves them by rolling back one transaction. Verify that the rolled-back transaction is automatically retried or returns a meaningful error. Test with high concurrency loads to increase the probability of triggering deadlocks. Monitor database deadlock detection mechanisms and verify they respond within acceptable timeframes. For banking, ensure that deadlock resolution does not leave accounts in inconsistent states with phantom debits or credits.', difficulty: 'Hard', category: 'Scenario' },
    { id: 'IQ-CCT-005', question: 'What tools do you use for concurrency testing in banking applications?', answer: 'Use JMeter or Gatling to simulate concurrent users executing banking transactions simultaneously. Apache Benchmark or wrk for raw API concurrency testing. Python concurrent.futures or asyncio for programmatic concurrency tests. Database-level tools like pgbench for PostgreSQL or SQLite stress testing. Thread-safe test frameworks that can manage shared test state across concurrent test executions. For banking-specific tests, custom test harnesses that simulate multi-channel access patterns like ATM, mobile, and internet banking hitting the same accounts simultaneously. Monitoring tools like Grafana to observe system behavior under concurrent load.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Technical' },
    { id: 'IQ-CCT-006', question: 'How do you test optimistic concurrency control in banking applications?', answer: 'Optimistic concurrency uses version numbers or timestamps to detect conflicts when multiple transactions modify the same record. Test by reading an account record, modifying the balance in two separate transactions, and submitting both updates. The second update should fail with a conflict error since the version number has changed. Verify that the failed transaction receives a meaningful error message and can retry with the updated version. Test that the retry mechanism handles concurrent conflicts gracefully. For banking, ensure that optimistic concurrency is applied to critical entities like account balances and loan records to prevent lost updates.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Practical' },
    { id: 'IQ-CCT-007', question: 'How do you validate data consistency after concurrent banking operations?', answer: 'Implement consistency checks that run after each concurrent test batch. Verify that the sum of all account balances matches the expected total calculated from all successful transactions. Check that every debit has a corresponding credit in the GL entries. Validate that transaction counts in the audit log match the number of successful operations. Test that running balances in account statements are consistent with the transaction sequence. For banking, perform end-of-test reconciliation comparing expected versus actual balances for all test accounts. Check for orphaned transactions that were debited but not credited or vice versa.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Practical' },
    { id: 'IQ-CCT-008', question: 'How do you test concurrent access to banking batch processes?', answer: 'Test scenarios where online transactions occur while batch processes like EOD interest calculation or NEFT settlement are running. Verify that batch processes properly lock records to prevent conflicting online updates. Test that online transactions queued during batch processing are applied correctly after the batch completes. Validate that batch process idempotency handles restarts without duplicating calculations. Test concurrent batch job execution where interest calculation and fee deduction batches run simultaneously on overlapping account sets. Verify that batch checkpointing allows safe resumption after failures without reprocessing completed records.', difficulty: 'Hard', category: 'Scenario' },
    { id: 'IQ-CCT-009', question: 'What are the common concurrency bugs found in banking applications?', answer: 'Common bugs include lost updates where two concurrent balance updates result in only one being applied, race conditions in sequential number generators creating duplicate transaction reference numbers, dirty reads where a transaction sees uncommitted balance changes from another transaction, phantom reads in report queries where new transactions appear mid-query, and double-spending where two concurrent withdrawals both pass the balance check because neither sees the other pending debit. Other issues include session state corruption when the same user accesses from multiple tabs, and cache inconsistency where cached balances serve stale data during concurrent updates.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Concept' }
  ],
  'contract-testing-arch': [
    { id: 'IQ-CTT-001', question: 'What is consumer-driven contract testing and how is it applied in banking?', answer: 'Consumer-driven contract testing is an approach where API consumers define contracts specifying the request format and expected response structure, and these contracts are verified against the provider. In banking microservices, the loan service (consumer) defines what it expects from the account service (provider) API. The account service runs these consumer contracts in its CI pipeline to ensure compatibility. This allows independent deployment of banking microservices while guaranteeing that a change to the account balance API does not break the payment processing service that depends on it.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Concept' },
    { id: 'IQ-CTT-002', question: 'How do you implement contract testing for banking API integrations with NPCI?', answer: 'NPCI publishes API specifications for UPI, IMPS, and other payment systems. Create provider contracts from these specifications defining exact request/response schemas, required headers, and response codes. Implement contract tests that validate the banks payment gateway correctly formats requests to NPCI and handles all documented response scenarios. Test with mock NPCI responses matching the contract for development-time testing. Use NPCI sandbox environment for contract validation in higher environments. Version contracts alongside NPCI API version changes. Alert when NPCI publishes specification updates that may break existing contracts.', difficulty: 'Hard', category: 'Practical' },
    { id: 'IQ-CTT-003', question: 'What tools are used for contract testing in banking microservices?', answer: 'Pact is the most widely used contract testing tool supporting consumer-driven contracts with a broker for contract sharing between teams. Spring Cloud Contract works well for Java-based banking microservices. Pactflow provides a managed Pact broker with advanced features like can-I-deploy checks. For OpenAPI-based contracts, tools like Prism validate responses against Swagger specifications. Dredd tests API implementations against API Blueprint or Swagger documents. For banking SOAP services, SoapUI can validate against WSDL contracts. Choose tools based on the tech stack and whether contracts are consumer-driven or provider-driven.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Technical' },
    { id: 'IQ-CTT-004', question: 'How do you manage contract versioning for banking APIs?', answer: 'Maintain contracts in a version-controlled contract broker like Pactflow that tracks consumer and provider versions. When the account service API adds a new field, existing contracts for v1 consumers continue to validate while new v2 contracts include the additional field. Implement compatibility checks that prevent deploying a provider version that breaks any active consumer contract. For banking, manage contract versions for each payment channel since mobile banking, internet banking, and third-party fintech consumers may be on different API versions. Archive deprecated contracts after all consumers migrate to newer versions.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Practical' },
    { id: 'IQ-CTT-005', question: 'How does contract testing fit into a banking CI/CD pipeline?', answer: 'Consumer services generate contracts during their CI build and publish them to a contract broker. Provider services download consumer contracts and verify them as part of their CI build. Before deployment, the can-I-deploy check queries the broker to verify that all consumer contracts are satisfied by the provider version being deployed. This prevents deploying a breaking change to the account service that would affect the payment or loan services. For banking, this is critical because breaking changes in CBS integration APIs could affect multiple downstream services across internet banking, mobile banking, and ATM channels simultaneously.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Technical' },
    { id: 'IQ-CTT-006', question: 'What is the difference between contract testing and integration testing for banking APIs?', answer: 'Integration testing deploys both consumer and provider services together and tests their actual interaction, requiring both services to be running and healthy. Contract testing tests each side independently using contract definitions, without needing the other service to be available. Integration tests are slower, more brittle, and harder to debug because failures could be in either service. Contract tests are fast, isolated, and clearly indicate whether the consumer or provider broke the contract. For banking, use contract tests in CI for fast feedback and reserve integration tests for end-to-end validation in staging environments where CBS and payment gateways are available.', difficulty: 'Easy', category: 'Concept' },
    { id: 'IQ-CTT-007', question: 'How do you handle contract testing for asynchronous banking events?', answer: 'Banking systems often use event-driven architectures where services communicate via message queues for scenarios like transaction notifications and batch processing triggers. Define contracts for message schemas specifying the structure of events published to topics like transaction-completed or payment-failed. Consumer tests verify that the consuming service correctly handles messages matching the contract schema. Provider tests verify that published messages conform to the contract. Use tools like Pact for message-based contract testing. For banking, test contracts for NEFT batch processing events, UPI callback messages, and CBS notification events.', difficulty: 'Hard', category: 'Technical' },
    { id: 'IQ-CTT-008', question: 'What are the challenges of implementing contract testing in banking organizations?', answer: 'Challenges include organizational resistance where teams managing different banking modules may not want to commit to consumer-defined contracts. Legacy CBS systems may not have well-defined API contracts making contract creation difficult. Third-party dependencies like NPCI and RBI reporting systems have fixed contracts that banks must conform to rather than define. Contract broker infrastructure setup and maintenance adds complexity. Ensuring all teams publish and verify contracts consistently requires governance. Solutions include starting with high-risk integration points, demonstrating value through preventing real integration failures, and providing team training on contract testing practices.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Behavioral' },
    { id: 'IQ-CTT-009', question: 'How do you test backward compatibility using contract testing for banking APIs?', answer: 'Maintain all active consumer contracts in the broker for each API version. When deploying a new provider version, verify it satisfies all existing consumer contracts. This ensures that adding a new field to the account balance response does not break existing consumers that do not expect that field. Test that deprecated fields continue to be included until all consumers have migrated. For banking, backward compatibility is critical because payment channel integrations may take months to update. Use can-I-deploy checks to prevent releasing a provider version that would break any active consumer. Tag contracts with environment labels to track which versions are deployed where.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Practical' }
  ],
  'dashboard': [
    { id: 'IQ-DSH-001', question: 'What are the key components of a banking test management dashboard?', answer: 'A comprehensive dashboard includes test execution summary showing pass, fail, skip, and blocked counts across banking modules. Defect metrics displaying open, in-progress, and resolved defect counts by severity and banking domain. Test coverage visualization showing which CBS modules and payment flows are covered. Environment health status for all test environments including CBS, NPCI sandbox, and payment gateway simulators. Sprint progress indicators showing testing velocity and burn-down. Release readiness indicators aggregating all quality metrics. Automation status showing automated versus manual test ratios per module.', difficulty: 'Easy', category: 'Concept' },
    { id: 'IQ-DSH-002', question: 'How do you design a real-time test monitoring dashboard for banking CI/CD?', answer: 'Implement a dashboard that subscribes to CI/CD pipeline events displaying live test execution progress. Show a pipeline visualization with stages from build through deployment with pass/fail indicators at each gate. Display real-time test execution logs for currently running tests. Include a test failure alert feed that highlights critical banking test failures immediately. Show deployment status across environments like SIT, UAT, staging, and production. Include metrics like build success rate, average pipeline duration, and deployment frequency. For banking, add specific panels for payment processing test results and regulatory compliance test status.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Technical' },
    { id: 'IQ-DSH-003', question: 'What metrics should a banking QA dashboard track?', answer: 'Track test execution metrics including total tests, pass rate, fail rate, and execution time trends. Defect metrics including defect density per banking module, mean time to detect, mean time to resolve, and defect leakage rate to production. Coverage metrics including requirement coverage, code coverage, and risk coverage. Automation metrics including automation percentage, automation ROI, and script maintenance effort. Banking-specific metrics including regulatory test compliance percentage, payment processing test success rate, and CBS integration test stability. Quality gates showing whether the release meets defined thresholds for each metric.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Practical' },
    { id: 'IQ-DSH-004', question: 'How do you implement drill-down capabilities in a banking test dashboard?', answer: 'Design hierarchical drill-down from high-level banking domain metrics to individual test results. Start with an enterprise view showing overall quality across all banking applications. Drill into a specific application like internet banking to see module-wise results. Further drill into a module like payments to see test suite results. Finally, drill into individual test cases to see execution details, screenshots, and logs. Each level provides context-appropriate information. For banking managers, show business impact metrics at the top level. For QA engineers, show technical details at the lowest level. Implement URL-based deep links for sharing specific dashboard views.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Technical' },
    { id: 'IQ-DSH-005', question: 'How do you handle dashboard data freshness and performance for large banking test suites?', answer: 'Implement data aggregation pipelines that process raw test results into summary metrics. Use time-series databases like InfluxDB or TimescaleDB for efficient metric storage and querying. Cache frequently accessed dashboard data with appropriate TTL. Implement incremental data processing where only new test results trigger metric recalculation. For large banking test suites with thousands of test cases, use pagination and lazy loading for detailed views. Pre-compute dashboard views for common time ranges like last sprint and last release. Set up data retention policies archiving detailed results older than 90 days while keeping summary metrics indefinitely.', difficulty: 'Hard', category: 'Technical' },
    { id: 'IQ-DSH-006', question: 'What role does a dashboard play in banking release decisions?', answer: 'The dashboard provides the data-driven basis for go/no-go release decisions. It shows whether all quality gates are met including test pass rate above threshold, zero critical defects open, regulatory compliance tests all passing, performance tests within SLA, and security scan findings addressed. For banking, the dashboard should clearly show the compliance testing status for each applicable RBI guideline. Stakeholders from QA, development, operations, compliance, and management review the dashboard before approving production deployment. The dashboard state at the time of release decision should be captured as an immutable snapshot for audit purposes.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Behavioral' },
    { id: 'IQ-DSH-007', question: 'How do you create effective data visualizations for banking test metrics?', answer: 'Use trend charts for metrics that change over time like test pass rate across sprints and defect density by month. Use heat maps to show test coverage across banking modules with color indicating coverage levels. Use pie or donut charts for test distribution by category and defect distribution by severity. Use bar charts for comparative metrics like automation percentage across banking modules. Use gauge charts for key thresholds like release readiness score. For banking, use a matrix view showing test status for each transaction type across each channel. Keep visualizations simple and actionable with clear color coding for pass, fail, and warning states.', difficulty: 'Easy', category: 'Practical' },
    { id: 'IQ-DSH-008', question: 'How do you build dashboards that serve different stakeholder needs in banking testing?', answer: 'Create role-based dashboard views. Executive dashboards show release readiness, quality trends, and risk indicators in business terms that bank CIOs and CTOs understand. Project manager dashboards show sprint progress, resource utilization, and schedule adherence. QA lead dashboards show detailed test execution results, environment status, and automation metrics. Developer dashboards show code quality metrics, unit test results, and pipeline status. Compliance officer dashboards show regulatory test coverage and compliance status. Allow each stakeholder to customize their view while maintaining a shared data source ensuring consistency across all views.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Behavioral' },
    { id: 'IQ-DSH-009', question: 'What technologies and frameworks are used to build banking test dashboards?', answer: 'Popular choices include Grafana for metric visualization with plugins for various data sources, Kibana for log-based analytics integrated with ELK stack, and custom React or Vue.js dashboards for tailored banking requirements. Backend data aggregation uses Python with pandas for processing test results. Data storage options include PostgreSQL for relational data, InfluxDB for time-series metrics, and Elasticsearch for log analysis. Allure reporting framework provides built-in test execution dashboards. For enterprise banking, tools like Micro Focus ALM, Azure DevOps, or Jira with Xray plugin provide integrated test management dashboards.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Technical' }
  ],
  'data-pipeline-arch': [
    { id: 'IQ-DPA-001', question: 'What is a data pipeline in banking and why does it need testing?', answer: 'A data pipeline in banking is an automated process that moves, transforms, and loads data between systems such as from CBS to the data warehouse for reporting, from transaction systems to fraud detection engines, or from multiple branches to centralized regulatory reporting systems. Testing is critical because data quality issues can cause incorrect financial reports, failed regulatory submissions, and wrong risk calculations. Pipeline failures can delay NEFT/RTGS processing, impact customer statements, and cause reconciliation mismatches. Testing validates data completeness, accuracy, timeliness, and transformation correctness at each pipeline stage.', difficulty: 'Easy', category: 'Concept' },
    { id: 'IQ-DPA-002', question: 'How do you test ETL processes in banking data pipelines?', answer: 'Test extraction by verifying that all source records from CBS tables are captured without data loss. Validate transformation logic including currency conversions, interest rate calculations, date format standardization, and account status mapping between source and target systems. Test loading by verifying that data is correctly inserted into the target data warehouse with proper data types and constraints. Test incremental loads that process only changed records since the last run. Validate error handling for malformed source data. Test pipeline restart and recovery from failures. For banking, specifically test that financial aggregations like branch-wise deposits match the source system totals.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Technical' },
    { id: 'IQ-DPA-003', question: 'How do you test data quality in banking data pipelines?', answer: 'Implement automated data quality checks at each pipeline stage. Completeness checks verify row counts match between source and target. Accuracy checks validate that calculated fields like interest accruals match expected values. Consistency checks ensure that referential integrity is maintained across related tables like customer, account, and transaction. Timeliness checks verify that data arrives within SLA windows. Uniqueness checks detect duplicate records. Format validation ensures fields like IFSC codes and PAN numbers conform to prescribed patterns. For banking, implement reconciliation checks comparing pipeline output aggregates with CBS control totals.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Practical' },
    { id: 'IQ-DPA-004', question: 'What are the testing challenges for real-time data streaming in banking?', answer: 'Real-time streaming pipelines for banking face challenges including testing under high throughput for UPI transaction volumes reaching millions per hour, validating exactly-once processing semantics to prevent duplicate transaction records, testing event ordering for transactions on the same account where debit must process before balance check, and handling late-arriving events from timeout recoveries. Test schema evolution when new fields are added to transaction events. Validate that the streaming pipeline handles backpressure when downstream systems like the fraud detection engine are slow. Test failover to ensure no data loss during pipeline component restarts.', difficulty: 'Hard', category: 'Scenario' },
    { id: 'IQ-DPA-005', question: 'How do you test data pipeline performance for banking batch processing?', answer: 'Measure end-to-end pipeline execution time with production-scale data volumes. Test that EOD batch processing completes within the banking window typically 2-4 hours. Identify bottlenecks in transformation stages that process millions of transaction records. Test with increasing data volumes to understand scaling characteristics. Validate that the pipeline meets SLA requirements for regulatory report generation which must be ready by market opening. Test concurrent pipeline executions for independent banking modules. Monitor resource utilization during pipeline execution to identify CPU, memory, or I/O bottlenecks. For banking, test with peak volume scenarios like quarter-end and year-end processing.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Technical' },
    { id: 'IQ-DPA-006', question: 'How do you implement data lineage testing in banking pipelines?', answer: 'Data lineage tracking traces data from source through transformations to the final destination. Test that every field in the target data warehouse can be traced back to its source in CBS or other operational systems. Validate transformation documentation matches actual behavior. Test that metadata captures the pipeline version, execution timestamp, and data quality metrics for each run. For banking regulatory reporting, lineage testing is critical because auditors must verify that reported figures can be traced back to individual transactions. Implement automated lineage validation that flags discrepancies between documented and actual data flows.', difficulty: 'Hard', category: 'Technical' },
    { id: 'IQ-DPA-007', question: 'How do you test data pipeline error handling and recovery?', answer: 'Test with deliberately malformed source data to verify that error records are captured in a dead letter queue without stopping the pipeline. Validate that error records include sufficient information for investigation and reprocessing. Test pipeline restart from the last checkpoint after mid-execution failure. Verify that reprocessing idempotency prevents duplicate records in the target system. Test alert mechanisms that notify the operations team when error rates exceed thresholds. For banking, test that financial transaction pipeline errors do not cause balance discrepancies between the operational system and the reporting system. Validate that error recovery maintains data consistency.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Practical' },
    { id: 'IQ-DPA-008', question: 'What is schema evolution testing in banking data pipelines?', answer: 'Schema evolution testing validates that pipelines correctly handle changes in source or target data structures. Test adding a new column to the transaction table to verify the pipeline includes it in processing. Test removing a deprecated column to ensure no downstream failures. Test data type changes like widening a numeric field for larger transaction amounts. Validate that schema changes are backward compatible so that in-flight pipeline executions complete successfully. For banking, test schema changes for regulatory reporting fields that are frequently updated by RBI. Implement schema registry integration to detect and alert on schema changes before pipeline execution.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Concept' },
    { id: 'IQ-DPA-009', question: 'How do you test data reconciliation between banking operational and analytical systems?', answer: 'Implement automated reconciliation checks comparing key financial metrics between the operational CBS and the analytical data warehouse. Verify that total deposits, total loans outstanding, number of active accounts, and branch-wise balances match within acceptable tolerance. Test reconciliation across different time boundaries since CBS may process transactions not yet reflected in the warehouse. Validate that reconciliation reports highlight discrepancies with sufficient detail for investigation. For banking, reconciliation is mandatory for regulatory submissions. Test end-of-month and end-of-quarter reconciliation which have stricter accuracy requirements.', difficulty: 'Hard', category: 'Practical' }
  ],
  'data-testing': [
    { id: 'IQ-DAT-001', question: 'What is data testing and why is it important in banking?', answer: 'Data testing validates the accuracy, completeness, consistency, and integrity of data stored and processed in banking systems. In banking, data quality directly impacts financial reporting accuracy, regulatory compliance, customer experience, and risk management decisions. Incorrect account balance data could lead to wrong interest calculations, failed transactions, and regulatory penalties. Data testing covers database integrity testing, data migration validation, ETL process testing, and data warehouse quality assurance. With Indian banking processing millions of daily transactions across NEFT, RTGS, UPI, and IMPS, ensuring data quality is fundamental to operational reliability.', difficulty: 'Easy', category: 'Concept' },
    { id: 'IQ-DAT-002', question: 'How do you test data migration during a CBS upgrade in Indian banking?', answer: 'Validate that all customer records, account details, transaction history, and configuration data are accurately transferred from the old CBS to the new system. Compare row counts for every table between source and target. Verify that financial amounts match to the paisa level with no rounding differences. Test that special characters in customer names are preserved. Validate that dormant and frozen account statuses are correctly migrated. Test that running balances computed from migrated transactions match the migrated balance figures. Verify that all IFSC mappings, product configurations, and interest rate tables are correctly migrated. Run parallel operations to compare outputs between old and new systems.', difficulty: 'Hard', category: 'Practical' },
    { id: 'IQ-DAT-003', question: 'How do you test referential integrity in banking databases?', answer: 'Verify that all foreign key relationships are enforced across banking tables. Test that every transaction record references a valid account, every account references a valid customer, and every loan references both a valid customer and a valid product configuration. Test that deleting a customer record is properly prevented when active accounts exist. Validate that orphan records cannot be created through any application workflow. For Indian banking, verify referential integrity between the account table and the IFSC master, between the KYC table and the customer record, and between the GL posting table and the chart of accounts.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Technical' },
    { id: 'IQ-DAT-004', question: 'How do you test data encryption and masking in banking databases?', answer: 'Verify that sensitive fields like passwords, PINs, and Aadhaar numbers are encrypted at rest using approved algorithms. Test that decryption works correctly when authorized applications access the data. Validate that data masking rules are applied consistently showing only the last 4 digits of account numbers and Aadhaar in application displays and API responses. Test that masked data cannot be reversed to reveal original values. Verify that encryption keys are rotated per schedule without data loss. Test database backups to ensure they contain encrypted data not plaintext. Validate that temporary tables and query logs do not contain unmasked sensitive data.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Technical' },
    { id: 'IQ-DAT-005', question: 'What is data profiling and how is it used in banking data testing?', answer: 'Data profiling analyzes data to understand its structure, content, and quality characteristics. In banking, profiling reveals data distributions like account balance ranges, transaction frequency patterns, and customer demographic profiles. It identifies anomalies like accounts with negative balances in non-overdraft products, transactions with future dates, or customers with invalid PAN formats. Profiling helps design effective test data by understanding production data patterns. For Indian banking, profile data to verify that IFSC codes follow the correct pattern, account numbers have proper check digits, and that data distributions across urban and rural branches are realistic for test environments.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Concept' },
    { id: 'IQ-DAT-006', question: 'How do you test data consistency across multiple banking channels?', answer: 'Verify that account balance displayed in internet banking, mobile banking, ATM, and branch systems matches in real-time. Execute a transaction through one channel and verify the updated data is immediately visible across all other channels. Test that transaction history is consistent and complete across all channels. Validate that hold amounts on accounts are reflected uniformly. Test scenarios where concurrent transactions from different channels update the same data, verifying final consistency. For Indian banking, test that UPI transactions initiated from third-party apps correctly reflect in the bank\'s own mobile and internet banking applications.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Practical' },
    { id: 'IQ-DAT-007', question: 'How do you test data archival and purging processes in banking systems?', answer: 'Verify that data archival correctly moves old records to archive storage based on defined retention policies while maintaining accessibility for regulatory queries. Test that archived transaction data can be retrieved for customer disputes and audit requests. Validate that purged data is irrecoverably deleted per data privacy requirements. Test that archival does not affect active account operations. Verify that archived data maintains referential integrity within the archive. For Indian banking, test that data retention meets RBI requirements of maintaining records for at least 8 years. Validate that the archival process handles high volumes during year-end batch archival windows.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Scenario' },
    { id: 'IQ-DAT-008', question: 'How do you validate data transformation rules in banking ETL processes?', answer: 'Create a transformation test matrix mapping each source field to its target field with the transformation rule applied. Test each rule independently with representative data including edge cases. Validate date format conversions from various CBS formats to the target standard. Test amount field transformations including currency conversion and decimal precision handling. Verify that code mappings like transaction type codes correctly translate between systems. Test aggregate transformations ensuring that summarized figures match the sum of detailed records. For banking, specifically test interest calculation transformations, tax computation logic, and regulatory classification rules.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Technical' },
    { id: 'IQ-DAT-009', question: 'What automated data testing frameworks do you use for banking applications?', answer: 'Great Expectations is a Python framework for defining data quality expectations as code with built-in support for testing row counts, column types, value ranges, and statistical distributions. dbt (data build tool) includes testing capabilities for data transformations in analytics pipelines. Apache Griffin provides data quality monitoring for big data environments. Custom pytest-based frameworks with database fixtures handle banking-specific data validation. For Indian banking, combine these with custom validators for IFSC code format, PAN structure, account number check digit algorithms, and CBS-specific data constraints. Integrate data test results into CI/CD dashboards alongside functional test results.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Technical' }
  ],
  'database-explorer': [
    { id: 'IQ-DBE-001', question: 'What are the key SQL skills needed for testing banking databases?', answer: 'Essential skills include writing SELECT queries with JOINs across banking tables like customer-account-transaction relationships, using aggregate functions for balance calculations and transaction summaries, understanding subqueries for finding anomalies like accounts with mismatched running balances, using window functions for running totals and ranking, and writing complex WHERE clauses for filtering by date ranges, transaction types, and account statuses. Knowledge of database-specific features like SQLite pragmas, PostgreSQL CTEs, or Oracle analytical functions used in CBS systems is important. Understanding indexing strategies for optimizing queries on large transaction tables is essential.', difficulty: 'Easy', category: 'Concept' },
    { id: 'IQ-DBE-002', question: 'How do you use SQL queries to verify banking transaction integrity?', answer: 'Write queries that check double-entry bookkeeping by verifying that total debits equal total credits for each transaction. Query to find accounts where the running balance does not match the calculated balance from transaction history. Check for orphan transactions that reference non-existent accounts. Verify that settlement records have matching entries in both the payer and payee accounts. Query for transactions with amounts exceeding configured limits. Check for duplicate transaction reference numbers. For Indian banking, query to verify NEFT batch totals match individual transaction sums, and check that RTGS transactions above Rs 2 lakhs are correctly classified.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Practical' },
    { id: 'IQ-DBE-003', question: 'How do you explore and understand an unfamiliar banking database schema?', answer: 'Start by querying system catalog tables to list all tables and their relationships. Examine foreign key constraints to understand entity relationships. Query a small sample of data from key tables like customer, account, transaction, and GL to understand data patterns. Look for audit tables that track changes. Identify reference data tables containing codes and configurations. For banking databases, identify the chart of accounts structure, product configuration tables, and parameter tables that control business rules. Use ER diagram generation tools to visualize the schema. Document your understanding progressively, focusing on the tables relevant to your testing scope.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Practical' },
    { id: 'IQ-DBE-004', question: 'What database testing scenarios are unique to Indian banking systems?', answer: 'Test that account numbering follows the banks scheme with proper check digits. Verify IFSC code mapping tables are current after bank branch mergers. Test that holiday calendar tables are correctly populated for NEFT/RTGS processing. Validate that tax deduction tables have current TDS rates for deposits and income categories. Check that NRI account tables correctly store currency denomination and repatriation flags. Test that CIBIL score update tables have proper timestamps and source identification. Verify that the PMLA threshold configuration table has correct values for CTR and STR triggers. Test that GST configuration tables correctly map services to rates.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Scenario' },
    { id: 'IQ-DBE-005', question: 'How do you test database performance for banking query patterns?', answer: 'Identify common query patterns from the application including account balance lookups, transaction history retrieval, and reporting aggregations. Run EXPLAIN ANALYZE on each query to understand execution plans and identify missing indexes. Test query performance with production-scale data volumes. Verify that indexes exist on columns used in WHERE clauses and JOIN conditions for banking tables. Test that the most frequent queries like balance inquiry execute within milliseconds. Monitor query performance under concurrent load simulating multiple banking users. For banking, specifically optimize queries for the transaction table which typically has the highest volume and most frequent access.', difficulty: 'Hard', category: 'Technical' },
    { id: 'IQ-DBE-006', question: 'How do you use SQL to validate interest calculations in banking?', answer: 'Write queries that recalculate interest based on daily balances and the applicable rate, then compare with the system-calculated interest entries. For savings accounts, compute daily product (balance multiplied by number of days) and apply the quarterly interest rate. For FDs, calculate using the compound interest formula matching the bank specified compounding frequency. For loans, verify EMI allocation between principal and interest using the reducing balance method. Compare calculated values with actual postings to identify discrepancies. For Indian banking, verify that interest calculations correctly handle leap years, partial periods, and rate changes mid-period.', difficulty: 'Hard', category: 'Technical' },
    { id: 'IQ-DBE-007', question: 'What database security testing should be performed for banking systems?', answer: 'Verify that database users have minimum necessary privileges following the principle of least privilege. Test that application service accounts cannot execute DDL statements like DROP TABLE. Validate that direct database access is restricted and all access goes through the application layer. Test that audit logging captures all database operations on sensitive tables. Verify that database backups are encrypted. Test that database connections use TLS encryption. Check that default database passwords are changed. For banking, verify row-level security where branch users can only access their branch data, and test that the DBA team has separate credentials from the application.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Technical' },
    { id: 'IQ-DBE-008', question: 'How do you validate data constraints and business rules in banking databases?', answer: 'Test that NOT NULL constraints are enforced on mandatory banking fields like account number, customer ID, and transaction amount. Verify CHECK constraints like ensuring account balance cannot go below the minimum balance threshold for the product type. Test UNIQUE constraints on natural keys like transaction reference numbers. Validate that TRIGGER-based business rules correctly fire such as updating the account balance after a transaction insert. For Indian banking, verify constraints like maximum dormancy period, minimum RTGS amount, and valid date ranges for FD maturity. Test that constraint violations return meaningful error messages.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Practical' },
    { id: 'IQ-DBE-009', question: 'How do you test database backup and recovery for banking systems?', answer: 'Test that database backups are created successfully at the scheduled frequency. Verify that backup files are complete by restoring to a test environment and validating data integrity. Test point-in-time recovery to verify that the database can be restored to any moment, which is critical for recovering from erroneous banking transactions. Validate that transaction log backups capture all committed transactions. Test the recovery time against the defined RTO for the banking system. For Indian banking, test that backup procedures meet RBI guidelines for data protection. Verify that cross-region backup replication works for disaster recovery.', difficulty: 'Hard', category: 'Scenario' }
  ],
  'defects': [
    { id: 'IQ-DEF-001', question: 'How do you classify defect severity for banking application bugs?', answer: 'Critical severity is for defects causing financial loss like incorrect fund transfer amounts, data corruption, or complete system unavailability. High severity covers functional failures in major banking operations like loan processing failure or incorrect interest calculation. Medium severity includes issues affecting specific scenarios like incorrect date formatting in statements or minor UI issues in non-critical screens. Low severity covers cosmetic issues and minor inconveniences. For Indian banking, any defect affecting RBI compliance, PMLA reporting, or NPCI integration is automatically elevated to critical or high severity regardless of the specific symptom.', difficulty: 'Easy', category: 'Concept' },
    { id: 'IQ-DEF-002', question: 'What information should a banking defect report contain?', answer: 'A banking defect report should include a clear title describing the business impact, detailed steps to reproduce with specific test data like account numbers and amounts, expected versus actual behavior with reference to the banking specification or RBI guideline, environment details including CBS version and browser, screenshots or video recordings, database evidence showing the incorrect state, logs with correlation IDs and timestamps, the banking module and transaction type affected, regulatory impact assessment if applicable, and workaround if available. For Indian banking, include references to relevant RBI circulars or NPCI specifications when the defect involves regulatory non-compliance.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Practical' },
    { id: 'IQ-DEF-003', question: 'How do you perform root cause analysis for banking defects?', answer: 'Start by reproducing the defect and gathering all evidence including application logs, database state, and CBS communication traces. Trace the transaction flow from the UI through the API layer, business logic, and database to identify where the incorrect behavior originates. For banking defects, check if the issue is in business rule implementation, CBS integration handling, or data transformation. Use the five whys technique to identify systemic causes. For example, incorrect interest was calculated because the rate table was not updated, because there is no automated rate propagation, because the process was not designed. Document root cause and recommend preventive measures.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Practical' },
    { id: 'IQ-DEF-004', question: 'How do you manage defect lifecycle in an agile banking project?', answer: 'Defects flow through states of new, triaged, assigned, in-progress, fixed, verified, and closed. In banking agile projects, triage happens daily with product owner involvement to assess business impact. Critical banking defects bypass the normal sprint flow and go into a hotfix process. Defects found in UAT by bank operations staff are triaged jointly with business analysts to confirm they are genuine defects versus misunderstanding of requirements. Deferred defects require documented risk acceptance from the banking product owner. Track aging of open defects with escalation thresholds. Maintain defect SLAs where critical banking defects must be resolved within 24 hours.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Behavioral' },
    { id: 'IQ-DEF-005', question: 'What is defect leakage and how do you measure and reduce it in banking projects?', answer: 'Defect leakage is when defects escape the testing phase and reach production or a later testing phase. Measure by counting defects found in production that should have been caught during testing. Calculate leakage rate as production defects divided by total defects found in testing plus production. For banking, leakage is particularly costly since production defects can cause financial losses and regulatory penalties. Reduce leakage by improving test coverage for critical banking flows, enhancing test data quality to cover edge cases, adding integration and end-to-end tests, implementing shift-left practices with earlier testing, and conducting defect causal analysis to identify systematic testing gaps.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Concept' },
    { id: 'IQ-DEF-006', question: 'How do you handle defect triage for banking regulatory compliance issues?', answer: 'Compliance defects are triaged with the compliance officer and legal team in addition to the development team. Assess the regulatory impact including potential penalties, reporting deadlines, and audit findings. Classify defects by the regulation affected such as RBI master direction, PMLA, PCI-DSS, or NPCI specification. Compliance defects that could lead to regulatory penalties or reporting failures are always treated as critical. Establish a compliance defect escalation matrix that reaches senior management for high-impact issues. Track compliance defects separately with specific SLAs. Document remediation plans with regulatory justification for any deferred compliance defects.', difficulty: 'Hard', category: 'Scenario' },
    { id: 'IQ-DEF-007', question: 'How do you track and report defect trends in banking projects?', answer: 'Track defect discovery rate over time to identify whether quality is improving or deteriorating. Monitor defect density per banking module to focus improvement efforts on problematic areas. Analyze defect root cause distribution to identify systemic issues like frequent CBS integration errors or recurring data validation gaps. Report defect aging showing how long defects remain open. Track reopened defects indicating fix quality issues. For banking, create module-wise trend reports showing defect patterns in payments, deposits, loans, and cards. Present trends in sprint reviews and steering committee meetings with actionable insights for process improvement.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Behavioral' },
    { id: 'IQ-DEF-008', question: 'What are common defect patterns in banking applications?', answer: 'Common patterns include off-by-one errors in interest calculation days causing incorrect daily interest, rounding errors in financial calculations when using floating-point instead of decimal types, race conditions in concurrent balance updates from multiple channels, incorrect timezone handling for cross-border transactions, boundary value errors at transaction limits where exactly at-limit transactions may fail or succeed incorrectly, null handling errors in optional fields like beneficiary address, and character encoding issues in multi-language customer names. CBS-specific patterns include message format errors in SOAP communication and timeout handling gaps in synchronous CBS calls.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Technical' },
    { id: 'IQ-DEF-009', question: 'How do you ensure defect resolution quality for critical banking defects?', answer: 'Require that every critical banking defect fix includes unit tests covering the specific failure scenario. Conduct peer code review of the fix with a reviewer knowledgeable in the banking domain. Verify the fix in an environment with production-equivalent configuration. Test regression to ensure the fix does not introduce new issues in related banking functionality. For financial calculation fixes, verify against manually calculated expected results. Track fix effectiveness by monitoring whether the same defect reoccurs. Require that root cause analysis is completed and preventive measures are implemented before closing critical defects. For regulatory defects, obtain compliance team sign-off on the fix.', difficulty: 'Hard', category: 'Practical' }
  ],
  'devops-guide': [
    { id: 'IQ-DVG-001', question: 'What is DevOps and how does it benefit banking application delivery?', answer: 'DevOps is a set of practices that combines software development and IT operations to shorten the development lifecycle while delivering quality software continuously. For banking, DevOps enables faster delivery of regulatory updates like new RBI-mandated features, quicker bug fixes for production issues affecting customer transactions, and more reliable deployments through automation. It bridges the gap between development teams and banking operations teams who manage CBS and payment infrastructure. Key benefits include reduced deployment failures through infrastructure as code, faster mean time to recovery from incidents, and improved collaboration between development, testing, and operations teams.', difficulty: 'Easy', category: 'Concept' },
    { id: 'IQ-DVG-002', question: 'How do you implement infrastructure as code for banking test environments?', answer: 'Use Terraform or CloudFormation to define banking test infrastructure including application servers, database instances, CBS simulators, and network configurations as version-controlled code. Create reusable modules for common banking infrastructure patterns like a three-tier web application with database and CBS integration layer. Implement environment templating where SIT, UAT, and pre-production environments are created from the same code with different parameter values. For Indian banking, include NPCI sandbox connectivity configuration and RBI reporting endpoint setup. Implement automated environment provisioning and teardown to optimize costs. Version environment configurations alongside application code.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Technical' },
    { id: 'IQ-DVG-003', question: 'How do you implement monitoring and alerting for banking applications in production?', answer: 'Implement the four golden signals: latency measuring transaction response times against SLAs, traffic monitoring transaction volumes across NEFT, RTGS, UPI, and IMPS, errors tracking transaction failure rates by type, and saturation monitoring CPU, memory, and database connection pool usage. Set up alerts with severity levels where transaction processing failures trigger immediate alerts while slow response times trigger warnings. Use tools like Prometheus for metrics collection, Grafana for visualization, and PagerDuty or Opsgenie for alert routing. For banking, create specific monitors for payment gateway health, CBS connectivity, and NPCI switch latency.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Technical' },
    { id: 'IQ-DVG-004', question: 'What is the role of configuration management in banking DevOps?', answer: 'Configuration management ensures consistent and auditable configuration across all banking environments. Use tools like Ansible, Chef, or Puppet to manage server configurations. Store application configurations in a centralized config server or environment variables with encryption for sensitive values. Implement configuration change tracking with audit trails showing who changed what and when, which is mandatory for banking regulatory compliance. Separate configuration from code so that environment-specific settings like CBS endpoints, NPCI URLs, and encryption keys are managed independently. Version configurations and require approval workflows for production configuration changes.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Practical' },
    { id: 'IQ-DVG-005', question: 'How do you handle secrets management in banking DevOps pipelines?', answer: 'Use dedicated secrets management tools like HashiCorp Vault, AWS Secrets Manager, or Azure Key Vault to store banking credentials, encryption keys, and API tokens. Never store secrets in code repositories, CI/CD pipeline configurations, or container images. Implement secret rotation policies with automated rotation for database passwords and API keys. Use short-lived credentials where possible. Inject secrets into applications at runtime through environment variables or mounted volumes. For banking, implement dual-control for accessing production secrets. Audit all secret access and changes. Encrypt secrets at rest and in transit. Test that applications handle secret rotation gracefully without downtime.', difficulty: 'Hard', category: 'Technical' },
    { id: 'IQ-DVG-006', question: 'What deployment strategies are suitable for banking applications?', answer: 'Blue-green deployment allows instant rollback by maintaining two production environments and switching traffic. Canary deployment routes a small percentage of transactions to the new version to detect issues before full rollout. Rolling updates gradually replace instances with the new version, suitable for stateless banking API services. Feature flags allow deploying code changes to production without activating new features until ready. For banking, the choice depends on the system type. Stateless API services can use rolling updates. Payment processing systems require blue-green for zero-downtime. New banking features use feature flags to decouple deployment from release. All strategies must ensure zero transaction loss during transitions.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Concept' },
    { id: 'IQ-DVG-007', question: 'How do you implement observability in a banking DevOps environment?', answer: 'Implement the three pillars of observability. Metrics collection using Prometheus with banking-specific metrics like transactions per second, payment success rate, and CBS response time. Structured logging with correlation IDs enabling end-to-end transaction tracing from API gateway through business logic to CBS and back. Distributed tracing using tools like Jaeger or Zipkin to visualize transaction flows across banking microservices. Create runbooks linking observability data to remediation actions. For banking, implement business-level observability that tracks financial metrics like daily transaction volumes, reconciliation status, and regulatory report generation alongside technical metrics.', difficulty: 'Hard', category: 'Technical' },
    { id: 'IQ-DVG-008', question: 'What is GitOps and how can it be applied to banking infrastructure management?', answer: 'GitOps uses Git as the single source of truth for declarative infrastructure and application configurations. Changes to banking infrastructure or application deployments are made through Git pull requests with review and approval workflows. Tools like ArgoCD or Flux automatically sync the desired state in Git with the actual cluster state. For banking, GitOps provides a complete audit trail of all infrastructure changes meeting regulatory requirements. Every deployment can be traced to a specific commit with approver information. Rollback is as simple as reverting a Git commit. This approach aligns with banking change management processes while enabling automation.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Concept' },
    { id: 'IQ-DVG-009', question: 'How do you ensure DevOps practices comply with banking audit requirements?', answer: 'Maintain complete audit trails for all pipeline executions, deployments, and configuration changes. Implement separation of duties where developers cannot deploy to production without operations approval. Use signed commits and verified container images to ensure code integrity. Implement change advisory board (CAB) processes for production changes with automated evidence collection. Store deployment artifacts with checksums for verification. For Indian banking, align DevOps practices with RBI IT governance guidelines including periodic access reviews, change management documentation, and incident management procedures. Generate compliance reports from CI/CD pipelines showing all quality gates passed for each deployment.', difficulty: 'Hard', category: 'Behavioral' }
  ],
  'devops-testing-lab': [
    { id: 'IQ-DTL-001', question: 'What is shift-left testing and how is it applied in banking DevOps?', answer: 'Shift-left testing moves testing activities earlier in the development lifecycle to find defects sooner when they are cheaper to fix. In banking DevOps, this means developers write unit tests for financial calculation logic before or alongside the code, static analysis runs on every commit to catch security vulnerabilities in payment handling code, and API contract tests are written as part of feature development. For banking, shift-left also means involving compliance experts during requirements to define regulatory test criteria upfront rather than discovering compliance gaps during UAT. This significantly reduces the cost of fixing defects in critical banking functionality.', difficulty: 'Easy', category: 'Concept' },
    { id: 'IQ-DTL-002', question: 'How do you implement test environment management in a DevOps pipeline for banking?', answer: 'Use infrastructure as code to create ephemeral test environments on demand for each feature branch. Implement a test environment orchestrator that provisions environments with the correct version of all banking services, database schemas, and mock CBS endpoints. Use Docker Compose or Kubernetes namespaces to isolate environments. Implement environment pooling where pre-provisioned environments are allocated to pipeline runs and returned after use. For banking, include pre-loaded test data sets with representative banking entities. Monitor environment health and automatically replace unhealthy environments. Track environment costs and implement automatic cleanup of unused environments.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Technical' },
    { id: 'IQ-DTL-003', question: 'How do you integrate performance testing into a banking DevOps pipeline?', answer: 'Run lightweight performance tests as part of every CI build to detect performance regressions early. Use tools like k6 or Gatling with banking transaction scripts for key flows like balance inquiry and fund transfer. Set performance budgets with thresholds like API response time p95 below 500ms. Run comprehensive performance tests nightly with production-scale load. For banking, create specific performance scenarios for peak banking operations like salary day and month-end processing. Compare performance trends across builds to identify degradation. Integrate performance results into the CI dashboard alongside functional test results. Block deployment if performance thresholds are breached.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Practical' },
    { id: 'IQ-DTL-004', question: 'What is test orchestration in the context of DevOps for banking?', answer: 'Test orchestration coordinates the execution of different test types across multiple stages of the banking CI/CD pipeline. It manages dependencies between test stages ensuring that integration tests run only after unit tests pass. Orchestrates parallel test execution across multiple test environments for faster feedback. Manages test data provisioning and cleanup across pipeline stages. For banking, orchestration ensures that CBS integration tests run against the correct CBS version, that security scans complete before deployment gates, and that regulatory compliance tests are included in every release candidate validation. Tools like Jenkins Pipeline, GitHub Actions, or Azure DevOps orchestrate these workflows.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Concept' },
    { id: 'IQ-DTL-005', question: 'How do you implement continuous testing for banking microservices?', answer: 'Continuous testing embeds automated tests at every stage of the delivery pipeline. At commit stage, run unit tests and static analysis. At build stage, run component tests with mocked dependencies. At integration stage, deploy to Kubernetes and run API contract tests and integration tests against real service instances. At staging, run end-to-end banking workflow tests covering complete transaction flows. At production, run synthetic monitoring tests executing real transactions against canary instances. For banking, each stage includes banking-specific validations like financial calculation accuracy checks, CBS response handling tests, and regulatory compliance verifications.', difficulty: 'Hard', category: 'Technical' },
    { id: 'IQ-DTL-006', question: 'How do you test infrastructure resilience in a banking DevOps environment?', answer: 'Implement automated infrastructure tests that verify server configurations, network connectivity, firewall rules, and SSL certificate validity. Test auto-scaling policies by simulating load increases and verifying new instances are provisioned correctly. Test self-healing mechanisms where failed containers are automatically replaced. Run chaos engineering experiments targeting infrastructure components like terminating random pods, introducing network latency, and filling disk space. For banking, specifically test that infrastructure failures do not cause transaction data loss. Verify that monitoring and alerting correctly detect and report infrastructure issues. Test disaster recovery procedures with automated failover validation.', difficulty: 'Hard', category: 'Practical' },
    { id: 'IQ-DTL-007', question: 'What is canary testing and how is it used for banking deployments?', answer: 'Canary testing deploys a new version to a small subset of production infrastructure and routes a percentage of real traffic to it. Monitor key metrics like transaction success rate, response time, and error rate comparing the canary to the stable version. If metrics are within acceptable thresholds, gradually increase traffic to the canary. If metrics degrade, automatically roll back. For banking, canary testing is valuable because it catches issues that only manifest with real production traffic patterns. Configure canary analysis to monitor banking-specific metrics like payment processing success rate and CBS integration latency. Ensure that canary routing does not split a single transaction across versions.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Concept' },
    { id: 'IQ-DTL-008', question: 'How do you implement test reporting and analytics in a banking DevOps pipeline?', answer: 'Aggregate test results from all pipeline stages into a unified reporting platform. Use tools like Allure for detailed test execution reports or custom dashboards with Grafana. Track metrics like test pass rate trends, test execution duration, flaky test count, and code coverage over time. Implement automated test failure analysis that categorizes failures as product bugs, test issues, or infrastructure problems. For banking, create domain-specific reports showing test coverage per banking module, regulatory test compliance status, and security scan findings. Integrate with Slack or Teams for real-time notification of critical test failures in banking payment modules.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Practical' },
    { id: 'IQ-DTL-009', question: 'How do you handle testing in a banking DevOps environment with legacy system dependencies?', answer: 'Create service virtualization layers that simulate legacy CBS and payment gateway behavior for development and early testing stages. Record production traffic patterns to build realistic virtual service responses. Use contract testing to ensure virtual service behavior matches the real legacy system. Reserve integration testing with real legacy systems for later pipeline stages due to environment constraints. Implement adapter patterns that abstract legacy system communication, making it testable with mocks. For Indian banking, maintain virtual services for NPCI protocols, CBS SOAP interfaces, and SWIFT messaging. Gradually replace virtual services with real integrations as code progresses through the pipeline.', difficulty: 'Hard', category: 'Scenario' }
  ],
  'documentation': [
    { id: 'IQ-DOC-001', question: 'What types of test documentation are essential for banking projects?', answer: 'Essential documentation includes the test strategy defining the overall approach for banking application testing, test plans for each banking module like payments, deposits, and loans, test case specifications with detailed steps and expected results, test data requirements documenting synthetic banking data needs, traceability matrices linking requirements to test cases, test execution reports with pass/fail evidence, and defect reports with root cause analysis. For Indian banking, include regulatory compliance test documentation referencing specific RBI circulars and NPCI specifications that is maintained for audit purposes.', difficulty: 'Easy', category: 'Concept' },
    { id: 'IQ-DOC-002', question: 'How do you maintain test documentation in an agile banking project?', answer: 'Use living documentation approaches where test artifacts are maintained alongside code in version control. Write test cases in Gherkin format that serves as both executable tests and readable documentation. Maintain a wiki or Confluence space with architecture decision records explaining testing strategy choices. Update test plans incrementally each sprint rather than as heavyweight upfront documents. For banking, maintain a compliance test register that is updated with each RBI circular. Use automated tools to generate test coverage reports from code. Keep API documentation synchronized with Swagger/OpenAPI specifications. Conduct quarterly documentation reviews with business and compliance stakeholders.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Practical' },
    { id: 'IQ-DOC-003', question: 'What should a test strategy document cover for a banking application?', answer: 'The test strategy should cover the scope defining which banking modules and transaction types are included, testing levels from unit through UAT, test types including functional, performance, security, and compliance testing, entry and exit criteria for each testing phase, test environment strategy including CBS and NPCI sandbox requirements, test data approach covering synthetic data generation and masking, automation strategy specifying which tests to automate and tools to use, risk assessment identifying high-risk banking areas requiring deeper testing, and defect management process. Include banking-specific sections on regulatory compliance testing approach and payment gateway integration testing strategy.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Concept' },
    { id: 'IQ-DOC-004', question: 'How do you document test evidence for banking regulatory audits?', answer: 'Capture comprehensive evidence including test execution screenshots with timestamps, API request and response logs for transaction validations, database query results showing correct data states, test coverage reports mapped to regulatory requirements, defect reports with resolution evidence, UAT sign-off records from business stakeholders, and performance test results against SLA targets. Organize evidence by RBI circular or regulatory requirement for easy auditor navigation. Store evidence in an immutable repository with access controls. Maintain a compliance testing calendar showing when each regulation was last tested. Create audit-ready summary reports that highlight compliance status with supporting evidence links.', difficulty: 'Hard', category: 'Practical' },
    { id: 'IQ-DOC-005', question: 'What is a requirements traceability matrix and why is it important for banking testing?', answer: 'A requirements traceability matrix (RTM) maps each business requirement to its corresponding test cases, providing visibility into test coverage. In banking, the RTM links RBI regulatory requirements to specific test cases ensuring nothing is missed. It traces from requirement through design, test case, test execution, and defect resolution. This is critical for banking audits where regulators need to verify that every mandated feature has been tested. The RTM helps identify untested requirements and redundant tests. For Indian banking, maintain separate RTM sections for RBI compliance, NPCI integration, PMLA monitoring, and data privacy requirements.', difficulty: 'Easy', category: 'Concept' },
    { id: 'IQ-DOC-006', question: 'How do you document API test specifications for banking services?', answer: 'Document each API endpoint with its URL pattern, HTTP method, request headers, request body schema with field descriptions and validation rules, response schemas for success and error cases, authentication requirements, and rate limits. Include banking-specific details like transaction type codes, IFSC format requirements, and amount precision rules. Provide example requests and responses for common banking scenarios. Document error codes with descriptions and resolution guidance. For Indian banking, reference NPCI specification numbers for payment APIs. Use OpenAPI/Swagger format for machine-readable documentation that can generate test stubs automatically.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Technical' },
    { id: 'IQ-DOC-007', question: 'How do you create effective test case documentation for banking workflows?', answer: 'Write test cases with clear preconditions specifying account states, balance requirements, and user roles. Document step-by-step actions with specific data values like account numbers, IFSC codes, and amounts. Define expected results for each step not just the final outcome. Include both positive and negative scenarios for each banking workflow. Add data-driven variations as parameterized test cases. Cross-reference related test cases for dependent banking flows. For Indian banking, tag test cases with the regulatory requirement they validate. Include cleanup steps to restore account states for test repeatability. Review test cases with banking domain experts to ensure business accuracy.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Practical' },
    { id: 'IQ-DOC-008', question: 'What is the role of release notes in banking software delivery?', answer: 'Release notes document all changes included in a banking application release for various audiences. For operations teams, they describe new features, changed behaviors, and known issues to prepare for customer inquiries. For compliance teams, they highlight regulatory changes implemented like new PMLA thresholds or updated RBI reporting formats. For technology teams, they detail infrastructure changes, configuration updates, and database migrations. For testing teams, they identify areas requiring regression testing. Release notes for banking must include any changes to transaction processing rules, fee structures, or limit configurations. They serve as audit evidence of change management compliance.', difficulty: 'Easy', category: 'Behavioral' },
    { id: 'IQ-DOC-009', question: 'How do you document test automation frameworks for banking project teams?', answer: 'Document the framework architecture including the technology stack, design patterns used like Page Object Model or API client abstraction, and layer responsibilities. Provide setup guides for new team members covering environment prerequisites, repository structure, and configuration steps. Document coding standards for test code including naming conventions for banking test classes and methods. Provide example tests demonstrating common banking test patterns. Document the data management approach including test data factories and cleanup procedures. Include a troubleshooting guide for common issues like CBS timeout handling and environment connectivity problems. Maintain a runbook for CI/CD pipeline configuration and monitoring.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Technical' }
  ],
  'document-verification': [
    { id: 'IQ-DVR-001', question: 'What types of documents are verified during banking KYC processes?', answer: 'Indian banking KYC requires verification of identity proof including Aadhaar card, PAN card, passport, voter ID, and driving license. Address proof documents include utility bills, bank statements, and Aadhaar. For corporate accounts, additional documents include Certificate of Incorporation, MOA/AOA, board resolution, and GST registration. NRI customers require passport with visa stamp and overseas address proof. High-net-worth individuals may need additional documents under Enhanced Due Diligence. Testing must cover all document types with valid, expired, tampered, and format variations to ensure the verification system handles each correctly.', difficulty: 'Easy', category: 'Concept' },
    { id: 'IQ-DVR-002', question: 'How do you test OCR-based document verification in banking applications?', answer: 'Test OCR accuracy across different document types with varying print quality, fonts, and layouts. Validate extraction of key fields like name, date of birth, document number, and address from Aadhaar, PAN, and passport images. Test with documents photographed at different angles, under varying lighting conditions, and with partial obstructions. Verify handling of multilingual documents where Indian documents may have text in both English and a regional language. Test with deliberately poor quality images to verify appropriate rejection with user-friendly error messages. Validate that extracted data matches the expected format for downstream banking systems.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Technical' },
    { id: 'IQ-DVR-003', question: 'How do you test document fraud detection in banking verification systems?', answer: 'Test with known tampered documents where names, dates, or document numbers have been digitally altered. Verify detection of photoshopped images with inconsistent lighting, shadows, or font rendering. Test with documents that have correct format but fake content to verify cross-reference validation against government databases. Validate detection of duplicate documents submitted by different applicants. Test with expired documents that have been digitally modified to show valid dates. For Indian banking, test Aadhaar verification against UIDAI databases, PAN verification with Income Tax Department, and company document verification with MCA portal.', difficulty: 'Hard', category: 'Scenario' },
    { id: 'IQ-DVR-004', question: 'How do you test Video KYC (V-KYC) verification for banking customer onboarding?', answer: 'Test the end-to-end V-KYC flow including customer scheduling, video call connection, live document capture during the call, facial matching against document photo, customer signature capture, and verification recording. Test with varying network conditions simulating real Indian internet quality. Verify that the video recording quality meets RBI minimum requirements. Test liveness detection to prevent photo or video replay attacks. Validate that the V-KYC official can verify all required documents during the session. Test the workflow for incomplete V-KYC sessions requiring rescheduling. Verify that V-KYC records are securely stored with proper audit trails.', difficulty: 'Hard', category: 'Practical' },
    { id: 'IQ-DVR-005', question: 'What compliance requirements affect document verification testing in Indian banking?', answer: 'RBI mandates that banks must verify customer identity using approved document types listed in the PMLA rules. UIDAI guidelines govern Aadhaar-based e-KYC including data encryption standards and consent requirements. The Supreme Court ruling restricts Aadhaar usage to voluntary consent-based verification. CKYC (Central KYC) registry integration is mandatory for sharing KYC records across financial institutions. RBI circular on Video KYC defines minimum requirements for remote verification. Testing must verify compliance with each requirement including proper consent capture, document retention periods, and data sharing restrictions.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Concept' },
    { id: 'IQ-DVR-006', question: 'How do you test document upload functionality in banking applications?', answer: 'Test file type validation ensuring only accepted formats like PDF, JPEG, and PNG are allowed while rejecting executable files and scripts. Verify file size limits are enforced to prevent oversized uploads. Test with corrupted files to ensure graceful error handling. Validate that uploaded documents are stored securely with encryption at rest. Test multi-document upload for accounts requiring several KYC documents. Verify that document metadata like upload timestamp, uploader identity, and document type is correctly captured. Test document replacement workflows for re-KYC scenarios. For banking, verify that the upload interface provides clear instructions for document requirements specific to each account type.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Practical' },
    { id: 'IQ-DVR-007', question: 'How do you test automated document classification in banking?', answer: 'Test that the system correctly classifies uploaded documents into categories like identity proof, address proof, income proof, and business documents. Validate classification accuracy across all document types supported by the bank. Test with ambiguous documents that could belong to multiple categories. Verify that misclassified documents can be manually reclassified by bank staff. Test classification performance with varying image quality. For Indian banking, test classification of all government ID types in both English and regional language versions. Validate that the classification system handles newer document formats like DigiLocker documents and e-Aadhaar XML files.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Technical' },
    { id: 'IQ-DVR-008', question: 'How do you test cross-reference document verification in banking?', answer: 'Test that customer name extracted from Aadhaar matches the name on PAN card accounting for acceptable variations in spelling. Verify that date of birth is consistent across all submitted documents. Test address matching with tolerance for formatting differences between documents. Validate that document numbers are verified against issuing authority databases. Test scenarios where cross-reference reveals discrepancies requiring manual review. For Indian banking, test that Aadhaar number on the PAN card matches the submitted Aadhaar, that the company name on GST registration matches the Certificate of Incorporation for corporate accounts, and that director information matches MCA records.', difficulty: 'Hard', category: 'Scenario' },
    { id: 'IQ-DVR-009', question: 'What are the test data requirements for document verification testing?', answer: 'Create a library of test documents covering all types in various conditions including clear, blurred, partial, and rotated images. Generate synthetic documents with valid formats but fictitious data to avoid privacy issues. Maintain a set of known invalid documents for negative testing including expired documents, tampered images, and wrong document types. For Indian banking, create test Aadhaar cards with valid 12-digit numbers following the Verhoeff checksum algorithm but not corresponding to real individuals. Generate test PAN cards with valid ABCDE1234F format. Include multilingual documents in Hindi, Tamil, and other regional languages. Version control test document sets and update when new document types are supported.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Practical' }
  ],
  'employee-testing': [
    { id: 'IQ-EMP-001', question: 'What are the key testing areas for employee-facing banking applications?', answer: 'Key areas include employee authentication and role-based access control ensuring tellers cannot approve loans and branch managers cannot access treasury operations. Test employee transaction processing workflows including customer service, account opening, and branch transactions. Validate maker-checker approval workflows for high-risk operations. Test employee self-service functions like leave management, performance review, and payroll access. Verify audit logging of all employee actions on customer accounts. Test employee access provisioning and de-provisioning when employees join, transfer, or leave. For Indian banking, test that employee transactions comply with RBI guidelines on staff accountability.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Practical' },
    { id: 'IQ-EMP-002', question: 'How do you test role-based access control for bank employees?', answer: 'Create a comprehensive role-permission matrix covering all employee roles from teller to branch manager to regional head. Test that each role can only access permitted functions by attempting unauthorized operations. Verify that role hierarchy works correctly where senior roles inherit junior role permissions. Test segregation of duties ensuring the same employee cannot both create and approve high-value transactions. Validate that access is correctly restricted by branch, region, or department. Test emergency access procedures that temporarily grant elevated privileges. For Indian banking, test that relationship managers can only access their assigned customer portfolios and that compliance officers have read-only access to transaction monitoring systems.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Technical' },
    { id: 'IQ-EMP-003', question: 'How do you test the maker-checker workflow for bank employee transactions?', answer: 'Verify that the maker can initiate a transaction like creating a demand draft or modifying account limits but cannot approve their own request. Test that the checker receives the pending request with full details for review. Validate that the checker can approve, reject, or return for modification. Test that rejected items include rejection reasons and return to the maker. Verify that approved transactions are processed within SLA timelines. Test multi-level approval workflows for high-value transactions requiring regional or head office approval. Validate the audit trail captures maker ID, checker ID, timestamps, and all transaction details. Test timeout handling for pending approvals.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Scenario' },
    { id: 'IQ-EMP-004', question: 'What security testing is specific to employee-facing banking systems?', answer: 'Test that employee sessions timeout after inactivity, especially on shared branch workstations. Verify that employees cannot access customer accounts outside their assigned branch or portfolio. Test that sensitive operations like viewing full Aadhaar or PAN require additional authentication. Validate that employee access is immediately revoked upon termination. Test that employees cannot export large volumes of customer data without authorization. Verify USB port restrictions on banking workstations. Test that screen capture and screenshot functionality is blocked on sensitive screens. For Indian banking, verify that employee trading restrictions are enforced to prevent insider trading.', difficulty: 'Hard', category: 'Technical' },
    { id: 'IQ-EMP-005', question: 'How do you test employee onboarding and offboarding processes in banking systems?', answer: 'Test that new employee account creation follows the defined approval workflow. Verify that initial access is limited to role-appropriate permissions. Test that system access is provisioned across all necessary banking applications simultaneously. Validate that training completion is verified before granting access to customer-facing systems. For offboarding, test that all access is revoked within the mandated timeframe. Verify that the former employee credentials are deactivated across all systems including CBS, email, VPN, and physical access. Test that pending maker-checker items from the departing employee are reassigned. For Indian banking, verify compliance with RBI guidelines on employee access management.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Practical' },
    { id: 'IQ-EMP-006', question: 'How do you test branch operations workflows in banking applications?', answer: 'Test cash management workflows including vault opening, cash counter allocation, and end-of-day cash reconciliation. Verify customer counter operations like deposit, withdrawal, and cheque encashment with proper validation. Test that branch transaction limits are enforced and exceeded amounts require supervisory override. Validate that the branch GL balances at end of day. Test inter-branch transaction processing. Verify that all customer interactions are logged in the CRM system. Test the branch closing workflow including pending transaction handling, cash verification, and system lockout. For Indian banking, test RBI-mandated customer queue management and service level compliance for different transaction types.', difficulty: 'Hard', category: 'Scenario' },
    { id: 'IQ-EMP-007', question: 'How do you test employee training and certification management in banking?', answer: 'Verify that employees cannot access certain banking functions until mandatory training modules are completed. Test that certification expiry triggers access restriction and renewal notifications. Validate that training records are correctly maintained for regulatory compliance. Test the learning management system integration with the banking application access control system. Verify that role changes trigger required training enrollment. For Indian banking, test compliance with RBI guidelines on employee training for anti-money laundering, cybersecurity awareness, and customer service standards. Validate that training completion reports can be generated for regulatory inspections.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Behavioral' },
    { id: 'IQ-EMP-008', question: 'What testing considerations exist for employee performance management in banking?', answer: 'Test that performance metrics are correctly calculated from banking operational data like transaction volumes processed, customer complaints resolved, and sales targets achieved. Verify that target setting and achievement tracking workflows function correctly. Test that performance reviews follow the defined approval chain. Validate that incentive calculations based on performance data are accurate. Test that performance data privacy is maintained where employees can only view their own data and managers can only view their direct reports. For Indian banking, test compliance with RBI guidelines on performance-based compensation for employees handling customer funds.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Practical' },
    { id: 'IQ-EMP-009', question: 'How do you test the audit trail for employee actions in banking applications?', answer: 'Verify that every employee action on a customer account creates an audit log entry with employee ID, action type, timestamp, customer account details, before and after values, and IP address. Test that audit logs are immutable and cannot be modified by any employee including administrators. Validate that audit log search and reporting functionality works for specific employees, date ranges, action types, and customer accounts. Test that audit logs capture failed access attempts and unauthorized operation attempts. For Indian banking, verify that audit retention meets the minimum 8-year requirement. Test that audit logs support regulatory investigation scenarios by enabling reconstruction of the complete activity timeline for any employee.', difficulty: 'Hard', category: 'Technical' }
  ],
  'error-testing-lab': [
    { id: 'IQ-ETL-001', question: 'What is error testing and why is it particularly important for banking applications?', answer: 'Error testing systematically validates how an application handles error conditions including invalid inputs, system failures, resource exhaustion, and unexpected states. In banking, error handling failures can be catastrophic since an unhandled error during a fund transfer could leave money debited from the sender without crediting the beneficiary. Proper error handling ensures financial data integrity, provides meaningful feedback to bank customers, maintains audit trails for failed operations, and prevents security vulnerabilities through information leakage in error messages. RBI mandates that banking systems must gracefully handle all error scenarios without data loss.', difficulty: 'Easy', category: 'Concept' },
    { id: 'IQ-ETL-002', question: 'How do you test error handling for CBS communication failures in banking?', answer: 'Simulate CBS unavailability and verify that the banking application returns appropriate error messages without exposing internal CBS details. Test timeout handling when CBS responds slowly, ensuring transactions are not left in an indeterminate state. Verify that connection pool exhaustion is handled gracefully with queuing or rejection. Test CBS response format errors where the XML response is malformed. Validate that partial failures where debit succeeds but credit fails trigger automatic reversal mechanisms. Test retry logic with exponential backoff for transient CBS errors. Verify that all CBS communication errors are logged with sufficient detail for troubleshooting while masking sensitive data.', difficulty: 'Hard', category: 'Scenario' },
    { id: 'IQ-ETL-003', question: 'What error scenarios should be tested for banking API endpoints?', answer: 'Test with missing required fields in request payloads. Send malformed JSON or XML bodies. Test with invalid data types like strings in numeric fields. Submit requests with values exceeding field length limits. Test with expired or invalid authentication tokens. Send requests exceeding rate limits. Test with invalid HTTP methods on endpoints. Submit requests with unsupported content types. For banking, specifically test with invalid IFSC codes, non-existent account numbers, amounts with incorrect decimal precision, and dates in wrong formats. Verify that all error responses follow the consistent error envelope format with error code, human-readable message, and correlation ID.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Practical' },
    { id: 'IQ-ETL-004', question: 'How do you test error recovery for banking batch processes?', answer: 'Simulate failures at various points during batch execution including database connection loss mid-batch, disk space exhaustion during report generation, and memory overflow during large dataset processing. Verify that the batch process can restart from the last checkpoint without reprocessing completed records. Test that partial batch results are marked as incomplete and do not feed downstream processes. Validate that error notification alerts are sent to operations teams. For banking EOD processing, test recovery scenarios for interest calculation batches, NEFT settlement batches, and regulatory report generation. Verify that restarted batches produce identical results to a clean run.', difficulty: 'Hard', category: 'Technical' },
    { id: 'IQ-ETL-005', question: 'How do you test error messages for banking customer-facing applications?', answer: 'Verify that error messages are user-friendly and do not expose technical details like stack traces, SQL queries, or internal service names. Test that error messages provide actionable guidance like please verify the IFSC code and try again rather than generic errors. Validate that error messages are consistent across all banking channels. Test that error codes are documented and support customer service representatives in resolving issues. For Indian banking, verify error messages in supported regional languages. Test that error messages for transaction failures include reference numbers for follow-up. Verify that security-sensitive errors like invalid credentials do not reveal which field was incorrect.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Practical' },
    { id: 'IQ-ETL-006', question: 'How do you test database error handling in banking applications?', answer: 'Simulate database connection failures and verify that the application degrades gracefully showing cached data where possible. Test constraint violation handling when attempting to insert duplicate transaction reference numbers. Verify deadlock detection and retry behavior for concurrent account updates. Test timeout handling for long-running database queries. Simulate disk full conditions and verify that no partial writes corrupt financial data. Test that database connection pool exhaustion returns meaningful errors rather than hanging. For banking, verify that all database errors trigger transaction rollback to prevent inconsistent account balances. Test that database errors are logged with sufficient detail including the SQL statement and parameters for investigation.', difficulty: 'Hard', category: 'Technical' },
    { id: 'IQ-ETL-007', question: 'How do you test error handling in payment gateway integrations?', answer: 'Simulate all NPCI response codes for UPI transactions including success, failure, timeout, and pending statuses. Test handling of network errors during payment processing. Verify that duplicate response handling prevents double processing. Test timeout scenarios where the payment gateway does not respond within the SLA window. Validate that reversal mechanisms are triggered for failed transactions. Test handling of inconsistent status where the gateway reports success but the bank detects a processing error. For Indian banking, test NEFT return scenarios with all RBI-defined return reasons, RTGS queue overflow handling, and IMPS timeout recovery. Verify that every error scenario leaves the customer account in a consistent state.', difficulty: 'Hard', category: 'Scenario' },
    { id: 'IQ-ETL-008', question: 'What is the error testing approach for banking mobile applications?', answer: 'Test error handling when the device loses network connectivity mid-transaction. Verify that app state is preserved during network interruptions and transactions can be resumed or properly cancelled. Test behavior when the app is backgrounded during a transaction. Validate error handling for biometric authentication failures including fingerprint and face ID. Test memory pressure handling when the device is low on resources. Verify that timeout errors on slow networks provide appropriate feedback. For Indian banking mobile apps, test error handling for UPI PIN entry failures, VPA resolution errors, and SMS-based OTP delivery failures. Test that partially completed transactions do not result in unauthorized debits.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Practical' },
    { id: 'IQ-ETL-009', question: 'How do you measure error handling effectiveness in banking applications?', answer: 'Track metrics including the percentage of error scenarios covered by automated tests, the ratio of gracefully handled errors versus unhandled exceptions in production, mean time to detect error handling failures, and customer complaint rate related to error messages. Monitor error log quality measuring how many production errors have sufficient information for root cause analysis versus generic messages. For banking, track financial impact of error handling failures such as stuck transactions, incorrect reversals, or duplicate processing. Conduct periodic error injection testing in production-like environments to verify that error handling remains effective as the application evolves.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Behavioral' }
  ],
  'f12-testing-lab': [
    { id: 'IQ-F12-001', question: 'What is F12 developer tools testing and why is it important for banking web applications?', answer: 'F12 testing uses browser developer tools to inspect, debug, and validate banking web application behavior at the network, DOM, and console levels. It is important for banking because it reveals hidden security issues like sensitive data in API responses that should be masked, insecure cookies without HttpOnly or Secure flags, client-side validation that can be bypassed, and JavaScript console errors that indicate potential functionality issues. Testers use F12 tools to verify that banking transactions are processed correctly at the API level, that no sensitive financial data leaks through client-side code, and that performance bottlenecks are identified.', difficulty: 'Easy', category: 'Concept' },
    { id: 'IQ-F12-002', question: 'How do you use the Network tab to test banking API calls?', answer: 'Monitor API requests and responses for banking operations like login, balance inquiry, and fund transfer. Verify that all requests use HTTPS with no mixed content. Check request headers for proper authentication tokens and security headers. Inspect response payloads to ensure sensitive data like full account numbers and Aadhaar are masked. Monitor response times to identify slow API calls affecting user experience. Check for unnecessary API calls that could indicate performance issues. For Indian banking, verify that UPI callback responses, NEFT processing status checks, and CBS API calls return expected data formats. Use the network tab to test offline behavior when connectivity is interrupted.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Practical' },
    { id: 'IQ-F12-003', question: 'How do you test security using browser developer tools in banking applications?', answer: 'Inspect cookies for missing security flags like HttpOnly, Secure, and SameSite. Check local storage and session storage for sensitive banking data that should not be stored client-side. Review the console for JavaScript errors that might reveal internal system information. Attempt to modify hidden form fields or JavaScript variables to bypass client-side validation for transaction amounts or beneficiary details. Use the console to test for DOM-based XSS vulnerabilities. Verify Content Security Policy headers in the response. For banking, check that session tokens, account numbers, and UPI PINs are never visible in browser storage or console logs.', difficulty: 'Hard', category: 'Technical' },
    { id: 'IQ-F12-004', question: 'How do you use the Performance tab for banking web application testing?', answer: 'Record performance profiles during key banking operations like loading the account dashboard with multiple accounts and transactions. Identify long-running JavaScript tasks that cause UI jank during transaction processing. Analyze layout shifts that might cause customers to click wrong buttons during fund transfer. Measure Time to First Contentful Paint and Largest Contentful Paint for banking pages. Identify memory leaks by monitoring heap size over extended sessions typical for branch banking users. For Indian banking, profile performance of pages displaying large transaction histories and account statements with thousands of entries.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Technical' },
    { id: 'IQ-F12-005', question: 'How do you test responsive design for banking applications using developer tools?', answer: 'Use the device toolbar to simulate various screen sizes from mobile phones to branch banking desktop monitors. Test that banking forms like fund transfer and account opening are usable on all screen sizes. Verify that financial data like account balances and transaction tables are readable without horizontal scrolling on mobile devices. Test that critical buttons like confirm transfer and cancel are accessible on small screens. Check that the virtual keyboard does not obscure input fields on mobile banking pages. For Indian banking, test on common Indian smartphone resolutions and verify that multi-language content renders correctly across screen sizes.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Practical' },
    { id: 'IQ-F12-006', question: 'How do you use the Application tab to test banking web application storage?', answer: 'Inspect local storage, session storage, and IndexedDB for any cached banking data. Verify that sensitive data like account numbers, balances, and transaction details are not persisted in browser storage. Check that service worker caching does not cache authenticated banking pages that should require fresh authentication. Verify cookie properties including expiry times, domain restrictions, and path settings. Test that clearing browser data properly removes all banking session data. For Indian banking, verify that UPI VPA details and saved beneficiary lists are handled securely in application storage and cleared on logout.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Technical' },
    { id: 'IQ-F12-007', question: 'How do you test API response manipulation using browser tools?', answer: 'Use the Network tab to capture banking API responses and attempt to replay or modify them using tools like the browser console or local override feature. This simulates attack scenarios where a malicious user intercepts and modifies API responses. Verify that the banking application validates server responses and does not blindly trust client-side data. Test that modifying the balance display in JavaScript does not allow processing transactions beyond the actual balance. For banking, attempt to modify transaction approval responses from failed to success to verify server-side validation. This testing reveals whether the application properly implements server-side authority for all banking operations.', difficulty: 'Hard', category: 'Scenario' },
    { id: 'IQ-F12-008', question: 'How do you use the Console tab for banking application testing?', answer: 'Monitor JavaScript errors during banking operations that might indicate functionality issues. Test for console warning messages about deprecated features or security issues. Verify that no sensitive banking data is logged to the console in production builds. Use the console to execute JavaScript testing banking client-side logic like form validation and amount formatting. Check for unhandled promise rejections during asynchronous banking operations like payment processing. For Indian banking, verify that console logging is disabled in production and that error messages do not reveal CBS endpoint details or internal API structures that could aid attackers.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Practical' },
    { id: 'IQ-F12-009', question: 'How do you test web socket connections in banking applications using developer tools?', answer: 'Monitor web socket frames in the Network tab for real-time banking features like live balance updates, transaction notifications, and chat support. Verify that web socket connections use WSS (secure web sockets) not WS. Check that authentication is validated before establishing the connection. Test reconnection behavior when the connection drops. Verify that web socket messages containing financial data are properly structured and do not include excessive information. For Indian banking, test web socket reliability for features like real-time UPI payment notifications and live stock trading updates in bank demat services. Verify that stale web socket data does not override more recent database state.', difficulty: 'Hard', category: 'Technical' }
  ],
  'frontend-testing': [
    { id: 'IQ-FET-001', question: 'What are the key areas of frontend testing for banking applications?', answer: 'Key areas include functional testing of banking UI components like forms, tables, and navigation. Visual regression testing to catch unintended UI changes in financial data displays. Cross-browser testing across Chrome, Firefox, Safari, and Edge since banking customers use diverse browsers. Accessibility testing for WCAG compliance. Performance testing for page load times and rendering efficiency. Security testing for XSS, CSRF, and client-side data exposure. Responsive design testing across devices. Internationalization testing for multi-language support. For Indian banking, test Rupee symbol rendering, Aadhaar masking in displays, and IFSC code formatting across all UI components.', difficulty: 'Easy', category: 'Concept' },
    { id: 'IQ-FET-002', question: 'How do you implement component testing for banking UI elements?', answer: 'Use testing libraries like React Testing Library or Vue Test Utils to test individual banking components in isolation. Test form components like fund transfer forms with valid and invalid inputs verifying proper validation messages. Test data display components like transaction tables with various data volumes and formats. Test interactive components like date pickers for statement filters and amount input fields with currency formatting. Mock API responses to test component behavior with different banking data scenarios. For Indian banking, test components that display monetary amounts with Indian number formatting using lakhs and crores, IFSC code input with auto-validation, and multi-language toggle behavior.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Technical' },
    { id: 'IQ-FET-003', question: 'How do you test form validation in banking web applications?', answer: 'Test client-side validation for all banking form fields including account number format validation, IFSC code pattern matching, amount field allowing only valid numeric input with up to two decimal places, and beneficiary name restricting special characters. Verify that validation messages are descriptive and positioned correctly. Test that form submission is prevented when validation fails. Verify that server-side validation catches issues that bypass client-side checks. Test the entire form submission flow including loading states and success/error feedback. For Indian banking, test PAN format validation, Aadhaar number validation with Verhoeff algorithm, and mobile number validation for 10-digit Indian numbers.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Practical' },
    { id: 'IQ-FET-004', question: 'How do you test end-to-end user journeys in banking frontend applications?', answer: 'Use Cypress, Playwright, or Selenium to automate critical banking user journeys from login through transaction completion. Test the complete fund transfer journey including login, selecting beneficiary, entering amount, OTP verification, and confirmation. Test account opening journey with form filling, document upload, and submission. Test loan application with eligibility check, document submission, and status tracking. Each journey test verifies the UI state at each step and the final outcome. For Indian banking, test complete UPI payment flows, cheque book request journeys, and fixed deposit creation workflows. Include both happy path and common error scenarios.', difficulty: 'Hard', category: 'Practical' },
    { id: 'IQ-FET-005', question: 'How do you handle authentication testing in banking frontend tests?', answer: 'Create test fixtures that manage authentication state without going through the full login flow for every test, reducing test execution time. Use API calls to obtain authentication tokens and inject them into the browser context. Test the complete login flow separately including username/password validation, CAPTCHA handling, OTP verification, and session establishment. Test session expiry handling in the UI verifying that users are redirected to login with a clear message. Test that authenticated routes are protected from direct URL access. For Indian banking, test the multi-factor authentication flow, device binding verification, and the session management across multiple tabs.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Technical' },
    { id: 'IQ-FET-006', question: 'How do you perform cross-browser testing for banking applications?', answer: 'Define a browser support matrix based on the banks customer analytics showing which browsers and versions are most used. Test all critical banking functions including transactions, statements, and account management across Chrome, Firefox, Safari, and Edge. Pay special attention to JavaScript features used in financial calculations ensuring cross-browser compatibility. Test CSS rendering for financial data tables, charts, and forms. Use tools like BrowserStack or Sauce Labs for automated cross-browser testing. For Indian banking, include testing on UC Browser and Samsung Internet which have significant market share in India. Verify PDF generation for bank statements across all browsers.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Practical' },
    { id: 'IQ-FET-007', question: 'How do you test state management in banking single-page applications?', answer: 'Test that application state correctly reflects banking data changes after transactions. Verify that navigating between pages preserves state like filter selections in transaction history. Test state synchronization when data is modified in another tab or by another user. Verify that state is properly cleared on logout preventing sensitive data from persisting. Test error state handling ensuring that failed API calls update the UI with appropriate error displays. For banking SPAs, test that the account balance displayed in the header updates immediately after a transaction without requiring a page refresh. Validate that form state is preserved when users navigate away and return using browser back button.', difficulty: 'Hard', category: 'Technical' },
    { id: 'IQ-FET-008', question: 'How do you test accessibility in banking frontend applications?', answer: 'Use automated tools like axe-core integrated into the test suite to catch common accessibility violations. Test keyboard navigation through all banking workflows ensuring every interactive element is reachable. Verify screen reader compatibility for financial data like account balances, transaction amounts, and status indicators using tools like NVDA and VoiceOver. Test color contrast ratios for all text displaying financial information. Verify that form error messages are announced by screen readers. Test that dynamic content updates like loading states and real-time notifications are accessible. For Indian banking, test accessibility for multi-language interfaces and verify ARIA labels are localized.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Practical' },
    { id: 'IQ-FET-009', question: 'How do you test error handling in banking frontend applications?', answer: 'Test that network errors during transactions show appropriate user feedback with retry options. Verify that API timeout errors display meaningful messages rather than generic error pages. Test that validation errors are clearly associated with the relevant form fields. Verify that the UI handles unexpected API response formats gracefully. Test error boundary components that catch JavaScript runtime errors preventing blank screen crashes. For banking, test specific error scenarios like session expiry during fund transfer, OTP timeout during transaction verification, and CBS unavailability affecting balance display. Verify that error states allow users to recover without losing entered transaction data.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Scenario' }
  ],
  'healthcheck': [
    { id: 'IQ-HCK-001', question: 'What is a health check endpoint and why is it important for banking applications?', answer: 'A health check endpoint is an API that reports the overall health status of a banking application and its dependencies. It returns information about the applications readiness to serve requests, database connectivity, CBS integration status, and external service availability. Health checks are critical for banking because they enable load balancers to route traffic away from unhealthy instances, allow monitoring systems to detect failures before they impact customer transactions, and support automated deployment verification. For Indian banking, health checks should verify NPCI connectivity, CBS availability, and payment gateway responsiveness.', difficulty: 'Easy', category: 'Concept' },
    { id: 'IQ-HCK-002', question: 'How do you design comprehensive health checks for a banking microservices system?', answer: 'Implement multi-level health checks. Liveness checks verify the application process is running and responsive. Readiness checks verify the application can serve requests by testing database connections, CBS connectivity, and cache availability. Dependency checks verify each external service is reachable including NPCI gateway, CBS, email service, and SMS gateway. Deep health checks run lightweight test queries against each dependency to verify functional correctness not just connectivity. For banking, include checks for encryption service availability, authentication service health, and payment processing readiness. Each check should return status, latency, and last successful time.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Technical' },
    { id: 'IQ-HCK-003', question: 'How do you test health check endpoints for banking applications?', answer: 'Test that health endpoints return correct status when all dependencies are healthy. Simulate individual dependency failures and verify the health check accurately reports the degraded dependency. Test that health check response time is within acceptable limits since slow health checks can cause false positives in load balancer configurations. Verify that health checks do not expose sensitive information like database connection strings or internal IP addresses. Test that health check endpoints do not require authentication since they need to be accessible to load balancers and monitoring systems. For banking, test health check behavior during peak load to ensure they remain responsive.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Practical' },
    { id: 'IQ-HCK-004', question: 'What is the difference between liveness and readiness probes in banking applications?', answer: 'Liveness probes check if the application is alive and should be restarted if unresponsive. A banking application that is deadlocked or in an infinite loop would fail the liveness probe, triggering a container restart. Readiness probes check if the application is ready to receive traffic. During startup, a banking service may be alive but not ready until it establishes CBS connections and loads configuration data. If the CBS connection is temporarily lost, the readiness probe fails, removing the instance from the load balancer without restarting it. This distinction prevents routing customer transactions to instances that cannot process them while avoiding unnecessary restarts.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Concept' },
    { id: 'IQ-HCK-005', question: 'How do you implement health check monitoring and alerting for banking systems?', answer: 'Configure monitoring tools like Prometheus to scrape health check endpoints at regular intervals. Create Grafana dashboards showing health status across all banking services. Implement tiered alerting where a single health check failure triggers a warning, sustained failures trigger a critical alert, and multiple service failures trigger a system-wide alert to the incident response team. For banking, set different alerting thresholds for customer-facing services versus internal batch processes. Configure alert routing to the appropriate team based on the failed dependency. Implement alert suppression during planned maintenance windows to avoid alert fatigue.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Practical' },
    { id: 'IQ-HCK-006', question: 'How do you test health check integration with container orchestration platforms?', answer: 'Test that Kubernetes liveness probes correctly restart pods when the banking application becomes unresponsive. Verify that readiness probes remove unhealthy pods from the service load balancer. Test the timing configuration ensuring probe intervals and failure thresholds are appropriate for banking applications. Verify that slow CBS responses do not cause false health check failures by setting appropriate timeout values. Test that pod restarts triggered by health check failures do not cause in-flight transaction loss. For banking, verify that health check-triggered restarts include graceful shutdown handling where current transactions complete before the pod terminates.', difficulty: 'Hard', category: 'Technical' },
    { id: 'IQ-HCK-007', question: 'What banking-specific health checks should be implemented?', answer: 'CBS connectivity check verifying the Core Banking System is reachable and responding. NPCI switch health check for payment processing readiness. Encryption service check verifying that the encryption key is loaded and functional. HSM (Hardware Security Module) availability check for production environments. Database connection pool check ensuring sufficient connections are available. Certificate validity check ensuring SSL certificates are not near expiry. Batch processing readiness check verifying that scheduled batch jobs can execute. For Indian banking, include UIDAI connectivity for Aadhaar-based services, CKYC registry availability, and SWIFT gateway health for international transactions.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Practical' },
    { id: 'IQ-HCK-008', question: 'How do you handle health check dependencies and cascading failures?', answer: 'Implement circuit breakers in health checks to prevent a single slow dependency from causing timeout cascading. Set timeout thresholds for each dependency check to prevent health check endpoint from blocking. Return partial health status showing which dependencies are healthy and which are degraded. For banking, distinguish between critical and non-critical dependencies. A failed SMS gateway should mark health as degraded not down, while a failed CBS connection should mark the transaction service as unavailable. Implement health check caching with short TTL to reduce load on dependencies. Test that health check failures in one microservice do not cascade to cause failures in dependent services.', difficulty: 'Hard', category: 'Scenario' },
    { id: 'IQ-HCK-009', question: 'How do you use health checks for deployment verification in banking systems?', answer: 'After deploying a new version of a banking service, the deployment orchestrator queries the health check endpoint to verify successful startup. The health check verifies database migration completion, configuration loading, CBS connection establishment, and cache warming before reporting readiness. Implement a deployment health check that runs a lightweight synthetic transaction to verify end-to-end functionality. If the health check fails within the deployment timeout, automatic rollback is triggered. For banking, the deployment health check should verify that the new version can successfully process a test transaction including database write, CBS call, and response formatting.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Technical' }
  ],
  'infra-testing-lab': [
    { id: 'IQ-ITL-001', question: 'What is infrastructure testing and why is it important for banking environments?', answer: 'Infrastructure testing validates that the underlying computing infrastructure including servers, networks, storage, and cloud services meets the requirements for running banking applications reliably and securely. It ensures that network configurations correctly isolate banking zones, storage performance supports transaction throughput requirements, backup systems function correctly, and security controls are properly implemented. For Indian banking, infrastructure testing verifies compliance with RBI IT governance requirements including data center standards, disaster recovery capabilities, and network security controls. Infrastructure failures directly impact banking availability and customer trust.', difficulty: 'Easy', category: 'Concept' },
    { id: 'IQ-ITL-002', question: 'How do you test network security configurations for banking infrastructure?', answer: 'Verify firewall rules ensure that only authorized traffic flows between banking network zones. Test that the DMZ correctly isolates internet-facing banking services from internal CBS systems. Validate VPN configurations for remote branch connectivity. Test network segmentation between different banking environments. Verify intrusion detection and prevention system effectiveness by simulating attack traffic. Test DDoS protection mechanisms for banking web portals. Validate that internal services are not accessible from the internet. For Indian banking, test compliance with RBI network security guidelines including isolation of SWIFT infrastructure, payment network segmentation, and internet banking zone security.', difficulty: 'Hard', category: 'Technical' },
    { id: 'IQ-ITL-003', question: 'How do you test database infrastructure for banking applications?', answer: 'Verify database server configuration meets banking performance requirements including memory allocation, connection pool sizing, and query cache configuration. Test database replication ensuring real-time synchronization between primary and replica nodes. Validate backup automation with scheduled backup verification through test restores. Test database failover verifying automatic switchover to the standby node with minimal downtime. Verify encryption at rest for all banking databases. Test that database monitoring alerts on slow queries, connection pool exhaustion, and replication lag. For Indian banking, verify that database infrastructure meets RBI guidelines for data protection and availability.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Practical' },
    { id: 'IQ-ITL-004', question: 'How do you test load balancer configurations for banking services?', answer: 'Verify that load balancers distribute traffic evenly across banking application instances. Test health check integration ensuring unhealthy instances are removed from the pool. Validate SSL/TLS termination at the load balancer with proper certificate configuration. Test session persistence configuration for banking applications requiring sticky sessions. Verify that load balancer failover works correctly with the standby load balancer taking over seamlessly. Test rate limiting and connection limiting rules at the load balancer level. For Indian banking, test that load balancers correctly handle the traffic patterns during peak banking hours and UPI transaction surges.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Technical' },
    { id: 'IQ-ITL-005', question: 'How do you test disaster recovery infrastructure for banking systems?', answer: 'Verify that DR site infrastructure mirrors production configuration. Test data replication between primary and DR sites validating real-time synchronization with minimal lag. Conduct DR failover drills by simulating primary site failure and verifying complete banking operations can resume at the DR site within the defined RTO. Test network failover including DNS switching and VPN reconfiguration. Validate that DR site capacity can handle full production load. Test failback procedures from DR back to primary after incident resolution. For Indian banking, verify that DR infrastructure meets RBI mandated RPO and RTO requirements and that DR drills are conducted at least twice annually.', difficulty: 'Hard', category: 'Scenario' },
    { id: 'IQ-ITL-006', question: 'How do you test storage infrastructure for banking data?', answer: 'Verify storage performance meets IOPS requirements for banking transaction processing. Test storage redundancy ensuring no data loss from individual disk failures. Validate storage snapshots for point-in-time recovery of banking databases. Test storage encryption for compliance with data-at-rest encryption requirements. Verify storage capacity planning and alert thresholds before reaching full capacity. Test storage tiering policies where hot transaction data is on fast storage and archived data moves to cost-effective tiers. For Indian banking, verify that storage infrastructure meets RBI guidelines for data retention and that archived banking records remain accessible for the mandated period.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Technical' },
    { id: 'IQ-ITL-007', question: 'How do you test certificate management infrastructure for banking?', answer: 'Verify that all SSL/TLS certificates are issued by approved Certificate Authorities. Test certificate renewal automation to prevent expiry-related outages. Validate certificate chain completeness across all banking endpoints. Test certificate pinning for mobile banking applications. Verify that expired or revoked certificates are properly handled. Test mutual TLS (mTLS) configuration for service-to-service communication in banking microservices. For Indian banking, verify that certificates for NPCI integration endpoints, SWIFT infrastructure, and RBI reporting endpoints are valid and properly configured. Implement certificate expiry monitoring with alerts at 30, 15, and 7 days before expiry.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Practical' },
    { id: 'IQ-ITL-008', question: 'How do you test monitoring and logging infrastructure for banking systems?', answer: 'Verify that all banking application logs are collected centrally using tools like ELK stack or Splunk. Test that log retention meets RBI-mandated requirements. Validate that monitoring tools correctly capture and alert on banking-specific metrics. Test log search and analysis capabilities for incident investigation scenarios. Verify that dashboards accurately display banking system health. Test alerting delivery through multiple channels including email, SMS, and PagerDuty. For Indian banking, verify that audit logs are stored in tamper-proof storage, that compliance monitoring dashboards are functional, and that SIEM (Security Information and Event Management) integration works correctly.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Practical' },
    { id: 'IQ-ITL-009', question: 'What is infrastructure as code testing and how does it apply to banking?', answer: 'Infrastructure as code testing validates Terraform, Ansible, or CloudFormation configurations before applying them to banking environments. Use tools like Terratest for testing Terraform modules, Ansible Molecule for testing Ansible roles, and cfn-lint for CloudFormation templates. Test that security group rules correctly restrict access to banking services. Validate that resource configurations meet banking compliance requirements like encryption enabled on all storage volumes. Test that infrastructure changes do not disrupt running banking services. For Indian banking, include policy-as-code tests that verify compliance with RBI infrastructure requirements automatically as part of the deployment pipeline.', difficulty: 'Hard', category: 'Technical' }
  ],
  'interview-prep': [
    { id: 'IQ-INP-001', question: 'How do you prepare for a banking domain testing interview?', answer: 'Start by understanding core banking concepts including CBS, GL, payment systems like NEFT, RTGS, UPI, and IMPS. Study RBI regulations affecting software testing like PMLA, KYC, and digital lending guidelines. Review common banking workflows for deposits, loans, payments, and cards. Prepare examples from your experience covering test strategy design, defect management, and test automation for financial applications. Practice explaining technical testing concepts in banking domain language. Understand the difference between testing requirements at different banking technology companies like product companies, system integrators, and fintech startups.', difficulty: 'Easy', category: 'Behavioral' },
    { id: 'IQ-INP-002', question: 'What banking domain knowledge should a QA tester possess?', answer: 'A QA tester in banking should understand account types and their rules including savings, current, FD, RD, NRE, and NRO accounts. Know payment systems including NEFT processing windows, RTGS real-time settlement, UPI flow, and IMPS functionality. Understand loan lifecycle from origination through EMI processing to closure. Know card operations including issuance, transaction authorization, and billing. Understand KYC and PMLA requirements. Know regulatory bodies RBI and NPCI and their guidelines affecting testing. Understand financial calculations like interest computation methods, EMI calculation, and TDS rules. This knowledge helps in designing meaningful test cases.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Concept' },
    { id: 'IQ-INP-003', question: 'How would you answer tell me about a challenging defect you found in a banking application?', answer: 'Structure your answer using the STAR method. Describe the Situation like testing a fund transfer module during CBS migration. Explain the Task of validating transaction accuracy. Describe the Action where you discovered that interest calculation for the last day of the month was off by one paisa due to a rounding error in the daily balance computation when the month had 31 days. This accumulated to significant amounts across thousands of accounts. Explain the Result that fixing this prevented financial discrepancies that could have affected lakhs of customers and triggered regulatory concerns. Highlight the testing technique you used and why it was effective.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Behavioral' },
    { id: 'IQ-INP-004', question: 'How do you demonstrate test automation skills in a banking interview?', answer: 'Prepare to discuss your automation framework design decisions and how they address banking testing challenges like handling CBS SOAP interfaces, managing test data for financial transactions, and implementing retry logic for flaky banking environment integrations. Show code samples demonstrating API test automation for banking endpoints with proper assertion patterns. Discuss your approach to the test automation pyramid in banking context. Explain how you have integrated automation into CI/CD pipelines with banking-specific quality gates. Be ready to write live code for testing a simple banking API endpoint during the interview.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Practical' },
    { id: 'IQ-INP-005', question: 'What questions should you ask the interviewer in a banking QA interview?', answer: 'Ask about the testing maturity level and automation coverage across banking modules. Inquire about the CBS platform used and the test environment availability challenges. Ask about the deployment frequency and CI/CD pipeline maturity. Understand the team structure and how QA collaborates with developers and business analysts. Ask about the regulatory compliance testing approach and how they handle RBI circular implementations. Inquire about test data management strategies for banking data privacy. Ask about performance testing practices for transaction-heavy banking operations. These questions demonstrate your understanding of banking testing challenges and your interest in the role.', difficulty: 'Easy', category: 'Behavioral' },
    { id: 'IQ-INP-006', question: 'How do you prepare for a scenario-based question in a banking testing interview?', answer: 'Practice breaking down banking scenarios into test cases systematically. For a scenario like test the NEFT transfer feature, start by identifying all the sub-components including beneficiary management, amount validation, schedule selection, authentication, processing, and confirmation. Then enumerate positive and negative test cases for each component. Include boundary values based on RBI-mandated limits. Consider integration points like CBS, NPCI, and notification services. Think about non-functional requirements like performance under peak load and security. Practice presenting your approach structured by test type covering functional, negative, boundary, security, and performance.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Practical' },
    { id: 'IQ-INP-007', question: 'How do you explain your experience with different testing types in banking context?', answer: 'Map each testing type to a banking example. Functional testing verifying that NEFT transfer debits the correct amount from the sender. Integration testing validating the end-to-end flow from banking app through CBS to NPCI. Regression testing ensuring that a new loan feature does not break existing deposit interest calculation. Performance testing simulating salary day transaction volumes to verify system handles the load. Security testing validating that API endpoints are protected against IDOR attacks on account data. Compliance testing verifying PMLA transaction monitoring rules are correctly implemented. Use specific metrics and outcomes to demonstrate impact.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Behavioral' },
    { id: 'IQ-INP-008', question: 'How do you handle technical coding challenges in banking QA interviews?', answer: 'Common coding challenges include writing SQL queries to find anomalies in banking transaction data, creating API test scripts for banking endpoints using Python/requests or Java/RestAssured, writing test assertions for financial calculations with proper decimal precision handling, and designing a test framework structure for banking services. Practice writing clean, well-structured code that handles banking edge cases like null amounts, negative values, and boundary conditions. Demonstrate knowledge of test design patterns like factory methods for test data generation and builder patterns for complex banking request construction. Show proficiency in assertion libraries and mocking frameworks.', difficulty: 'Hard', category: 'Technical' },
    { id: 'IQ-INP-009', question: 'How do you discuss salary expectations and career growth in banking QA?', answer: 'Research market rates for banking QA roles at your experience level using platforms like Glassdoor and levels.fyi for Indian banking companies. Understand that banking domain expertise commands a premium over generic QA roles. Discuss career growth paths from QA engineer to senior QA to QA lead to QA architect or test manager in banking. Highlight certifications like ISTQB, AWS, or domain-specific certifications that add value. Discuss how banking QA skills are transferable across the financial services industry including insurance, fintech, and payment companies. Frame your expectations based on the value you bring in terms of domain knowledge, technical skills, and quality impact.', difficulty: 'Easy', category: 'Behavioral' }
  ],
  'interview-strategies': [
    { id: 'IQ-INS-001', question: 'What is the STAR method and how do you apply it in banking QA interviews?', answer: 'STAR stands for Situation, Task, Action, Result and is used to structure behavioral interview answers. In banking QA, use it to describe testing achievements. Situation: Our bank was migrating from a legacy CBS to Finacle and needed comprehensive migration testing. Task: I was responsible for designing the test strategy for account balance verification across 2 million accounts. Action: I developed automated reconciliation scripts that compared balance snapshots between old and new systems, flagging discrepancies above Re 1. Result: We identified 847 accounts with balance mismatches before go-live, preventing Rs 2.3 crores in potential discrepancies. Always quantify results.', difficulty: 'Easy', category: 'Behavioral' },
    { id: 'IQ-INS-002', question: 'How do you handle what is your greatest weakness type questions in banking context?', answer: 'Choose a genuine area for improvement that you are actively addressing. For banking QA, you might say you initially focused too much on functional testing and underinvested in performance testing for banking batch processes. Explain how you recognized this gap when an EOD processing batch took longer than expected in production, and how you since built performance testing skills by implementing load tests for NEFT batch processing using JMeter. Demonstrate self-awareness, a growth mindset, and connection to banking domain relevance. Avoid cliche answers and always show what you have done to address the weakness.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Behavioral' },
    { id: 'IQ-INS-003', question: 'How do you prepare for whiteboard or system design questions in banking QA interviews?', answer: 'Practice designing test strategies for banking systems on a whiteboard. Common questions include how would you test a payment system or design a test automation framework for mobile banking. Start by clarifying requirements and constraints like transaction volume, security requirements, and regulatory compliance needs. Draw the system architecture showing key components and their interactions. Identify test levels and types for each component. Discuss test data, environment, and tooling strategies. For banking, always address security testing, compliance testing, and performance testing explicitly. Practice explaining your design decisions verbally while drawing.', difficulty: 'Hard', category: 'Practical' },
    { id: 'IQ-INS-004', question: 'How do you demonstrate leadership skills in a banking QA interview?', answer: 'Share examples of leading test strategy decisions like recommending contract testing adoption for banking microservices. Describe how you mentored junior testers on banking domain concepts helping them write more effective test cases. Discuss how you collaborated with cross-functional teams to resolve testing bottlenecks such as convincing the DBA team to provide faster test data provisioning. Show examples of process improvements you initiated like implementing automated regression in the CI/CD pipeline that reduced release cycle time by 40%. In banking, leadership also means championing quality practices like security testing and compliance verification.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Behavioral' },
    { id: 'IQ-INS-005', question: 'How do you handle scenario-based questions about test prioritization in banking?', answer: 'Explain your risk-based approach to test prioritization. High priority tests are those that validate financial transaction accuracy, regulatory compliance, and security controls. Medium priority covers standard functional flows and integration points. Lower priority includes cosmetic issues and edge cases with low business impact. In a banking context, prioritize testing payment processing over report formatting, loan disbursement accuracy over UI alignment, and PMLA compliance over minor feature enhancements. Always relate prioritization to business risk and customer impact. Show awareness that in banking, missed defects can have financial and regulatory consequences.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Practical' },
    { id: 'IQ-INS-006', question: 'How do you answer questions about handling disagreements with developers in banking projects?', answer: 'Describe a specific instance where you had a professional disagreement and how you resolved it constructively. For example, a developer argued that a transaction rounding issue was not a bug because the difference was only one paisa. You demonstrated that across millions of daily transactions, this could accumulate to significant amounts and violate RBI interest calculation guidelines. You presented data showing the potential financial impact and regulatory risk. The outcome was agreement to fix the issue. Emphasize that you focus on facts and data, respect different perspectives, and prioritize the customer and regulatory requirements rather than personal preferences.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Behavioral' },
    { id: 'IQ-INS-007', question: 'How do you prepare for technical depth questions about testing tools and frameworks?', answer: 'Be prepared to discuss at least two testing tools in depth for each category. For API testing, know Postman and RestAssured or Python requests with implementation details. For UI testing, know Selenium/Cypress/Playwright with architecture patterns. For performance testing, know JMeter or k6 with banking scenario scripting. For CI/CD, know Jenkins or GitHub Actions with pipeline configuration. For each tool, prepare to discuss real implementation challenges you faced in banking projects and how you resolved them. Be honest about tools you have not used and discuss how you would approach learning them based on your experience with similar tools.', difficulty: 'Hard', category: 'Technical' },
    { id: 'IQ-INS-008', question: 'How do you present your banking domain expertise during an interview?', answer: 'Weave banking knowledge naturally into your answers rather than listing facts. When discussing test cases, reference specific banking rules like RBI mandated minimum RTGS amount of Rs 2 lakhs. When discussing automation, mention handling CBS SOAP interfaces and NPCI API integration. When discussing defects, describe banking-specific issues like interest calculation discrepancies during quarter-end processing. Use proper banking terminology like IFSC, PMLA, CKYC, CBS, NPA, and EMI correctly. This demonstrates you understand the domain deeply rather than just having memorized terms. Share insights about how banking domain knowledge improved your testing effectiveness.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Behavioral' },
    { id: 'IQ-INS-009', question: 'How do you follow up after a banking QA interview?', answer: 'Send a thank-you email within 24 hours referencing specific discussion points from the interview. If you discussed a testing challenge during the interview, include a brief follow-up thought or article link that adds value to the conversation. If there were technical questions you could not fully answer, acknowledge them and share your research findings. Express continued interest in the role and the specific banking testing challenges discussed. If asked to complete a take-home assessment like designing test cases for a banking feature, submit it within the deadline with thorough documentation. Follow up weekly if you have not heard back, maintaining professional persistence.', difficulty: 'Easy', category: 'Behavioral' }
  ],
  'jira-workflow': [
    { id: 'IQ-JIR-001', question: 'How do you configure Jira workflows for banking test management?', answer: 'Configure a test case workflow with states like draft, reviewed, approved, and retired. Create a defect workflow with states including new, triaged, assigned, in-progress, fixed, verified, closed, and reopened. Add custom fields for banking-specific information like affected banking module, transaction type, regulatory impact, and CBS version. Configure transitions with conditions such as requiring compliance team approval for closing regulatory defects. Set up notifications to alert banking stakeholders when critical defects are raised. For Indian banking, include custom fields for RBI circular reference, NPCI specification version, and compliance classification.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Technical' },
    { id: 'IQ-JIR-002', question: 'How do you use Jira for test traceability in banking projects?', answer: 'Link Jira requirements (epics/stories) to test cases using the traces-to relationship. Link test execution issues to test cases and to defects found during execution. Use Jira plugins like Xray, Zephyr, or TestRail integration for comprehensive test management. Create dashboards showing traceability coverage with filters for banking modules. For RBI compliance, generate traceability reports mapping regulatory requirements to user stories to test cases to test execution results. Use Jira JQL queries to find untested requirements and requirements without linked test cases. This traceability is essential for banking audits where regulators need to verify comprehensive testing.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Practical' },
    { id: 'IQ-JIR-003', question: 'How do you manage banking defect lifecycle in Jira?', answer: 'Configure a defect workflow with mandatory fields at each transition. When creating a defect, require banking module, severity, and environment details. When triaging, require priority assignment and sprint allocation. When assigning, require estimated effort. When marking as fixed, require the fix version and root cause classification. When verifying, require the testing environment and test evidence. For banking defects, add a compliance impact field that triggers additional approval when set. Configure SLA tracking with different targets for critical and high severity banking defects. Set up escalation rules when SLA is about to breach. Generate weekly defect reports by banking module for management review.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Practical' },
    { id: 'IQ-JIR-004', question: 'How do you create effective Jira dashboards for banking test management?', answer: 'Create dashboards with gadgets showing test execution progress per sprint with pie charts for pass/fail/blocked/not-run. Add a defect summary gadget showing open defects by severity and banking module. Include a burndown chart tracking test execution against the sprint plan. Add a two-dimensional filter showing defects by module and severity as a heat map. Include a recently created defects stream for real-time awareness. For banking, add specific gadgets for regulatory compliance test status, payment integration test results, and CBS regression test trends. Create separate dashboards for different stakeholders like QA leads, project managers, and compliance officers.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Technical' },
    { id: 'IQ-JIR-005', question: 'How do you integrate Jira with test automation tools for banking projects?', answer: 'Integrate Jira with CI/CD pipelines so that automated test results from pytest, Selenium, or Cypress automatically update test case execution status in Jira through plugins like Xray or Zephyr. Configure automated defect creation when critical banking tests fail in the pipeline. Link automated test scripts to Jira test cases using custom fields for the test script path and test class name. Set up webhooks to trigger Jira transitions when builds complete. For banking, configure the integration to include banking-specific metadata like the CBS version tested, environment name, and test data set used. Generate automated test execution reports in Jira for sprint reviews.', difficulty: 'Hard', category: 'Technical' },
    { id: 'IQ-JIR-006', question: 'How do you manage test cycles and test plans in Jira for banking releases?', answer: 'Create test plans for each banking release containing all test cycles organized by testing phase. Structure test cycles by banking module like payments test cycle, deposits test cycle, and loans test cycle. Within each cycle, include test cases prioritized by risk. Track execution status with assignments to specific testers. For banking, create separate test cycles for regulatory compliance testing, performance testing, and security testing. Use Jira versions to track which test plans are associated with which release. Generate release readiness reports from Jira showing test execution completion and defect closure across all test cycles.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Practical' },
    { id: 'IQ-JIR-007', question: 'How do you use Jira for managing banking UAT?', answer: 'Create a dedicated UAT project or board in Jira for bank business users to log findings. Provide simplified issue types like UAT Observation and UAT Defect with banking-friendly fields. Configure a workflow that routes UAT findings to the development team with proper triage. Create a UAT dashboard showing overall testing progress, findings by banking module, and sign-off status. Use Jira Service Management for business users who are not familiar with Jira software interface. For Indian banking, configure UAT workflows that include compliance team review for regulatory findings. Track UAT sign-off with electronic approval capturing the approver name, role, and timestamp.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Practical' },
    { id: 'IQ-JIR-008', question: 'How do you generate reports from Jira for banking test management?', answer: 'Use Jira built-in reports for sprint velocity, burndown, and control charts. Configure custom filters with JQL for banking-specific reports like all critical defects in the payments module open for more than 3 days. Use Jira dashboards for real-time reporting and export capabilities for formal reports. For detailed analytics, use plugins like eazyBI or Power BI integration for cross-project banking test metrics. Generate compliance reports listing all regulatory test cases with their execution status and evidence links. Create management reports summarizing quality metrics across all banking modules for steering committee presentations.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Technical' },
    { id: 'IQ-JIR-009', question: 'What best practices should you follow for Jira usage in banking test management?', answer: 'Maintain consistent naming conventions for test cases and defects including banking module prefixes. Keep defect descriptions detailed with steps to reproduce, expected versus actual behavior, and environment details. Update issue status promptly to maintain dashboard accuracy. Use labels and components for filtering by banking domain area. Avoid cluttering issues with unnecessary comments and keep discussions focused. For banking, ensure that all security-related defects are marked with appropriate security level restrictions. Implement data retention policies for closed test artifacts. Conduct periodic Jira hygiene reviews to archive completed sprints and clean up stale issues.', difficulty: 'Easy', category: 'Behavioral' }
  ],
  'job-workflow-lab': [
    { id: 'IQ-JWL-001', question: 'What are common job workflows in banking applications that require testing?', answer: 'Banking job workflows include batch processing jobs like EOD (End of Day) processing for interest calculation and balance consolidation, NEFT batch settlement running at scheduled intervals, cheque clearing batch processing, standing instruction execution, NACH mandate processing for EMI and SIP debits, regulatory report generation for RBI submissions, dormant account identification, NPA classification batch, and TDS computation batch. Each job has specific scheduling requirements, input dependencies, processing logic, error handling, and output validation needs. Testing these jobs is critical because they directly affect financial calculations and regulatory compliance.', difficulty: 'Easy', category: 'Concept' },
    { id: 'IQ-JWL-002', question: 'How do you test job scheduling and dependency management in banking systems?', answer: 'Verify that jobs execute at their configured schedule times. Test dependency chains where Job B should only start after Job A completes successfully, like interest accrual must complete before EOD balance update. Validate that failed prerequisite jobs block dependent jobs from executing. Test parallel job execution ensuring jobs that can run concurrently do so without conflicts. Verify that job scheduling respects banking holidays and non-processing days. Test timezone handling for jobs that process data across time zones. For Indian banking, verify that NEFT batch processing jobs align with RBI-defined settlement windows and that regulatory report generation jobs complete before submission deadlines.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Technical' },
    { id: 'IQ-JWL-003', question: 'How do you test job error handling and recovery in banking batch processing?', answer: 'Simulate various failure points during job execution including database connection loss, file system errors, and memory exhaustion. Verify that jobs implement checkpoint mechanisms allowing restart from the last successful point rather than from the beginning. Test that failed jobs create detailed error logs identifying the specific record that caused the failure. Validate that job alerts notify operations teams immediately on failure. Test manual retry mechanisms for failed jobs. Verify that restarted jobs are idempotent and do not duplicate processing. For banking, test that financial batch job failures do not leave accounts in inconsistent states and that recovery procedures maintain the integrity of the general ledger.', difficulty: 'Hard', category: 'Practical' },
    { id: 'IQ-JWL-004', question: 'How do you test job monitoring and alerting for banking operations?', answer: 'Verify that job execution status is visible in the operations monitoring dashboard with start time, current progress, and estimated completion time. Test that alert notifications are sent for job failures, delayed starts, and abnormal execution durations. Validate that job metrics including execution time, records processed, and error count are captured for trend analysis. Test that operations teams can view job logs in real-time during execution. Verify that SLA breach alerts are triggered when jobs exceed expected duration. For banking, test that critical batch job monitoring includes business impact indicators like number of accounts affected and transaction value processed.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Practical' },
    { id: 'IQ-JWL-005', question: 'How do you test the EOD (End of Day) processing workflow in banking?', answer: 'Verify the EOD processing sequence starting with transaction cutoff enforcement, followed by interest accrual calculation, fee and charge application, standing instruction execution, GL posting and balancing, regulatory data extraction, and report generation. Test each step independently for correctness and then test the complete chain. Verify that the transaction cutoff correctly blocks new transactions during EOD processing. Test that interest calculation handles all account types correctly including savings, FD, RD, and loan accounts. Validate that the GL balances after EOD processing and that all reports are generated correctly. Test the total EOD execution time against the SLA.', difficulty: 'Hard', category: 'Scenario' },
    { id: 'IQ-JWL-006', question: 'How do you validate job output for banking batch processes?', answer: 'Compare job output against independently calculated expected results. For interest calculation jobs, manually compute interest for a sample of accounts using the applicable formula and rate, then compare with the batch output. For regulatory reports, validate record counts, total amounts, and format compliance against specifications. For settlement files, verify that file formats match NPCI or RBI prescribed standards. Test that output files are generated in the correct location with proper naming conventions including dates. For banking, validate that job outputs balance across related processes ensuring total debits equal total credits in batch posting files.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Technical' },
    { id: 'IQ-JWL-007', question: 'How do you test job performance for high-volume banking batch processes?', answer: 'Execute jobs with production-scale data volumes to measure processing time. Profile job execution to identify bottlenecks in database queries, file I/O, or computation logic. Test with increasing data volumes to understand scaling behavior. Optimize job performance by implementing bulk operations, parallel processing for independent records, and database query tuning. For banking, test that NEFT batch processing handles the expected daily volume within the settlement window. Test that month-end jobs processing interest for millions of accounts complete within the overnight batch window. Compare execution times across releases to detect performance regressions.', difficulty: 'Hard', category: 'Technical' },
    { id: 'IQ-JWL-008', question: 'How do you test job security and access control in banking systems?', answer: 'Verify that only authorized operations staff can trigger, modify, or cancel job executions. Test that job configurations like schedules, parameters, and file paths require proper authorization to change. Validate that job execution credentials follow the principle of least privilege with only necessary database and file system permissions. Test that job logs do not contain sensitive information like account numbers and customer names in plain text. Verify that job files are transmitted securely using encrypted channels. For Indian banking, verify that regulatory job outputs are stored securely and that access is restricted to authorized compliance personnel only.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Practical' },
    { id: 'IQ-JWL-009', question: 'How do you test job idempotency for banking batch processes?', answer: 'Execute a batch job successfully, then deliberately run it again without resetting state. Verify that the second execution does not create duplicate entries like double interest credits or duplicate transaction records. Test that the job correctly identifies already-processed records and skips them. Validate that forced re-execution for specific records works correctly for error recovery scenarios. For banking, idempotency is critical because double-processing could mean double interest credit or duplicate fund transfers. Test that the idempotency mechanism uses reliable identifiers like unique batch run IDs rather than timestamps which might collide. Verify that partial reruns for error recovery maintain data consistency.', difficulty: 'Hard', category: 'Scenario' }
  ],
  'json-test-data': [
    { id: 'IQ-JTD-001', question: 'Why is JSON commonly used for test data in banking API testing?', answer: 'JSON is the standard data format for REST APIs used in modern banking applications including UPI, IMPS, and open banking integrations. It is lightweight, human-readable, and easily parsed by all programming languages. For banking test automation, JSON test data files store request payloads for fund transfers, account inquiries, and loan applications. JSON schemas validate the structure and data types of banking API responses. JSON supports nested structures needed for complex banking objects like customer profiles with multiple accounts and transaction histories. Test data management in JSON enables easy versioning, sharing, and parameterization of banking test scenarios.', difficulty: 'Easy', category: 'Concept' },
    { id: 'IQ-JTD-002', question: 'How do you structure JSON test data for banking API test suites?', answer: 'Organize JSON test data in a hierarchical structure with top-level folders for each banking module like accounts, payments, and loans. Each module contains request templates, response expectations, and test data sets. Use JSON files for parameterized test data where each entry represents a test case with input fields and expected outcomes. Create separate files for positive and negative test scenarios. Include metadata fields like test case ID, description, and priority. For Indian banking, structure test data to include IFSC code databases, valid account number patterns, UPI VPA formats, and NPCI response code mappings used across multiple test scenarios.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Practical' },
    { id: 'IQ-JTD-003', question: 'How do you validate JSON schemas for banking API responses?', answer: 'Use JSON Schema specifications to define expected response structures for each banking API endpoint. Define required fields, data types, format constraints, and value ranges. For account balance response, the schema should specify that the balance field is a number with exactly 2 decimal places, currency is a 3-letter ISO code, and account number matches the bank specific pattern. Use libraries like jsonschema in Python or Ajv in JavaScript to validate responses against schemas automatically. For Indian banking, create schemas that validate IFSC code format, PAN pattern, Aadhaar masked format, and NPCI standard response structures.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Technical' },
    { id: 'IQ-JTD-004', question: 'How do you manage large JSON test data sets for banking performance testing?', answer: 'Generate large JSON datasets programmatically using factory functions that create realistic banking entities. Use streaming JSON parsers for large files to avoid memory issues. Implement data generation scripts that create millions of synthetic transactions with realistic distributions matching production patterns. Store large datasets in compressed format and decompress during test execution. For Indian banking, generate datasets with proper distributions of transaction types across NEFT, RTGS, UPI, and IMPS with realistic amounts, timestamps, and geographic patterns. Use database seeding scripts that load JSON test data into test environment databases efficiently.', difficulty: 'Hard', category: 'Technical' },
    { id: 'IQ-JTD-005', question: 'How do you handle sensitive data in JSON test files for banking?', answer: 'Never include real customer data like actual Aadhaar numbers, PAN cards, or account numbers in JSON test files. Generate synthetic data using format-preserving algorithms that produce valid patterns but non-existent identities. Use environment-specific JSON configuration files where sensitive values like API keys and database credentials are replaced with placeholders that are resolved at runtime from environment variables. Implement a pre-commit hook that scans JSON files for patterns matching sensitive banking data like 12-digit Aadhaar patterns or PAN format. For test files checked into version control, use masked or tokenized values.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Practical' },
    { id: 'IQ-JTD-006', question: 'How do you use JSON for data-driven testing in banking applications?', answer: 'Create JSON arrays where each element represents a test case with input parameters and expected outcomes. For fund transfer testing, each JSON object contains source account, destination account, IFSC code, amount, and expected result status. Test frameworks iterate through the array executing the same test logic with different data. Support nested JSON for complex banking scenarios like loan applications with multiple applicants and guarantors. Implement JSON data inheritance where common fields are defined once and specific test cases override only the varying fields. This approach enables comprehensive coverage of banking rules with minimal test code.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Practical' },
    { id: 'IQ-JTD-007', question: 'How do you compare JSON responses in banking API testing?', answer: 'Use deep comparison libraries that compare JSON objects field by field. Handle dynamic fields like transaction IDs and timestamps by excluding them from comparison or using pattern matching. Implement custom comparators for banking-specific fields like comparing amounts with tolerance for rounding differences. Handle field ordering differences since JSON objects are unordered. For arrays like transaction lists, compare by matching on a unique key like transaction reference number rather than by position. For Indian banking, implement comparators that handle different date formats returned by CBS versus modern APIs and that compare amounts regardless of whether they include decimal places or not.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Technical' },
    { id: 'IQ-JTD-008', question: 'How do you create JSON fixtures for banking test automation?', answer: 'Build a fixture library of reusable JSON objects representing common banking entities. Create customer fixtures with different profiles like salaried, self-employed, NRI, and minor. Build account fixtures for savings, current, FD, and loan accounts with various states. Create transaction fixtures representing different payment types. Implement builder patterns that construct complex JSON objects from simple components. For Indian banking, create fixtures for NEFT requests, RTGS messages, UPI collect requests, and NPCI callback payloads. Version fixtures alongside test code and update them when API contracts change. Include both valid and intentionally invalid fixtures for negative testing.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Practical' },
    { id: 'IQ-JTD-009', question: 'How do you handle JSON test data versioning for evolving banking APIs?', answer: 'Maintain JSON test data files in version control alongside the API version they correspond to. When a banking API version changes, create new test data files for the new version while retaining old versions for backward compatibility testing. Use directory naming that includes the API version like v1 and v2. Implement migration scripts that transform existing JSON test data to match new API schemas. Track changes in a changelog documenting which fields were added, modified, or removed. For Indian banking, this is critical when NPCI updates payment API specifications or when the bank introduces new API versions for open banking compliance.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Concept' }
  ],
  'linkedin-posts': [
    { id: 'IQ-LKP-001', question: 'How do you use LinkedIn to build a professional brand as a banking QA professional?', answer: 'Share insights about banking testing challenges and solutions you have encountered. Post about new RBI guidelines and their testing implications. Write about automation strategies for banking applications with practical examples. Share knowledge about tools and frameworks useful for financial domain testing. Engage with banking and fintech communities by commenting on industry posts. Write articles about topics like testing UPI integrations, CBS migration testing, or compliance test automation. Use relevant hashtags like BankingTechnology, QAAutomation, and FintechTesting. Consistency in posting builds credibility and attracts opportunities in the banking QA space.', difficulty: 'Easy', category: 'Behavioral' },
    { id: 'IQ-LKP-002', question: 'What types of LinkedIn content are effective for banking QA professionals?', answer: 'Effective content types include how-to posts explaining testing approaches for specific banking scenarios like testing NEFT batch processing or UPI timeout handling. Share infographics summarizing testing frameworks or banking domain concepts. Post about lessons learned from banking project experiences without revealing confidential details. Create carousel posts explaining complex topics like the test automation pyramid for banking or the PMLA compliance testing checklist. Share book and tool recommendations relevant to banking QA. Post about industry trends like AI in banking testing or shift-left testing adoption in financial services.', difficulty: 'Easy', category: 'Practical' },
    { id: 'IQ-LKP-003', question: 'How can LinkedIn networking help advance a career in banking QA?', answer: 'Connect with banking technology leaders, QA managers at banks and fintech companies, and fellow testing professionals in the financial domain. Join LinkedIn groups focused on banking technology, software testing, and Indian fintech. Engage meaningfully with posts from thought leaders in banking IT. Share your experiences and insights to attract attention from recruiters specializing in banking technology roles. Participate in discussions about emerging technologies like AI testing, cloud-native banking, and open banking APIs. LinkedIn networking can lead to mentorship opportunities, speaking invitations, and referrals for banking QA positions at top financial institutions.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Behavioral' },
    { id: 'IQ-LKP-004', question: 'How do you write an effective LinkedIn post about a banking testing achievement?', answer: 'Start with a hook that captures attention like we reduced production defects by 60% in our banking payment module. Describe the challenge in business terms that non-technical people understand. Explain the approach you took using accessible language. Share specific results with quantifiable metrics. End with a takeaway or insight that others can apply. For banking QA, examples include how you automated CBS regression testing saving 200 hours per release cycle, how you designed a test data strategy that enabled parallel testing across 5 teams, or how you implemented performance testing that prevented a production outage during salary processing day.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Practical' },
    { id: 'IQ-LKP-005', question: 'How do you showcase banking domain expertise on your LinkedIn profile?', answer: 'Write a headline that highlights your banking QA specialization like Senior QA Engineer specializing in Banking and Payment Systems Testing. In the about section, describe your expertise in CBS testing, payment integration, regulatory compliance testing, and test automation for financial applications. List banking-specific skills like NEFT/RTGS testing, UPI integration testing, PMLA compliance validation, and CBS migration testing. Include certifications relevant to banking like ISTQB, domain certifications, and cloud certifications. Request recommendations from banking project colleagues highlighting your domain contributions. Feature banking-related articles and posts in the featured section.', difficulty: 'Easy', category: 'Behavioral' },
    { id: 'IQ-LKP-006', question: 'What are the dos and donts of posting about banking testing on LinkedIn?', answer: 'Do share general testing insights and methodologies without revealing proprietary information. Do discuss publicly available banking concepts like RBI guidelines and NPCI specifications. Do share tool recommendations and framework comparisons. Do not share screenshots of banking applications or internal test reports. Do not reveal specific security vulnerabilities discovered during testing. Do not disclose client names or project details without permission. Do not share production data even if anonymized without proper authorization. For banking professionals, always verify with your organizations social media policy before posting about work-related topics, as financial institutions typically have strict communication guidelines.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Behavioral' },
    { id: 'IQ-LKP-007', question: 'How do you use LinkedIn to stay updated on banking testing trends?', answer: 'Follow key organizations like RBI, NPCI, NASSCOM, and major banking technology providers. Follow thought leaders in banking technology and software testing. Subscribe to newsletters from banking technology publications. Join groups focused on Indian banking IT, software testing, and fintech. Follow hashtags like BankingTechnology, DigitalBanking, and SoftwareTesting. Engage with posts from banking technology conferences like NASSCOM Banking Technology Summit and Fintech Festival India. Set up Google Alerts for banking testing topics and share relevant findings on LinkedIn. This keeps you informed about regulatory changes, technology trends, and industry best practices.', difficulty: 'Easy', category: 'Practical' },
    { id: 'IQ-LKP-008', question: 'How can you use LinkedIn Learning for banking QA skill development?', answer: 'LinkedIn Learning offers courses on testing fundamentals, automation tools like Selenium and Cypress, API testing with Postman and RestAssured, and DevOps practices. While banking-specific courses are limited, combine general testing courses with domain knowledge from RBI publications and banking technology articles. Complete certification preparation courses for ISTQB and cloud certifications. Share your course completions on your profile to demonstrate continuous learning. For banking QA, prioritize courses on API testing since banking is increasingly API-driven, security testing for financial application protection, and database testing for CBS data validation.', difficulty: 'Easy', category: 'Concept' },
    { id: 'IQ-LKP-009', question: 'How do you leverage LinkedIn for finding banking QA job opportunities?', answer: 'Set your profile to open to work with targeting for QA roles in banking and fintech companies. Use job search filters for keywords like banking QA, financial testing, payment testing, and CBS testing. Follow company pages of major banks, fintech companies, and banking technology providers for job postings. Connect with recruiters specializing in banking technology roles. Join banking QA community groups where job opportunities are shared. Engage regularly with content so your profile appears in searches. For Indian banking QA roles, target companies like TCS, Infosys, Wipro banking units, as well as banks like HDFC, ICICI, and SBI for in-house QA positions, and fintech companies like Paytm, PhonePe, and Razorpay.', difficulty: 'Easy', category: 'Behavioral' }
  ],
  'log-trace': [
    { id: 'IQ-LGT-001', question: 'What is log analysis and why is it important for banking application testing?', answer: 'Log analysis involves examining application, server, and transaction logs to understand system behavior, identify errors, and trace transaction flows. In banking, logs are critical for debugging transaction failures, verifying audit trails, investigating security incidents, and demonstrating regulatory compliance. When a customer reports a failed NEFT transfer, log analysis traces the transaction from the API request through business logic, CBS communication, and NPCI processing to identify the failure point. Proper logging and log analysis are mandatory under RBI IT governance guidelines for incident management and fraud investigation in banking systems.', difficulty: 'Easy', category: 'Concept' },
    { id: 'IQ-LGT-002', question: 'How do you use distributed tracing for testing banking microservices?', answer: 'Distributed tracing assigns a unique trace ID to each banking transaction that propagates across all microservices involved in processing. For a UPI payment, the trace follows the request from the API gateway through the authentication service, payment orchestrator, CBS connector, and NPCI integration. Tools like Jaeger, Zipkin, or AWS X-Ray visualize the complete transaction flow with timing information for each service hop. During testing, verify that traces are complete, latencies are within SLAs at each hop, and error propagation is correct. For banking, use traces to identify which service causes transaction timeouts and to verify that all services participate in the expected transaction flow.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Technical' },
    { id: 'IQ-LGT-003', question: 'How do you test structured logging in banking applications?', answer: 'Verify that all log entries follow the JSON structured format with required fields including timestamp in UTC, log level, logger name, message, and correlation ID. Test that banking transaction logs include additional context like account number (masked), transaction type, amount, and processing status. Verify that sensitive data like passwords, PINs, and full Aadhaar numbers are never logged. Test log levels ensuring that production runs at INFO level with appropriate use of DEBUG, WARNING, and ERROR levels. Validate that correlation IDs link related log entries across a single banking transaction. For Indian banking, verify that log retention meets the RBI-mandated minimum period.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Practical' },
    { id: 'IQ-LGT-004', question: 'How do you use log analysis to investigate banking transaction failures?', answer: 'Start by searching for the transaction reference number or correlation ID across all service logs. Follow the chronological sequence of log entries to identify where the transaction deviated from the expected flow. Look for error messages, exception stack traces, and timeout indicators. Check CBS communication logs for request/response details. Verify database transaction logs for commit or rollback events. For Indian banking, check NPCI response codes in payment processing logs, CBS error codes in core banking integration logs, and notification service logs for customer alert delivery. Use log aggregation tools like ELK stack or Splunk to search across multiple services simultaneously.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Practical' },
    { id: 'IQ-LGT-005', question: 'What logging best practices should be verified during banking application testing?', answer: 'Verify that every API request is logged with a unique correlation ID at entry and exit points. Test that error logs include sufficient context for debugging without exposing sensitive data. Validate that log levels are appropriate where routine operations use INFO, recoverable issues use WARNING, and failures use ERROR. Verify that external service calls including CBS, NPCI, and notification services are logged with request/response timing. Test that log volume does not impact application performance under load. For banking, verify that audit-relevant events like login attempts, transaction authorizations, and configuration changes are logged at a level that is always captured regardless of the configured log level.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Technical' },
    { id: 'IQ-LGT-006', question: 'How do you test log aggregation and search for banking systems?', answer: 'Verify that logs from all banking microservices are collected in a centralized logging platform. Test search functionality by querying for specific transaction IDs and verifying that all related log entries across services are returned. Validate that log timestamps are synchronized across services using NTP to ensure correct chronological ordering. Test log retention policies verifying that logs older than the retention period are archived or purged. Verify that log storage scales with banking transaction volumes without data loss during peak periods. For Indian banking, test that log search supports compliance investigation scenarios like retrieving all transactions for a specific customer account over a date range.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Technical' },
    { id: 'IQ-LGT-007', question: 'How do you use logs to verify banking compliance requirements?', answer: 'Verify that login and logout events are logged with user identity, timestamp, and IP address for access audit. Test that all financial transaction logs include maker and checker identification for dual-control verification. Validate that privileged operations like configuration changes and user management are logged with sufficient detail for audit reconstruction. Check that log entries for regulatory reporting data extraction are timestamped and include the data range processed. For Indian banking, verify that PMLA-related activity logs capture all suspicious transaction monitoring events, that KYC verification events are logged for CKYC audit purposes, and that RBI reporting submission events include the report type and period.', difficulty: 'Hard', category: 'Scenario' },
    { id: 'IQ-LGT-008', question: 'How do you test log-based alerting for banking applications?', answer: 'Configure log pattern matching rules that trigger alerts for critical banking events. Test that repeated authentication failures generate security alerts. Verify that transaction processing errors exceeding a threshold trigger operations alerts. Test that CBS communication failures generate immediate notifications. Validate that alert routing sends the right alerts to the right teams like security alerts to the SOC team and transaction alerts to the operations team. Test alert suppression during maintenance windows. For Indian banking, test alerts for PMLA threshold transactions, NPCI connectivity loss, and regulatory report generation failures. Verify that alert fatigue is minimized through proper threshold configuration.', difficulty: 'Hard', category: 'Practical' },
    { id: 'IQ-LGT-009', question: 'How do you use tracing to test performance bottlenecks in banking transactions?', answer: 'Analyze trace spans to identify which service in the banking transaction chain contributes the most latency. Look for services where the time spent exceeds the SLA budget. Identify sequential processing steps that could be parallelized. Detect unnecessary service calls that add latency without value. For banking, use traces to identify whether CBS round trips, database queries, encryption operations, or NPCI communication is the primary bottleneck. Compare trace profiles between fast and slow transactions to identify variable latency sources. Use trace data to create performance baselines and detect regressions when new code is deployed to banking services.', difficulty: 'Hard', category: 'Technical' }
  ],
  'mainframe-banking-arch': [
    { id: 'IQ-MBA-001', question: 'What role do mainframes play in Indian banking and why are they relevant for testing?', answer: 'Mainframes remain the backbone of many large Indian banks running core banking operations including account management, transaction processing, and regulatory reporting. They handle millions of daily transactions with high reliability and security. Testing mainframe banking applications requires understanding COBOL programs, JCL (Job Control Language) for batch processing, CICS for online transaction processing, and DB2 or IMS for data management. Many modernization projects involve integrating mainframe CBS with modern web and mobile channels, requiring testing of the integration layer. Testers must understand both the mainframe world and modern API-based systems.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Concept' },
    { id: 'IQ-MBA-002', question: 'How do you test mainframe-based banking batch processes?', answer: 'Test JCL job streams that execute banking batch processes like EOD processing, interest calculation, and report generation. Verify job step dependencies and conditional execution logic. Test with production-scale data volumes to validate processing time. Verify output file formats and record counts. Test abend (abnormal end) handling and restart procedures. Validate that batch processing correctly updates mainframe databases and generates expected audit trails. For Indian banking, test mainframe batch jobs for NEFT batch processing, RBI regulatory report generation, and monthly interest posting. Use tools like File-AID for data manipulation and Compuware for debugging.', difficulty: 'Hard', category: 'Technical' },
    { id: 'IQ-MBA-003', question: 'How do you test the integration between mainframe CBS and modern banking APIs?', answer: 'Test the middleware layer that translates between mainframe COBOL copybook structures and modern JSON/REST APIs. Verify data type mapping between mainframe packed decimal fields and API numeric types. Test character encoding conversion between EBCDIC mainframe data and UTF-8 API data. Validate that transaction processing through the API layer correctly updates mainframe records. Test timeout handling for mainframe response delays. For Indian banking, test that fund transfers initiated through UPI correctly process on the mainframe CBS with proper account updates, GL postings, and audit trail creation in the mainframe environment.', difficulty: 'Hard', category: 'Scenario' },
    { id: 'IQ-MBA-004', question: 'What testing challenges are unique to mainframe banking environments?', answer: 'Challenges include limited test environment availability since mainframe resources are expensive and shared. COBOL code is difficult to understand for testers trained in modern languages. Test data creation on mainframes requires specialized knowledge of VSAM files, DB2 tables, and data relationships. Debugging mainframe applications requires specific tools and skills. Batch job scheduling complexity with hundreds of interdependent jobs. For Indian banking, additional challenges include testing the migration from mainframe to modern platforms while maintaining backward compatibility, testing green screen interfaces that banking staff have used for decades, and ensuring that mainframe security configurations meet RBI audit requirements.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Concept' },
    { id: 'IQ-MBA-005', question: 'How do you test CICS online transactions in mainframe banking?', answer: 'Test CICS transactions using 3270 terminal emulators to simulate banking staff interactions. Verify that online transactions like account inquiry, fund transfer, and customer update process correctly in real-time. Test transaction response times under concurrent user load. Verify CICS region recovery after abnormal terminations. Test BMS (Basic Mapping Support) screen definitions for correct field positioning and data entry validation. Validate that CICS transactions correctly interact with DB2 databases and VSAM files. For Indian banking, test branch teller transactions including cash deposit, withdrawal, and cheque encashment through CICS interfaces.', difficulty: 'Hard', category: 'Technical' },
    { id: 'IQ-MBA-006', question: 'How do you test data migration from mainframe to modern banking platforms?', answer: 'Test the extraction of data from mainframe sources including DB2 tables, VSAM files, and flat files. Verify data transformation logic converting mainframe data formats to the target platform format. Validate that all records are migrated with no data loss by comparing counts and checksums. Test specific field-level accuracy for financial data like balances, interest rates, and transaction history. Verify that migrated data maintains referential integrity in the target system. For Indian banking, test migration of account data with proper IFSC mapping, customer data with KYC status preservation, and loan data with EMI schedule accuracy.', difficulty: 'Hard', category: 'Practical' },
    { id: 'IQ-MBA-007', question: 'What tools are used for mainframe banking application testing?', answer: 'IBM Rational Quality Manager for test management. Compuware Topaz for COBOL program understanding and debugging. File-AID for mainframe data manipulation and comparison. IBM Debug Tool for COBOL program debugging. CA Gen for test data generation. Micro Focus Enterprise Server for mainframe application testing on distributed platforms. Custom JCL-based test harnesses for batch testing. For screen-based testing, tools like BlueZone or Attachmate provide 3270 terminal automation. For Indian banking, many banks also use custom in-house test frameworks built around their specific CBS implementation on the mainframe platform.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Technical' },
    { id: 'IQ-MBA-008', question: 'How do you test mainframe performance for banking transaction processing?', answer: 'Measure CICS transaction response times under various load levels. Monitor CPU utilization, memory usage, and I/O rates during peak processing. Test batch job throughput with production-scale data volumes. Identify DB2 query performance issues using EXPLAIN statements. Monitor VSAM file access patterns for buffer optimization. Test concurrent user capacity for online banking transactions. For Indian banking, stress test the mainframe during simulated peak events like salary day processing and quarter-end reporting. Monitor zIIP and zAAP utilization for Java and XML workloads running on the mainframe. Ensure that mainframe capacity planning accounts for growing digital banking volumes.', difficulty: 'Hard', category: 'Scenario' },
    { id: 'IQ-MBA-009', question: 'How do you approach mainframe modernization testing in banking?', answer: 'Test the modernization approach whether it is replatforming, refactoring, or replacing. For replatforming, verify that COBOL programs run correctly on the new platform with identical outputs. For refactoring, verify that modernized code in Java or Python produces the same results as the original COBOL programs for all banking calculations. Implement comprehensive comparison testing running transactions through both old and new systems and comparing results. Test the coexistence period where both systems run in parallel. For Indian banking, prioritize testing of interest calculation, fee computation, and regulatory reporting modules during modernization since financial accuracy is non-negotiable.', difficulty: 'Hard', category: 'Practical' }
  ],
  'message-queue-arch': [
    { id: 'IQ-MQA-001', question: 'What role do message queues play in banking system architecture?', answer: 'Message queues enable asynchronous communication between banking services, handling scenarios where immediate response is not required like NEFT batch processing, notification delivery, and report generation. They provide reliability by persisting messages until consumers process them, preventing data loss during service failures. Queues decouple services so that a payment processing spike does not overwhelm the notification service. Common implementations in banking include IBM MQ for mainframe integration, RabbitMQ for microservice communication, and Apache Kafka for event streaming. For Indian banking, message queues handle the asynchronous nature of NEFT settlement, regulatory report generation, and bulk payment processing.', difficulty: 'Easy', category: 'Concept' },
    { id: 'IQ-MQA-002', question: 'How do you test message queue reliability in banking systems?', answer: 'Test message persistence by verifying that messages survive queue broker restarts. Simulate consumer failures and verify that unacknowledged messages are redelivered. Test message ordering guarantees for banking transactions that must be processed in sequence. Verify that duplicate detection prevents the same transaction from being processed twice. Test dead letter queue handling for messages that cannot be processed after maximum retry attempts. For banking, test that NEFT batch messages queued during a processing window are all delivered even if the settlement service temporarily goes down. Verify that payment notifications are eventually delivered even during notification service outages.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Technical' },
    { id: 'IQ-MQA-003', question: 'How do you test message serialization and deserialization for banking events?', answer: 'Verify that banking event messages are correctly serialized to the wire format like JSON or Avro with all required fields. Test that consumer services correctly deserialize messages including handling of different data types for amounts, dates, and identifiers. Test backward compatibility when new fields are added to message schemas. Validate message size limits and handling of oversized messages. For Indian banking, test that transaction event messages include all required fields like transaction reference, IFSC code, amount with correct precision, and timestamp. Verify that messages with special characters in customer names or addresses are serialized without data corruption.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Practical' },
    { id: 'IQ-MQA-004', question: 'How do you test event-driven architecture patterns in banking?', answer: 'Test that events published by banking services are received by all subscribed consumers. Verify event ordering for account-level events where debit events must be processed before balance check events. Test that event consumers handle duplicate events idempotently. Validate that event replay functionality works correctly for reconstructing banking state from event history. Test event schema evolution when new fields are added. For Indian banking, test the event flow for UPI transactions from payment initiation through authorization to settlement notification. Verify that CBS posting events correctly trigger GL update events and customer notification events in the right sequence.', difficulty: 'Hard', category: 'Scenario' },
    { id: 'IQ-MQA-005', question: 'How do you test Kafka-based event streaming for banking transaction processing?', answer: 'Test Kafka topic partitioning strategy ensuring that events for the same account go to the same partition for ordering guarantees. Verify consumer group behavior where multiple instances of the payment processor correctly share the workload. Test consumer lag monitoring and alerting when processing falls behind. Validate exactly-once processing semantics for financial transactions using Kafka transactions. Test Kafka Connect integration for streaming banking events to data warehouses. For Indian banking, test that real-time transaction streaming supports fraud detection with sub-second latency, that NEFT batch processing events are correctly partitioned by settlement window, and that Kafka retention policies meet audit requirements.', difficulty: 'Hard', category: 'Technical' },
    { id: 'IQ-MQA-006', question: 'How do you test dead letter queue handling for banking messages?', answer: 'Verify that messages failing processing after the configured retry count are moved to the dead letter queue with the original message content, error details, and retry history. Test that dead letter queue monitoring alerts operations teams for investigation. Validate the message reprocessing workflow where operations staff can inspect failed messages, fix the underlying issue, and replay them. For banking, test DLQ scenarios for failed payment processing messages, undeliverable notification messages, and malformed CBS integration messages. Verify that reprocessed financial messages maintain idempotency and do not create duplicate transactions.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Practical' },
    { id: 'IQ-MQA-007', question: 'How do you test message queue performance for high-volume banking operations?', answer: 'Measure message throughput by publishing and consuming banking events at expected peak volumes. Test queue latency measuring the time from publish to consume under various load conditions. Verify that the queue handles burst traffic during peak banking hours without message loss. Test consumer scaling by adding and removing consumer instances under load. Monitor queue depth trends to ensure consumers keep up with producers. For Indian banking, test with UPI-scale message volumes reaching millions per hour. Verify that batch processing queues handle month-end volumes efficiently. Test that priority queues correctly prioritize RTGS messages over NEFT messages during high-volume periods.', difficulty: 'Hard', category: 'Technical' },
    { id: 'IQ-MQA-008', question: 'How do you test message security in banking queue systems?', answer: 'Verify that messages containing sensitive banking data are encrypted in transit using TLS. Test that message payloads with account numbers, customer details, or transaction amounts are encrypted at rest in the queue. Validate access controls ensuring only authorized services can publish or consume from specific banking queues. Test that queue administration requires proper authentication and authorization. Verify that sensitive fields in banking messages are masked in queue monitoring dashboards and logs. For Indian banking, verify compliance with RBI guidelines on data protection for messages containing PII like Aadhaar or PAN flowing through the message infrastructure.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Technical' },
    { id: 'IQ-MQA-009', question: 'How do you test message queue failover and disaster recovery for banking?', answer: 'Test queue cluster failover by terminating the primary broker and verifying consumers automatically reconnect to the standby. Validate that no messages are lost during failover. Test cross-datacenter replication for disaster recovery ensuring banking events are replicated to the DR site. Verify recovery time for queue infrastructure restoring service within the defined RTO. Test that producers handle queue unavailability gracefully by buffering messages and retrying. For Indian banking, test that payment processing queue failover completes within seconds to maintain NPCI SLA compliance. Verify that queued NEFT batches are preserved during infrastructure failures and processed in the next available window.', difficulty: 'Hard', category: 'Scenario' }
  ],
  'microservices-arch': [
    { id: 'IQ-MSA-001', question: 'How does a microservices architecture impact testing strategy for banking applications?', answer: 'Microservices require testing at multiple levels. Unit tests within each service verify business logic isolation. Component tests verify each service independently with mocked dependencies. Contract tests ensure API compatibility between services. Integration tests verify actual service interactions. End-to-end tests validate complete banking workflows across services. The testing strategy must account for distributed system challenges like network failures, eventual consistency, and service version mismatches. For Indian banking, the microservices strategy must cover service interactions for account service, payment service, loan service, and notification service with proper testing of CBS integration adapters.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Concept' },
    { id: 'IQ-MSA-002', question: 'How do you test inter-service communication in banking microservices?', answer: 'Test synchronous REST/gRPC calls between banking services verifying request/response contracts, timeout handling, and error propagation. Test asynchronous message-based communication verifying message delivery, ordering, and idempotent processing. Validate service discovery ensuring services can locate each other dynamically. Test circuit breaker behavior when dependent services are unavailable. Verify retry logic with exponential backoff for transient failures. For Indian banking, test the communication chain from the internet banking frontend through the API gateway, authentication service, account service, and CBS adapter. Verify that a failure in the notification service does not block payment processing.', difficulty: 'Hard', category: 'Technical' },
    { id: 'IQ-MSA-003', question: 'How do you test data consistency across banking microservices?', answer: 'Test the saga pattern implementation for distributed transactions like fund transfers involving debit from one account service instance and credit to another. Verify compensating transactions when any step in the saga fails. Test eventual consistency by verifying that all services converge to the correct state within the defined consistency window. Test the outbox pattern ensuring that database changes and event publications are atomic. For banking, verify that account balance across the account service, statement service, and notification service are consistent. Test scenarios where network partitions cause temporary inconsistency and verify automatic reconciliation.', difficulty: 'Hard', category: 'Scenario' },
    { id: 'IQ-MSA-004', question: 'How do you implement service virtualization for banking microservice testing?', answer: 'Create virtual service stubs that simulate the behavior of dependent banking services. Use tools like WireMock, Mountebank, or Hoverfly to create stubs from recorded service interactions. Configure stubs to simulate various response scenarios including success, failure, slow response, and malformed data. For banking, create virtual services for CBS integration endpoints, NPCI payment gateway, Aadhaar verification service, and credit bureau APIs. This enables teams to test their service independently without waiting for dependent services to be available. Update virtual services when the real service contract changes to maintain test accuracy.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Practical' },
    { id: 'IQ-MSA-005', question: 'How do you test API gateway configurations for banking microservices?', answer: 'Test request routing ensuring that banking API calls are directed to the correct backend service. Verify rate limiting at the gateway level preventing API abuse. Test authentication and authorization enforcement at the gateway. Validate request/response transformation for banking payload formats. Test load balancing across multiple instances of banking services. Verify SSL/TLS termination and certificate management. For Indian banking, test that the API gateway correctly routes UPI callbacks to the payment service, that open banking API access is properly authenticated, and that the gateway enforces different rate limits for different partner tiers as per RBI open banking guidelines.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Technical' },
    { id: 'IQ-MSA-006', question: 'How do you test database-per-service pattern in banking microservices?', answer: 'Verify that each banking service owns and manages its own database exclusively. Test that services do not directly access another services database. Validate that cross-service data access goes through API calls. Test data synchronization between services through events for data that needs to be shared. For banking, verify that the account service database is the authoritative source for account balances, the loan service database manages loan lifecycle data, and the notification service maintains its own delivery tracking data. Test that data reports requiring cross-service data use properly aggregated APIs rather than direct database queries across services.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Concept' },
    { id: 'IQ-MSA-007', question: 'How do you test deployment strategies for banking microservices?', answer: 'Test independent service deployment verifying that updating the payment service does not require redeploying the account service. Validate rolling deployments ensuring zero-downtime during updates. Test canary deployments by routing a percentage of banking transactions to the new version. Verify blue-green deployments with instant rollback capability. Test that database migrations are backward compatible allowing old and new service versions to coexist during deployment. For Indian banking, verify that deploying a new version of the payment service does not disrupt active NEFT batches or in-flight UPI transactions. Test rollback procedures for quick recovery from failed deployments.', difficulty: 'Hard', category: 'Practical' },
    { id: 'IQ-MSA-008', question: 'How do you test observability in banking microservices?', answer: 'Verify that each service emits structured logs with correlation IDs enabling end-to-end transaction tracing. Test that distributed tracing captures the complete banking transaction flow across all services. Validate that service metrics including request rate, error rate, and latency are collected and accessible in dashboards. Test alerting rules that trigger on SLA breaches for banking operations. Verify health check endpoints for each service. For Indian banking, test that observability tools can trace a UPI payment from the customer app through the PSP service, NPCI integration, and CBS posting, identifying the exact point of failure or latency for any transaction.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Technical' },
    { id: 'IQ-MSA-009', question: 'What are the testing challenges specific to banking microservices and how do you address them?', answer: 'Challenges include test environment complexity with many services requiring orchestration, network reliability affecting test consistency, version management across multiple independently deployed services, and end-to-end test brittleness due to multiple moving parts. Address by investing in contract testing to reduce integration test dependency, implementing service virtualization for isolation testing, using container orchestration for reproducible test environments, and maintaining a comprehensive test pyramid with more unit and component tests than end-to-end tests. For banking, additional challenges include testing distributed transaction consistency and maintaining test data coherence across service databases.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Behavioral' }
  ],
  'mobile-testing': [
    { id: 'IQ-MOB-001', question: 'What are the key testing areas for banking mobile applications?', answer: 'Key areas include functional testing of banking features like balance inquiry, fund transfer, and bill payment. Security testing for biometric authentication, session management, and data encryption. Performance testing for app startup time, transaction processing speed, and memory usage. Usability testing for intuitive navigation and clear financial data presentation. Compatibility testing across Android and iOS versions and device types. Network testing under 2G, 3G, 4G, 5G, and offline conditions. Accessibility testing for screen readers. For Indian banking, specific areas include UPI integration, MPIN handling, device binding, and regional language support.', difficulty: 'Easy', category: 'Concept' },
    { id: 'IQ-MOB-002', question: 'How do you test mobile banking app security?', answer: 'Test that sensitive data like account numbers, balances, and PINs are not stored in plaintext on the device. Verify that the app detects rooted/jailbroken devices and restricts functionality. Test certificate pinning to prevent man-in-the-middle attacks. Validate that biometric authentication cannot be bypassed. Test session management ensuring sessions expire after inactivity and do not persist after app kill. Verify that the app prevents screen recording and screenshots on sensitive screens. Test that clipboard does not retain financial data. For Indian banking, test UPI PIN entry security, MPIN validation, and device binding verification against mobile number registered with the bank.', difficulty: 'Hard', category: 'Technical' },
    { id: 'IQ-MOB-003', question: 'How do you test mobile banking offline functionality?', answer: 'Verify what features are available offline like viewing cached account summary and recent transactions. Test that the app gracefully handles transition from online to offline mid-transaction. Validate that queued offline actions like fund transfer requests are properly synchronized when connectivity resumes. Test that the app displays clear indicators of offline mode. Verify that cached data has a staleness indicator showing when it was last updated. For Indian banking, test that UPI payments require online connectivity and properly reject offline attempts. Test behavior when the network drops during OTP validation and ensure the transaction state is preserved correctly.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Scenario' },
    { id: 'IQ-MOB-004', question: 'How do you test push notifications for banking mobile apps?', answer: 'Verify that transaction alerts are delivered promptly after each banking operation. Test notification content for accuracy ensuring the amount, account, and transaction type are correct. Verify that notifications do not display full account numbers for security. Test notification delivery when the app is in foreground, background, and killed states. Validate deep linking from notifications to specific app screens like transaction details. Test notification preferences allowing users to customize which alerts they receive. For Indian banking, test notifications for UPI payment requests, NEFT credit confirmations, EMI reminders, and suspicious activity alerts. Test notification delivery across different mobile platforms and device manufacturers.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Practical' },
    { id: 'IQ-MOB-005', question: 'How do you test mobile banking app performance?', answer: 'Measure app cold start time targeting under 3 seconds. Test screen transition times between banking features. Monitor memory usage during extended sessions typical for branch banking tablets. Test battery consumption during active usage. Measure API response times over mobile networks with varying latency. Test with large data sets like viewing 12 months of transaction history. For Indian banking, test performance on budget Android devices with limited RAM that are commonly used by a large portion of the customer base. Test UPI payment completion time against NPCI SLA requirements. Monitor app size and optimize for users with limited device storage.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Technical' },
    { id: 'IQ-MOB-006', question: 'How do you test biometric authentication in banking apps?', answer: 'Test fingerprint authentication with valid registered fingerprints, unregistered fingerprints, and wet or dirty fingers. Test facial recognition under varying lighting conditions. Verify fallback to MPIN when biometric fails. Test that biometric settings are properly protected requiring current MPIN to modify. Verify that biometric data is stored securely in the device hardware security module. Test the enrollment flow for registering biometric authentication. For Indian banking, test Aadhaar-based biometric authentication for e-KYC using fingerprint and iris scanners. Verify that biometric authentication complies with RBI guidelines for additional factor authentication.', difficulty: 'Hard', category: 'Practical' },
    { id: 'IQ-MOB-007', question: 'How do you test mobile banking app updates and version management?', answer: 'Test that forced update mechanisms work when a security-critical update is released. Verify that optional updates provide a clear prompt without disrupting current sessions. Test backward compatibility ensuring the new app version works with the existing backend API. Validate that user data like saved beneficiaries and preferences are preserved during updates. Test the migration logic that converts local data from old format to new format. For Indian banking, test that UPI registration and device binding persist through app updates. Verify that transaction history cached locally is accessible after the update. Test that the app correctly handles version mismatch with the banking server.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Practical' },
    { id: 'IQ-MOB-008', question: 'What device and OS testing strategy do you use for Indian banking mobile apps?', answer: 'Create a device matrix based on analytics of actual customer device usage data. Include top Samsung, Xiaomi, Vivo, Oppo, and Realme models that dominate the Indian market. Test across Android versions from the minimum supported version typically Android 8 through the latest. Include iOS testing for iPhone models commonly used in India. Prioritize testing on devices with 2-3 GB RAM which represent a significant portion of the Indian market. Use cloud device farms like BrowserStack for broad coverage and maintain a physical device lab for critical testing. Test across different screen sizes from 5-inch budget phones to 6.7-inch premium devices.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Concept' },
    { id: 'IQ-MOB-009', question: 'How do you test the app installation and first-time setup for banking mobile apps?', answer: 'Test the complete installation flow from app store download through first launch. Verify the permissions request flow ensuring only necessary permissions are requested with clear explanations. Test the registration and device binding process with mobile number verification. Validate MPIN setup and biometric enrollment flows. Test the first-time login with internet banking credentials. Verify that the welcome tutorial and feature walkthrough function correctly. For Indian banking, test the UPI registration flow including VPA creation and bank account linking. Test SIM binding verification for security. Verify that the app correctly handles interrupted setup flows allowing users to resume from where they left off.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Scenario' }
  ],
  'mobile-testing-lab': [
    { id: 'IQ-MTL-001', question: 'What tools and frameworks are used for mobile banking app test automation?', answer: 'Appium is the most widely used cross-platform mobile automation framework supporting both Android and iOS banking apps. Espresso for native Android UI testing offers fast execution within the app process. XCUITest for native iOS testing integrates with Xcode. Detox for React Native banking apps provides gray-box testing capabilities. Maestro provides a simplified YAML-based approach for mobile UI testing. For API testing within mobile banking, use Postman or RestAssured. For performance testing, use Android Profiler and Xcode Instruments. Cloud device farms like BrowserStack, Sauce Labs, and AWS Device Farm provide real device testing infrastructure.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Technical' },
    { id: 'IQ-MTL-002', question: 'How do you set up a mobile testing lab for banking application testing?', answer: 'Provision a mix of physical devices and cloud device farm subscriptions. Physical devices should include the top 10 most-used devices by the banks customer base. Set up a device management system for tracking device checkout, OS versions, and health status. Configure a CI/CD integration that triggers mobile test suites on device farms for every build. Set up network simulation tools to test under various Indian network conditions including 2G, 3G, 4G, and 5G. Install monitoring tools on test devices for performance profiling. For banking, maintain separate device profiles for retail mobile banking and corporate mobile banking testing with different security configurations.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Practical' },
    { id: 'IQ-MTL-003', question: 'How do you implement the Page Object Model for mobile banking test automation?', answer: 'Create page classes for each banking app screen like LoginPage, DashboardPage, TransferPage, and SettingsPage. Each page class contains element locators using accessibility IDs, resource IDs, or XPath for mobile elements. Encapsulate user interactions as methods like LoginPage.enterMPIN(), TransferPage.selectBeneficiary(), and TransferPage.confirmTransfer(). Handle platform differences by creating a base page class with common utilities and platform-specific implementations. For banking apps, create specialized page objects for UPI payment flows, OTP entry screens, and biometric authentication dialogs that handle timing-sensitive interactions like OTP countdown timers.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Technical' },
    { id: 'IQ-MTL-004', question: 'How do you handle test data management for mobile banking test automation?', answer: 'Use API calls to set up test data before mobile test execution rather than creating data through the UI. Maintain a pool of test accounts with known balances and states for repeatable testing. Create test data factory classes that generate valid banking entities for each test scenario. Reset test data after each test run to ensure isolation. For banking mobile tests, manage test data for different user profiles including first-time users, regular users, and blocked users. Handle OTP challenges by integrating with SMS gateway test APIs or using test mode flags that accept fixed OTPs. Maintain separate test data sets for different testing environments.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Practical' },
    { id: 'IQ-MTL-005', question: 'How do you test mobile banking app interruptions?', answer: 'Test behavior when an incoming call interrupts a banking transaction. Verify that the app correctly resumes after coming back from the background. Test behavior when a push notification arrives during a fund transfer. Verify handling of device orientation changes during form filling. Test behavior when memory pressure causes the app to be killed and relaunched. Test interruption by system alerts like low battery and software update prompts. For Indian banking, test behavior when the OTP SMS arrives while the user is on the OTP entry screen. Test that active UPI payment sessions are not disrupted by incoming UPI collect requests from other apps.', difficulty: 'Hard', category: 'Scenario' },
    { id: 'IQ-MTL-006', question: 'How do you automate accessibility testing for banking mobile apps?', answer: 'Use automated accessibility scanning tools like Accessibility Scanner for Android and Accessibility Inspector for iOS integrated into the test suite. Verify that all interactive elements have meaningful content descriptions for screen readers. Test that touch target sizes meet the minimum 48dp requirement for banking buttons and controls. Validate that text contrast ratios meet WCAG AA standards for financial data displays. Automate navigation testing to verify that all banking features are reachable using accessibility services. For Indian banking apps, test that amount and date fields are properly announced by screen readers in the correct language and format.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Practical' },
    { id: 'IQ-MTL-007', question: 'How do you implement continuous mobile testing in CI/CD for banking?', answer: 'Integrate mobile test execution into the CI/CD pipeline triggered on every build. Run a fast smoke test suite on every commit covering login, balance check, and basic transfer. Run the full regression suite nightly on a broader device matrix. Configure parallel test execution across multiple devices to reduce total execution time. Set up test result reporting with screenshots and device logs for failure investigation. For banking, configure different test suites for different risk levels where payment module changes trigger the full payment test suite while UI changes trigger visual regression tests. Monitor test execution trends to identify flaky tests requiring attention.', difficulty: 'Hard', category: 'Technical' },
    { id: 'IQ-MTL-008', question: 'How do you test deep linking and app-to-app integration for banking?', answer: 'Test deep links that navigate users directly to specific banking app screens from SMS, email, and web links. Verify that payment links from merchants open the banking app at the correct payment screen. Test UPI intent handling where payment requests from third-party apps correctly launch the banking app with pre-filled details. Validate app-to-app switching for features like document upload from camera or file manager. For Indian banking, test the complete UPI intent flow from a merchant app through the banking app UPI payment screen back to the merchant app with payment confirmation. Test that deep links enforce authentication before showing sensitive content.', difficulty: 'Hard', category: 'Scenario' },
    { id: 'IQ-MTL-009', question: 'How do you measure and report mobile testing effectiveness for banking apps?', answer: 'Track metrics including device coverage as a percentage of the customer device base, automation coverage of mobile test cases, test execution pass rate trends, average defect detection time for mobile-specific issues, and production crash rate from crash analytics tools like Firebase Crashlytics. Monitor app store ratings and reviews for quality indicators. For banking, track mobile-specific metrics like payment transaction success rate, biometric authentication failure rate, and app session duration. Report testing effectiveness in terms of production defect escapes caught versus missed. Use analytics to identify undertested device and OS combinations that contribute to production issues.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Behavioral' }
  ],
  'observability-lab': [
    { id: 'IQ-OBL-001', question: 'What are the three pillars of observability and how do they apply to banking?', answer: 'The three pillars are metrics, logs, and traces. Metrics are numerical measurements like transaction count per second, API response time percentiles, and error rates for banking services. Logs are detailed textual records of events including transaction processing steps, error details, and audit entries. Traces track individual transactions across multiple banking microservices showing the path and timing of each service interaction. In banking, all three work together. When metrics show increased payment failure rates, logs reveal the specific error, and traces identify which service in the payment chain is causing the failure. Together they provide comprehensive visibility into banking system health.', difficulty: 'Easy', category: 'Concept' },
    { id: 'IQ-OBL-002', question: 'How do you test metrics collection for banking applications?', answer: 'Verify that application metrics are emitted correctly by generating known workloads and checking metric values. Test that business metrics like transaction counts and amounts match actual processing. Validate that technical metrics like CPU usage, memory, and response times are accurately captured. Test metric labels ensuring they correctly categorize data by banking module, transaction type, and environment. Verify metric aggregation and rollup at different time intervals. For Indian banking, test specific metrics for UPI transaction success rate, NEFT batch processing throughput, and CBS response time. Validate that dashboards built on these metrics accurately reflect the banking system state.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Technical' },
    { id: 'IQ-OBL-003', question: 'How do you test alerting rules for banking observability systems?', answer: 'Create test scenarios that deliberately trigger alert conditions. Test that transaction failure rate exceeding the threshold generates a critical alert. Verify that CBS response time degradation triggers a warning before it impacts SLA. Test alert routing ensuring payment alerts reach the payment operations team and security alerts reach the SOC. Validate alert escalation when primary responders do not acknowledge within the defined timeframe. Test alert suppression during planned maintenance. For Indian banking, test specific alert rules for NPCI connectivity loss, regulatory report generation failure, and PMLA threshold transaction detection. Verify that alert fatigue is minimized through proper threshold tuning.', difficulty: 'Hard', category: 'Practical' },
    { id: 'IQ-OBL-004', question: 'How do you test distributed tracing implementation in banking microservices?', answer: 'Verify that trace context is correctly propagated across all banking service boundaries including REST calls, message queue communications, and database operations. Test that trace IDs appear in logs enabling correlation between trace spans and log entries. Validate trace completeness by verifying that all services in a banking transaction flow appear in the trace. Test that trace data includes sufficient metadata like account identifiers and transaction types for meaningful analysis. For Indian banking, verify that traces capture the complete UPI payment flow across PSP service, NPCI integration, and CBS posting, enabling pinpoint identification of latency sources.', difficulty: 'Hard', category: 'Technical' },
    { id: 'IQ-OBL-005', question: 'How do you use observability for banking performance optimization?', answer: 'Analyze trace data to identify the slowest service hops in banking transaction flows. Use metrics dashboards to identify patterns like increased latency during specific times or for specific transaction types. Analyze log data for recurring errors that indicate performance issues like database connection timeouts. Create SLO (Service Level Objective) dashboards that show banking services against their performance targets. For Indian banking, use observability data to optimize NEFT batch processing by identifying the bottleneck step, to tune UPI response times by identifying which service adds the most latency, and to capacity plan for peak banking periods by analyzing historical metric trends.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Practical' },
    { id: 'IQ-OBL-006', question: 'How do you test log aggregation and analysis for banking compliance?', answer: 'Verify that audit logs from all banking services are collected in the central log platform without gaps. Test that log search can retrieve all actions by a specific employee or all transactions for a specific customer within a date range. Validate that log retention policies meet RBI-mandated periods. Test that logs are stored in tamper-proof storage preventing modification. Verify that compliance reports can be generated from log data including access reports, transaction summaries, and anomaly detection results. For Indian banking, test that PMLA-related event logs are available for FIU-IND investigation requests and that KYC audit logs support regulatory inspection requirements.', difficulty: 'Hard', category: 'Scenario' },
    { id: 'IQ-OBL-007', question: 'What observability tools are commonly used in banking environments?', answer: 'Prometheus for metric collection with Grafana for visualization is a popular open-source combination. ELK Stack (Elasticsearch, Logstash, Kibana) for log aggregation and analysis. Jaeger or Zipkin for distributed tracing. Splunk is widely used in enterprise banking for log management and security analytics. Datadog and New Relic provide all-in-one observability platforms. PagerDuty or Opsgenie for alert management and incident response. For Indian banks, many use a combination of open-source tools for cost efficiency with commercial tools for security-critical monitoring. The choice depends on infrastructure, scale, compliance requirements, and team expertise.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Concept' },
    { id: 'IQ-OBL-008', question: 'How do you create effective banking observability dashboards?', answer: 'Design dashboards with a top-level overview showing overall banking platform health including transaction success rate, error rate, and system availability. Create module-specific dashboards for payments, deposits, loans, and cards with relevant KPIs. Include infrastructure dashboards showing server health, database performance, and network status. Add business intelligence dashboards showing transaction volumes, channel usage, and peak load patterns. For Indian banking, create specific dashboards for UPI transaction monitoring, NEFT/RTGS processing status, and CBS health. Ensure dashboards load quickly, update in real-time, and provide drill-down capability from summary to detail.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Practical' },
    { id: 'IQ-OBL-009', question: 'How do you test SLO (Service Level Objective) monitoring for banking services?', answer: 'Define SLOs for each banking service such as 99.9% availability for the payment service and p99 latency below 500ms for balance inquiry. Implement SLI (Service Level Indicator) measurement using observability metrics. Test that the SLO monitoring dashboard correctly calculates error budgets showing remaining acceptable failures for the period. Verify that error budget alerts trigger when consumption rate indicates the SLO will be breached before the period ends. For Indian banking, align SLOs with NPCI-mandated SLAs for payment processing. Test that SLO breach alerts include sufficient context for the operations team to take corrective action.', difficulty: 'Hard', category: 'Technical' }
  ],
  'observability-testing': [
    { id: 'IQ-OBT-001', question: 'What is observability testing and how does it differ from monitoring?', answer: 'Observability testing validates that the banking application provides sufficient internal visibility to understand its behavior and debug issues without deploying new code. Monitoring checks known conditions against thresholds, while observability enables answering new questions about system behavior that were not anticipated during design. Testing observability means verifying that you can answer questions like why did this UPI transaction take 5 seconds or which customers were affected by the CBS timeout. It validates the completeness and quality of metrics, logs, and traces rather than the application functionality itself.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Concept' },
    { id: 'IQ-OBT-002', question: 'How do you test that banking application logging is sufficient for debugging?', answer: 'Simulate various failure scenarios and attempt to diagnose them using only the application logs. Verify that each transaction step is logged with enough context to reconstruct the flow. Test that error logs include the exception message, stack trace, input data that caused the error (with sensitive fields masked), and suggested remediation. Verify that correlation IDs link all log entries for a single banking transaction. For Indian banking, simulate CBS communication failures and verify that logs capture the request payload, response or error, timing, and retry attempts, enabling root cause analysis without accessing the CBS system directly.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Practical' },
    { id: 'IQ-OBT-003', question: 'How do you test the correlation between metrics, logs, and traces in banking systems?', answer: 'Generate a known banking transaction and verify that the metric counter increments, a log entry is created with the correct correlation ID, and a trace span is created linking all service interactions. Test that when a metric shows an anomaly like increased error rate, you can drill down to find corresponding error logs, and from those logs navigate to the trace showing the full transaction flow. For banking, test this end-to-end correlation for a fund transfer: verify the payment success metric decrements, the error log shows the CBS timeout, and the trace reveals which CBS call timed out, enabling the complete debugging workflow.', difficulty: 'Hard', category: 'Technical' },
    { id: 'IQ-OBT-004', question: 'How do you test custom banking business metrics?', answer: 'Verify that custom metrics for banking KPIs are correctly calculated. Test that transaction volume by type accurately counts NEFT, RTGS, UPI, and IMPS transactions separately. Validate that average transaction value metrics correctly compute the mean. Test that error categorization metrics properly classify failures by type such as CBS timeout, insufficient balance, and invalid beneficiary. Verify that metric labels correctly identify the banking channel, branch, and customer segment. For Indian banking, test metrics for daily transaction limits compliance, PMLA threshold monitoring, and regulatory report generation success rates.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Practical' },
    { id: 'IQ-OBT-005', question: 'How do you test observability during chaos engineering experiments in banking?', answer: 'During chaos experiments, verify that observability systems detect the injected failure within the defined detection time. Test that metrics show the expected degradation pattern like increased error rate when CBS is unavailable. Verify that logs capture the failure details with appropriate severity levels. Test that traces show the impact on transaction flows with clear failure points. Validate that alerts fire correctly and reach the right teams. For banking, during a simulated NPCI connectivity failure, verify that the dashboard shows UPI transaction success rate dropping, logs show connection timeout errors, and traces show the NPCI service call failing within the payment flow.', difficulty: 'Hard', category: 'Scenario' },
    { id: 'IQ-OBT-006', question: 'How do you test log volume and storage management for banking systems?', answer: 'Verify that log volumes under normal and peak banking operations are within the storage capacity. Test that log rotation policies prevent disk space exhaustion. Validate that log compression reduces storage requirements without losing data. Test that archived logs are searchable for historical analysis. Verify that log ingestion rate does not exceed the logging infrastructure capacity during peak periods. For Indian banking, test log volumes during salary day processing, festival period UPI surges, and quarter-end regulatory reporting. Validate that the logging system handles burst logging from error scenarios without dropping log entries.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Technical' },
    { id: 'IQ-OBT-007', question: 'How do you test trace sampling strategies for banking applications?', answer: 'Test that the sampling strategy captures representative transaction samples while managing storage costs. Verify that critical transactions like RTGS transfers are always traced regardless of sampling rate. Test that error transactions are always captured in traces for debugging. Validate that the sampling rate provides statistically meaningful data for metric derivation. Test adaptive sampling that increases the rate during anomalies. For banking, verify that the sampling strategy ensures every transaction type including NEFT, RTGS, UPI, and IMPS is represented in traces. Test that high-value transactions above a configurable threshold are always traced for audit purposes.', difficulty: 'Hard', category: 'Technical' },
    { id: 'IQ-OBT-008', question: 'How do you test the operational runbook integration with observability?', answer: 'Verify that observability dashboards link to relevant runbook procedures for each alert type. Test that the diagnostic steps in runbooks can be executed using available observability data. Validate that runbooks reference the correct metrics, log queries, and trace filters for each banking incident scenario. Test that the complete diagnosis and remediation workflow from alert through investigation to resolution can be performed using observability tools. For Indian banking, test runbook integration for common scenarios like CBS connectivity loss, NPCI switch failure, and payment reconciliation mismatch, verifying that each step references the correct observability data source.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Practical' },
    { id: 'IQ-OBT-009', question: 'How do you test observability data security in banking systems?', answer: 'Verify that observability data does not contain sensitive information like full account numbers, passwords, or PINs. Test that log masking rules correctly redact PII before storage. Validate that access to observability platforms is restricted to authorized personnel with role-based access control. Test that observability data transmitted between services and the collection platform is encrypted. Verify that retention policies delete observability data after the mandated period. For Indian banking, test that Aadhaar and PAN numbers are masked in all observability data, that access to compliance-related logs requires additional authorization, and that observability data storage complies with RBI data localization requirements.', difficulty: 'Hard', category: 'Scenario' }
  ],
  'operation-flow': [
    { id: 'IQ-OPF-001', question: 'What are the key operational flows in a banking application that require testing?', answer: 'Key operational flows include customer onboarding from application through KYC to account activation, transaction processing from initiation through validation to settlement, loan origination from application through credit assessment to disbursement, end-of-day processing from transaction cutoff through interest calculation to GL balancing, and incident management from detection through resolution to root cause analysis. Each flow spans multiple banking modules and involves both automated and manual steps. For Indian banking, specific flows include NEFT batch settlement, UPI real-time payment processing, and RBI regulatory report generation and submission.', difficulty: 'Easy', category: 'Concept' },
    { id: 'IQ-OPF-002', question: 'How do you test the end-to-end account opening operational flow?', answer: 'Test the complete sequence from customer arrival through KYC document submission, identity verification via Aadhaar e-KYC or V-KYC, risk categorization, account number generation, initial deposit processing, debit card issuance, internet banking credential creation, and welcome kit dispatch. Verify that each step correctly feeds the next with proper data handoff. Test exception flows like KYC document rejection, duplicate customer detection, and account opening in restricted categories. For Indian banking, test CKYC record creation, PAN verification with Income Tax department, and FATCA/CRS declaration for NRI accounts. Validate the complete audit trail of the onboarding process.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Practical' },
    { id: 'IQ-OPF-003', question: 'How do you test the payment settlement operational flow?', answer: 'Test the complete settlement flow from payment initiation through clearing to final settlement. For NEFT, verify batch collection during each window, submission to RBI clearing system, settlement message processing, and beneficiary credit posting. For RTGS, test real-time individual settlement with queue management. For UPI, test the instant settlement between payer and payee banks through NPCI switch. Validate reconciliation between sent and received settlement messages. Test exception handling for rejected payments, returned transactions, and reconciliation mismatches. For Indian banking, test compliance with RBI settlement timelines and NPCI-mandated response time requirements.', difficulty: 'Hard', category: 'Scenario' },
    { id: 'IQ-OPF-004', question: 'How do you test maker-checker operational flows in banking?', answer: 'Verify the complete maker-checker cycle from transaction initiation by the maker through review and approval by the checker. Test that the maker cannot approve their own transactions. Validate that the checker sees complete transaction details for informed decision-making. Test rejection flows with mandatory rejection reasons that return to the maker. Test escalation paths when the checker is unavailable. Verify that approved transactions process within SLA timelines. Test multi-level approval workflows for high-value transactions. For Indian banking, test maker-checker for operations like beneficiary addition, transaction limit changes, and user access management.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Practical' },
    { id: 'IQ-OPF-005', question: 'How do you document and test banking process flows using flowcharts?', answer: 'Create detailed flowcharts mapping each step in the banking process with decision points, system interactions, and exception paths. Use standard symbols for processes, decisions, data stores, and external systems. Document timing constraints at each step. Identify all integration points between banking modules. Test each path through the flowchart including happy path, alternate paths, and exception paths. For Indian banking, create flowcharts for critical processes like loan disbursement, cheque clearing, and NEFT settlement. Use these flowcharts as the basis for test case design ensuring every path and decision point is covered by at least one test case.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Concept' },
    { id: 'IQ-OPF-006', question: 'How do you test exception handling in banking operational flows?', answer: 'Identify all exception points in the operational flow where processing can fail or deviate from the normal path. Test CBS unavailability during transaction processing. Verify timeout handling at each integration point. Test invalid data detection at each validation step. Validate error recovery mechanisms that allow the flow to resume after transient failures. Test compensating actions like automatic reversal when a downstream step fails after debit is processed. For Indian banking, test exceptions in NEFT processing when beneficiary bank rejects the credit, in UPI when NPCI returns a timeout response, and in loan processing when the credit bureau is unavailable.', difficulty: 'Hard', category: 'Scenario' },
    { id: 'IQ-OPF-007', question: 'How do you test SLA compliance in banking operational flows?', answer: 'Define SLA targets for each step in the operational flow and measure actual performance against these targets. Test that NEFT processing completes within the RBI-defined settlement window. Verify that RTGS transactions settle within 30 minutes. Test that loan application processing completes within the bank committed turnaround time. Monitor end-to-end flow completion time as the aggregate of all step durations. Test SLA compliance under peak load conditions. For Indian banking, test compliance with NPCI SLA for UPI response times of under 3 seconds, RBI timelines for regulatory report submission, and customer-facing SLAs for account opening and complaint resolution.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Technical' },
    { id: 'IQ-OPF-008', question: 'How do you test parallel and asynchronous steps in banking operational flows?', answer: 'Identify steps that can execute in parallel like sending customer notification while updating the transaction database. Test that parallel steps correctly synchronize at join points. Verify that asynchronous steps complete within expected timeframes. Test that the flow correctly handles scenarios where one parallel branch fails while others succeed. Validate that asynchronous processing results are correctly captured and available for subsequent steps. For banking, test that loan processing can simultaneously verify documents, check credit score, and validate collateral. Test that UPI payment processing runs the fraud check and balance check in parallel for faster response.', difficulty: 'Hard', category: 'Technical' },
    { id: 'IQ-OPF-009', question: 'How do you test operational flow monitoring and visibility in banking?', answer: 'Verify that each step in the operational flow updates a status that is visible in the operations dashboard. Test that the flow visualization shows current state, elapsed time, and next expected step. Validate that stuck or delayed flows are highlighted with appropriate alerts. Test that operations staff can drill down from the flow overview to detailed step information. For Indian banking, test that NEFT batch processing shows progress through each settlement window, that loan application tracking shows the current processing stage to both staff and customers, and that regulatory report generation shows completion percentage with estimated completion time.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Practical' }
  ],
  'payment-gateway-arch': [
    { id: 'IQ-PGA-001', question: 'What is a payment gateway and how does it function in Indian banking?', answer: 'A payment gateway is a technology platform that facilitates electronic payment transactions by securely transmitting payment data between the customer, merchant, and banking systems. In India, payment gateways integrate with NPCI for UPI and IMPS, RBI for NEFT and RTGS, card networks like Visa, Mastercard, and RuPay, and net banking integrations with multiple banks. The gateway handles transaction routing, encryption, authentication, and settlement. Testing covers the complete transaction lifecycle from payment initiation through authorization to settlement, including failure handling, reconciliation, and refund processing.', difficulty: 'Easy', category: 'Concept' },
    { id: 'IQ-PGA-002', question: 'How do you test payment gateway transaction routing?', answer: 'Verify that transactions are routed to the correct payment processor based on the payment method. Test UPI routing to NPCI, card transactions to the appropriate card network, net banking to the correct bank, and wallet payments to the wallet provider. Test routing fallback when the primary processor is unavailable. Verify that routing rules correctly handle special cases like co-branded cards, international cards, and EMI transactions. For Indian banking, test that RuPay card transactions route through NPCI while Visa and Mastercard route through their respective networks. Test that the gateway correctly identifies the issuing bank for net banking transactions.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Technical' },
    { id: 'IQ-PGA-003', question: 'How do you test payment gateway security?', answer: 'Verify PCI-DSS compliance including card data encryption, tokenization, and secure storage. Test 3D Secure authentication flow for card transactions. Validate that payment pages are served over HTTPS with proper certificate configuration. Test for common vulnerabilities like SQL injection, XSS, and CSRF in payment forms. Verify that card numbers are never logged or stored in plain text. Test transaction signing to prevent tampering. For Indian banking, test UPI PIN security, MPIN handling, and OTP-based additional factor authentication mandated by RBI. Verify that the gateway implements NPCI-mandated security standards for UPI transactions.', difficulty: 'Hard', category: 'Technical' },
    { id: 'IQ-PGA-004', question: 'How do you test payment gateway reconciliation?', answer: 'Verify that the gateway reconciliation process matches transactions across the merchant system, gateway, and bank records. Test that all successful transactions are reconciled with corresponding bank settlements. Validate identification of discrepancies like transactions successful at gateway but not settled by bank. Test reconciliation for different payment modes separately as each has different settlement cycles. Verify that reconciliation reports include sufficient detail for discrepancy investigation. For Indian banking, test UPI reconciliation against NPCI settlement files, card reconciliation against network clearing files, and net banking reconciliation against individual bank confirmation files.', difficulty: 'Hard', category: 'Practical' },
    { id: 'IQ-PGA-005', question: 'How do you test refund processing through the payment gateway?', answer: 'Test full refund and partial refund flows for each payment method. Verify that refund requests are routed to the correct processor. Test refund processing timelines ensuring they meet regulatory requirements. Validate that refund amounts do not exceed the original transaction amount. Test duplicate refund prevention. Verify refund reconciliation between merchant, gateway, and bank. For Indian banking, test that UPI refunds process through NPCI within the mandated timeline, that card refunds are processed within 5-7 business days, and that the refund amount appears correctly in the customer account statement. Test bulk refund processing for scenarios like event cancellations.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Practical' },
    { id: 'IQ-PGA-006', question: 'How do you test payment gateway failover and high availability?', answer: 'Test that the payment gateway continues processing transactions when one server instance fails. Verify that active transactions are not lost during failover. Test DNS-based failover to the DR site when the primary site is unavailable. Validate that the gateway handles database failover without transaction loss. Test load balancer health check integration. For Indian banking, verify that the gateway meets NPCI-mandated uptime requirements of 99.95%. Test that failover completes within seconds to avoid UPI transaction timeouts. Verify that no duplicate transactions occur during the failover transition period. Test the complete recovery procedure including data reconciliation after failback.', difficulty: 'Hard', category: 'Scenario' },
    { id: 'IQ-PGA-007', question: 'How do you test payment gateway webhook notifications?', answer: 'Verify that webhooks are triggered for all transaction state changes including success, failure, pending, and refund. Test webhook payload structure for completeness and accuracy. Validate webhook retry logic when the merchant endpoint is temporarily unavailable. Test webhook signature verification preventing spoofed notifications. Verify idempotent webhook handling where the merchant can safely process duplicate notifications. Test webhook delivery timing ensuring notifications arrive within the SLA. For Indian banking, test webhooks for UPI mandate execution notifications, recurring payment status updates, and settlement completion notifications. Verify that webhook payloads do not contain full card numbers or sensitive customer data.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Technical' },
    { id: 'IQ-PGA-008', question: 'How do you test international payment processing through the gateway?', answer: 'Test multi-currency transaction processing with real-time exchange rate application. Verify that FEMA limits are enforced for outward remittances. Test dynamic currency conversion where the customer can choose to pay in their home currency. Validate that GST on forex conversion markup is correctly calculated. Test international card acceptance with 3D Secure 2.0 authentication. Verify sanctions screening against OFAC and other restricted lists before processing international transactions. For Indian banking, test RBI Liberalized Remittance Scheme (LRS) limit enforcement, purpose code validation for outward remittances, and compliance with RBI guidelines on cross-border payment processing.', difficulty: 'Hard', category: 'Scenario' },
    { id: 'IQ-PGA-009', question: 'How do you perform load testing for a banking payment gateway?', answer: 'Model realistic transaction patterns based on production traffic analysis including payment method distribution, transaction amount ranges, and time-of-day patterns. Use load testing tools like JMeter or k6 to simulate concurrent payment transactions. Test with gradually increasing load to identify the breaking point. Monitor gateway response times, error rates, and throughput under load. Test peak load scenarios like flash sales, salary day payments, and festival period traffic. For Indian banking, simulate UPI transaction volumes based on NPCI published statistics which can reach hundreds of millions daily. Test that the gateway maintains sub-second response times at peak load and gracefully degrades rather than failing completely when capacity is exceeded.', difficulty: 'Hard', category: 'Technical' }
  ],
  'performance-testing': [
    { id: 'IQ-PFT-001', question: 'What types of performance testing are relevant for banking applications?', answer: 'Load testing verifies system behavior under expected peak user volumes like salary day processing. Stress testing pushes beyond normal capacity to find breaking points. Endurance testing runs sustained load over extended periods to detect memory leaks and resource exhaustion. Spike testing simulates sudden traffic surges like UPI payment spikes during IPL matches. Scalability testing measures how performance changes when resources are added. Capacity testing determines the maximum number of concurrent users the system can handle. For Indian banking, each type addresses specific scenarios from normal daily operations to festival season peaks and year-end batch processing.', difficulty: 'Easy', category: 'Concept' },
    { id: 'IQ-PFT-002', question: 'How do you design a performance test plan for a banking application?', answer: 'Start by defining performance objectives based on SLAs like API response time p95 below 500ms and transaction throughput of 1000 TPS. Identify critical banking transactions to test including balance inquiry, fund transfer, and loan EMI processing. Model workload based on production traffic analysis with realistic user behavior patterns. Define the test environment ensuring it mirrors production capacity or document the scaling factor. Create test data at production scale. Define entry and exit criteria. Plan for multiple test iterations with tuning between runs. For Indian banking, include specific scenarios for NEFT batch processing, UPI peak handling, and CBS integration performance.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Practical' },
    { id: 'IQ-PFT-003', question: 'How do you create realistic load models for banking performance testing?', answer: 'Analyze production access logs to determine concurrent user patterns throughout the day. Identify the peak hour workload distribution across transaction types. Model think times between user actions based on actual user behavior. Create user journey scripts that follow realistic banking workflows including login, balance check, fund transfer, and logout. Mix transaction types in proportions matching production ratios. For Indian banking, model the salary day spike where NEFT volumes increase 3-5 times normal, the month-end EMI processing batch, and the festival period UPI surge. Include ramp-up periods that simulate gradual user arrival rather than instant full load.', difficulty: 'Hard', category: 'Technical' },
    { id: 'IQ-PFT-004', question: 'What performance metrics do you monitor during banking performance tests?', answer: 'Monitor response time at various percentiles including p50, p90, p95, and p99 for each banking API. Track throughput in transactions per second. Measure error rate percentage of failed transactions. Monitor server-side metrics including CPU utilization, memory usage, disk I/O, and network bandwidth. Track database metrics including query execution time, connection pool usage, and lock wait times. Monitor application-specific metrics like thread pool utilization and garbage collection frequency. For Indian banking, track CBS response times separately as they are often the bottleneck, and monitor NPCI gateway latency for payment transactions.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Technical' },
    { id: 'IQ-PFT-005', question: 'How do you identify and resolve performance bottlenecks in banking applications?', answer: 'Use APM tools to identify slow code paths and resource-intensive operations. Analyze database slow query logs to find unoptimized SQL queries often in transaction history retrieval. Check for N+1 query patterns in batch processing. Monitor thread dumps for deadlocks or thread starvation. Profile memory usage for leaks in long-running processes. Check network latency between application and CBS servers. For Indian banking, common bottlenecks include CBS round-trip latency for each transaction, database locking during concurrent balance updates, JSON serialization overhead for large API responses, and encryption processing time for sensitive data. Address with connection pooling, query optimization, caching, and asynchronous processing.', difficulty: 'Hard', category: 'Practical' },
    { id: 'IQ-PFT-006', question: 'How do you performance test banking batch processing?', answer: 'Measure the total execution time for complete batch runs with production-scale data volumes. Test that EOD processing completes within the overnight batch window. Profile individual batch steps to identify the most time-consuming operations. Test with incrementally larger datasets to understand scaling behavior. Monitor resource utilization during batch execution to identify constraints. Test concurrent batch execution where independent batches run simultaneously. For Indian banking, test NEFT batch processing throughput during each settlement window, month-end interest calculation batch for millions of accounts, and regulatory report generation batch for RBI submission deadlines.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Scenario' },
    { id: 'IQ-PFT-007', question: 'What tools do you use for banking performance testing?', answer: 'Apache JMeter is widely used for its extensive protocol support and banking-specific plugins. Gatling provides code-based test scripts with excellent reporting. k6 offers modern JavaScript-based scripting with cloud execution capabilities. Locust provides Python-based load testing suitable for teams with Python expertise. For monitoring, use Grafana with Prometheus for real-time dashboards during tests. Use Application Performance Monitoring tools like New Relic, Dynatrace, or open-source alternatives. For Indian banking, JMeter is most common due to its support for SOAP protocols used by CBS systems and its ability to handle complex correlation for banking session management.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Technical' },
    { id: 'IQ-PFT-008', question: 'How do you report performance test results for banking stakeholders?', answer: 'Create an executive summary showing whether performance SLAs are met or breached with clear pass/fail indicators. Include response time distribution charts showing percentile curves for critical banking transactions. Present throughput trends showing the system capacity envelope. Highlight bottleneck analysis with specific recommendations for optimization. Compare results across test iterations showing improvement after tuning. For banking, include specific sections for payment processing performance, CBS integration performance, and batch processing duration. Provide risk assessment for scenarios where performance approaches SLA boundaries. Include test environment specifications and any caveats about extrapolating to production scale.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Behavioral' },
    { id: 'IQ-PFT-009', question: 'How do you integrate performance testing into the CI/CD pipeline for banking?', answer: 'Run lightweight performance tests as part of every CI build checking for performance regressions. Define performance budgets with automated pass/fail criteria like API response time p95 must be below 200ms. Run comprehensive performance test suites nightly against the latest build. Trend performance metrics across builds to detect gradual degradation. For banking, configure different performance test profiles for different change types where changes to payment processing code trigger the full payment performance suite. Set up automated comparison between current and baseline results with alerts on significant degradation. Include database query performance checks in the pipeline.', difficulty: 'Hard', category: 'Practical' }
  ],
  'process-flow': [
    { id: 'IQ-PRF-001', question: 'How do you map banking process flows for test case design?', answer: 'Start by interviewing business stakeholders to understand the end-to-end process. Document each step with its inputs, outputs, processing rules, and exceptions. Create swimlane diagrams showing interactions between different banking departments and systems. Identify decision points with all possible outcomes. Map data flows between banking modules like CIF, deposits, payments, and GL. For Indian banking, document regulatory touchpoints where RBI or NPCI rules affect the process. Use these process flow maps to derive test cases ensuring every path, decision point, and exception is covered. Maintain process flows as living documents updated when banking rules change.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Practical' },
    { id: 'IQ-PRF-002', question: 'What is a banking transaction lifecycle and how do you test it?', answer: 'A banking transaction lifecycle includes initiation where the customer or system creates the transaction, validation where business rules and limits are checked, authorization where maker-checker or customer authentication occurs, processing where the actual debit/credit is executed, settlement where inter-bank clearing occurs, and notification where the customer is informed. Test each phase independently and then test the complete lifecycle. Test state transitions ensuring transactions move correctly from pending to authorized to processed to settled. For Indian banking, the lifecycle varies by payment type with UPI being near-instant and NEFT being batch-based.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Concept' },
    { id: 'IQ-PRF-003', question: 'How do you test the loan origination process flow?', answer: 'Test the complete flow from customer application through document collection, income verification, credit bureau check, property valuation for secured loans, credit committee approval, sanction letter generation, legal documentation, mortgage registration, and final disbursement. Test decision points including credit score thresholds, debt-to-income ratios, and collateral adequacy. Test exception paths like additional document requests, conditional approvals, and rejection with reasons. For Indian banking, test CIBIL score integration, CERSAI charge registration, PMAY subsidy eligibility check, and regulatory reporting of loan sanctions. Verify SLA compliance at each process step.', difficulty: 'Hard', category: 'Scenario' },
    { id: 'IQ-PRF-004', question: 'How do you test the cheque clearing process flow in CTS?', answer: 'Test the complete CTS (Cheque Truncation System) flow from cheque deposit capture through image scanning, MICR data extraction, outward clearing submission to the clearing house, return memo processing, and final credit to the depositor account. Test positive pay verification for cheques above Rs 50,000 as per RBI mandate. Test return scenarios with all standard return reasons. Verify that the clearing cycle respects banking holidays and cut-off times. For Indian banking, test both local and intercity clearing processes. Validate that cheque images meet CTS quality standards. Test the dispute resolution flow for returned cheques.', difficulty: 'Hard', category: 'Practical' },
    { id: 'IQ-PRF-005', question: 'How do you test the NEFT batch processing flow?', answer: 'Test the complete NEFT flow from transaction collection during each settlement window, batch file creation in the prescribed format, submission to the NEFT clearing system, processing of inward clearing messages, credit to beneficiary accounts, and status update notification to originators. Test boundary conditions at window cut-off times. Verify handling of rejected transactions with proper return reasons. Test reconciliation between outward and inward clearing totals. For Indian banking, NEFT now operates 24x7 with half-hourly settlement windows. Test that transactions submitted just before the window cut-off are included in the current batch. Verify compliance with RBI NEFT processing guidelines.', difficulty: 'Hard', category: 'Technical' },
    { id: 'IQ-PRF-006', question: 'How do you test the customer complaint resolution process flow?', answer: 'Test the complete complaint flow from lodging through acknowledgment, assignment to the appropriate department, investigation, resolution, and closure with customer confirmation. Test SLA tracking at each step with escalation when resolution exceeds defined timelines. Test that the customer can track complaint status through all channels. Verify that complaint data feeds regulatory reporting. For Indian banking, test compliance with RBI guidelines on complaint resolution timelines, integration with the Banking Ombudsman complaint management system, and the grievance redressal mechanism as per the RBI charter of customer rights. Test that complaints trigger proper investigation workflows in the operations team.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Practical' },
    { id: 'IQ-PRF-007', question: 'How do you test the anti-money laundering (AML) process flow?', answer: 'Test the complete AML flow from transaction monitoring through alert generation, investigation, case management, and regulatory reporting. Verify that the transaction monitoring engine correctly identifies suspicious patterns including structuring, rapid movement of funds, and transactions with high-risk jurisdictions. Test alert prioritization based on risk scoring. Verify the investigation workflow where compliance officers review alerts with supporting transaction data. Test STR filing with FIU-IND in the prescribed format. For Indian banking, test CTR generation for cash transactions above Rs 10 lakhs, cross-border wire transfer monitoring, and PEP (Politically Exposed Person) screening.', difficulty: 'Hard', category: 'Scenario' },
    { id: 'IQ-PRF-008', question: 'How do you test process flow integration between banking channels?', answer: 'Verify that processes initiated on one channel can be continued on another. Test that a fund transfer started on mobile banking but interrupted can be resumed on internet banking. Verify that loan applications submitted at the branch reflect in the internet banking portal for status tracking. Test that beneficiary addition through any channel is immediately available across all channels. For Indian banking, test that UPI mandates set up through one PSP app appear in the bank direct app. Verify that transaction limits are enforced consistently across channels so the same daily limit applies whether transfers are made through mobile, internet banking, or branch.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Practical' },
    { id: 'IQ-PRF-009', question: 'How do you use process mining for banking test optimization?', answer: 'Process mining analyzes actual process execution data from banking system logs to discover how processes really work compared to how they are designed. It reveals process variants, bottlenecks, and deviations from the standard flow. For testing, process mining identifies the most frequently executed paths that need thorough testing, unusual process variants that might indicate defects or workarounds, and steps with high failure rates requiring focused testing. In Indian banking, process mining of loan processing logs might reveal that 30% of applications follow an unofficial fast-track path, or that a specific branch regularly bypasses the standard KYC verification step.', difficulty: 'Hard', category: 'Concept' }
  ],
  'rag-testing-lab': [
    { id: 'IQ-RAG-001', question: 'What is RAG (Retrieval-Augmented Generation) and how is it used in banking?', answer: 'RAG combines information retrieval with language generation to create AI systems that can answer questions based on specific knowledge bases. In banking, RAG is used for customer service chatbots that answer questions about bank products and policies, internal knowledge assistants that help employees navigate complex regulatory requirements, document analysis systems that extract information from loan applications, and compliance tools that map RBI circulars to internal processes. RAG enables accurate, contextual answers grounded in the banks actual documentation rather than generic LLM knowledge which might be outdated or incorrect.', difficulty: 'Easy', category: 'Concept' },
    { id: 'IQ-RAG-002', question: 'How do you test the retrieval component of a banking RAG system?', answer: 'Test retrieval accuracy by creating a benchmark dataset of banking queries mapped to the correct source documents. Verify that queries about interest rates retrieve the current rate card rather than outdated versions. Test semantic search quality ensuring that queries phrased differently but meaning the same thing retrieve relevant documents. Measure retrieval metrics including precision at k, recall, and mean reciprocal rank. Test with domain-specific banking terminology to ensure the embedding model understands terms like CRAR, NPA, and IRAC norms. For Indian banking, test retrieval across multiple document types including RBI circulars, internal policies, product manuals, and FAQ documents.', difficulty: 'Hard', category: 'Technical' },
    { id: 'IQ-RAG-003', question: 'How do you test the generation component of a banking RAG system?', answer: 'Test that generated answers are faithful to the retrieved source documents without hallucination. Verify that financial figures like interest rates, charges, and limits exactly match the source document values. Test that the system correctly handles conflicting information across documents by prioritizing the most recent version. Validate that generated answers include proper citations referencing the source document. Test with adversarial queries designed to induce hallucination. For Indian banking, verify that answers about RBI regulations reference the correct circular number and date, that product information matches the current offerings, and that the system does not generate financial advice that could create regulatory liability.', difficulty: 'Hard', category: 'Practical' },
    { id: 'IQ-RAG-004', question: 'How do you test RAG system accuracy for banking compliance queries?', answer: 'Create a test dataset of compliance questions with verified correct answers reviewed by the compliance team. Test questions covering PMLA thresholds, KYC requirements, digital lending guidelines, and PCI-DSS requirements. Verify that answers reference the correct regulatory source. Test with questions that have changed answers due to recent regulatory updates to verify the system uses current information. Measure accuracy as the percentage of answers that match compliance team validated correct answers. For Indian banking, test with questions about recent RBI circulars to verify that the document index is current and that outdated guidance is not provided.', difficulty: 'Hard', category: 'Scenario' },
    { id: 'IQ-RAG-005', question: 'How do you test RAG document indexing for banking knowledge bases?', answer: 'Verify that all banking documents are successfully indexed including PDFs, Word documents, and HTML pages. Test that document chunking preserves context by verifying that answers requiring information from a single document section are complete. Validate that document updates are reflected in the index within the expected refresh interval. Test that document deletion removes the content from search results. Verify that metadata like document date, category, and authority level is correctly extracted and stored. For Indian banking, test indexing of RBI circular PDFs, NPCI technical specifications, internal policy documents, and product brochures with proper handling of tables and structured content.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Technical' },
    { id: 'IQ-RAG-006', question: 'How do you test RAG system security for banking applications?', answer: 'Verify that the RAG system enforces access controls ensuring users can only retrieve documents they are authorized to view. Test that confidential banking documents like audit reports and risk assessments are not retrievable by unauthorized users. Validate that prompt injection attacks cannot bypass access controls or extract restricted information. Test that the system does not leak information about the existence of restricted documents. For Indian banking, verify that customer PII in indexed documents is properly handled, that internal investigation reports are restricted to compliance users, and that the system cannot be manipulated to reveal proprietary banking strategies or upcoming product details.', difficulty: 'Hard', category: 'Technical' },
    { id: 'IQ-RAG-007', question: 'How do you measure RAG system performance for banking use cases?', answer: 'Measure end-to-end response time from query submission to answer generation targeting under 3 seconds for customer-facing applications. Track retrieval latency separately from generation latency to identify bottlenecks. Measure throughput in queries per second to ensure the system handles concurrent banking staff queries. Monitor embedding computation time for new documents added to the knowledge base. For Indian banking, test performance with the expected number of concurrent users including branch staff, customer service agents, and compliance officers. Verify that the system maintains performance as the document corpus grows with new RBI circulars and policy updates.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Technical' },
    { id: 'IQ-RAG-008', question: 'How do you test multilingual RAG systems for Indian banking?', answer: 'Test query understanding and response generation in Hindi, English, and other supported regional languages. Verify that queries in Hindi correctly retrieve documents that may be in English. Test code-mixed queries using Hinglish which is common among Indian banking customers. Validate that financial terminology is correctly understood across languages. Test that numerical data like amounts and rates are accurately presented in the response language. For Indian banking, verify that the system handles banking-specific Hindi terms, correctly translates technical regulatory terms, and provides accurate responses regardless of the query language used.', difficulty: 'Hard', category: 'Practical' },
    { id: 'IQ-RAG-009', question: 'How do you perform regression testing on a banking RAG system after updates?', answer: 'Maintain a golden dataset of banking queries with verified correct answers covering all major topic areas. After any system update including model changes, document additions, or configuration modifications, run the complete golden dataset and compare answers against baselines. Track answer quality metrics including faithfulness, relevance, and completeness over time. Use automated evaluation tools that check for factual consistency with source documents. For Indian banking, the golden dataset should cover current RBI regulations, product information, operational procedures, and compliance requirements. Flag any answer changes for human review before deploying the updated system.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Practical' }
  ],
  'regression-testing': [
    { id: 'IQ-RGT-001', question: 'What is regression testing and why is it critical for banking applications?', answer: 'Regression testing verifies that previously working functionality continues to work correctly after code changes, configuration updates, or environment modifications. In banking, regression testing is critical because a code change to the loan module could inadvertently affect interest calculation in the deposits module due to shared libraries or database dependencies. Every banking release requires extensive regression testing to ensure that NEFT, RTGS, UPI, and other payment channels continue functioning correctly. RBI expects banks to demonstrate thorough regression testing as part of their change management process.', difficulty: 'Easy', category: 'Concept' },
    { id: 'IQ-RGT-002', question: 'How do you design an effective regression test suite for banking applications?', answer: 'Prioritize test cases based on risk and usage frequency. Include critical path tests for core banking operations like fund transfer, balance inquiry, and account management. Add integration tests covering CBS, NPCI, and payment gateway interactions. Include boundary value tests for transaction limits and financial calculations. Organize the suite by banking module for selective execution. Tag tests with priority levels to enable risk-based test selection for smaller changes. For Indian banking, ensure the regression suite covers all payment types, all account types, regulatory compliance checks, and cross-channel consistency tests. Review and update the suite quarterly to align with application changes.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Practical' },
    { id: 'IQ-RGT-003', question: 'How do you optimize regression test execution time for banking applications?', answer: 'Implement test parallelization to run independent tests concurrently across multiple environments. Use test impact analysis to identify and run only tests affected by the specific code change. Prioritize tests by risk so that critical banking tests run first, enabling early feedback. Move stable tests to the API level rather than UI level for faster execution. Implement test data pre-provisioning to eliminate setup time. For Indian banking, create tiered regression suites where a 30-minute smoke suite runs on every commit, a 2-hour core regression runs on merge, and the full 8-hour regression suite runs nightly. Use selective regression based on the changed banking module.', difficulty: 'Hard', category: 'Technical' },
    { id: 'IQ-RGT-004', question: 'How do you handle regression testing for banking regulatory changes?', answer: 'When RBI issues a new circular affecting banking software, identify all impacted functionality through impact analysis. Run the full regression suite for affected banking modules. Create new test cases for the regulatory change and add them to the permanent regression suite. Test that the regulatory change does not break existing functionality in other modules. For Indian banking, regulatory changes like updated NEFT processing windows, revised transaction limits, or new PMLA reporting requirements need targeted regression testing. Maintain a regulatory change impact matrix that maps each type of RBI directive to the affected banking modules and their regression test suites.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Scenario' },
    { id: 'IQ-RGT-005', question: 'What is the role of automation in banking regression testing?', answer: 'Automation is essential for regression testing in banking due to the large number of test cases that must be executed for every release. Automated regression tests provide consistent execution eliminating human error in repetitive test steps. They enable faster feedback allowing teams to identify regression issues within hours rather than days. Automation supports continuous integration by running regression tests on every code commit. For banking, automate the highest priority regression tests covering core transactions, financial calculations, and regulatory checks first. Target 70-80% automation of the regression suite while keeping exploratory and complex scenario testing manual.', difficulty: 'Easy', category: 'Concept' },
    { id: 'IQ-RGT-006', question: 'How do you manage regression test data for banking applications?', answer: 'Create a dedicated regression test data set that is reset to a known state before each test run. Include accounts with specific balances, preconfigured beneficiaries, and known transaction histories needed by regression tests. Implement automated data seeding that creates the required test data state programmatically. Use database snapshots for quick environment reset. For banking, maintain test accounts representing different products like savings, current, FD, NRE and different states like active, dormant, frozen. Ensure test data includes the full range of IFSC codes, transaction types, and customer profiles needed for comprehensive regression coverage.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Practical' },
    { id: 'IQ-RGT-007', question: 'How do you measure regression testing effectiveness for banking releases?', answer: 'Track defect detection rate measuring what percentage of regression defects are found before production. Monitor regression test coverage against the total application functionality. Measure regression test execution time and trend it across releases. Track false positive rate where tests fail due to test issues rather than application defects. Monitor the number of production incidents caused by regression issues. For banking, measure the effectiveness of regression testing for each banking module separately. Calculate the cost of quality by comparing the cost of regression testing versus the cost of production defects it prevents. Present effectiveness metrics to stakeholders as justification for testing investment.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Behavioral' },
    { id: 'IQ-RGT-008', question: 'How do you handle regression testing for database schema changes in banking?', answer: 'Test that data migration scripts correctly transform existing data to the new schema. Verify that application queries work correctly with the new schema. Test backward compatibility if old and new application versions must coexist during deployment. Run the full regression suite against the new schema to detect any SQL query incompatibilities. For banking, schema changes affecting transaction tables require extensive regression testing of all banking operations that access those tables. Test that stored procedures and database triggers function correctly with the new schema. Verify that reporting queries and regulatory data extractions produce correct results after the schema change.', difficulty: 'Hard', category: 'Technical' },
    { id: 'IQ-RGT-009', question: 'How do you prioritize regression tests when time is limited for a banking release?', answer: 'Use risk-based prioritization considering the probability of failure and the business impact of failure. Highest priority goes to tests covering recently changed code, tests for core financial transactions, tests for regulatory compliance features, and tests for areas with high defect history. Medium priority for tests covering integration points and cross-module interactions. Lower priority for tests covering unchanged stable modules and cosmetic validations. For Indian banking, always prioritize payment processing tests since failures directly impact customers and regulatory compliance tests since failures risk penalties. Use code change analysis to focus testing on modules affected by the specific release changes.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Practical' }
  ],
  'reports': [
    { id: 'IQ-RPT-001', question: 'What types of test reports are produced in banking testing projects?', answer: 'Banking testing projects produce daily test execution status reports showing progress against the test plan, defect summary reports categorized by severity and banking module, test coverage reports showing requirement-to-test-case mapping, sprint or iteration test completion reports, UAT status reports for business stakeholder review, performance test results reports with SLA compliance analysis, security scan reports with vulnerability findings, and final release readiness reports aggregating all quality metrics. For Indian banking, additional reports include regulatory compliance test coverage reports, NPCI integration test certification reports, and audit-ready test evidence packages.', difficulty: 'Easy', category: 'Concept' },
    { id: 'IQ-RPT-002', question: 'How do you create an effective test execution report for banking stakeholders?', answer: 'Structure the report with an executive summary showing overall test status using a red/amber/green indicator. Include a progress section showing planned versus executed test counts by banking module. Present defect summary with counts by severity, status, and module with trend graphs. Highlight critical risks and blockers affecting testing progress. Include environment status showing test infrastructure health. For banking, add sections for regulatory test coverage, payment integration test results, and CBS regression status. Use visualizations like heat maps showing module-wise quality and trend charts showing quality improvement over sprints. Include actionable recommendations for risk mitigation.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Practical' },
    { id: 'IQ-RPT-003', question: 'How do you generate automated test reports in banking CI/CD pipelines?', answer: 'Integrate reporting tools like Allure, ExtentReports, or custom HTML report generators into the test automation framework. Configure CI/CD pipelines to generate and publish reports after each test execution. Include test execution summary, detailed results for each test case, failure screenshots and logs, and execution time analysis. Publish reports to a shared location accessible to all stakeholders. For banking, configure automated reports to highlight critical banking test failures separately, include payment processing test results in a dedicated section, and generate comparison reports between current and previous builds showing quality trends.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Technical' },
    { id: 'IQ-RPT-004', question: 'How do you create a release readiness report for banking applications?', answer: 'Aggregate quality metrics from all testing phases into a comprehensive release readiness assessment. Include test execution completion percentage, open defect summary by severity, performance test results against SLAs, security scan findings with remediation status, regression test results, and UAT sign-off status. Define clear go/no-go criteria based on these metrics. For Indian banking, include regulatory compliance test status referencing specific RBI guidelines, NPCI certification status for payment integrations, and data migration validation results for CBS changes. Present the report to the Change Advisory Board for formal release approval. Document any accepted risks with mitigation plans.', difficulty: 'Hard', category: 'Practical' },
    { id: 'IQ-RPT-005', question: 'How do you report test metrics to different audiences in banking?', answer: 'Tailor reports to each audience. For bank CIOs and CTOs, provide high-level quality dashboards with business impact indicators like customer-impacting defects and regulatory compliance status. For project managers, provide sprint-level test progress, defect trends, and schedule risk indicators. For QA leads, provide detailed test execution results, automation metrics, and environment issues. For developers, provide defect details with reproduction steps and failure analysis. For compliance officers, provide regulatory test coverage and compliance evidence. For RBI auditors, provide comprehensive test documentation with requirement traceability and test evidence.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Behavioral' },
    { id: 'IQ-RPT-006', question: 'How do you create performance test reports for banking applications?', answer: 'Include test environment specifications with hardware, software, and network configuration. Present response time analysis with percentile charts for each banking transaction type. Show throughput curves indicating system capacity. Include error rate analysis categorized by error type. Present resource utilization graphs for servers, databases, and network. Highlight bottleneck analysis with specific recommendations. Compare results against defined SLAs with clear pass/fail indicators. For Indian banking, include specific analysis for CBS integration performance, NPCI gateway response times, and batch processing duration. Provide capacity planning recommendations based on projected transaction volume growth.', difficulty: 'Hard', category: 'Technical' },
    { id: 'IQ-RPT-007', question: 'What defect analysis reports are important for banking quality improvement?', answer: 'Root cause distribution report showing what percentage of defects come from requirements, design, coding, or environment issues. Module-wise defect density report identifying problematic banking modules. Defect age analysis showing how long defects remain open. Defect trend report showing whether quality is improving across releases. Escaped defect analysis identifying defects that reached production and why testing missed them. For Indian banking, create specific analysis for payment processing defects, CBS integration defects, and regulatory compliance defects. Use these reports to drive targeted quality improvement initiatives and testing process enhancements.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Behavioral' },
    { id: 'IQ-RPT-008', question: 'How do you create test coverage reports for banking compliance audits?', answer: 'Map every testable regulatory requirement to specific test cases with execution results. Create a compliance matrix showing each RBI guideline, NPCI specification, and PCI-DSS requirement alongside the test cases that validate them and their latest execution status. Include evidence links for each test execution like screenshots, API responses, and database records. Document any compliance gaps with risk assessment and remediation timelines. For Indian banking, organize the report by regulatory authority with separate sections for RBI compliance, NPCI compliance, PCI-DSS compliance, and PMLA compliance. This report must be maintained as a living document updated with each release.', difficulty: 'Hard', category: 'Practical' },
    { id: 'IQ-RPT-009', question: 'How do you use test reporting data for continuous improvement in banking projects?', answer: 'Analyze defect trends to identify recurring issues in specific banking modules and invest in better testing or code quality for those areas. Use escaped defect analysis to improve test case design and coverage. Track automation ROI by comparing manual testing effort before and after automation implementation. Monitor test cycle time trends to identify efficiency improvements or degradations. For Indian banking, use reporting data to justify investment in testing tools, team training, and environment improvements. Present quarterly quality improvement reports to management showing measurable improvements in defect rates, test efficiency, and production quality metrics.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Behavioral' }
  ],
  'rpa-testing-lab': [
    { id: 'IQ-RPA-001', question: 'What is RPA and how is it used in banking operations?', answer: 'Robotic Process Automation (RPA) uses software robots to automate repetitive rule-based tasks in banking operations. Common banking RPA use cases include account opening data entry from physical forms into CBS, reconciliation between multiple banking systems, regulatory report data compilation from multiple sources, KYC document processing and verification, loan processing workflow automation, and customer query resolution using data from multiple systems. For Indian banking, RPA is extensively used for NEFT/RTGS processing, cheque clearing operations, and RBI return filing. Testing RPA bots is critical because errors in automated banking processes can have widespread financial impact.', difficulty: 'Easy', category: 'Concept' },
    { id: 'IQ-RPA-002', question: 'How do you test RPA bots for banking data entry accuracy?', answer: 'Create test datasets with known inputs and expected outputs for the banking process being automated. Test with various data types including numeric amounts, alphanumeric account numbers, and text fields with special characters. Verify that the bot correctly handles different data formats like dates in DD/MM/YYYY versus MM/DD/YYYY. Test boundary values for amount fields and character limits. Validate that the bot correctly identifies and handles invalid input data. For Indian banking, test with multi-language customer names, various IFSC code formats, and different PAN and Aadhaar number patterns. Measure the bot error rate against manual processing error rate.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Practical' },
    { id: 'IQ-RPA-003', question: 'How do you test RPA bot error handling and exception management?', answer: 'Simulate application unavailability when the bot attempts to access CBS or other banking systems. Test behavior when expected UI elements are not found due to application changes. Verify that the bot correctly handles timeout scenarios for slow-responding banking applications. Test with malformed input data that the bot should reject. Validate that exception records are properly logged with sufficient detail for investigation. Test the bot recovery mechanism ensuring it can resume from the last successful step after an error. For banking, verify that RPA bot errors do not leave financial transactions in incomplete states and that exception items are routed to human operators for resolution.', difficulty: 'Hard', category: 'Scenario' },
    { id: 'IQ-RPA-004', question: 'How do you test RPA bot performance and scalability for banking operations?', answer: 'Measure bot execution time for processing a single banking transaction and for batch processing. Test with increasing data volumes to understand processing capacity. Verify that multiple bots running concurrently on the same banking application do not cause conflicts or performance degradation. Test bot performance during peak banking hours when underlying applications are under load. For Indian banking, test bot capacity for high-volume operations like salary processing for large corporate accounts, bulk NEFT processing, and month-end reconciliation runs. Compare bot processing throughput against manual processing capacity to validate ROI projections.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Technical' },
    { id: 'IQ-RPA-005', question: 'How do you test RPA bot security in banking environments?', answer: 'Verify that bot credentials are securely stored using credential vaults rather than hardcoded in scripts. Test that bots operate with minimum necessary privileges following the principle of least access. Validate that bot actions are logged in the banking audit trail just like human user actions. Test that bot sessions are properly terminated after task completion. Verify that bots cannot be manipulated to perform unauthorized transactions. For Indian banking, test that RPA bots handling customer data comply with data privacy requirements, that bot access to CBS follows the same authentication rules as human users, and that bot actions in regulatory processes are auditable for RBI compliance.', difficulty: 'Hard', category: 'Technical' },
    { id: 'IQ-RPA-006', question: 'How do you perform regression testing on banking RPA bots after application updates?', answer: 'Maintain a regression test suite that validates all bot functionality against each banking application the bot interacts with. Run regression tests whenever the underlying banking application is updated since UI changes can break bot element locators. Test with the specific application version deployed in each environment. Maintain bot test environments that mirror production application configurations. For Indian banking, coordinate RPA regression testing with CBS upgrade schedules, internet banking portal updates, and regulatory application changes. Implement automated regression that runs the complete bot workflow after every application deployment to catch breaking changes early.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Practical' },
    { id: 'IQ-RPA-007', question: 'How do you validate RPA bot output for banking reconciliation processes?', answer: 'Compare bot reconciliation output against independently calculated expected results. Verify that the bot correctly identifies matching records across systems. Validate that discrepancy detection is accurate with no false positives or missed mismatches. Test with deliberately introduced discrepancies to verify detection capability. Verify that reconciliation reports generated by the bot match the required format. For Indian banking, test reconciliation bots for NEFT/RTGS settlement matching, ATM switch reconciliation, card settlement reconciliation, and GL balancing. Validate that the bot handles different date formats, amount formats, and reference number formats across the systems being reconciled.', difficulty: 'Hard', category: 'Practical' },
    { id: 'IQ-RPA-008', question: 'What are the challenges of testing RPA in banking and how do you address them?', answer: 'Challenges include bot sensitivity to UI changes in banking applications where minor layout changes break element locators. Test environment access is limited since bots need the same environment as manual users. Bot testing requires the same banking test data as functional testing creating competition for limited resources. Timing issues where bots execute faster than the application can respond cause intermittent failures. Address by implementing resilient locator strategies using multiple identification methods, maintaining dedicated bot test environments, implementing dynamic wait mechanisms, and creating bot-specific test data pools. For banking, coordinate bot testing schedules with other testing activities.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Behavioral' },
    { id: 'IQ-RPA-009', question: 'How do you monitor RPA bot health in banking production environments?', answer: 'Implement real-time monitoring dashboards showing bot execution status, processing volumes, error rates, and queue depths. Configure alerts for bot failures, processing delays, and error rate spikes. Track bot utilization metrics to optimize scheduling and capacity. Monitor the health of underlying banking applications that bots depend on. For Indian banking, create specific monitors for regulatory process bots ensuring that RBI return filing bots complete before submission deadlines, reconciliation bots process within the banking day window, and KYC processing bots maintain throughput targets. Generate daily bot performance reports for operations teams to review and act on anomalies.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Practical' }
  ],
  'security-layer-lab': [
    { id: 'IQ-SLL-001', question: 'What are the security layers in a banking application architecture?', answer: 'Banking applications implement defense-in-depth with multiple security layers. Network layer includes firewalls, IDS/IPS, and DDoS protection. Transport layer uses TLS 1.2+ for all communications with certificate pinning for mobile apps. Application layer implements authentication, authorization, input validation, and output encoding. Data layer uses encryption at rest, column-level encryption for sensitive fields, and data masking. Infrastructure layer includes server hardening, container security, and secrets management. For Indian banking, additional layers include RBI-mandated cybersecurity controls, NPCI security specifications for payment processing, and PCI-DSS requirements for card data handling.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Concept' },
    { id: 'IQ-SLL-002', question: 'How do you test network security for banking applications?', answer: 'Test firewall rules to verify that only authorized ports and protocols are open. Verify network segmentation between DMZ, application zone, and database zone. Test that internal banking services are not accessible from the internet. Validate VPN configurations for remote branch connectivity. Test intrusion detection system alerting by simulating attack traffic. Verify DDoS protection mechanisms for banking web portals. Test that management interfaces are on separate networks. For Indian banking, test SWIFT network isolation as mandated by SWIFT CSCF requirements, verify PCI-DSS network segmentation for card processing systems, and test that the NPCI connectivity zone is properly isolated from general internet traffic.', difficulty: 'Hard', category: 'Technical' },
    { id: 'IQ-SLL-003', question: 'How do you test application-level security for banking web applications?', answer: 'Test for OWASP Top 10 vulnerabilities including injection attacks in all input fields like account number, IFSC, and beneficiary name. Verify proper authentication with MFA enforcement for banking transactions. Test authorization ensuring customers cannot access other customers accounts. Validate session management with proper timeout, token rotation, and concurrent session control. Test input validation for all form fields. Verify output encoding to prevent XSS in transaction displays. For Indian banking, test that OTP validation cannot be bypassed, that transaction signing prevents tampering, and that the application correctly implements RBI-mandated additional factor authentication.', difficulty: 'Hard', category: 'Practical' },
    { id: 'IQ-SLL-004', question: 'How do you test data encryption in banking applications?', answer: 'Verify that all sensitive data is encrypted at rest using approved algorithms like AES-256. Test that data in transit uses TLS 1.2 or higher. Validate that encryption keys are stored in HSM or secure key management systems. Test key rotation procedures verifying that data encrypted with old keys remains accessible. Verify that database column-level encryption is applied to sensitive fields like Aadhaar, PAN, and account numbers. For Indian banking, verify that UPI PIN encryption follows NPCI specifications, that card data encryption meets PCI-DSS requirements, and that Aadhaar data encryption uses 2048-bit RSA as mandated by UIDAI.', difficulty: 'Hard', category: 'Technical' },
    { id: 'IQ-SLL-005', question: 'How do you test API security for banking services?', answer: 'Test authentication enforcement on all API endpoints ensuring no endpoint is accidentally left unprotected. Verify authorization checks for each API operation. Test rate limiting to prevent brute force attacks. Validate input sanitization for all request parameters. Test for BOLA (Broken Object Level Authorization) by attempting to access other users resources through API parameter manipulation. Verify that API responses do not contain excessive data beyond what the caller needs. For Indian banking, test open banking API security compliance with RBI account aggregator framework guidelines. Test that API keys have proper scope restrictions and that OAuth tokens are properly validated.', difficulty: 'Hard', category: 'Practical' },
    { id: 'IQ-SLL-006', question: 'How do you test security headers in banking web applications?', answer: 'Verify that all responses include Content-Security-Policy restricting resource loading sources. Test X-Frame-Options set to DENY preventing clickjacking attacks on banking pages. Validate Strict-Transport-Security enforcing HTTPS with appropriate max-age. Check X-Content-Type-Options set to nosniff preventing MIME type sniffing. Verify X-XSS-Protection as a defense-in-depth measure. Test Referrer-Policy to prevent leaking sensitive URL parameters. Validate Permissions-Policy restricting access to device features. For banking, these headers are critical since clickjacking on a fund transfer page or content injection on a banking portal could lead to direct financial fraud.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Technical' },
    { id: 'IQ-SLL-007', question: 'How do you test vulnerability management for banking applications?', answer: 'Implement regular vulnerability scanning using tools like Nessus, Qualys, or OpenVAS for infrastructure and OWASP ZAP or Burp Suite for application scanning. Test that identified vulnerabilities are patched within the defined SLA based on severity. Verify that patch deployment does not break banking functionality through regression testing. Track vulnerability remediation metrics including mean time to remediate by severity. For Indian banking, align vulnerability management with RBI cybersecurity framework requirements. Conduct annual VAPT (Vulnerability Assessment and Penetration Testing) as mandated by RBI. Test that the vulnerability management process covers all banking infrastructure including CBS servers, payment gateways, and internet banking platforms.', difficulty: 'Hard', category: 'Scenario' },
    { id: 'IQ-SLL-008', question: 'How do you test security incident detection capabilities in banking?', answer: 'Simulate various attack scenarios and verify that the SIEM system detects and alerts on them. Test detection of brute force login attempts, SQL injection attacks, privilege escalation attempts, and data exfiltration patterns. Verify alert response times and escalation procedures. Test that forensic data is captured for incident investigation. For Indian banking, test detection of unauthorized fund transfers, mass account data access attempts, CBS configuration changes, and after-hours privileged access. Verify integration with CERT-In (Computer Emergency Response Team India) reporting requirements. Test the complete incident response cycle from detection through containment, eradication, and recovery.', difficulty: 'Hard', category: 'Scenario' },
    { id: 'IQ-SLL-009', question: 'How do you test security in banking mobile applications?', answer: 'Test that the app does not store sensitive data in unprotected local storage. Verify certificate pinning prevents MITM attacks. Test that the app detects rooted or jailbroken devices. Validate that biometric authentication is properly implemented using the devices secure enclave. Test that the app obfuscates sensitive code to prevent reverse engineering. Verify that the app does not log sensitive information. Test clipboard protection ensuring account numbers and OTPs are not accessible through clipboard. For Indian banking, test UPI PIN storage security, MPIN encryption, and device binding mechanism. Verify compliance with RBI guidelines on mobile banking security including required encryption standards.', difficulty: 'Hard', category: 'Technical' }
  ],
  'security-levels-lab': [
    { id: 'IQ-SLV-001', question: 'What are the different security testing levels for banking applications?', answer: 'Security testing levels include Level 1 automated vulnerability scanning using tools like OWASP ZAP for common vulnerabilities. Level 2 manual security testing covering business logic flaws, authentication bypass, and authorization issues specific to banking workflows. Level 3 penetration testing simulating real-world attack scenarios targeting banking assets. Level 4 red team exercises with adversarial simulation targeting the complete banking infrastructure. Level 5 security architecture review evaluating the design-level security of banking systems. Each level provides different depth and coverage. For Indian banking, RBI mandates at least annual penetration testing by CERT-In empaneled auditors.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Concept' },
    { id: 'IQ-SLV-002', question: 'How do you perform Level 1 automated security scanning for banking applications?', answer: 'Configure automated scanners like OWASP ZAP, Burp Suite, or Checkmarx to scan banking application endpoints. Set up authenticated scanning using valid banking credentials to test protected functionality. Configure scan policies to check for SQL injection, XSS, CSRF, and other common vulnerabilities. Exclude sensitive endpoints that could trigger real transactions during scanning. Schedule regular scans as part of the CI/CD pipeline. For Indian banking, configure custom scan rules for banking-specific vulnerabilities like IFSC code injection, account number enumeration, and OTP bypass attempts. Review scan results to eliminate false positives before reporting to the development team.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Practical' },
    { id: 'IQ-SLV-003', question: 'How do you test business logic security flaws in banking applications?', answer: 'Business logic flaws are application-specific vulnerabilities that automated scanners cannot detect. Test for transaction limit bypass by manipulating request parameters. Verify that negative amounts cannot be transferred to reverse fund flow. Test that currency conversion logic cannot be exploited for arbitrage. Verify that loyalty points or rewards cannot be duplicated through race conditions. Test that account closure properly terminates all associated services. For Indian banking, test that UPI collect requests cannot be manipulated to change the amount after approval, that NEFT transactions cannot bypass the beneficiary registration cooling period, and that loan EMI calculation cannot be manipulated to reduce payments.', difficulty: 'Hard', category: 'Scenario' },
    { id: 'IQ-SLV-004', question: 'How do you perform penetration testing for internet banking applications?', answer: 'Follow a structured methodology like OWASP Testing Guide or PTES. Start with reconnaissance gathering information about the banking application architecture and technologies. Test authentication mechanisms for weaknesses including credential stuffing and session fixation. Test authorization by attempting to access other users accounts through IDOR vulnerabilities. Test input handling for injection attacks across all banking forms and APIs. Test session management for hijacking opportunities. Test file upload functionality for malicious file injection. For Indian banking, focus on payment flow manipulation, OTP bypass techniques, and CBS integration security. Document all findings with risk ratings, proof of concept, and remediation recommendations.', difficulty: 'Hard', category: 'Technical' },
    { id: 'IQ-SLV-005', question: 'What is DAST and SAST and how do they complement each other in banking security testing?', answer: 'SAST (Static Application Security Testing) analyzes source code for security vulnerabilities without running the application. It catches issues like SQL injection patterns, hardcoded credentials, and weak cryptographic usage early in development. DAST (Dynamic Application Security Testing) tests the running banking application from the outside, finding runtime vulnerabilities like authentication bypass and improper error handling. SAST finds issues early but produces false positives. DAST finds real exploitable issues but only in deployed code. For banking, use SAST in the CI pipeline for early detection and DAST against deployed test environments for runtime validation. Together they provide comprehensive security coverage.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Concept' },
    { id: 'IQ-SLV-006', question: 'How do you test security for banking API integrations with third parties?', answer: 'Verify that API keys and tokens have minimum necessary scope and are rotated regularly. Test that third-party API calls are made over encrypted channels. Validate input from third-party responses before processing since external data is untrusted. Test that the banking application handles third-party service failures gracefully without exposing internal details. Verify that rate limiting prevents third-party abuse. For Indian banking, test security of account aggregator API integrations per RBI framework, UPI third-party app integrations per NPCI specifications, and credit bureau API integrations. Verify that customer consent is properly enforced for all third-party data sharing.', difficulty: 'Hard', category: 'Practical' },
    { id: 'IQ-SLV-007', question: 'How do you test security awareness and social engineering resistance in banking?', answer: 'Conduct phishing simulations targeting banking employees with realistic emails mimicking internal communications or customer requests. Test whether employees click on suspicious links or provide credentials to fake banking portals. Test physical security by attempting tailgating into restricted banking areas. Test phone-based social engineering where attackers impersonate IT support requesting remote access credentials. For Indian banking, simulate RBI or NPCI impersonation attempts requesting urgent system access. Measure the percentage of employees who fall for each attack type and use results to target security awareness training. This testing complements technical security testing by addressing the human element.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Behavioral' },
    { id: 'IQ-SLV-008', question: 'How do you create a security testing report for banking regulatory compliance?', answer: 'Structure the report with an executive summary showing overall security posture, a detailed findings section with each vulnerability categorized by CVSS score and business impact, evidence including screenshots and proof of concept demonstrations, remediation recommendations with priority and timeline, and a risk assessment mapping findings to banking regulatory requirements. For Indian banking, map findings to RBI cybersecurity framework requirements, NPCI security standards, and PCI-DSS requirements where applicable. Include a remediation tracking section showing the status of previously identified vulnerabilities. This report is reviewed by the banks CISO and submitted to the board-level IT committee as per RBI governance requirements.', difficulty: 'Hard', category: 'Practical' },
    { id: 'IQ-SLV-009', question: 'How do you test security for banking containerized environments?', answer: 'Scan container images for known vulnerabilities before deployment using Trivy or Clair. Verify that containers run as non-root users. Test that container orchestration platforms enforce pod security policies. Validate network policies isolating banking workloads. Test that secrets are injected at runtime and not baked into container images. Verify that container runtime security tools detect and alert on anomalous behavior like unexpected process execution or network connections. For Indian banking, test that containerized banking applications meet the same security standards as traditional deployments, that container-specific attack vectors are mitigated, and that container audit logs meet RBI requirements.', difficulty: 'Hard', category: 'Technical' }
  ],
  'security-scenarios': [
    { id: 'IQ-SEC-001', question: 'What are the most critical security test scenarios for internet banking?', answer: 'Critical scenarios include brute force attack on login with account lockout verification, session hijacking through token theft or fixation, cross-site scripting in transaction forms and beneficiary name fields, SQL injection in search and filter parameters, CSRF attacks on fund transfer endpoints, insecure direct object reference to access other users accounts, man-in-the-middle attack on banking communications, and privilege escalation from customer to admin access. For Indian banking, add UPI PIN brute force protection testing, OTP interception prevention, and Aadhaar number enumeration prevention.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Practical' },
    { id: 'IQ-SEC-002', question: 'How do you test for account takeover vulnerabilities in banking applications?', answer: 'Test password reset flows for weak verification that allows takeover through email or phone access alone. Verify that account recovery does not reveal whether an account exists for a given email or phone. Test credential stuffing resistance by attempting logins with commonly leaked password lists. Verify that session tokens are invalidated after password changes. Test for insecure forgotten password flows that reveal security question answers. For Indian banking, test that mobile banking device binding cannot be transferred without proper authentication, that UPI VPA deregistration requires strong verification, and that internet banking credential reset requires multi-channel verification matching registered mobile and email.', difficulty: 'Hard', category: 'Scenario' },
    { id: 'IQ-SEC-003', question: 'How do you test for data leakage in banking applications?', answer: 'Check that error messages do not reveal internal system information like database names, SQL queries, or server paths. Verify that API responses do not include excessive data beyond what the client needs. Test that HTTP headers do not expose server technology details. Check that client-side code does not contain hardcoded API keys, internal URLs, or debugging information. Verify that browser caching controls prevent sensitive banking data from being stored. For Indian banking, verify that Aadhaar and PAN numbers are masked everywhere they appear, that account numbers are partially hidden, and that transaction details in URLs or logs do not expose complete financial information.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Technical' },
    { id: 'IQ-SEC-004', question: 'How do you test for authorization bypass in banking applications?', answer: 'Attempt to access customer A account details using customer B authenticated session. Try modifying account number parameters in URLs and API requests to access unauthorized accounts. Test horizontal privilege escalation between customers and vertical escalation from customer to admin. Verify that API endpoints enforce authorization at the data level not just the endpoint level. Test that batch operations like bulk transfer verify authorization for each individual record. For Indian banking, test that branch-level staff cannot access accounts outside their branch, that relationship managers can only view their assigned customers, and that corporate internet banking users respect the authorization matrix defined by the company admin.', difficulty: 'Hard', category: 'Scenario' },
    { id: 'IQ-SEC-005', question: 'How do you test for injection vulnerabilities in banking applications?', answer: 'Test SQL injection in all database-backed input fields including account search, transaction filter, and report parameters. Test NoSQL injection for applications using MongoDB or similar databases. Test OS command injection in any feature that interacts with the operating system like report generation or file processing. Test LDAP injection in login or directory search functions. Test XML injection in SOAP-based CBS integration endpoints. For Indian banking, test injection in IFSC code lookup fields, account number search, beneficiary name fields, and any free-text fields in loan applications or complaint forms. Verify that parameterized queries are used throughout.', difficulty: 'Hard', category: 'Technical' },
    { id: 'IQ-SEC-006', question: 'How do you test multi-factor authentication security in banking?', answer: 'Test that MFA cannot be bypassed by directly accessing post-authentication URLs. Verify that OTP codes expire within a short timeframe typically 3-5 minutes. Test that OTP brute force is prevented with rate limiting and lockout. Verify that used OTPs cannot be reused. Test that the MFA challenge is presented consistently and cannot be skipped through session manipulation. Verify that SMS and email OTP delivery is timely and reliable. For Indian banking, test that RBI-mandated additional factor authentication is enforced for all online transactions. Test that hardware token and soft token authentication cannot be cloned or intercepted.', difficulty: 'Hard', category: 'Practical' },
    { id: 'IQ-SEC-007', question: 'How do you test for cross-site scripting (XSS) in banking applications?', answer: 'Test reflected XSS by injecting script tags in URL parameters, search fields, and error messages. Test stored XSS in beneficiary names, transaction remarks, and customer profile fields that are displayed to other users or administrators. Test DOM-based XSS in client-side JavaScript that processes URL fragments or local storage data. Use payloads that attempt to steal session cookies, redirect to phishing sites, or modify displayed banking information like account balances. For Indian banking, test XSS in all customer-facing displays where user-provided data is shown, including transaction history, beneficiary lists, and complaint tracking pages. Verify that Content Security Policy headers effectively mitigate XSS risks.', difficulty: 'Hard', category: 'Technical' },
    { id: 'IQ-SEC-008', question: 'How do you test for insecure file handling in banking document management?', answer: 'Test file upload for path traversal vulnerabilities by uploading files with names containing directory traversal characters. Verify that only allowed file types are accepted and that file type validation checks the actual content not just the extension. Test for malicious file upload including web shells, macro-enabled documents, and files with embedded scripts. Verify that uploaded files are stored outside the web root and served through a controlled mechanism. Test file size limits to prevent denial of service. For Indian banking, test document upload security for KYC documents, loan applications, and trade finance documents. Verify that uploaded documents are scanned for malware before processing.', difficulty: 'Hard', category: 'Scenario' },
    { id: 'IQ-SEC-009', question: 'How do you test security for banking API webhooks and callbacks?', answer: 'Verify that webhook endpoints validate the source of incoming requests using signature verification with HMAC. Test that the banking application does not blindly trust callback data by cross-referencing with its own records. Verify that webhook URLs are not vulnerable to SSRF (Server-Side Request Forgery) allowing attackers to scan internal networks. Test that failed webhook deliveries are securely retried without creating security risks. For Indian banking, test security of NPCI UPI callback endpoints, payment gateway notification URLs, and SMS gateway delivery receipt callbacks. Verify that callback processing does not create race conditions that could be exploited for double spending or transaction status manipulation.', difficulty: 'Hard', category: 'Technical' }
  ],
  'selenium-cucumber': [
    { id: 'IQ-SLC-001', question: 'How do you integrate Selenium with Cucumber for banking application testing?', answer: 'Selenium provides browser automation capabilities while Cucumber enables BDD with Gherkin syntax that banking business users can understand. Feature files describe banking scenarios in plain language like Given a customer with savings account balance of Rs 50000 When they transfer Rs 10000 to a valid beneficiary Then the account balance should be Rs 40000. Step definitions map Gherkin steps to Selenium actions. Page Object Model classes encapsulate banking page interactions. This combination enables business stakeholders to validate test coverage by reading the feature files while testers implement automation. The approach is widely used in Indian banking for UAT automation.', difficulty: 'Easy', category: 'Concept' },
    { id: 'IQ-SLC-002', question: 'How do you write effective Gherkin scenarios for banking test automation?', answer: 'Write scenarios focused on business behavior rather than UI interactions. Use domain-specific banking language that operations staff understand. Keep scenarios concise with 3-7 steps covering a single business rule. Use Scenario Outline with Examples table for data-driven banking tests covering multiple transaction types or amounts. Organize features by banking module with tags for categorization like @payments, @deposits, @regression. For Indian banking, create scenarios covering NEFT transfer validation, UPI payment processing, loan EMI calculation verification, and FD interest computation. Use Background steps for common preconditions like customer authentication.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Practical' },
    { id: 'IQ-SLC-003', question: 'How do you implement Page Object Model with Selenium for internet banking?', answer: 'Create a BasePage class with common methods for element waiting, clicking, typing, and screenshot capture. Build page classes for each banking screen including LoginPage with methods for credential entry and CAPTCHA handling, DashboardPage for account selection and balance display, TransferPage for beneficiary selection, amount entry, and OTP verification, and StatementPage for date range selection and download. Use WebElement annotations with descriptive names. Implement fluent API pattern where methods return the next expected page like loginPage.login(credentials).selectAccount(accountNumber). For banking, handle dynamic elements like transaction reference numbers and OTP timers.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Technical' },
    { id: 'IQ-SLC-004', question: 'How do you handle authentication and OTP in Selenium banking test automation?', answer: 'For test environments, configure a test mode that accepts a fixed OTP value, eliminating the dependency on SMS delivery. Alternatively, integrate with the SMS gateway test API to programmatically retrieve OTPs. For CAPTCHA, use a test bypass mechanism in non-production environments or integrate with CAPTCHA solving services for realistic testing. Implement a reusable authentication helper that handles the complete login flow and returns an authenticated session. Cache authentication state using browser cookies or local storage to avoid repeating login for every test. For Indian banking, handle multi-factor authentication including password, OTP, and optional device verification.', difficulty: 'Hard', category: 'Practical' },
    { id: 'IQ-SLC-005', question: 'How do you handle test data in Cucumber scenarios for banking?', answer: 'Use Cucumber DataTable and Examples for inline test data in scenarios. Create a TestDataFactory that generates valid banking test data including account numbers, IFSC codes, and customer details. Implement data setup and teardown hooks that create and clean up test data using API calls rather than UI interactions. Use Scenario-scoped data sharing through a context object that passes data between steps like transaction reference numbers generated during a transfer. For Indian banking, maintain test data pools for different account types and transaction types. Store environment-specific test data in YAML configuration files separate from feature files.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Technical' },
    { id: 'IQ-SLC-006', question: 'How do you run Selenium-Cucumber tests in a CI/CD pipeline for banking?', answer: 'Configure Maven or Gradle with Cucumber runner to execute tests from the CI/CD pipeline. Use Selenium Grid or cloud services like BrowserStack for distributed browser execution. Set up parallel test execution using cucumber-jvm-parallel-plugin or JUnit platform. Generate Cucumber HTML reports and Allure reports for detailed execution analysis. Configure the pipeline to run smoke tests on every commit, regression on merge, and cross-browser tests nightly. For banking, tag critical payment tests separately so they run as the first quality gate. Set up failure screenshots and video recording for debugging failed banking transaction tests.', difficulty: 'Hard', category: 'Technical' },
    { id: 'IQ-SLC-007', question: 'How do you implement cross-browser testing with Selenium for banking applications?', answer: 'Configure WebDriver factory to instantiate Chrome, Firefox, Safari, and Edge drivers based on configuration. Use Selenium Grid for parallel execution across browsers. Focus cross-browser testing on critical banking journeys like fund transfer, account opening, and statement download. Test browser-specific issues like PDF rendering for bank statements, date picker behavior in transaction filters, and file upload for KYC documents. For Indian banking, include UC Browser and Samsung Internet which have notable market share. Use BrowserStack or Sauce Labs for broad browser and device coverage. Prioritize browsers based on actual customer usage analytics.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Practical' },
    { id: 'IQ-SLC-008', question: 'How do you handle dynamic web elements in banking Selenium tests?', answer: 'Use explicit waits with ExpectedConditions for elements that appear after AJAX calls like balance updates and transaction confirmations. Implement custom wait conditions for banking-specific scenarios like waiting for a transaction status to change from processing to completed. Handle dynamic tables in transaction history using XPath with contains functions and indexing. Deal with dynamically generated IDs by using CSS selectors based on stable attributes. For Indian banking, handle elements that change based on transaction type selection, dynamic beneficiary lists loaded via API, and real-time exchange rate displays that update periodically.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Technical' },
    { id: 'IQ-SLC-009', question: 'How do you generate and manage test reports for Selenium-Cucumber banking tests?', answer: 'Use Cucumber built-in HTML reporting for basic execution results. Integrate Allure reporting for rich interactive reports with screenshots, steps, and attachments. Configure ExtentReports for customizable HTML dashboards. Embed screenshots on failure for visual debugging of banking UI issues. Include API response details in reports for integration test failures. For banking, create custom report sections showing test results by banking module, payment type, and test priority. Generate trend reports across multiple runs showing quality improvement. Share reports automatically with stakeholders through email or Slack notifications. Archive reports for audit compliance documentation.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Practical' }
  ],
  'soap-interview-guide': [
    { id: 'IQ-SIG-001', question: 'What is SOAP and why is it still relevant in banking?', answer: 'SOAP (Simple Object Access Protocol) is an XML-based messaging protocol for web services. Despite REST gaining popularity, SOAP remains critical in banking because many CBS systems like Finacle, Flexcube, and TCS BaNCS expose their services via SOAP interfaces. SOAP provides built-in standards for security (WS-Security), reliability (WS-ReliableMessaging), and transactions (WS-AtomicTransaction) that are important for financial operations. SWIFT messaging for international payments uses XML formats similar to SOAP. For Indian banking, core banking integrations, payment gateway interfaces, and regulatory reporting often rely on SOAP web services.', difficulty: 'Easy', category: 'Concept' },
    { id: 'IQ-SIG-002', question: 'How does SOAP differ from REST in the context of banking API testing?', answer: 'SOAP uses XML exclusively with strict schema enforcement through WSDL (Web Services Description Language), while REST typically uses JSON with flexible schemas. SOAP has built-in error handling through SOAP faults with standardized error codes, while REST uses HTTP status codes. SOAP supports WS-Security for message-level encryption and digital signatures critical for banking transactions, while REST relies on transport-level security (TLS). For testing, SOAP requires understanding XML namespaces, WSDL parsing, and complex request construction, while REST testing is simpler with JSON payloads. Indian banking uses SOAP for CBS integration and REST for customer-facing APIs.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Concept' },
    { id: 'IQ-SIG-003', question: 'How do you test SOAP web services for banking CBS integration?', answer: 'Start by analyzing the WSDL to understand available operations, input/output message structures, and data types. Create test requests for each operation with valid data. Test with boundary values for financial fields. Verify SOAP fault handling for error scenarios. Test WS-Security implementation including username tokens, timestamps, and message signing. Validate response XML against the WSDL-defined schema. For Indian banking CBS systems, test operations like getAccountBalance, processTransfer, getStatementEntries, and createCustomer. Test with various account types and transaction types. Verify that CBS SOAP services handle concurrent requests correctly.', difficulty: 'Hard', category: 'Technical' },
    { id: 'IQ-SIG-004', question: 'What tools are used for SOAP web service testing in banking?', answer: 'SoapUI is the primary tool for SOAP testing offering WSDL import, request generation, assertion capabilities, and mock service creation. Postman supports SOAP testing through raw XML requests. For automation, Python zeep library generates client stubs from WSDL for scripted testing. Java Apache CXF provides robust SOAP client capabilities for enterprise banking automation. Wireshark captures and analyzes SOAP traffic for debugging. For Indian banking, SoapUI is most widely used due to its comprehensive SOAP support and ability to handle complex CBS WSDL definitions. SoapUI Pro adds data-driven testing and advanced reporting features.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Technical' },
    { id: 'IQ-SIG-005', question: 'How do you handle SOAP security testing for banking web services?', answer: 'Test WS-Security implementation by verifying username token authentication, timestamp validation, and message expiry enforcement. Test message-level encryption ensuring sensitive banking data is encrypted in the SOAP body. Verify digital signature validation preventing message tampering. Test certificate-based authentication for service-to-service calls. Validate that SOAP faults do not reveal sensitive information. Test for XML bomb and XML external entity (XXE) injection attacks. For Indian banking, verify that CBS SOAP services enforce proper authentication, that payment processing SOAP calls use message-level security, and that SWIFT-related SOAP interfaces implement required security standards.', difficulty: 'Hard', category: 'Practical' },
    { id: 'IQ-SIG-006', question: 'How do you create mock SOAP services for banking testing?', answer: 'Use SoapUI to create mock services from WSDL definitions. Configure mock responses for each operation with both success and error scenarios. Add conditional responses based on input parameters so that specific account numbers return specific balances. Implement stateful mocking where a transfer operation updates the balance returned by subsequent balance inquiry calls. For Indian banking, create mock CBS services that simulate account operations, mock NPCI SOAP services for payment integration testing, and mock credit bureau services for loan processing testing. Deploy mocks in test environments when actual backend systems are unavailable.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Practical' },
    { id: 'IQ-SIG-007', question: 'How do you perform data-driven SOAP testing for banking operations?', answer: 'Create data sources containing multiple test scenarios with varying input parameters for each SOAP operation. For account balance inquiry, create datasets with different account numbers, account types, and expected balances. For fund transfer, parameterize source account, destination account, amount, and expected result. Use SoapUI DataSource and DataLoop test steps or Python scripts with zeep to iterate through test data. For Indian banking, create comprehensive data sets covering all CBS operations with valid, invalid, and boundary test data. Include edge cases like dormant accounts, frozen accounts, and accounts under lien.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Practical' },
    { id: 'IQ-SIG-008', question: 'How do you test SOAP web service performance for banking?', answer: 'Use SoapUI Load Test or JMeter SOAP sampler to simulate concurrent SOAP requests. Measure response times for each CBS SOAP operation under increasing load. Test with production-scale message sizes since banking SOAP messages can be large with transaction details. Monitor server resources during load tests. Test connection pool behavior under sustained load. For Indian banking, load test CBS SOAP services with realistic transaction volumes for each operation type. Test peak scenarios like month-end when multiple batch processes issue concurrent CBS SOAP calls. Compare SOAP service performance with equivalent REST API performance where both are available.', difficulty: 'Hard', category: 'Technical' },
    { id: 'IQ-SIG-009', question: 'What are common SOAP testing challenges in banking and how do you overcome them?', answer: 'Complex WSDL structures with dozens of namespaces and nested types make request construction difficult. Address by using tools that auto-generate requests from WSDL. CBS SOAP services may have strict message ordering requirements. Handle by implementing proper correlation in test scripts. Large response messages with thousands of transaction records require efficient parsing. Use streaming XML parsers. WS-Security configuration with certificates and keystores is complex. Maintain a dedicated security configuration for test environments. For Indian banking, CBS vendor-specific SOAP extensions add complexity. Create wrapper libraries that abstract CBS-specific SOAP details from test code.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Behavioral' }
  ],
  'soap-ui-guide': [
    { id: 'IQ-SUG-001', question: 'What are the key features of SoapUI for banking web service testing?', answer: 'SoapUI offers WSDL import that auto-generates test requests for all CBS SOAP operations. Assertions validate response content, XPath expressions, schema compliance, and SLA thresholds. Groovy scripting enables complex test logic for banking scenarios like conditional processing based on account type. Mock services simulate CBS backends when actual systems are unavailable. Data-driven testing connects to external data sources for parameterized banking test execution. Load testing simulates concurrent users for performance validation. For Indian banking, SoapUI is particularly useful for testing Finacle, Flexcube, and BaNCS SOAP interfaces with their complex WSDL structures.', difficulty: 'Easy', category: 'Concept' },
    { id: 'IQ-SUG-002', question: 'How do you set up a SoapUI project for banking CBS testing?', answer: 'Create a new SOAP project by importing the CBS WSDL URL or file. SoapUI generates interfaces for all services defined in the WSDL. Create test suites organized by banking module like AccountService, PaymentService, and LoanService. Within each suite, create test cases for each operation covering positive, negative, and boundary scenarios. Configure project-level properties for environment-specific values like CBS URLs, credentials, and test account numbers. Set up WS-Security configurations with the required authentication tokens. For Indian banking, configure multiple test environments for SIT, UAT, and pre-production CBS instances.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Practical' },
    { id: 'IQ-SUG-003', question: 'How do you use SoapUI assertions for validating banking SOAP responses?', answer: 'Use Contains assertion to verify expected response elements like transaction status. XPath assertion extracts and validates specific values like account balance or transaction reference number. Schema compliance assertion validates the response against the WSDL-defined schema. SLA assertion verifies response time is within banking SLA thresholds. Not SOAP Fault assertion confirms successful processing. Script assertion uses Groovy to implement complex validation logic like verifying that the returned balance equals the previous balance minus the transfer amount. For Indian banking, create custom assertions validating IFSC code format, account number structure, and NPCI response code ranges.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Technical' },
    { id: 'IQ-SUG-004', question: 'How do you create test suites for end-to-end banking workflows in SoapUI?', answer: 'Chain multiple SOAP requests in a test case to simulate complete banking workflows. Use property transfer steps to pass data between requests like using the account ID from a customer creation response in the subsequent account creation request. Add Groovy script steps for data transformation and conditional logic. For a fund transfer workflow, chain getAccountBalance to verify available funds, then processTransfer to execute the debit-credit, then getAccountBalance again to verify updated balance, and finally getTransactionDetails to verify the transaction record. For Indian banking, create end-to-end suites for loan origination, cheque clearing, and NEFT processing.', difficulty: 'Hard', category: 'Practical' },
    { id: 'IQ-SUG-005', question: 'How do you use SoapUI Groovy scripts for banking test automation?', answer: 'Use Groovy scripts in SoapUI for dynamic test data generation like creating unique transaction reference numbers. Implement complex assertions comparing financial calculations against expected values with decimal precision handling. Parse large XML responses to extract and validate multiple data points. Create reusable Groovy libraries for common banking operations like interest calculation verification. Use Groovy to read test data from external files and databases. For Indian banking, write scripts to validate IFSC code format, verify PAN number check digits, calculate expected EMI amounts, and compare SOAP response amounts with database values.', difficulty: 'Hard', category: 'Technical' },
    { id: 'IQ-SUG-006', question: 'How do you perform load testing with SoapUI for banking services?', answer: 'Create a load test by selecting the banking test case to run under load. Configure the number of threads representing concurrent CBS users. Set the test duration matching banking processing windows. Add assertions for response time SLA compliance. Monitor metrics including throughput, average response time, and error count during execution. Test with gradually increasing load to find the breaking point. For Indian banking, load test CBS account inquiry operations during peak branch hours, test payment processing services during salary day volumes, and test batch processing SOAP calls during EOD window simulation. Compare load test results against NPCI and RBI performance requirements.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Practical' },
    { id: 'IQ-SUG-007', question: 'How do you configure SoapUI for WS-Security in banking tests?', answer: 'Configure outgoing WS-Security at the project or request level. Add UsernameToken configuration with the CBS service credentials. Configure timestamp inclusion with appropriate time-to-live values. Set up message signing with X.509 certificates from the banks keystore. Configure encryption for sensitive elements in the SOAP body like account numbers and amounts. For mutual authentication, configure both client and server certificates. For Indian banking, different CBS services may require different security configurations. Create security profiles for each CBS environment (SIT, UAT, production) with appropriate credentials and certificates.', difficulty: 'Hard', category: 'Technical' },
    { id: 'IQ-SUG-008', question: 'How do you use SoapUI for REST API testing in banking alongside SOAP?', answer: 'Create REST projects in SoapUI for modern banking API testing alongside SOAP projects for CBS integration. Import OpenAPI/Swagger specifications to auto-generate REST test requests. Use the same assertion and scripting capabilities for both SOAP and REST tests. Create composite test suites that test the complete banking flow from REST API call through the middleware to SOAP CBS call and back. For Indian banking, test the internet banking REST API that internally calls CBS SOAP services, verifying end-to-end data consistency. This hybrid approach is common in banks modernizing their API layer while maintaining SOAP CBS backends.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Practical' },
    { id: 'IQ-SUG-009', question: 'How do you manage SoapUI test projects for large banking testing teams?', answer: 'Store SoapUI project files in version control enabling team collaboration. Use SoapUI project properties for environment-specific configuration avoiding hardcoded values. Create shared test step libraries for reusable banking operations. Implement naming conventions for test suites, test cases, and test steps. Use SoapUI test runners (testrunner.sh) for CI/CD integration. For large banking teams, partition test suites by module so different teams can work independently. Use SoapUI Pro composite projects to share common resources across module-specific projects. For Indian banking, maintain separate project configurations for each CBS environment and version.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Behavioral' }
  ],
  'soap-ui-workflow': [
    { id: 'IQ-SUW-001', question: 'How do you design a SoapUI workflow for testing banking SOAP services end-to-end?', answer: 'Start with project setup importing the CBS WSDL and configuring security profiles. Create a workflow test suite with test cases ordered by the banking process flow. Use setup scripts to prepare test data. Chain SOAP requests using property transfers to pass data between steps. Add assertions at each step to validate intermediate results. Include teardown scripts to clean up test data. For Indian banking, design workflows for account lifecycle from creation through transactions to closure, loan lifecycle from application through disbursement to repayment, and payment lifecycle from initiation through settlement to reconciliation.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Practical' },
    { id: 'IQ-SUW-002', question: 'How do you implement conditional test execution in SoapUI banking workflows?', answer: 'Use Groovy script test steps to evaluate conditions and control workflow execution. Implement if-else logic based on SOAP response values like skipping OTP verification steps in test mode. Use SoapUI conditional goto to branch the test flow based on transaction type. Implement retry logic for operations that may have transient failures. For Indian banking, implement conditional flows where NEFT transactions follow the batch processing path while RTGS transactions follow the real-time path. Handle conditional validation where different account types have different balance calculation rules. Create recovery branches that handle CBS timeout responses with retry.', difficulty: 'Hard', category: 'Technical' },
    { id: 'IQ-SUW-003', question: 'How do you handle data dependencies in SoapUI banking test workflows?', answer: 'Use SoapUI properties to store and share data across test steps within a test case. Use property transfer steps to extract values from SOAP responses using XPath and set them as inputs for subsequent requests. Implement Groovy scripts for complex data transformations between steps. Use test suite level properties for data shared across test cases. For banking workflows, extract the transaction reference number from a fund transfer response and use it in the subsequent status inquiry request. Extract the account ID from a customer creation response for use in account-related operations throughout the test suite.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Technical' },
    { id: 'IQ-SUW-004', question: 'How do you integrate SoapUI tests with CI/CD pipelines for banking?', answer: 'Use the SoapUI command-line test runner (testrunner.sh or testrunner.bat) to execute tests from CI/CD tools. Configure Maven or Gradle plugins for SoapUI integration. Export JUnit-format test results for CI dashboard integration. Set up test suite execution profiles for different pipeline stages. For banking, configure smoke test execution after every deployment, regression suite execution nightly, and performance test execution weekly. Pass environment-specific parameters like CBS URL and credentials through CI/CD variables. Generate detailed test reports and archive them for audit purposes. Configure failure notifications for critical banking test failures.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Practical' },
    { id: 'IQ-SUW-005', question: 'How do you create SoapUI mock services for banking CBS simulation?', answer: 'Import the CBS WSDL into SoapUI and create a mock service. Configure mock responses for each operation mapping input parameters to appropriate responses. Use Groovy scripts in mock dispatch to implement dynamic response logic like returning balance based on account number. Configure realistic response delays simulating CBS processing time. Implement stateful mocking where transaction operations update the mock internal state. For Indian banking, create comprehensive CBS mocks that simulate account inquiry, fund transfer, standing instruction processing, and loan operations. Deploy mocks to serve as a reliable test backend when CBS test environments are unavailable.', difficulty: 'Hard', category: 'Technical' },
    { id: 'IQ-SUW-006', question: 'How do you troubleshoot SoapUI test failures in banking SOAP testing?', answer: 'Enable SoapUI HTTP log to capture raw request and response XML for analysis. Check SOAP fault messages for specific error codes and descriptions. Verify WS-Security configuration including credentials, timestamps, and certificates. Test manually in SoapUI before running automated suites to isolate issues. Check CBS server logs for correlation with test failures. For Indian banking, common issues include CBS session timeout requiring re-authentication, XML namespace mismatches between different CBS versions, timestamp synchronization issues with WS-Security, and data format differences between CBS test and production instances. Maintain a troubleshooting guide specific to each CBS platform.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Practical' },
    { id: 'IQ-SUW-007', question: 'How do you manage environment configurations for SoapUI banking tests?', answer: 'Create SoapUI environments or use project properties to manage configuration for SIT, UAT, and pre-production. Store CBS endpoint URLs, credentials, certificate paths, and test data references as environment-specific properties. Switch between environments using property files loaded at runtime. For banking, each environment may have different CBS versions, different security configurations, and different test data. Maintain environment configuration files in version control. Implement environment validation test cases that verify CBS connectivity and configuration correctness before running the full test suite. Document environment-specific quirks and known issues.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Technical' },
    { id: 'IQ-SUW-008', question: 'How do you validate SOAP XML schema compliance for banking messages?', answer: 'Use SoapUI Schema Compliance assertion to validate responses against the WSDL-defined schema. Test with deliberately malformed requests to verify that the CBS properly rejects schema-invalid messages. Validate complex type structures specific to banking like nested account holder information, multi-leg transaction details, and structured address formats. For Indian banking, validate that CBS responses include all required fields as defined in the WSDL and that data types match specifications. Pay special attention to financial amount precision, date formats, and enumerated values like account type codes and transaction status codes. Report schema compliance issues as defects.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Technical' },
    { id: 'IQ-SUW-009', question: 'How do you document SoapUI test workflows for banking team knowledge sharing?', answer: 'Create detailed documentation for each SoapUI project including the project structure, test suite organization, and naming conventions. Document the WSDL operations covered and those excluded with reasons. Maintain a test data guide explaining the required test data for each workflow. Document environment setup procedures including WS-Security configuration. Create a workflow execution guide with prerequisites, execution steps, and result interpretation. For Indian banking, document CBS-specific configurations, known CBS behavior quirks, and workarounds for CBS test environment limitations. Maintain this documentation in the team wiki and update it with each CBS version change.', difficulty: 'Easy', category: 'Behavioral' }
  ],
  'sql-editor': [
    { id: 'IQ-SQE-001', question: 'How do you use SQL editors for banking database testing?', answer: 'SQL editors like DBeaver, Azure Data Studio, or pgAdmin provide interfaces for writing, executing, and analyzing SQL queries against banking databases. Use them to verify transaction data after test execution, validate database constraints and referential integrity, analyze data distributions for test data quality assessment, and investigate defects by querying the database state. For banking testing, SQL editors are essential for verifying that fund transfers correctly update account balances, that GL postings balance correctly, and that audit trail records capture complete transaction details. Always use read-only connections in production environments.', difficulty: 'Easy', category: 'Concept' },
    { id: 'IQ-SQE-002', question: 'How do you write SQL queries to validate banking transaction integrity?', answer: 'Write queries that verify double-entry bookkeeping by summing debits and credits per transaction ensuring they balance. Query for accounts where the running balance does not equal the initial balance plus sum of credits minus sum of debits. Check for transactions without corresponding GL entries. Verify that settlement records match across payer and payee accounts. For Indian banking, write queries to validate NEFT batch totals against individual transactions, verify that RTGS transactions above Rs 2 lakhs are correctly classified, and confirm that interest accrual entries match calculated expected amounts based on the daily product method.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Practical' },
    { id: 'IQ-SQE-003', question: 'What SQL queries help identify data quality issues in banking databases?', answer: 'Write queries to find NULL values in mandatory fields like customer name, account number, and KYC status. Identify duplicate records in customer and account tables. Find orphan records like transactions referencing non-existent accounts. Check for data consistency issues like accounts with negative balances without overdraft facility. Identify date anomalies like future-dated transactions or account opening dates before the customer birth date. For Indian banking, query for invalid IFSC codes, PAN numbers not matching the required format, Aadhaar numbers failing the Verhoeff checksum, and accounts missing mandatory KYC documentation status.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Technical' },
    { id: 'IQ-SQE-004', question: 'How do you use SQL to verify interest calculations in banking?', answer: 'Write queries that calculate expected interest using the applicable formula and compare against system-posted interest amounts. For savings accounts, compute daily product (balance * days) for each balance change period, sum the products, and apply the annual rate divided by 365. For FDs, calculate compound interest at the specified frequency. For loans, verify EMI allocation between principal and interest using the reducing balance method. For Indian banking, verify quarterly interest posting for savings accounts, TDS deduction on FD interest exceeding Rs 40,000, and penal interest calculation for loan overdue amounts. Flag any discrepancy exceeding one paisa.', difficulty: 'Hard', category: 'Technical' },
    { id: 'IQ-SQE-005', question: 'How do you write SQL for banking regulatory reporting validation?', answer: 'Write aggregation queries that calculate the same figures as regulatory reports and compare them against the generated reports. Verify deposit totals by category for SLR/CRR reporting. Calculate NPA amounts by classification category for RBI returns. Aggregate transaction data for PMLA CTR reporting thresholds. For Indian banking, write queries to validate CRILC reporting data for large credit exposures, Basel III capital adequacy ratios, priority sector lending percentages, and SMA (Special Mention Accounts) classification. These validation queries serve as independent checks against the automated report generation process, catching errors before regulatory submission.', difficulty: 'Hard', category: 'Scenario' },
    { id: 'IQ-SQE-006', question: 'How do you optimize SQL queries for banking database testing?', answer: 'Use EXPLAIN ANALYZE to understand query execution plans and identify performance issues. Add appropriate indexes for columns used in WHERE and JOIN clauses. Use CTEs (Common Table Expressions) for complex multi-step banking queries to improve readability. Avoid SELECT * in favor of specific columns needed. Use pagination with LIMIT and OFFSET for large result sets. For banking databases, optimize queries against the transaction table which typically has the highest volume. Use date range filtering to limit scan scope. Create covering indexes for frequently used query patterns like account balance lookup and transaction history retrieval.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Technical' },
    { id: 'IQ-SQE-007', question: 'How do you use SQL for test data creation in banking databases?', answer: 'Write INSERT scripts to create test customers, accounts, and initial transaction records. Use sequences or functions to generate valid account numbers with check digits. Create stored procedures for complex test data setup like creating a customer with multiple account types, linked beneficiaries, and transaction history. Use COPY or bulk insert for large test data volumes. For Indian banking, create scripts that generate valid IFSC codes, PAN numbers, and masked Aadhaar numbers for test customers. Maintain reusable SQL scripts for common test data patterns like creating a loan account with EMI schedule and generating a year worth of savings account transactions.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Practical' },
    { id: 'IQ-SQE-008', question: 'How do you use SQL to validate data migration in banking CBS upgrades?', answer: 'Write count comparison queries verifying row counts match between source and target for every table. Create checksum queries comparing aggregate values like total deposits, total loans, and total GL balances. Write detailed comparison queries for sample records verifying field-level accuracy. Check that referential integrity is maintained in the target system. Validate that no data truncation occurred for text fields. For Indian banking, specifically validate migration of account-level details including balance, interest accrued, dormancy status, KYC status, and linked mandates. Create a migration validation report listing all checked areas with pass/fail status and discrepancy details.', difficulty: 'Hard', category: 'Practical' },
    { id: 'IQ-SQE-009', question: 'What security precautions should you take when using SQL editors for banking databases?', answer: 'Always use read-only database connections for test validation to prevent accidental data modification. Never connect SQL editors directly to production databases without formal approval and read-only access. Use parameterized queries to avoid SQL injection even in test tools. Never store database credentials in SQL editor configurations on shared machines. For banking, implement connection naming with clear environment labels to prevent accidentally running queries against the wrong environment. Use transaction wrappers with ROLLBACK for any queries that might modify data. Log all SQL editor sessions and queries for audit trail. Follow the banks data access policy for query execution in all environments.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Behavioral' }
  ],
  'sql-master-guide': [
    { id: 'IQ-SQM-001', question: 'What SQL concepts are essential for banking database testing?', answer: 'Essential concepts include JOINs for querying across related banking tables like customer-account-transaction, aggregate functions (SUM, AVG, COUNT) for financial calculations and reconciliation, subqueries for complex banking validations like finding accounts with unusual patterns, window functions for running balances and rank-based analysis, CTEs for readable multi-step financial queries, transaction control (BEGIN, COMMIT, ROLLBACK) for understanding data consistency, and indexing concepts for performance optimization. For Indian banking, understand date functions for interest calculation periods, decimal precision handling for financial amounts, and string functions for IFSC and PAN pattern validation.', difficulty: 'Easy', category: 'Concept' },
    { id: 'IQ-SQM-002', question: 'How do you use JOIN queries for banking data validation?', answer: 'Use INNER JOIN to validate relationships between customers and accounts ensuring every account has a valid customer. Use LEFT JOIN to find orphan records like transactions without matching accounts. Use self-joins on the transaction table to find related transactions like debit-credit pairs. Join account and product tables to verify that account configurations match product definitions. For Indian banking, join customer and KYC tables to find accounts with expired KYC documentation, join account and mandate tables to verify standing instruction setup, and join transaction and GL tables to verify that every transaction has corresponding GL entries.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Technical' },
    { id: 'IQ-SQM-003', question: 'How do you use window functions for banking data analysis?', answer: 'Use ROW_NUMBER() to identify duplicate transactions based on amount, date, and account. Use SUM() OVER (ORDER BY date) to calculate running balances and verify they match stored running balance values. Use LAG() to compare consecutive transaction amounts for anomaly detection. Use RANK() OVER (PARTITION BY account ORDER BY amount DESC) to find the highest-value transactions per account for audit analysis. For Indian banking, use window functions to analyze transaction patterns for PMLA monitoring, to calculate daily average balances for interest computation verification, and to identify accounts with sudden activity changes that might indicate fraud.', difficulty: 'Hard', category: 'Technical' },
    { id: 'IQ-SQM-004', question: 'How do you write SQL for financial reconciliation in banking?', answer: 'Write queries that match records across two systems or tables using key fields like transaction reference, amount, and date. Use FULL OUTER JOIN to identify records present in one system but missing in the other. Aggregate matched and unmatched records by category for reconciliation reports. Handle tolerance-based matching where amounts may differ by small rounding differences. For Indian banking, write reconciliation queries for NEFT settlements matching outward and inward files, ATM switch reconciliation matching ATM transactions with CBS postings, card settlement reconciliation matching acquiring and issuing transactions, and inter-branch reconciliation comparing GL entries across branches.', difficulty: 'Hard', category: 'Practical' },
    { id: 'IQ-SQM-005', question: 'How do you use SQL for banking audit trail verification?', answer: 'Query audit tables to verify that all user actions on financial data are logged with complete details including user ID, timestamp, action type, table affected, and before/after values. Write queries to reconstruct the complete history of changes for a specific account or customer. Verify that audit records are immutable by checking for gaps in sequence numbers or timestamps. For Indian banking, write queries to verify that maker-checker audit trails are complete, that high-value transaction approvals are logged with the approver details, and that user access changes are audited with proper authorization evidence. Generate audit reports for RBI inspection readiness.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Practical' },
    { id: 'IQ-SQM-006', question: 'How do you use SQL aggregations for banking report validation?', answer: 'Use GROUP BY with SUM to calculate total deposits by branch, total loans by product, and total transactions by type. Use HAVING to filter aggregated results like branches with deposits exceeding a threshold. Use multiple levels of aggregation with ROLLUP or CUBE for hierarchical banking reports. Use CASE statements within aggregations to categorize data like classifying loans as performing or non-performing. For Indian banking, use aggregations to validate RBI return data including deposit concentration reports, sector-wise lending reports, NPA classification reports, and Basel III risk-weighted asset calculations.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Technical' },
    { id: 'IQ-SQM-007', question: 'How do you handle date and time operations in banking SQL?', answer: 'Use date functions to calculate interest periods between transactions. Handle business day calculations excluding weekends and banking holidays. Calculate maturity dates for fixed deposits and loan repayment schedules. Handle month-end date calculations for interest posting where February needs special handling. For Indian banking, maintain and query a bank holiday calendar table for NEFT/RTGS processing day calculations. Calculate age from date of birth for minor-to-major account conversion. Use date arithmetic for dormancy period calculation and KYC re-verification due dates. Handle financial year April to March boundaries for tax-related calculations.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Technical' },
    { id: 'IQ-SQM-008', question: 'How do you write SQL for banking performance optimization?', answer: 'Create appropriate indexes on frequently queried columns like account_number, transaction_date, and customer_id. Use composite indexes for common query patterns like (account_number, transaction_date) for statement retrieval. Analyze slow queries using EXPLAIN and optimize JOIN order and WHERE clause selectivity. Use materialized views for complex report queries that are run frequently. Partition large tables like transactions by date range for efficient querying. For Indian banking, optimize queries for the most frequent operations like balance inquiry and transaction history retrieval. Monitor and tune queries that support real-time payment processing to meet NPCI latency requirements.', difficulty: 'Hard', category: 'Technical' },
    { id: 'IQ-SQM-009', question: 'How do you use SQL for banking test data management?', answer: 'Write scripts to create comprehensive test data sets with proper relationships across banking entities. Use CTEs and temporary tables to build complex test scenarios step by step. Implement cleanup scripts that remove test data without affecting other data. Create data snapshot and restore scripts for repeatable test environments. For Indian banking, develop scripts to generate realistic transaction patterns for different account types, create test data for specific regulatory testing scenarios like PMLA threshold testing, and build comprehensive test customer profiles with multiple account types, mandates, and transaction histories. Version control all test data scripts for traceability.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Practical' }
  ],
  'swagger-api-docs': [
    { id: 'IQ-SWG-001', question: 'What is Swagger/OpenAPI and how is it used in banking API testing?', answer: 'Swagger/OpenAPI is a specification for describing REST APIs in a machine-readable format. It defines API endpoints, request/response schemas, authentication methods, and error codes. For banking, the OpenAPI spec serves as the single source of truth for API contracts between teams. Test teams use it to auto-generate test cases covering all endpoints, validate response schemas, and create API client stubs. For Indian banking, OpenAPI specs document account management APIs, payment processing endpoints, and open banking APIs. The spec enables consistent API documentation across internet banking, mobile banking, and third-party partner integrations.', difficulty: 'Easy', category: 'Concept' },
    { id: 'IQ-SWG-002', question: 'How do you use Swagger UI for exploratory testing of banking APIs?', answer: 'Use Swagger UI interactive documentation to explore API endpoints without writing code. Test each banking endpoint by providing parameters and executing requests directly. Verify response formats and status codes match the specification. Test authentication flows by configuring API keys or OAuth tokens in the Swagger UI authorize dialog. For Indian banking, use Swagger UI to explore account inquiry APIs, fund transfer endpoints, and beneficiary management APIs. This is particularly useful during development when the API is evolving and formal test scripts have not yet been written. Share Swagger UI URL with business analysts for API behavior validation.', difficulty: 'Easy', category: 'Practical' },
    { id: 'IQ-SWG-003', question: 'How do you generate test cases from OpenAPI specifications for banking APIs?', answer: 'Parse the OpenAPI spec to identify all endpoints, HTTP methods, required and optional parameters, and response codes. Generate positive test cases for each endpoint with valid parameters. Generate negative test cases with missing required parameters, invalid data types, and out-of-range values. Generate boundary test cases using min/max values defined in the schema. Create schema validation tests that verify actual responses match defined schemas. For Indian banking, generate tests for all account types defined in the spec, all payment methods listed in the payment API, and all error codes documented for transaction failure scenarios.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Technical' },
    { id: 'IQ-SWG-004', question: 'How do you validate that a banking API implementation matches its OpenAPI specification?', answer: 'Use tools like Prism, Dredd, or custom scripts to validate API responses against the OpenAPI schema. Test that required fields are present in all responses. Verify data types match for example ensuring amounts are numbers not strings. Validate enumeration values like transaction status are within defined allowed values. Test that error responses follow the documented error schema. For Indian banking, verify that account balance API returns amount with correct decimal precision, that IFSC code field matches the documented regex pattern, and that paginated endpoints respect the documented limit parameter constraints. Run specification compliance tests as part of the CI/CD pipeline.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Technical' },
    { id: 'IQ-SWG-005', question: 'How do you use OpenAPI for API versioning in banking?', answer: 'Maintain separate OpenAPI specifications for each API version. Document breaking changes between versions including removed endpoints, changed data types, and new required fields. Use spec comparison tools to automatically detect breaking changes when the spec is updated. For banking, version management is critical because multiple consumers like mobile apps, internet banking, and fintech partners may be on different versions. Document deprecation notices in the spec with migration guidance. Use spec versioning to generate version-specific test suites ensuring backward compatibility. Track which consumers are on which version to plan deprecation timelines.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Concept' },
    { id: 'IQ-SWG-006', question: 'How do you use OpenAPI specs for mock server generation in banking testing?', answer: 'Use tools like Prism, WireMock, or MockServer to generate mock APIs from OpenAPI specifications. Configure dynamic mock responses using example values defined in the spec. Implement conditional mocking where different request parameters return different responses. For banking testing, generate mock payment gateway APIs, mock CBS REST endpoints, and mock third-party service APIs. This enables frontend and mobile teams to develop and test against realistic API responses before the backend is complete. For Indian banking, create mocks for NPCI APIs, credit bureau APIs, and Aadhaar verification APIs using their published specifications.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Practical' },
    { id: 'IQ-SWG-007', question: 'How do you document banking API security in OpenAPI specifications?', answer: 'Define security schemes in the OpenAPI spec including OAuth 2.0 flows for user authentication, API key authentication for server-to-server calls, and bearer token for session management. Specify which security scheme applies to each endpoint. Document required scopes for OAuth tokens specifying what operations each scope allows. For Indian banking, document the additional factor authentication requirements for transaction APIs, the API key restrictions for open banking partner APIs, and the certificate-based mTLS requirements for NPCI integration APIs. Clear security documentation enables test teams to configure proper authentication for automated testing.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Technical' },
    { id: 'IQ-SWG-008', question: 'How do you maintain OpenAPI specifications for evolving banking APIs?', answer: 'Store OpenAPI specs in version control alongside the API code. Implement spec-first development where the spec is updated before code changes. Use linting tools like Spectral to enforce spec quality standards. Configure CI/CD checks that fail if the API implementation drifts from the spec. For banking, establish a governance process where spec changes require review from API consumers before implementation. Document all changes in a changelog within the spec file. For Indian banking, coordinate spec updates across teams when shared banking domain objects like account or transaction schemas change. Maintain backward compatibility rules in the spec review process.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Practical' },
    { id: 'IQ-SWG-009', question: 'How do you use OpenAPI for API testing automation in banking CI/CD?', answer: 'Integrate OpenAPI-based test generation into the CI/CD pipeline. Use tools like Schemathesis or RESTler to automatically generate and run fuzz tests from the spec. Validate all API responses against the spec schema as part of every build. Generate API client SDKs from the spec for type-safe test automation. For banking, configure different test profiles where commit builds run schema validation tests, integration builds run generated positive and negative tests, and nightly builds run comprehensive fuzz testing. Track spec coverage metrics showing what percentage of documented endpoints have automated tests. Alert on any spec changes that are not accompanied by test updates.', difficulty: 'Hard', category: 'Technical' }
  ],
  'system-health-lab': [
    { id: 'IQ-SHL-001', question: 'What does system health monitoring cover in banking applications?', answer: 'System health monitoring covers infrastructure health including server CPU, memory, disk, and network metrics, application health including response times, error rates, and throughput, database health including connection pool usage, query performance, and replication lag, external dependency health including CBS availability, NPCI connectivity, and payment gateway status, and security health including failed authentication attempts, certificate expiry, and intrusion detection alerts. For Indian banking, system health must cover all components critical to maintaining banking operations across all channels from branch banking to digital platforms.', difficulty: 'Easy', category: 'Concept' },
    { id: 'IQ-SHL-002', question: 'How do you test system health monitoring for banking applications?', answer: 'Verify that all health metrics are correctly collected by comparing displayed values against actual system state. Test alert thresholds by deliberately approaching and exceeding limits. Verify that health dashboards update in real-time without significant lag. Test that health check endpoints return accurate status for each dependency. For Indian banking, test monitoring for CBS connectivity health, NPCI switch availability, payment gateway responsiveness, and batch processing status. Simulate various failure scenarios and verify that the monitoring system detects and alerts appropriately. Test that monitoring data retention meets audit requirements for historical analysis.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Practical' },
    { id: 'IQ-SHL-003', question: 'How do you design health checks for banking system dependencies?', answer: 'Create hierarchical health checks from shallow connectivity tests to deep functional validation. Shallow checks verify TCP connectivity and TLS handshake. Medium checks verify authentication and basic API response. Deep checks execute a lightweight test transaction to verify end-to-end functionality. For banking, design dependency-specific health checks including CBS health via a lightweight account inquiry call, NPCI health via connectivity check to the switch, database health via a simple query with timing measurement, and notification service health via a test message delivery. Configure appropriate check intervals and timeout values for each dependency type.', difficulty: 'Hard', category: 'Technical' },
    { id: 'IQ-SHL-004', question: 'How do you test capacity planning and resource monitoring for banking systems?', answer: 'Monitor resource usage trends over time to project when capacity limits will be reached. Test that capacity alerts trigger when usage exceeds 80% threshold. Verify that auto-scaling provisions additional resources before capacity is exhausted. Test resource monitoring accuracy by generating known workloads and comparing measured utilization against expected values. For Indian banking, plan capacity for projected growth in UPI transaction volumes, seasonal peaks during festivals, and year-end processing loads. Test that monitoring captures resource usage patterns that inform procurement decisions for CBS infrastructure expansion.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Practical' },
    { id: 'IQ-SHL-005', question: 'How do you test incident management integration with health monitoring?', answer: 'Verify that health monitoring alerts automatically create incidents in the incident management system like ServiceNow or PagerDuty. Test that incident severity is correctly mapped from alert severity. Verify that incident routing assigns the correct team based on the affected banking component. Test escalation rules when incidents are not acknowledged within the defined timeframe. Validate that incident resolution automatically clears the monitoring alert. For Indian banking, test that critical banking system incidents follow the RBI-mandated incident response procedures and that CERT-In notification requirements are met for security incidents.', difficulty: 'Hard', category: 'Scenario' },
    { id: 'IQ-SHL-006', question: 'How do you test synthetic monitoring for banking applications?', answer: 'Synthetic monitoring uses automated scripts to simulate real user transactions at regular intervals. Verify that synthetic transactions execute successfully and measure response times accurately. Test that synthetic monitoring detects outages before real users are impacted. Validate that synthetic test results correlate with real user experience metrics. For Indian banking, implement synthetic monitors for critical banking journeys including login to balance check, login to fund transfer, and UPI payment processing. Configure synthetic monitors from multiple geographic locations reflecting the banks customer base distribution. Set appropriate alert thresholds based on SLA requirements.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Technical' },
    { id: 'IQ-SHL-007', question: 'How do you test system health during banking maintenance windows?', answer: 'Verify that planned maintenance activities are properly reflected in the monitoring system with suppressed alerts. Test that the monitoring system correctly tracks the maintenance window schedule. Verify that health checks resume normal operation after the maintenance window closes. Test that any issues occurring outside the planned window are properly alerted. For Indian banking, test monitoring behavior during CBS maintenance windows, NPCI scheduled downtime periods, and database maintenance activities. Verify that customer-facing channels display appropriate maintenance messages during planned outages and that the monitoring system tracks the maintenance progress.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Practical' },
    { id: 'IQ-SHL-008', question: 'How do you test log-based health monitoring for banking compliance?', answer: 'Verify that security-relevant logs are monitored in real-time for suspicious patterns. Test that failed authentication attempts exceeding thresholds trigger security alerts. Validate that unauthorized data access attempts are detected from audit logs. Verify that configuration change logs are monitored for unauthorized modifications. For Indian banking, test that PMLA-related transaction monitoring generates appropriate alerts, that suspicious login patterns trigger security review workflows, and that privileged access outside business hours is flagged. Verify that log-based monitoring meets RBI cybersecurity framework requirements for continuous monitoring.', difficulty: 'Hard', category: 'Scenario' },
    { id: 'IQ-SHL-009', question: 'How do you test disaster recovery readiness through health monitoring?', answer: 'Verify that health monitoring covers both primary and DR sites. Test that failover detection triggers appropriate alerts and DR activation procedures. Monitor data replication lag between primary and DR to ensure RPO compliance. Test that DR site health checks verify complete banking functionality readiness. For Indian banking, verify that monitoring tracks RTO and RPO metrics continuously, that DR drill results are captured by the monitoring system, and that the monitoring platform itself has DR capability ensuring visibility during primary site failures. Test that communication channels for DR notification like SMS and email alerts function correctly during simulated DR events.', difficulty: 'Hard', category: 'Technical' }
  ],
  'tech-stack': [
    { id: 'IQ-TSK-001', question: 'What technology stacks are commonly used in Indian banking applications?', answer: 'Indian banks use diverse technology stacks. CBS platforms include Oracle Flexcube, Infosys Finacle, TCS BaNCS, and FIS Profile. Application servers include IBM WebSphere, Oracle WebLogic, and Apache Tomcat. Databases include Oracle DB, IBM DB2, Microsoft SQL Server, and increasingly PostgreSQL. Modern digital banking layers use Java Spring Boot, Node.js, Python FastAPI, and React/Angular for frontend. Mobile apps use native Android/iOS or React Native/Flutter. Cloud platforms include AWS, Azure, and private cloud deployments. For testing, understanding the tech stack is crucial for selecting appropriate testing tools and designing effective test strategies.', difficulty: 'Easy', category: 'Concept' },
    { id: 'IQ-TSK-002', question: 'How does the technology stack affect testing strategy for banking applications?', answer: 'The tech stack determines available testing tools and approaches. Java-based CBS requires JUnit/TestNG for unit testing and RestAssured for API testing. SOAP-based CBS interfaces need SoapUI or zeep. Oracle databases require SQL*Plus or TOAD for data validation. React frontends need React Testing Library and Cypress. Mobile apps need Appium for cross-platform testing. Microservices architectures need contract testing with Pact and distributed tracing for debugging. For Indian banking, the mix of legacy CBS on mainframes with modern digital layers requires testers proficient in both traditional and modern testing approaches.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Technical' },
    { id: 'IQ-TSK-003', question: 'What testing tools are essential for each layer of the banking tech stack?', answer: 'Frontend layer needs Cypress or Playwright for E2E testing, Jest for unit testing, and Storybook for component testing. API layer needs Postman for manual testing, RestAssured or pytest for automation, and Pact for contract testing. Backend needs JUnit or pytest for unit testing, Mockito or unittest.mock for mocking, and SoapUI for SOAP testing. Database needs SQL tools for data validation and DbUnit for test data management. Infrastructure needs Terraform testing tools, Docker for containerized testing, and k6 or JMeter for performance testing. Security needs OWASP ZAP for DAST and Bandit or SonarQube for SAST.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Technical' },
    { id: 'IQ-TSK-004', question: 'How do you select the right automation framework for a banking project?', answer: 'Consider factors including the application tech stack compatibility, team skill set, CI/CD integration capabilities, reporting needs, and maintenance effort. For Java-based banking backends, choose TestNG or JUnit with RestAssured. For Python backends, use pytest with requests. For UI testing of banking portals, evaluate Cypress for fast feedback or Playwright for cross-browser needs. For mobile banking, Appium provides cross-platform capability. Consider BDD frameworks like Cucumber if business stakeholders will review test scenarios. For Indian banking, ensure the framework supports SOAP testing for CBS integration and can handle complex authentication flows.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Practical' },
    { id: 'IQ-TSK-005', question: 'How do you handle testing across different technology layers in a banking application?', answer: 'Implement the test pyramid approach with unit tests at each layer testing isolated components. Create integration tests at layer boundaries like API to database and API to CBS. Design end-to-end tests that traverse all layers from UI through API to database and external systems. For banking, test the complete transaction flow from the React frontend through the Spring Boot API layer, through the CBS SOAP adapter, to the Oracle database. Use contract testing between layers to enable independent testing. For Indian banking, the challenge is testing across modern digital layers and legacy CBS systems with different technology paradigms.', difficulty: 'Hard', category: 'Scenario' },
    { id: 'IQ-TSK-006', question: 'What DevOps tools are used in banking technology environments?', answer: 'Version control with Git and GitHub/GitLab/Bitbucket. CI/CD with Jenkins, GitHub Actions, or Azure DevOps. Container orchestration with Kubernetes or OpenShift. Configuration management with Ansible or Terraform. Monitoring with Prometheus, Grafana, and ELK stack. Artifact management with JFrog Artifactory or Nexus. For Indian banking, tool selection is influenced by regulatory requirements for on-premises deployment, security certifications, and vendor support availability in India. Many banks use a combination of enterprise tools for production and open-source tools for development and testing environments to balance cost and capability.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Concept' },
    { id: 'IQ-TSK-007', question: 'How do you evaluate new technologies for banking test automation?', answer: 'Evaluate based on compatibility with existing banking tech stack, learning curve for the team, community support and documentation quality, enterprise support availability, security compliance for handling banking data, CI/CD integration capability, and total cost of ownership. Conduct a proof of concept with a representative banking test scenario to validate effectiveness. For Indian banking, also consider vendor presence in India for support, compliance with RBI IT governance requirements, and ability to integrate with existing banking tools. Compare against current tools to quantify improvement in test execution time, maintainability, and coverage.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Behavioral' },
    { id: 'IQ-SQM-008', question: 'How do cloud technologies impact banking testing strategies?', answer: 'Cloud technologies enable on-demand test environment provisioning reducing wait times for banking test environments. Cloud device farms provide broad mobile device coverage for banking app testing. Cloud-based load testing generates realistic geographic distribution of banking traffic. However, cloud adoption in banking faces challenges including data residency requirements where RBI mandates that banking data must be stored in India, security concerns about test data in cloud environments, and network latency between cloud test environments and on-premises CBS systems. For Indian banking, many banks use a hybrid approach with cloud for non-sensitive testing and on-premises for tests involving customer data.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Concept' },
    { id: 'IQ-TSK-009', question: 'How do you stay current with evolving banking technology stacks?', answer: 'Follow banking technology publications and RBI technology guidelines. Participate in banking technology conferences and webinars. Engage with vendor communities for CBS platforms like Finacle and Flexcube. Experiment with new testing tools in sandbox environments. Take online courses on emerging technologies relevant to banking like AI/ML testing, cloud-native testing, and API security testing. Join professional communities focused on banking QA. For Indian banking, follow NPCI technical updates for payment technology changes, RBI digital banking guidelines for technology requirements, and IDRBT (Institute for Development and Research in Banking Technology) publications for industry research.', difficulty: 'Easy', category: 'Behavioral' }
  ],
  'test-cases': [
    { id: 'IQ-TCE-001', question: 'What are the key elements of a well-written test case for banking applications?', answer: 'A banking test case should include a unique ID with module prefix, a clear title describing the test objective, preconditions specifying account state, balance, and user role, detailed test steps with specific data values like account numbers and amounts, expected results for each step not just the final outcome, priority and severity rating, the banking module and feature being tested, and traceability to the requirement or RBI guideline. For Indian banking, include the applicable transaction type, regulatory reference if testing compliance, and test data requirements including valid IFSC codes and account number formats.', difficulty: 'Easy', category: 'Concept' },
    { id: 'IQ-TCE-002', question: 'How do you design test cases for banking fund transfer functionality?', answer: 'Cover positive scenarios including successful NEFT, RTGS, IMPS, and UPI transfers with various amounts. Design negative scenarios including insufficient balance, invalid IFSC code, non-existent beneficiary account, and exceeded daily limit. Include boundary value tests at minimum and maximum transfer amounts for each payment type. Add security test cases for unauthorized transfer attempts. Include performance test cases for transfer under peak load. For Indian banking, test RTGS minimum of Rs 2 lakhs, IMPS maximum of Rs 5 lakhs, NEFT batch processing, and UPI VPA validation. Include test cases for transfer scheduling, beneficiary management, and transaction confirmation workflows.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Practical' },
    { id: 'IQ-TCE-003', question: 'How do you write test cases for banking interest calculation?', answer: 'Test daily interest calculation for savings accounts using the daily product method. Verify interest for complete months, partial months, and accounts opened mid-month. Test compound interest for FDs with different compounding frequencies. Verify EMI calculation using the reducing balance method for loan accounts. Test interest calculation during rate changes mid-period. Include edge cases like leap year calculations, month-end processing on 28th, 29th, 30th, and 31st, and accounts with zero balance periods. For Indian banking, test TDS deduction on FD interest exceeding Rs 40,000, quarterly interest crediting for savings accounts, and penal interest for loan overdue.', difficulty: 'Hard', category: 'Technical' },
    { id: 'IQ-TCE-004', question: 'How do you derive test cases using equivalence partitioning for banking?', answer: 'Divide input values into groups that should be treated equivalently by the system. For a transfer amount field, partitions might include zero (invalid), Re 1 to Rs 2 lakhs (NEFT eligible), Rs 2 lakhs and above (RTGS eligible), negative values (invalid), and amounts exceeding daily limit (rejected). Test one value from each partition rather than all possible values. For Indian banking, apply equivalence partitioning to account types, transaction types, customer categories, and amount ranges. This technique reduces test case count while maintaining coverage across all meaningful input categories.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Concept' },
    { id: 'IQ-TCE-005', question: 'How do you design test cases for banking security features?', answer: 'Test login with valid credentials, invalid password, locked account, and expired password. Design test cases for OTP validation including valid OTP, expired OTP, wrong OTP, and maximum retry exceeded. Test session management including idle timeout, concurrent session prevention, and session invalidation on logout. Design test cases for RBAC verifying each user role can only access permitted functions. Test encryption by verifying sensitive data is encrypted in database and API responses. For Indian banking, include test cases for Aadhaar-based authentication, UPI PIN validation, device binding verification, and RBI-mandated additional factor authentication for online transactions.', difficulty: 'Hard', category: 'Practical' },
    { id: 'IQ-TCE-006', question: 'How do you organize test cases for a banking regression test suite?', answer: 'Organize by banking module with folders for deposits, loans, payments, cards, and customer management. Within each module, categorize by feature like account opening, fund transfer, and statement generation. Tag test cases with priority (P0, P1, P2), test type (functional, integration, security), and automation status. Create test case groupings for different execution scenarios like smoke, sanity, and full regression. For Indian banking, create specific groupings for regulatory compliance tests tagged with the relevant RBI guideline, payment channel tests grouped by payment type, and cross-module integration tests. Maintain a test case inventory with coverage metrics per module.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Practical' },
    { id: 'IQ-TCE-007', question: 'How do you review and maintain banking test cases over time?', answer: 'Conduct quarterly test case reviews with banking domain experts to ensure relevance and accuracy. Remove obsolete test cases for deprecated features. Update test cases when banking regulations change like new RBI guidelines on transaction limits. Add new test cases for each new feature or defect fix. Review test case effectiveness by analyzing which tests catch the most defects. For Indian banking, trigger test case reviews whenever new RBI circulars are issued, when CBS versions are upgraded, or when new payment channels are launched. Maintain version history for test cases showing what changed and why. Track test case reuse across projects to identify candidates for the shared test case library.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Behavioral' },
    { id: 'IQ-TCE-008', question: 'How do you design test cases for banking edge cases and error scenarios?', answer: 'Identify edge cases from boundary analysis, production defect history, and domain expertise. Test with zero amounts, maximum field lengths, special characters in name fields, and simultaneous transactions. Design error scenarios for network failures during transactions, CBS timeout during processing, and database constraint violations. Test with corrupted input data like invalid XML in SOAP requests and malformed JSON in REST calls. For Indian banking, test edge cases like transactions during NEFT window transitions, fund transfers on bank merger transition dates, and interest calculation for accounts opened and closed within the same interest period.', difficulty: 'Hard', category: 'Scenario' },
    { id: 'IQ-TCE-009', question: 'How do you estimate test case count for a banking project?', answer: 'Use requirement decomposition to identify testable requirements. Apply test design techniques like equivalence partitioning and boundary analysis to determine the minimum test cases per requirement. Consider positive, negative, and boundary test cases for each input field. Factor in integration test cases for each system interface. Add security and performance test cases. For Indian banking, estimate separately for each banking module considering the number of transaction types, account types, and user roles. Use historical data from similar banking projects to calibrate estimates. Include buffer for regulatory test cases that may be added when new RBI circulars are issued. Present estimates with confidence ranges.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Concept' }
  ],
  'test-execution': [
    { id: 'IQ-TXE-001', question: 'What is the test execution process for banking applications?', answer: 'Test execution follows a structured process starting with entry criteria verification including environment readiness, test data availability, and build deployment confirmation. Execution proceeds through smoke testing to verify basic functionality, followed by planned test case execution prioritized by risk. Defects are logged immediately with complete banking context. Daily execution status is reported to stakeholders. Exit criteria include achieving planned coverage, resolving critical defects, and obtaining sign-offs. For Indian banking, the execution process includes specific checkpoints for regulatory compliance testing, CBS integration verification, and payment channel validation.', difficulty: 'Easy', category: 'Concept' },
    { id: 'IQ-TXE-002', question: 'How do you manage test execution in parallel across banking modules?', answer: 'Assign dedicated testers to each banking module like payments, deposits, and loans. Coordinate shared test data and environment access through a booking system. Use a test management tool to track execution progress across all modules simultaneously. Implement a daily sync meeting to identify cross-module dependencies and blockers. For Indian banking, coordinate CBS access since multiple teams testing different modules share the same CBS instance. Partition test data by module to prevent conflicts. Establish priority rules for shared environment access during peak testing periods. Use automated smoke tests to verify environment stability before each testing session.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Practical' },
    { id: 'IQ-TXE-003', question: 'How do you handle test execution blockers in banking projects?', answer: 'Identify and categorize blockers by type including environment issues, test data problems, CBS unavailability, and defects blocking test paths. Implement an escalation matrix with defined timelines for each blocker type. Switch to testing unblocked areas while blockers are being resolved. Maintain a blocked test queue that is prioritized for execution once blockers are cleared. For Indian banking, common blockers include CBS test environment downtime, NPCI sandbox unavailability, and test data refresh delays. Mitigate by implementing service virtualization for external dependencies, maintaining pre-provisioned test data pools, and having fallback test plans for environment outages.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Scenario' },
    { id: 'IQ-TXE-004', question: 'How do you ensure test execution quality in banking projects?', answer: 'Implement peer review of test results where another tester validates a sample of executed test cases. Verify that test evidence including screenshots, API responses, and database queries is captured for each test case. Review defect reports for completeness and accuracy before submission. Monitor test execution metrics like test cases executed per day, pass rate, and defect detection rate. For banking, ensure that financial calculation verification includes independent calculation comparison. Verify that regulatory compliance test evidence meets audit requirements. Conduct spot checks on passed test cases to confirm thorough execution rather than superficial checking.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Behavioral' },
    { id: 'IQ-TXE-005', question: 'How do you manage test execution for banking UAT?', answer: 'Set up a user-friendly test execution environment for banking business users who may not be familiar with formal testing tools. Provide clear test scripts written in banking business language. Assign QA team members as support for business users during UAT execution. Create a simplified defect reporting mechanism for business users. Track UAT execution progress separately from technical testing. For Indian banking, involve operations staff from different banking departments including retail, corporate, and treasury for comprehensive UAT coverage. Schedule UAT execution around banking business hours when domain experts are available. Capture business user sign-off for each tested banking module.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Practical' },
    { id: 'IQ-TXE-006', question: 'How do you handle re-execution of failed tests in banking projects?', answer: 'Categorize failed tests by root cause including genuine defects, environment issues, test data problems, and test script errors. Re-execute tests that failed due to non-defect reasons after the issue is resolved. For genuine defects, re-execute the test case after the fix is deployed in the test environment. Track re-execution cycles and measure the number of test executions needed to achieve a stable pass. For banking, prioritize re-execution of critical payment and financial calculation tests. Implement automated re-execution for tests that failed due to transient environment issues. Report re-execution metrics to identify systemic issues requiring structural solutions.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Practical' },
    { id: 'IQ-TXE-007', question: 'How do you manage test execution across multiple banking environments?', answer: 'Define which test types execute in each environment. Unit and component tests run in development, integration tests in SIT, business scenarios in UAT, and performance tests in pre-production. Manage environment-specific configurations for test data, URLs, and credentials. Track test execution status per environment independently. Implement promotion criteria defining what tests must pass before code moves to the next environment. For Indian banking, manage CBS environment versions since SIT may have a different CBS version than UAT. Coordinate NPCI sandbox access across environments. Ensure that environment-specific test data is refreshed according to the testing schedule.', difficulty: 'Hard', category: 'Technical' },
    { id: 'IQ-TXE-008', question: 'How do you use test execution data for release decisions in banking?', answer: 'Aggregate test execution data into a release readiness dashboard showing overall pass rate, critical defect status, module-wise execution progress, and compliance test coverage. Define clear go/no-go criteria like all P0 tests must pass, zero critical defects open, and regulatory tests 100% complete. Present execution data to the Change Advisory Board for formal release approval. For Indian banking, include specific release criteria for NPCI certification tests, RBI compliance validation, and CBS regression completeness. Document the release decision with the supporting test execution data as evidence for audit purposes. Include risk assessment for any exceptions to the standard release criteria.', difficulty: 'Hard', category: 'Behavioral' },
    { id: 'IQ-TXE-009', question: 'How do you handle test execution during banking production incidents?', answer: 'When a production incident requires an emergency fix, implement a fast-track test execution process. Execute a focused regression suite covering the affected banking module and its integration points. Perform targeted testing of the fix with the specific scenario that caused the incident. Execute a smoke test suite covering all critical banking transactions. For Indian banking, ensure that emergency test execution covers payment processing functionality since it is the most customer-impacting. Document the abbreviated test execution with justification for the reduced scope. Schedule a full regression test execution in the next available window to verify no side effects from the emergency fix.', difficulty: 'Hard', category: 'Scenario' }
  ],
  'testing-checklist': [
    { id: 'IQ-TCL-001', question: 'What should a comprehensive banking testing checklist include?', answer: 'A banking testing checklist should cover functional verification for all transaction types, data integrity checks for financial calculations, security validation for authentication and authorization, performance verification against SLAs, compliance checks for RBI and NPCI requirements, UI/UX validation for customer-facing screens, integration verification for CBS, payment gateways, and external services, accessibility compliance for WCAG standards, cross-browser and device compatibility, and documentation completeness. For Indian banking, add specific checklist items for NEFT/RTGS processing validation, UPI integration verification, KYC workflow validation, and PMLA monitoring verification.', difficulty: 'Easy', category: 'Concept' },
    { id: 'IQ-TCL-002', question: 'How do you create a pre-deployment testing checklist for banking releases?', answer: 'Include smoke test completion for all banking modules, regression test pass rate above threshold, zero open critical and high severity defects, security scan completion with no critical findings, performance test results within SLA, database migration script validation, configuration change verification, rollback procedure testing, monitoring and alerting verification, and stakeholder sign-off. For Indian banking, add CBS integration validation, NPCI connectivity verification, regulatory compliance test completion, and DR readiness check. This checklist serves as the final quality gate before production deployment and is reviewed by the Change Advisory Board.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Practical' },
    { id: 'IQ-TCL-003', question: 'What items should be on a banking API testing checklist?', answer: 'Include endpoint accessibility and authentication verification, request schema validation for required fields, response schema validation against OpenAPI spec, HTTP status code correctness for success and error cases, error message clarity without sensitive data exposure, pagination support for list endpoints, rate limiting enforcement, idempotency for write operations, timeout and retry behavior, backward compatibility with previous versions, and API documentation accuracy. For Indian banking, add IFSC code validation, account number format verification, transaction amount precision handling, and NPCI response code mapping verification.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Technical' },
    { id: 'IQ-TCL-004', question: 'How do you create a security testing checklist for banking applications?', answer: 'Include OWASP Top 10 vulnerability checks, authentication mechanism validation, session management security, input validation across all entry points, encryption at rest and in transit verification, access control testing for all user roles, certificate management and TLS configuration, sensitive data handling and masking, security header presence and configuration, and third-party dependency vulnerability scanning. For Indian banking, add UPI PIN security, Aadhaar data handling compliance, PCI-DSS checklist for card processing, RBI cybersecurity framework compliance, and NPCI security standard adherence.', difficulty: 'Hard', category: 'Practical' },
    { id: 'IQ-TCL-005', question: 'What should a banking database testing checklist contain?', answer: 'Include referential integrity verification across all table relationships, data type and constraint validation, index presence on query columns, stored procedure and trigger correctness, backup and recovery procedure testing, data migration accuracy for schema changes, query performance for critical banking queries, encryption verification for sensitive columns, audit trail completeness, and data retention policy compliance. For Indian banking, add GL balancing verification, interest calculation accuracy, NPA classification correctness, PMLA threshold monitoring data accuracy, and CKYC record completeness.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Technical' },
    { id: 'IQ-TCL-006', question: 'How do you create a UAT testing checklist for banking business users?', answer: 'Create a business-friendly checklist with items phrased in banking operational terms. Include account opening workflow completion, customer search and verification, fund transfer for each payment type, balance inquiry accuracy, statement generation and download, loan application processing, fixed deposit creation and maturity, standing instruction setup and execution, and customer complaint registration. For Indian banking, add UPI registration and payment testing, NEFT and RTGS processing verification, cheque book request workflow, and net banking registration. Keep technical details minimal and focus on business outcome verification.', difficulty: 'Easy', category: 'Practical' },
    { id: 'IQ-TCL-007', question: 'What should an accessibility testing checklist include for banking applications?', answer: 'Include keyboard navigation for all banking workflows, screen reader compatibility for financial data displays, color contrast ratios meeting WCAG AA standards, form labels and error messages properly associated with fields, focus management for modal dialogs and dynamic content, alternative text for all informational images, time limit accommodations for OTP and session timeout, language declaration in HTML for multi-language support, heading hierarchy for document structure, and ARIA labels for custom components. For Indian banking, add accessibility testing for PDF statements, mobile banking app accessibility, and multi-language interface accessibility.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Practical' },
    { id: 'IQ-TCL-008', question: 'How do you create a performance testing checklist for banking applications?', answer: 'Include response time validation for critical banking APIs, throughput verification at expected peak load, stress test to identify breaking point, endurance test for long-running sessions, database query performance under load, concurrent user capacity verification, resource utilization monitoring, batch processing duration validation, network bandwidth adequacy, and CDN performance for static assets. For Indian banking, add UPI transaction response time against NPCI SLA, NEFT batch processing throughput, CBS integration performance under concurrent load, and mobile banking app performance on budget devices.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Technical' },
    { id: 'IQ-TCL-009', question: 'How do you maintain and update banking testing checklists over time?', answer: 'Review checklists after every release incorporating lessons learned from defects found in production. Update when new banking regulations are issued by RBI or NPCI. Add items for new technologies adopted by the banking platform. Remove items for deprecated features. Conduct annual comprehensive review with input from development, operations, compliance, and security teams. For Indian banking, trigger checklist updates when new RBI circulars are issued, when NPCI updates payment specifications, when the bank launches new products or channels, and when security incidents reveal testing gaps. Version control checklists and maintain change history for audit purposes.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Behavioral' }
  ],
  'testing-types': [
    { id: 'IQ-TST-001', question: 'What are the essential testing types for banking applications?', answer: 'Essential types include functional testing verifying business logic correctness, integration testing validating system interactions, regression testing ensuring stability after changes, performance testing measuring response times and throughput, security testing identifying vulnerabilities, compliance testing verifying regulatory adherence, usability testing evaluating user experience, accessibility testing ensuring inclusive access, data testing validating data integrity, and acceptance testing confirming business requirements. For Indian banking, add specific types like payment gateway testing, CBS integration testing, and regulatory compliance testing for RBI guidelines.', difficulty: 'Easy', category: 'Concept' },
    { id: 'IQ-TST-002', question: 'How do you decide which testing types to apply for a banking feature?', answer: 'Assess the feature risk profile considering financial impact, customer visibility, regulatory implications, and technical complexity. A new payment feature requires functional, integration, security, performance, and compliance testing. A UI redesign needs functional, usability, accessibility, cross-browser, and regression testing. A database optimization needs performance, data integrity, and regression testing. For Indian banking, any feature touching financial transactions requires security testing and compliance testing by default. Features involving customer data need data privacy testing. Features integrating with NPCI need certification testing. Use a risk matrix to formalize testing type selection decisions.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Practical' },
    { id: 'IQ-TST-003', question: 'What is the difference between smoke testing and sanity testing in banking?', answer: 'Smoke testing is a broad shallow test verifying that the most critical banking functions work after a new build. It covers login, balance inquiry, basic fund transfer, and account management to determine if the build is stable enough for further testing. Sanity testing is a narrow deep test verifying that a specific fix or feature works correctly. After fixing an interest calculation bug, sanity testing deeply validates interest computation across multiple account types and scenarios. For Indian banking, smoke tests verify all payment channels are functional while sanity tests might deeply verify that a specific NEFT batch processing fix resolves the identified issue.', difficulty: 'Easy', category: 'Concept' },
    { id: 'IQ-TST-004', question: 'How do you perform integration testing for banking systems?', answer: 'Test the interaction between banking application components and external systems. Verify API integration between the web/mobile layer and backend services. Test CBS integration verifying correct request/response handling for account operations. Test payment gateway integration for NEFT, RTGS, UPI, and IMPS processing. Validate notification service integration for SMS and email alerts. Test credit bureau integration for loan processing. For Indian banking, integration testing is critical because the banking ecosystem involves multiple external parties including NPCI for payments, UIDAI for Aadhaar verification, CERSAI for charge registration, and credit bureaus for credit scoring. Each integration point needs thorough testing.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Technical' },
    { id: 'IQ-TST-005', question: 'What is exploratory testing and how do you apply it in banking?', answer: 'Exploratory testing combines test design and execution simultaneously where the tester uses domain knowledge and intuition to discover defects not covered by scripted tests. In banking, exploratory testing is valuable for testing complex workflows with many decision paths, discovering edge cases in financial calculations, testing new features where requirements may be incomplete, and investigating areas with a history of defects. For Indian banking, explore UPI payment flows with unusual VPA formats, test CBS interactions with edge case account states, and investigate multi-channel scenarios like starting a transaction on mobile and checking status on internet banking.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Practical' },
    { id: 'IQ-TST-006', question: 'How do you perform end-to-end testing for banking applications?', answer: 'Design tests that traverse the complete banking workflow from user action through all system layers to the final outcome. For a fund transfer, test from login on the web portal through beneficiary selection, amount entry, OTP verification, transaction processing via CBS, NEFT submission to clearing house, beneficiary credit confirmation, and notification delivery. Verify the complete data flow by checking database state at each stage. Test cross-channel scenarios where actions on one channel reflect on another. For Indian banking, create E2E tests for complete loan origination, full account lifecycle, and payment processing across all channels.', difficulty: 'Hard', category: 'Scenario' },
    { id: 'IQ-TST-007', question: 'What is the role of negative testing in banking applications?', answer: 'Negative testing verifies that the banking application handles invalid inputs, unauthorized access, and error conditions correctly. Test with SQL injection in account number fields, XSS in beneficiary name fields, negative transfer amounts, amounts exceeding limits, and expired authentication tokens. Verify that error messages are informative without exposing internal details. Test that the application maintains data integrity even when processing invalid requests. For Indian banking, negative tests include transfers with invalid IFSC codes, UPI payments to non-existent VPAs, loan applications with incomplete KYC, and accessing accounts without proper authentication.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Practical' },
    { id: 'IQ-TST-008', question: 'How do you perform compatibility testing for banking applications?', answer: 'Test across different browsers (Chrome, Firefox, Safari, Edge) and versions for internet banking. Test across Android and iOS versions for mobile banking. Test across different screen resolutions and device types. Verify that financial data displays correctly across all platforms including currency formatting, date formats, and table layouts. Test that banking forms work correctly including date pickers, dropdowns, and file uploads. For Indian banking, include testing on popular Indian device brands and on UC Browser which has notable market share. Verify that multi-language interfaces render correctly across all supported platforms.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Technical' },
    { id: 'IQ-TST-009', question: 'What is the importance of data-driven testing for banking applications?', answer: 'Data-driven testing separates test data from test logic enabling the same test case to run with hundreds of input combinations. For banking, this is valuable because financial calculations must be verified across many account types, interest rates, tenures, and amounts. A single interest calculation test function can be driven by data covering savings, FD, RD, and loan accounts with varying parameters. This ensures comprehensive coverage of business rules with minimal test code. For Indian banking, data-driven testing is essential for verifying transaction processing across all payment types, amount boundaries, and customer categories defined by RBI and NPCI.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Concept' }
  ],
  'ui-ux-testing-lab': [
    { id: 'IQ-UUX-001', question: 'What are the key UI/UX testing areas for banking applications?', answer: 'Key areas include layout and visual design consistency across banking pages, navigation flow intuitiveness for banking workflows, form usability for complex banking inputs like fund transfer and loan application, data visualization clarity for account dashboards and transaction history, error message helpfulness and positioning, responsive design across devices, loading state feedback during transaction processing, and accessibility compliance. For Indian banking, additional areas include Rupee currency formatting, multi-language interface testing, UPI QR code display quality, and financial data table readability on mobile screens.', difficulty: 'Easy', category: 'Concept' },
    { id: 'IQ-UUX-002', question: 'How do you test usability of banking transaction workflows?', answer: 'Evaluate the number of steps required to complete a transaction and whether they can be reduced. Test that the workflow provides clear progress indicators showing the current step. Verify that validation errors are shown inline near the relevant field with clear corrective guidance. Test that users can review transaction details before final confirmation. Verify that confirmation screens display all critical information like amount, beneficiary, and reference number. For Indian banking, test that NEFT/RTGS selection is intuitive based on amount, that beneficiary management is straightforward, and that the UPI payment flow matches the seamless experience users expect from third-party UPI apps.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Practical' },
    { id: 'IQ-UUX-003', question: 'How do you perform visual regression testing for banking UI?', answer: 'Capture baseline screenshots of all banking screens in a stable version. After code changes, capture new screenshots and compare pixel-by-pixel against baselines. Use tools like Percy, Applitools, or BackstopJS for automated visual comparison. Configure acceptable diff thresholds to ignore anti-aliasing and rendering differences. Focus on critical banking screens like account dashboard, fund transfer form, and transaction history. For Indian banking, pay attention to currency amount formatting, account number masking display, transaction status color coding, and financial chart rendering. Review visual diffs manually for financial data displays where even small changes could confuse customers.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Technical' },
    { id: 'IQ-UUX-004', question: 'How do you test error handling UX in banking applications?', answer: 'Verify that error messages are displayed prominently near the source of the error. Test that error text uses customer-friendly language avoiding technical jargon. Verify that errors provide actionable guidance for resolution. Test that form errors do not clear previously entered valid data. Verify that transaction failure screens include the transaction reference for customer support follow-up. Test that network error handling shows retry options. For Indian banking, verify that CBS error codes are translated to meaningful customer messages, that NPCI response codes are properly interpreted, and that session timeout warnings give sufficient time to complete in-progress transactions.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Practical' },
    { id: 'IQ-UUX-005', question: 'How do you test the mobile banking user experience?', answer: 'Test one-handed usability ensuring critical actions are reachable with thumb on large screens. Verify that touch targets for banking buttons are at least 48dp. Test gesture-based interactions like swipe to view more accounts. Verify that the virtual keyboard does not obscure important form fields during data entry. Test that the app responds to system font size changes without breaking layouts. For Indian banking, test the UPI payment experience comparing smoothness and speed with competitor apps. Verify that the quick balance feature is easily accessible. Test that biometric login provides a fast and secure experience. Evaluate whether frequently used banking functions are accessible within two taps from the home screen.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Scenario' },
    { id: 'IQ-UUX-006', question: 'How do you test loading states and feedback in banking applications?', answer: 'Verify that all asynchronous operations show appropriate loading indicators. Test that loading spinners appear within 100ms of action initiation. Verify that long-running operations like report generation show progress indicators. Test that the UI remains responsive during background operations. Verify that skeleton screens are used for data loading on dashboards. Test that optimistic UI updates are eventually confirmed by server responses. For Indian banking, test loading feedback during CBS communication delays, UPI payment processing which should show real-time status updates, and statement generation for large date ranges. Verify that users cannot accidentally submit duplicate transactions by clicking during loading states.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Technical' },
    { id: 'IQ-UUX-007', question: 'How do you conduct usability testing with banking customers?', answer: 'Recruit representative banking customers including different age groups, tech-savviness levels, and banking needs. Design task-based scenarios like transfer Rs 5000 to your sister using UPI. Observe users completing tasks without guidance, noting confusion points, errors, and workarounds. Measure task completion rate, time on task, and error rate. Collect qualitative feedback through post-task interviews. For Indian banking, include participants from both urban and rural demographics, test with users comfortable in different languages, and include senior citizens who may face specific usability challenges. Use findings to prioritize UX improvements for the next release.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Behavioral' },
    { id: 'IQ-UUX-008', question: 'How do you test multi-language UI in Indian banking applications?', answer: 'Verify that all UI text is properly translated in each supported language including Hindi, Tamil, Telugu, and other regional languages. Test that translated text does not overflow UI containers since some languages require more space than English. Verify that currency, date, and number formatting follows regional conventions. Test language switching mid-session ensuring all content updates consistently. Verify that mixed-language content like English transaction types with Hindi labels renders correctly. Test that right-to-left languages if supported are properly handled. Validate that all banking terminology is correctly translated by domain experts rather than just machine translation.', difficulty: 'Hard', category: 'Practical' },
    { id: 'IQ-UUX-009', question: 'How do you test data visualization in banking dashboards?', answer: 'Verify that account balance charts accurately represent the underlying data. Test that transaction category pie charts add up to 100% of total spending. Verify that trend lines correctly show spending or saving patterns over time. Test chart interactivity including hover tooltips, zoom, and drill-down. Verify that charts are responsive and readable on mobile screens. Test with extreme data sets including zero transactions, thousands of transactions, and outlier amounts. For Indian banking, test that spending analytics correctly categorize UPI, card, and cash transactions, that investment portfolio visualizations accurately show mutual fund and FD distribution, and that loan repayment progress charts reflect actual EMI payment status.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Technical' }
  ],
  'use-case-editor': [
    { id: 'IQ-UCE-001', question: 'What is a use case editor and how does it support banking test design?', answer: 'A use case editor is a tool that helps create, manage, and visualize banking use cases that describe how users interact with the banking system. It captures actors like customer, teller, and manager, their goals, preconditions, main flow, alternative flows, and exception flows. For banking test design, use cases provide a structured basis for deriving test scenarios. Each flow path through a use case maps to one or more test cases. The editor helps ensure that all banking scenarios are documented and that no flow paths are missed during test case derivation.', difficulty: 'Easy', category: 'Concept' },
    { id: 'IQ-UCE-002', question: 'How do you create banking use cases for test scenario derivation?', answer: 'Start by identifying the actor and their banking goal. Document preconditions like the customer must be authenticated and have an active savings account. Write the main success flow step by step for the standard banking transaction. Add alternative flows for valid variations like choosing NEFT versus RTGS based on amount. Document exception flows for error conditions like insufficient balance and invalid beneficiary. For Indian banking, create use cases for all major banking operations including account management, fund transfers, loan processing, and regulatory compliance. Include flows specific to Indian banking like Aadhaar-based KYC, UPI payment, and NEFT batch processing.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Practical' },
    { id: 'IQ-UCE-003', question: 'How do you derive test cases from banking use cases?', answer: 'Map each flow path through the use case to at least one test case. The main success flow becomes the primary positive test case. Each alternative flow generates additional positive test cases covering different valid paths. Each exception flow generates negative test cases verifying error handling. Boundary conditions at decision points generate boundary value test cases. For Indian banking, a fund transfer use case might generate 20-30 test cases covering different payment types, amounts at NEFT/RTGS boundaries, various error conditions, and different customer types. Use a test case derivation matrix to ensure traceability from each use case flow to its test cases.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Technical' },
    { id: 'IQ-UCE-004', question: 'How do you manage use cases for evolving banking requirements?', answer: 'Maintain use cases in a version-controlled repository alongside the banking application code. Update use cases when requirements change due to new RBI guidelines, product modifications, or customer feedback. Track changes with clear versioning showing what was added, modified, or removed and why. Review impacted test cases whenever use cases are updated. For Indian banking, use case updates are frequently triggered by RBI circulars, NPCI specification changes, and competitive feature additions. Maintain a change log linking use case modifications to the driving requirement change. Ensure that test teams are notified of use case changes so they can update test cases accordingly.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Behavioral' },
    { id: 'IQ-UCE-005', question: 'How do you write use cases for complex banking workflows like loan origination?', answer: 'Break the complex workflow into multiple connected use cases. The loan origination workflow might include use cases for loan application submission, document collection, credit assessment, loan sanctioning, legal documentation, and disbursement. Each use case has its own flows and exception handling. Define the relationships and dependencies between use cases. For Indian banking loan origination, include use cases for CIBIL score verification, property valuation for secured loans, CERSAI charge registration, and regulatory compliance checks. Document the complete use case chain showing how data flows from one use case to the next and what triggers each subsequent use case.', difficulty: 'Hard', category: 'Scenario' },
    { id: 'IQ-UCE-006', question: 'How do use case diagrams help visualize banking system interactions?', answer: 'Use case diagrams show actors (customers, tellers, systems) and their interactions with the banking system at a high level. They help identify all system users and their banking functions. Include and extend relationships show shared and optional behavior. System boundary boxes delineate what is inside the banking application versus external systems. For Indian banking, create diagrams showing customer interactions with internet banking, teller interactions with CBS, and system interactions with NPCI, RBI reporting, and credit bureaus. These diagrams provide a quick overview for stakeholders and help identify gaps in test coverage.', difficulty: 'Easy', category: 'Concept' },
    { id: 'IQ-UCE-007', question: 'How do you review use cases with banking domain experts?', answer: 'Schedule focused review sessions with banking operations staff, business analysts, and compliance officers. Walk through each use case flow verifying that the documented behavior matches actual banking practices. Confirm that all exception flows are identified including rare but important scenarios. Validate business rules and regulatory requirements referenced in the use cases. For Indian banking, involve compliance officers for use cases involving PMLA, KYC, and regulatory reporting. Involve operations staff for transaction processing use cases to capture real-world scenarios that business analysts might miss. Document review feedback and update use cases accordingly.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Behavioral' },
    { id: 'IQ-UCE-008', question: 'How do you use use cases for estimating banking testing effort?', answer: 'Count the number of use cases and their complexity based on the number of flows and decision points. Estimate test cases per use case based on historical averages for similar banking functionality. Factor in the testing types needed per use case like functional, security, and performance. Account for integration complexity with CBS, NPCI, and other external systems. For Indian banking, add effort for regulatory compliance testing, multi-language testing, and cross-channel consistency testing. Use the use case-based estimate as a bottom-up complement to top-down estimation techniques. Present effort estimates with the use case inventory as supporting evidence.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Practical' },
    { id: 'IQ-UCE-009', question: 'How do you integrate use case management with test management tools?', answer: 'Link use cases in the documentation system to test cases in the test management tool like Jira/Xray or TestRail. Implement traceability so that each test case references the use case flow it validates. Generate coverage reports showing which use case flows have corresponding test cases and their execution status. Alert when use cases are updated so that affected test cases can be reviewed. For banking, this integration provides auditors with clear evidence of comprehensive testing by showing the link from banking requirement through use case through test case to test execution result. Maintain bidirectional traceability for complete audit trail.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Technical' }
  ],
  'web-accessibility-lab': [
    { id: 'IQ-WAL-001', question: 'What is web accessibility testing and why is it mandatory for banking?', answer: 'Web accessibility testing verifies that banking web applications are usable by people with disabilities including visual, auditory, motor, and cognitive impairments. RBI has issued guidelines requiring banks to make digital banking services accessible to all customers. The Rights of Persons with Disabilities Act 2016 mandates equal access to services including banking. Inaccessible banking websites exclude millions of potential customers and expose banks to legal liability. Testing covers WCAG 2.1 Level AA compliance including perceivable content, operable interfaces, understandable information, and robust technical implementation.', difficulty: 'Easy', category: 'Concept' },
    { id: 'IQ-WAL-002', question: 'How do you perform automated accessibility testing for banking web portals?', answer: 'Integrate axe-core or pa11y into the test automation framework for automated WCAG rule checking. Configure Lighthouse accessibility audits in the CI/CD pipeline. Use WAVE browser extension for visual accessibility feedback during manual testing. Run automated scans on all banking pages including login, dashboard, transfer, statements, and settings. For Indian banking, scan multi-language pages to verify accessibility in both English and regional language versions. Note that automated tools catch about 30-40% of accessibility issues. Complement with manual testing for keyboard navigation, screen reader compatibility, and cognitive accessibility that automated tools cannot evaluate.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Technical' },
    { id: 'IQ-WAL-003', question: 'How do you test keyboard accessibility for banking applications?', answer: 'Navigate through every banking workflow using only the keyboard (Tab, Shift+Tab, Enter, Space, Escape, Arrow keys). Verify that all interactive elements receive visible focus indicators. Test that the focus order follows a logical reading sequence. Verify that keyboard shortcuts do not conflict with screen reader shortcuts. Test that modal dialogs properly trap and return focus. For Indian banking, test complete keyboard navigation for fund transfer including beneficiary selection, amount entry, and OTP verification. Test keyboard accessibility for complex banking components like date range pickers in statement filters, dropdown menus for account selection, and expandable transaction detail rows.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Practical' },
    { id: 'IQ-WAL-004', question: 'How do you test screen reader compatibility for banking financial data?', answer: 'Use NVDA or JAWS on Windows and VoiceOver on macOS to test banking pages. Verify that account balances are announced with proper currency context like fifty thousand rupees. Test that transaction tables have proper header associations so screen readers announce column headers with each cell value. Verify that transaction status indicators like success and failed have text alternatives. Test that dynamic content updates like real-time balance changes are announced using ARIA live regions. For Indian banking, verify that IFSC codes, account numbers, and transaction reference numbers are announced character by character for accuracy. Test that masked Aadhaar and PAN fields are announced appropriately.', difficulty: 'Hard', category: 'Technical' },
    { id: 'IQ-WAL-005', question: 'How do you test color contrast and visual accessibility for banking?', answer: 'Verify that all text meets WCAG AA contrast ratios of 4.5:1 for normal text and 3:1 for large text. Test that information is not conveyed through color alone by verifying that transaction status uses icons or text labels in addition to colors. Verify that the banking interface is usable in high contrast mode. Test that text is readable when zoomed to 200% without loss of functionality. For Indian banking, pay special attention to contrast in financial data tables, chart legends, and transaction status indicators. Verify that red for debit and green for credit have sufficient contrast against the background and include additional indicators like plus and minus signs.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Practical' },
    { id: 'IQ-WAL-006', question: 'How do you test form accessibility in banking applications?', answer: 'Verify that all form fields have associated labels using the label element or ARIA. Test that required fields are indicated both visually and programmatically. Verify that error messages are associated with the relevant field and announced by screen readers. Test that form validation does not rely solely on color changes. Verify that complex form controls like date pickers and amount spinners are keyboard accessible and screen reader compatible. For Indian banking, test accessibility of Aadhaar and PAN input fields, IFSC code lookup fields, and multi-step fund transfer forms. Verify that auto-fill suggestions for beneficiary selection are accessible.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Technical' },
    { id: 'IQ-WAL-007', question: 'How do you test ARIA implementation in banking single-page applications?', answer: 'Verify that ARIA roles, states, and properties are correctly applied to custom banking UI components. Test that ARIA live regions announce dynamic content updates like transaction confirmations and balance changes. Verify that ARIA landmarks help screen reader users navigate between banking page sections. Test that custom dropdown menus, tab panels, and accordion components follow WAI-ARIA authoring practices. For Indian banking, test ARIA implementation for UPI payment status updates, real-time transaction notifications, and dynamic form sections that appear based on transaction type selection. Verify that ARIA attributes are not misused which can worsen accessibility rather than improve it.', difficulty: 'Hard', category: 'Technical' },
    { id: 'IQ-WAL-008', question: 'How do you create an accessibility testing report for banking compliance?', answer: 'Document findings against WCAG 2.1 success criteria organized by principle (perceivable, operable, understandable, robust). For each finding, include the affected banking page, the specific WCAG criterion violated, a description of the issue, its impact on users with disabilities, screenshots or video recordings demonstrating the issue, and recommended remediation. Prioritize findings by severity and frequency of the affected banking workflow. For Indian banking, map findings to RBI accessibility guidelines and the Rights of Persons with Disabilities Act requirements. Include a VPAT (Voluntary Product Accessibility Template) conformance report for formal compliance documentation.', difficulty: 'Hard', category: 'Practical' },
    { id: 'IQ-WAL-009', question: 'How do you integrate accessibility testing into the banking development lifecycle?', answer: 'Implement shift-left accessibility by including WCAG requirements in banking feature specifications. Train developers on accessible coding practices for financial applications. Integrate automated accessibility scanning into the CI/CD pipeline failing builds on critical violations. Include accessibility testing in the definition of done for each sprint. Conduct manual accessibility testing with screen readers during each testing cycle. For Indian banking, establish accessibility champions within each banking module development team. Create a reusable accessibility test suite for common banking components. Track accessibility metrics over time showing improvement trends. Include accessibility testing in pre-release checklists.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Behavioral' }
  ],
  'web-app-testing-hub': [
    { id: 'IQ-WAT-001', question: 'What is a web application testing hub and how does it serve banking testing teams?', answer: 'A web application testing hub is a centralized platform that provides tools, resources, and infrastructure for testing banking web applications. It includes test environment provisioning, test data management, automated test execution infrastructure, cross-browser testing capabilities, test result aggregation, and reporting dashboards. For banking teams, the hub provides standardized testing practices, reusable test components for common banking workflows, and centralized visibility into testing status across all web banking applications including internet banking, customer portals, and admin interfaces.', difficulty: 'Easy', category: 'Concept' },
    { id: 'IQ-WAT-002', question: 'How do you test web application security for banking portals?', answer: 'Implement comprehensive security testing covering authentication bypass attempts, session management vulnerabilities, XSS in all input fields, CSRF on state-changing operations, SQL injection in search and filter parameters, and insecure direct object references for account access. Test HTTP security headers including CSP, HSTS, and X-Frame-Options. Verify TLS configuration and certificate management. For Indian banking, focus on internet banking portal security including OTP validation, transaction signing, and multi-factor authentication. Test that the banking portal is protected against common web attacks identified in the OWASP Top 10 and banking-specific attack vectors.', difficulty: 'Hard', category: 'Technical' },
    { id: 'IQ-WAT-003', question: 'How do you test web application performance for banking portals?', answer: 'Measure page load times for critical banking pages including login, dashboard, and transaction pages. Test Core Web Vitals including Largest Contentful Paint, First Input Delay, and Cumulative Layout Shift. Monitor JavaScript execution time for complex banking operations. Test API response times from the browser perspective. Measure performance under concurrent user load simulating peak banking hours. For Indian banking, test performance on Indian internet connections with varying bandwidth. Test that the banking portal degrades gracefully on slow connections showing content progressively rather than blank screens. Optimize for the large percentage of users accessing from mobile browsers.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Technical' },
    { id: 'IQ-WAT-004', question: 'How do you test single-page application architecture for banking?', answer: 'Test client-side routing ensuring that banking URLs are bookmarkable and shareable. Verify that browser back and forward buttons work correctly across banking workflows. Test deep linking to specific banking pages like a particular account statement. Verify that page state is correctly managed during navigation. Test that data is properly cached and refreshed to show current account information. For Indian banking, test that SPA navigation does not break banking session management, that financial data is consistently displayed across route changes, and that the application handles authentication token refresh seamlessly during long banking sessions.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Practical' },
    { id: 'IQ-WAT-005', question: 'How do you test web application error handling for banking portals?', answer: 'Test that 404 pages for invalid banking URLs provide helpful navigation. Verify that 500 errors show user-friendly messages without technical details. Test that network errors during transactions show clear status with recovery options. Verify that JavaScript runtime errors do not crash the banking application. Test that validation errors are clearly associated with form fields. For Indian banking, test error handling for CBS unavailability showing maintenance messages, for NPCI timeout providing transaction status check options, and for session expiry redirecting to login with a clear message. Verify that errors during fund transfer leave the customer with a clear understanding of whether the transaction was processed.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Scenario' },
    { id: 'IQ-WAT-006', question: 'How do you test responsive web design for banking applications?', answer: 'Test at standard breakpoints for desktop, tablet, and mobile viewports. Verify that banking navigation adapts properly with hamburger menus on mobile. Test that financial data tables transform to card layouts or horizontal scroll on small screens. Verify that banking forms are usable with appropriate input types like numeric keyboards for amount fields on mobile. Test that transaction confirmation dialogs are fully visible on all screen sizes. For Indian banking, test responsive behavior for account dashboards with multiple account types, transaction history with many columns, and complex banking forms like loan applications.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Practical' },
    { id: 'IQ-WAT-007', question: 'How do you test web application caching for banking portals?', answer: 'Verify that sensitive banking data is not cached by the browser using appropriate Cache-Control headers. Test that static assets like CSS and JavaScript are cached with proper versioning for performance. Verify that after logout, cached banking pages are not accessible through the browser back button. Test that service worker caching for PWA banking apps does not serve stale financial data. For Indian banking, verify that account balances and transaction lists always show current data from the server rather than cached versions. Test that banking session data is properly cleared from all browser storage on logout including local storage, session storage, and service worker caches.', difficulty: 'Hard', category: 'Technical' },
    { id: 'IQ-WAT-008', question: 'How do you test third-party integrations in banking web applications?', answer: 'Test analytics integration like Google Analytics ensuring it does not capture sensitive banking data. Verify chat widget integration for customer support operates securely within the banking portal. Test payment gateway integration for bill payments and credit card payments. Verify that third-party JavaScript libraries are loaded securely and do not introduce vulnerabilities. Test Content Security Policy to restrict unauthorized scripts. For Indian banking, test integration with UPI intent handling for web payments, DigiLocker integration for document verification, and credit bureau integration for instant credit score display. Verify that third-party components comply with the banks security standards.', difficulty: 'Hard', category: 'Scenario' },
    { id: 'IQ-WAT-009', question: 'How do you organize and manage web testing activities for a banking testing hub?', answer: 'Create a structured testing program covering all web banking applications with defined test suites, execution schedules, and reporting cadences. Implement a centralized test asset repository with reusable test components for common banking patterns. Establish browser and device compatibility matrices maintained and updated quarterly. Set up automated regression suites running on CI/CD triggers. For Indian banking, organize testing activities by banking module with dedicated teams and shared infrastructure. Create a testing knowledge base with banking-specific testing patterns, common defect catalogs, and troubleshooting guides. Generate weekly quality dashboards for management showing web application quality trends across all banking portals.', difficulty: 'Medium', category: 'Behavioral' }
  ],
};

